"_Ai et al.-2025-Integrating Image Recognition, S.txt","Integrating Image Recognition, Sentiment Analysis, and UWB Tracking for Urban Heritage Tourism: A Multimodal Case Study in Macau",D. Ai; D. Kuang; Y. Tao; F. Zeng,,2025,journal article,Sustainability,"MDPI, Basel, Switzerland",https://doi.org/10.3390/su17177573,https://doi.org/10.3390/ su17177573,,,Cultural Heritage; Urban Heritage,cultural; historic,historic districts; urban cultural heritage; Macau’s Historic Centre; UNESCO World Heritage Site; Company of Jesus Square; A-Ma Temple; Senado Square; historic urban centers,heritage conservation; heritage authenticity; UNESCO World Heritage Site; cultural heritage; historic; cultural; religious; historical; cultural identity; cultural symbolism; cultural visibility; cultural facilities; cultural hybridity; cultural activities; cultural expression; cultural atmosphere; cultural contexts; cultural spatial revitalization; cultural immersion; cultural governance; cultural showcases; cultural attachment; cultural legibility; cultural heritage laws,,spiritual; emotional; economic; educational; symbolic; artistic; historic; aesthetic,heritage protection; heritage conservation; urban governance; crowd-management strategies; heritage revitalization; wayfinding,Macau’s Historic Centre; Company of Jesus Square; A-Ma Temple; Senado Square; Barra Square; St. Dominic’s Square; Lilau Square; Cathedral Square; St. Augustine’s Square; Camoes Square; St. Paul’s; Na Tcha Temple; Leal Senado Building; Holy House of Mercy; St. Anthony’s Church; St. Joseph’s Seminary and Church; Dom Pedro V Theatre; St. Lazarus’ Theatre,Macau; New York; Seoul; China,Portuguese Baroque,UNESCO inscription in 2005,"While existing studies have focused on macro-level tourism patterns or specific data types, there remains a lack of fine-grained micro-spatial research integrating diverse data sources [9].","1. How can multimodal social media data be leveraged to accurately characterize tourists’ spatial behaviors and emotional responses in historic districts? 2. Are there identifiable patterns of coupling among spatial structure, movement behavior, and emotional expression? 3. How can such empirical insights be translated into actionable strategies for heritage protection and urban governance?",Weibo; Instagram; Trip Advisor,images; text; geotagged metadata; UGC,geotagged posts,spatial hotspots; spatial clustering; spatial distribution; spatial heterogeneity,temporal sensitivity; temporal patterns,,,,,7932,1910,,,,March 2023 to March 2024,Historic Centre of Macau,Using the Weibo Open Platform API,Weibo Open Platform API,"manual de-duplication (hash + near-duplicate screening), semantic relevance filtering, and location validity checks within the study polygon","manual de-duplication (hash + near-duplicate screening), semantic relevance filtering, and location validity checks within the study polygon","semantic relevance filtering, and location validity checks within the study polygon","Posts were labeled into three sentiment categories: positive, neutral, and negative.",,"['positive', 'neutral', 'negative']",,object detection; sentiment analysis; classification,Transformer models,YOLOv8; BERT,"pre-trained YOLO-based image recognition model, fine-tuned BERT model",,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,"['tourist mobility networks', 'social-network analysis']",,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],heat maps,"['Historical buildings accounted for 14.75% of all recognized elements, representing the dominant visual category in the historic center.', 'Commercial spaces were the most frequently identified visual element, comprising 27.00% of all images.', 'Dining-related elements were the second most represented category at 18.26%.', 'Cultural facilities comprised 8.26% of the imagery', 'Urban ecological elements were the least frequently identified, comprising just 1.62% of total image content.']",[],"['Terms such as “Company of Jesus Square” (16.77%), “A-Ma Temple” (15.61%), “church,” and “cultural heritage” function as key cultural anchors.', 'These words are often accompanied by evaluative terms like “solemn,” “peaceful,” and “worth seeing,” reflecting a deep emotional connection to religious structures, collective memory, and localized traditions.', 'Dining venues often co-occur with “streets” and “tourist photo,” underscoring their role as hybrid spaces for both social interaction and spatial experience—reinforcing Macau’s identity as a “City of Gastronomy.”']","['overall emotions expressed in Macau’s historic center are predominantly positive (58%), followed by neutral (35%) and negative (7%) sentiments.', 'Senado Square exhibits intense and polarized emotional expression, manifesting a classic ‘dual-peak’ pattern. Negative sentiment keywords include “crowded,” “noisy,” and “queuing,” while positive expressions are dominated by “romantic,” “lighting,” and “aesthetic.”', 'St. Augustine’s Square and Camoes Square are characterized by low-density but highquality emotional environments, often described with terms such as “tranquil,” “photo spot,” and “surprising,” indicating their potential as alternative experiential spaces.', 'Barra Square is frequently associated with “solemn,” “peaceful,” and “historical,” reflecting a blend of cultural and religious identity.', 'Company of Jesus Square, while visually prominent, exhibited a relatively neutral emotional profile, suggesting opportunities to enhance its affective resonance.']",[],"['negative sentiment increases significantly during holidays, whereas positive sentiment becomes more prevalent during off-peak periods.']",['scenario testing for crowding mitigation and narrative enhancement'],"['strengthening accessibility and continuity, removing barriers, and enhancing wayfinding and amenities can integrate routes, nodes, and edges into a cohesive structure.', 'Policy incentives for local artisans, creative industries, and communitybased food services can thicken the spatial economy and cultural atmosphere.']","['The integration of image recognition, BERT-based sentiment, and UWB trajectories within a georegistered digital-twin yields reproducible, fine-grained insights that single-source approaches cannot provide, enabling threshold detection and scenario testing for design and management.']",[],[],"['Sentiment analysis was constrained by the capacity of the BERT model, suggesting that multidimensional and multilingual schemes could enhance classification accuracy.', 'The trajectory dataset was limited by the composition of volunteers and the observation duration; integrating mobile application logs or Bluetooth-based positioning could expand spatial and temporal coverage.', 'The one-year observation window did not capture responses to seasonal festivals or unexpected events.', 'Reliance on Chinese social media facilitated data access but introduced language–platform bias, potentially underrepresenting non-Chinese visitor segments; integrating multilingual, multi-platform sources could improve representativeness and cross-cultural generalizability.']",['The trajectory data were anonymized.'],"['Reliance on Chinese social media facilitated data access but introduced language–platform bias, potentially underrepresenting non-Chinese visitor segments; integrating multilingual, multi-platform sources could improve representativeness and cross-cultural generalizability.']",[],[],[],[],[],"['Sentiment analysis was constrained by the capacity of the BERT model, suggesting that multidimensional and multilingual schemes could enhance classification accuracy.', 'The trajectory dataset was limited by the composition of volunteers and the observation duration; integrating mobile application logs or Bluetooth-based positioning could expand spatial and temporal coverage.', 'The one-year observation window did not capture responses to seasonal festivals or unexpected events.', 'Reliance on Chinese social media facilitated data access but introduced language–platform bias, potentially underrepresenting non-Chinese visitor segments; integrating multilingual, multi-platform sources could improve representativeness and cross-cultural generalizability.']",,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,,,,,,"precision urban governance; geotagged social media images; ultra- wideband (UWB) pedestrian trajectories; Li DAR-derived 3D digital twin; spatial configuration; movement; affect; Visual content; tourist photos; You Only Look Once (YOLOv8); sentiment polarity; Weibo posts; fine-tuned Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Trans- formers (BERT) model; UWB data; fine-grained trajectories; digital twin; iconic landmarks; pedestrian density; positive sentiment; peripheral sites; lower footfall; strong emotional resonance; coupling typologies; tourist experiences; spatial contexts; multimodal research; historic urban centers; reproducible framework; image; text; trajectory data; microscale patterns; spatial configuration; movement intensity; affective expression; experiential quality; Macau’s Historic Centre; empirical testbed; heritage revitalization; wayfinding; crowd-management strategies; emotional geography; digital twin; spatial image theory; cognitive maps; landmarks; paths; edges; nodes; districts; tourism behavior; environmental psychology; tourist experience; cognition; emotion; sampling bias; limited representativeness; weak temporal sensitivity; real-time perceptions; fine-scale mobility; social media proliferation; AI advances; user-generated content (UGC); computer vision; natural-language processing; dense urban morphology; rich intercultural architectural heritage; East–West synthesis; Company of Jesus Square; A-Ma Temple; Senado Square; hybridized cultural, religious, and everyday spaces; urban vitality; spatial saturation; macro-level tourism patterns; fine-grained micro-spatial research; tourism experiences; movement trajectories; cultural perception; affective responses; 