_Aydin-2017-Decoding kashgar Participatory.txt,Decoding Kashgar: Participatory Digital Heritage Making,Aydin,,2017,other,"TVX '17 Adjunct, June 14-16, 2017, Hilversum, Netherlands",ACM,http://dx.doi.org/10.1145/3084289.3084291,http://resources.richesproject.eu/research/taxonomy/terms-and-definitions/,,,digital heritage (7); cultural heritage (5),tangible (2); virtual (2),architectural elements (2); Kashgar (15); Kashgar Old Town (2); historical town; traditional Islamic city; heritage items,authenticity (10); heritage information; heritage value (4); virtual heritage environments; digital heritage applications; digital heritage platform; heritage making; indexical authenticity; digital heritage; cultural capital,,"{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The text discusses the importance of reflecting what locals identify with in the renewal of Kashgar, suggesting a concern for cultural identity and communal value.'}; {'value': 'Historic-Artistic', 'reasoning': ""The text mentions Kashgar being considered one of “the best-preserved example of a traditional Islamic city to be found anywhere in Central Asia”, indicating the city's value as a rare and unique example of historical architecture.""}; {'value': 'Aesthetical', 'reasoning': 'The text describes the commodification of heritage through a top-down authorship, where the new appearance of the historical town operates as a reminder, suggesting a focus on the visual and artistic aspects of the heritage.'}",,Kashgar; Kashgar Old Town,China; Central Asia; Netherlands,,old town’s renewal,"Broadening these circumstances found in Kashgar, what is confronted in this research is unduly loose tests of authenticity in participatory and engaging digital heritage applications.","designing state-of-the-art digital, interactive and online environments for the dissemination of Kashgar’s heritage value to the online community; understanding the dynamics of user-generated digital content; and developing a design-led methodology via online and digital tools to reproduce it in new means.",,user-generated content (7); screenshots; digital model,,,,,location; angle of view; distance,,,,,,,,,,data-mined,,,,,,,[],,association rule mining,,FPGrowth algorithm,,,,user-generated content,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,support; confidence,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],Confidence graph,[],[],[],[],[],[],['improve and evaluate digital heritage applications'],[],"['novel cross-disciplinary methods from the studies of online media, computational architecture and game design and game data mining']",[],['new perspectives on a very real ethical issue of authenticity in heritage making via digital online environments'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,game data mining; virtual Kashgar museum; expressive authenticity; unmediated Cultural Heritage; indexical authenticity,,,
_Azevedo et al.-2021-Innovation on Research Methods.txt,Innovation on Research Methods:,A. Azevedo; M. P. M. Duenas; G. B. Costas,"School of Economics and Management, University of Minho, Braga, Portugal; Lab2PT – Landscape, Heritage and Territory Research Unit, University of Minho, Braga,; University School of Business Studies, University of Vigo, Vigo, Spain",,other,,,,,,,Cultural Heritage,tangible; intangible,maritime cultural landscape (2); maritime (marine) heritage; coastal landscape (2); maritime heritage trail; Celtic village,maritime heritage (2); maritime cultural heritage (2); maritime cultural landscapes; maritime landscape; maritime heritage trail; cultural resources; cultural ecosystem services (CES) (10); cultural services; immovable heritage; movable heritage; intangible heritage; fishing heritage; heritage knowledge; heritage sites; heritage assets; heritage trail; heritage buildings,,"{'value': 'Social (Emotional collective)', 'reasoning': ""The paper discusses the importance of preserving local collective memory and how the materiality and history of port cities affect people's interactions and create ambiances, suggesting a focus on cultural identity and sense of place.""}; {'value': 'Economic (Entertainment)', 'reasoning': 'The paper explores coastal tourism and recreational activities, aiming to enhance social awareness for the coastal landscape and its potential sustainable resources, which can benefit stakeholders besides tourists, such as residents or firms.'}; {'value': 'Political (Educational)', 'reasoning': 'The paper emphasizes the promotion of marine literacy and marine citizenship, suggesting an educational role of heritage assets for political purposes, particularly in conservation policies and civic engagement in environmental stewardship action.'}; {'value': 'Historic (Symbolic)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions historical-maritime centers and port cities embodying elements of past land uses, representing the local collective memory, indicating an association with important past events.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical (Artistic)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses landscape esthetics and beauty, mentioning photography as a way to capture the cognitive landscape of the photographer, suggesting an original product of creativity and imagination.'}; {'value': 'Age (Existential)', 'reasoning': ""The paper refers to the preservation of cities' materiality and history as port cities, affecting how people interact with material elements and create ambiances, representing memory and the lives of past generations.""}",conservation; preservation (2); Integrated Coastal Zone Management (ICZM) (3); management; protection,"La Guardia, Galicia, Spain; Atlantic coast of Galicia; Adriatic coast and islands; Cape Hatteras National Seashore, North Carolina; South Wales; Isla Cristina, Spain; Brunswick County, North Carolina; central coast of Chile; Andalusia, Spain; Saxony, Germany; South West England; Adriatic coast and islands; Riverine zone of Zurich (Switzerland)",Galicia; Spain (6); Portugal (2); Europe; European Union; Andalusia; Euskadi; Catalunya; Adriatic; North Carolina; Chile; South Wales; Germany; Switzerland; Ireland,,,"Research about Marine and coastal CES is scarce compared to other ecosystem service categories (Garcia Rodrigues et al. 2017), so this work aims to ﬁll some of the gaps around CES research. On the other hand, there is insufﬁcient knowledge on CES related to intangible values such as spiritual interactions, inspirational experiences, cultural identity, sense of place, bequest, and existence values (Chan et al. 2012a, b; Garcia Rodrigues et al. 2017; Milcu et al. 2013).","Therefore, this chapter aims to enhance the social awareness for the coastal landscape and their potential sustainable resources that can beneﬁt all categories of stakeholders besides tourists, such as residents or ﬁrms among others.",Instagram (2); Flickr (7); Panoramio,geotagged photographs (5); text; URL,geotagged; GPS; latitude; longitude; coordinates,,date; daily,views,photo ID; owner ID; taken date of photo; title; tags; latitude; longitude; accuracy; number of views; text description; URL,1294,,,,,,,2004 and 2019,La Guardia,"Flickr is accessed through the publicly available Flickr Application Programming Interface (API). This method allows selecting all the geotagged public photographs uploaded on the Flickr social network within a rectangular box around the case study area, deﬁned using real coordinates.",Flickr Application Programming Interface (API),"selecting all the geotagged public photographs uploaded on the Flickr social network within a rectangular box around the case study area, deﬁned using real coordinates",The following step was to validate their geographic location and exclude the photos that did not ﬁt the scope of this research.,Also excluded were photos taken in an indoor context.,content analysis,,[],,word frequency analysis,,,,,,photos’ title,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,City Marine Park; marine literacy; marine citizenship; marine heritage; treasure hunting; destructive underwater salvage work; semiotic processing theories; cognitive landscape; tourist gaze; Portuguese Coastal Saint James Way,,,
_Azevedo-2023-Pull and Push Drivers of Giant-W.txt,"Pull and Push Drivers of Giant-Wave Spectators in Nazaré, Portugal: A Cultural Ecosystem Services Assessment Based on Geo-Tagged Photos",A. Azevedo,,2023,journal article,Land,"MDPI, Basel, Switzerland",10.3390/land12020360,https://doi.org/ 10.3390/land12020360,,14,,,,cultural ecosystem services (CES) (12); maritime knowledge (1),,,,"Nazaré, Portugal; Praia do Norte (Nazaré, Portugal)",Portugal; USA; Germany; Spain; France; Italy; The Netherlands; UK; Africa; Asia; Oceania; Eastern Atlantic Ocean,XIX century,COVID-19 pandemic,"there is a gap in the literature because the assessment and spatial mapping of the cultural ecosystem services provided by this nature attraction were not investigated in previous studies. Moreover, the extant literature did not analyze the relationship between these CES and the push and pull drivers of several groups of individuals/stakeholders who play different roles and perform different activities in this coastal hotspot. Furthermore, few papers have examined the motivations and behaviors of water sport spectators.","RQ1. What kind of cultural ecosystem services (CES) are provided by the Nazaré undersea canyon? In particular, this paper will focus on the recreational, aesthetical, and spiritual CES that are imbedded in the surf tourism experience journey. RQ2.What are the influences of external factors on the spatial distribution of cultural services? RQ3. What are the push/pull motivations and drivers of the giant-wave chasers/spectators? RQ4. How is the giant-wave tourist’s experience journey? This paper provides a new tool for destination managers and tourism operators by proposing a holistic theoretical framework that describes the inﬂuences of the push and pull drivers at different stages of the tourist’s journey.",You Tube (2); Facebook (3); Flickr (5),photos (7); videos (3); text descriptions (2); geotagged photos (3),geotagged photos (3),spatial distribution (3),,views (2); comments (1),photo ID (1); owner ID (1); taken date of photo (1); title (2); tags (2); latitude (2); longitude (2); accuracy (1); number of views (1); text description (2); URL (2),6914 photos (3),165 photographers (1),,6914 (1),,,44 (1),,5 km radio around Nazaré’s lighthouse building (1),API method (3),"Flickr’s API method, called ﬂickr.photos.search (3)","selection of the photos was based on the content analysis of the photo, on the signiﬁcant content of the title, tags, and text descriptions (1)",,"The major criterion included every new portrayed cultural ecosystem service, thus aiming for maximum diversiﬁcation of the CES inventory. (1)",content analysis (2),the author (1),['classiﬁcation taxonomy of marine and coastal CES provided by the Common International Classiﬁcation of Ecosystem services (CICES) v5.1 (2)'],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],"['At the end of the journey, the tourist shares his/her “peak” moments and emotions on social media, thus promoting the electronic word-of-mouth among family, friends, and peers.']",[],[],[],['the spatial distributions of CES are also inﬂuenced by the concentration of inhabitants as suggested by previous studies'],['Tourism operators and business entrepreneurs can use this mapping tool to support their business location decisions.'],"['Public decision-makers, destination marketing organizations, tourism operators, and business entrepreneurs must acknowledge the relevance of journey mapping in order to identify the moments of stress and the touchpoints associated with peak/positive experiences generated by these CES.']",[],[],['this paper proposes a theoretical framework (see Figure 7) describing the push and pull factors that inﬂuence the tourist’s experience journey of the giant-wave spectator/chaser.'],['The comments from the user-generated photos and videos did not provide explicit evidence that giant-wave chasers are conscious of the motivations related to risk recreation and imminent/spectacular death voyeurism.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Data is available on request.,,,,,,giant waves (14); storm chasing (3); risk recreation (10); spectacular death voyeurism (5); awe-inspiring experiences (2); sense of place (7); social relations (10); cultural heritage (6); surf tourism (7); big-wave surfers (5); local residents (3); surf culture (2); electronic word-of-mouth (2); push factors (10); pull drivers (5); tourist gaze (2); tidal bore-watching (1); mortality salience (1); terror management theory (1); storm-chasing tourism (2); peak emotions (1); peak adventure (1); flow state (3); adventure recreation (7); hedonic (2); eudaemonic (2); therapeutic (2); subjective well-being (SWB) (2); Nazaré Canyon (3); Praia do Norte (5); Guinness records (2); World Surf League (WSL) awards (1); HBO documentary “One hundred-foot wave” (1); Statistics Portugal (2); Nazaré Qualiﬁca (1); Hydrographic Institute (2); Portuguese Navy (1); Visitportugal (1); Tudor Nazaré Tow Surﬁng Challenge (1),,,
_Azizifard et al.-2022-Wiki Loves Monuments Crowdsourc.txt,Wiki Loves Monuments: Crowdsourcing the Collective Image of the,N. Azizifard; Riccardo Volpato; Giovanni Quattrone; Nic Weber; Dario Taraborelli,,2022,journal article,"ACM Journal on Computing and Cultural Heritage, Vol. 16, No. 1, Article 20",ACM,,http://wikilovesmonuments.org. 2https://commons.wikimedia.org/.,,,Cultural heritage (12); built cultural heritage (6); digital cultural heritage (3); tangible cultural heritage (2); immovable heritage (1); natural heritage (2); maritime heritage (1); landscape (1); immaterial heritage (1),tangible; immovable; natural; immaterial,built heritage component (1); architectural heritage (4); historical sites (2); historic city centers (1); archaeological site (1); heritage sites (3); monuments (3); listed building (1); scheduled monument (1); monument historique (1),"promotion and preservation (1); cultural institutions (2); built environment (1); digital acquisition, storage, conservation, representation, and promotion (1); UNESCO World Heritage sites (1); heritage discourses (1); antiquity laws (2); national heritage (1); heritage organizations (1); heritage lists (1); Freedom of Panorama (1)",,,preservation (1); conservation (2),,Netherlands (2); United Kingdom (1); France (2); Brazil (2); Europe (4); Asia (2); North/South America (1); Africa (2); Egypt (1); European countries (1); Anglosphere (1); Poland (2); European language (1); Thailand (2); Georgia (2); Serbia (3); South American (1); Colombia (3); Argentina (4); Chile (5); Uruguay (3); Bolivia (2); Southeast Asia (1); China (3); Nepal (2); Ireland (2); Philippines (2); Brazil (2); South Africa (2); Ukraine (3); Italy (3); United States (2); Armenia (2); Estonia (2); Algeria (2); Hungary (2); North and South Italy (1); Belgium (2); Germany (2); India (2); Canada (3); Romania (2); Belarus (2); Austria (2); Portugal (2); Iran (2); Pakistan (2); Bangladesh (2),19th century (1),,"In this work, we focus on the Wiki Loves Monuments1 (WLM) initiative, an annual, international photography contest in which volunteers are invited to take pictures of built cultural heritage and upload them to Wikimedia Commons,2 a sister project and the shared media repository of Wikipedia.","To characterize the impact of the competition, we provide a quantitative analysis of the WLM photographic collection along the dimensions of space, time, and topical coverage.",online social networks (2); multimedia sharing platforms (2); Wikipedia (6); Wikimedia Commons (5); Facebook (1); Twitter (1),images (10); photos (18); metadata (5); textual reference (1); EXIF data (1),geographical coordinates (2),,time frame (2010–2021) (1); temporal variability (1); temporal evolution (1),,identifier (1); filename (1); geographical coordinates (1),2.8M pictures (2),86K participants (1),,2.66M images (1),,,,2010–2021 (2),93 national competitions (1),collect all the submitted photos with the corresponding metadata (1),,"randomly sample up to 1,000 photos as the representatives of the activity of a participant (1)","Images were downloaded at 300px format on the longest side and resized to a 224px or 150px square format, according to the input requirements of the vision pipelines in Sections 3.1 and 4. (1)",only countries with at least 5K images remaining are considered (1),,,[],,scene recognition (3); object detection (2); multi-class classification (3),convolutional neural network (CNN) (3); deep learning (4),VGG16 (2); Xception (4); Image Net (3); Places365 (6),pre-trained on Image Net (2),visual features (1); visual vectors (2); feature vectors (2),,image pixels (1),,80–20 split for training and test sets; 20% of the training data is held as a validation set for hyperparameter tuning (1),,,1e−4 (1),,,,,{},SVM (1); random forest baselines (1),,,,,,,"we freeze the model weights except for the top layer, which is customized to solve our multi-class problem. At last, we run a fine-tuning routine, which consists of unfreezing the entire model from the previous step and re-training it on the WLM data with a low learning rate (1e−4) (1)",no,[],,,,,,,,,,Precision (2); recall (2); F1-score (4); MCC (3); accuracy (3),"['F1-score of 0.45 (1)', 'MCC = 0.44 (1)', 'a photo’s true country is\n63% in the top 3 and 71% in the top 5 predicted countries (1)']",[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['assisting in the maintenance and suggestion of labels in the Wikimedia universe (1)'],[],[],[],[],"['Due to the original objective of the competition, which is limited to built cultural heritage, this study is not able to fully characterize the different dimensions within the cultural heritage concept, focusing only on a fraction of it (1)', 'The data used in this study suffers from a variety of sample biases that are intrinsic to the nature of the contest and the platform used for their collection (1)', 'The image processing pipeline adopted in our work is based on deep convolutional neural network architectures that do not easily provide human-readable explanations for their choices (1)']",[],['The sites that are selected to be included in these lists are often selected by conservative mechanisms that may favor colonial-era heritage and limited viewpoints of what constitutes heritage (1)'],[],[],[],[],"['copyright restrictions (1)', 'lack of Freedom of Panorama (1)']",[],,,,,,Keras (1); Python (2),,digital gateway (2); crowdsourcing (3); collaborative online platforms (1); digital libraries (2); digital tools (1); information and communication technologies (ICTs) (1); Semantic Web technologies (1); Io T systems (1); Augmented Reality (AR) (2); Virtual Reality (VR) (2); memory work (1); visual arts (1); transfer learning (4); scene detection (2); photogrammetric analysis (1); historical buildings classification (1); deep learning model (2); artistic styles (3); art historians (3); iconologist (2); chrono-typology (1); archaeologist (2); digital humanities (2); stylistics (1); Big Data (1); d