oads', 'public cultural facilities', 'green space parks', 'traffic nodes', 'scientific and education', 'commercial entertainment', 'special buildings', 'rural landscapes', 'natural landscapes']",[],[],[],[],['hotspot spaces of the city image show obvious aggregation characteristics and are distributed mainly in the central urban area'],[],"['the urban imagery of the natural landscape should be strengthened', 'the completeness and accessibility of the spatial structure of urban imagery should be improved', 'we should focus on the inheritance of traditional culture']",['constructed a city image perception model'],[],[],"['owing to the age limitation of social media users, 72% of users are between 18 and 34 years old, which can lead to the neglect of the perceptions of the elderly and underage children', 'human perceptions are also affected by subjective factors such as past experiences, level of education, and family culture']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,Arc GIS; Open CV; Python; Jieba; s DNA,,city image; social media; spatial perception; big data; Nanjing; check-in data; POI data; road network; street view images; functional perception; visual perception; emotion perception; traffic perception; GWR; kernel density analysis; spatial nonstationarity; geographically weighted regression; night-time light; mixing degree of functional areas; pyramid scene phrase network; PSPNet; text sentiment analysis model; Spatial Design Network Analytics; connectivity; closeness; betweenness; Kernel density estimation; KDE; OLS; covariance test; VIF; sky view factor; streetscape greenery; streetscape color; SHDI; BTE 1000; MED 3200; MED 6000; MED 8500,,,
_Bai et al.-2024-Semantics-enriched spatiotempora.txt,Semantics-enriched spatiotemporal mapping of public risk perceptions for cultural heritage during radical events,Nan Bai; Pirouz Nourian; Tao Cheng; Ana Pereira Roders,"UNESCO Chair in Heritage and Values: Heritage and the Reshaping of Urban Conservation for Sustainability, Department of Architectural Engineering and Technology, Delft University of Technology, 2628BL Delft, The Netherlands; Public Administration and Policy Group, Wageningen University and Research, 6706KN Wageningen, The Netherlands; Faculty of Geo-Information Science and Earth Observation, University of Twente, 7522NH Enschede, The Netherlands; Space Time Lab, Department of Civil, Environmental and Geomatic Engineer, University College London, Gower Street, WC1E 6BT London, United Kingdom",2024,journal article,International Journal of Disaster Risk Reduction,Elsevier Ltd.,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijdrr.2024.104857,https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-47971044,,,Cultural Heritage (3); World Heritage (7); UNESCO World Heritage (2),cultural (2); tangible (1),World Heritage site (1); built cultural heritage (1); Historic Urban Landscape (1),Outstanding Universal Value (OUV) (2); cultural significance (4); heritage values (1); urban conservation (1); disaster risk preparedness (1); response (2); recovery (2); Historic Urban Landscape (1); conservation (1); protection (1); preservation (1); heritage management (4); heritage at risk (1); UNESCO (2); WH (2),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study analyzes public opinions and emotional attachments shared on social media concerning Heritage-Related Events, indicating a focus on cultural identity, motivation, and a sense of place attachment.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study aims to extract useful information for heritage managers from unstructured social media data, which can be used to educate and inform about past events and their impact on cultural heritage.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Management – The study explores how social media data can help heritage administrators make more informed and inclusive decisions for heritage at risk, indicating an incorporation of the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Notable – The study focuses on the Notre Dame fire and the Venice flood, both of which are notable examples of cultural heritage sites with artistic and architectural significance.'}",urban conservation (2); risk management (3); disaster risk management (2); heritage management (2),Notre Dame de Paris; Venice,The Netherlands; London; France (3); Turkey (1); Syria (1); Venice (5); Italy (2); North and South Carolina (1); EU countries (1); United States (2); United Kingdom (1); China (1),,"fire in Notre Dame de Paris, and its tower designed by Eugène Viollet-le-Duc in April 2019, the terrible earthquake in Turkey and Syria destroying ancient UNESCO World Heritage (WH) sites in February 2023, or the more regular occasions of floods in Venice","In the digital age, however, new challenges and opportunities are created by ubiquitous and large-scale user-generated data, which has not yet been addressed in the documents mentioned above.",This paper aims to explore the online public discussions during Heritage-Related Events (HREs) and propose a methodological framework to extract useful information for heritage managers from unstructured social media data.,Twitter (7); Weibo (1); Tik Tok (1),text (4); images (2); geo-tagged tweets (2); emoji’s (1),geo-tagged tweets (2); geo-locations (4); latitude and longitude (3); geo-ID (1),spatiotemporal patterns (3); spatial representation (1); spatial pattern (3); spatial aspects (1),timeline (2); time-stamped posts (1); temporal (5); timestamp (2); temporal trends (1),,user ID (2); language code (1),"In Notre Dame fire, the total number of tweets 𝐾= |D| = 198, 061, while in Venice flood, 𝐾= |D| = 15,641","In Notre Dame fire, the number of users || = 42,036, while in Venice flood, || = 3541",,,,,,"A period of two weeks (one week before the event, and one week after), i.e., 08–22 April 2019 for the Notre Dame fire, and 05–19 November 2019 for the Venice flood",global scale,A three-step procedure was followed to collect the raw data,Twitter API v2,"local search first queries for geo-tagged tweets in a fixed radius from the hypothetical core of HREs, i.e., 1.5 km from the Cathedral of Notre-Dame de Paris (48.852737N 2.350699E) and 8 km from the centre of Venetian Island (45.438759N 12.327145E); A global search then queries for geo-tagged tweets that also mentioned the name of the place in several languages (not the event, thus not with words ‘‘flood’’ and ‘‘fire’’)","Tweet sentences are tokenized with the Tweet Tokenizer11 of NLTK Python library [75]; The tokens are normalized by lower-casing the letters, transforming any user IDs into ‘@USER’ token, changing any internet link into ‘HTTPURL’ token, and de-emojizing the emojis into corresponding verbal descriptions using Emoji Python library12; The normalized tokens are joined back as sentences and translated into English using Google Translator API from the Deep Translator library.",Cities whose names were originally written in another language were translated into English using Google Translator API from the Deep Translator python library,manual,expert,[],,Topic modelling (4); sentiment analysis (3); emotion detection (3); text classification (1),deep-learning models (1); machine learning models (2); Transformer models (2),BERT (4); ULMFiT (3); BERTweet (2),pre-trained,sentence embeddings (1),,textual data (2),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,"['directed multi-graph (1)', 'social network (1)']",tweets (2),conversational links and user links,"In Notre-Dame fire, the number of tweets appearing as nodes in the conversational network || = 179, 758 (79.3% of ||), while in Venice flood, || = 11,961 (76.5% of ||)","In Notre-Dame fire, the number of conversational links including interactions of different users and consecutive posting of the same users |CONV ⋃USER| = 221, 285, while in Venice flood, |CONV ⋃USER| = 12,106",,,,,modelling the conversational sequences and the spatiotemporal contexts,,[],[],[],"Kruskal–Wallis H-tests showed a significant difference across the periods (before, during, and after HREs), 𝐻(2) = 26, 449.3, 𝑝< .001 in Notre-Dame and 𝐻(2) = 374.2, 𝑝< .001 in Venice; Post-hoc two-tailed Mann–Whitney U-tests in Table 1 showed significant differences in the medians of locality among all pairs of HREs periods; Chi-square Contingency tests",,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],timelines (5); Venn Diagram (1); violin plots (1); rank–size plots (1); heatmap (1),[],[],[],[],[],[],['knowledge documentation tool useful for inclusive heritage management and planning under constant climatic and environmental changes including the impacts from disasters'],['facilitating inclusive heritage management processes and reducing risks against cultural significance'],['presents a methodological framework to investigate the collective behaviour on social media when radical Heritage-related events (HREs) happen'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['The data collected in the study was restricted to the ones that were either initially given a geo-location or directly connected to the tweets with geo-locations, which automatically limited the scope of the study since only a small proportion of tweets are geotagged [24].', 'Only collecting data from Twitter users with a few languages also limit the pool of information, which may lead to biases and exclusiveness.']",The code of this work is available on 4TUResearch Data Repository.,,,,,NLTK Python library; Geo Py Python library; Pingouin Python library; BERTopic python library; Scikit-Learn Python library,,Heritage-Related Events (HREs) (8); mediatisation of heritage (1); heritage communities (1); risk perceptions (2); heritage values (1); cultural significance (2); heritage attributes (1); disaster risks (3); social med