_Bigné et al.-2019-Harnessing stakeholder input on.txt,Harnessing stakeholder input on Twitter: A case study of short breaks in Spanish,Enrique Bigné; Enrique Oltra; Luisa Andreu,"University of Valencia, Facultat d’Economia, Department of Marketing, Av. Naranjos s/n 46022 Valencia, Spain.; Instituto Serrallarga, Spain. Address: c/ Joan Benejam, 1. Blanes (Gerona), Spain.; University of Valencia, Facultat d’Economia, Department of Marketing, Av. Naranjos s/n 46022 Valencia, Spain.",2019,journal article,Tourism Management,Elsevier,,,,,,,,,,,,,Spain (3); Spanish (2); US cities,,Easter Week holiday (6),Knowledge of how destination marketing organisations (DMOs) use Twitter is still limited.,This study aimed to assess how DMOs’ Twitter activity affects hotel occupancy in short-break holidays.,Twitter (33); Facebook (4),text (2); tweets (10); retweets (10); replies (6),,,,likes (6); replies (6); retweets (10); followers (10),,,,,,,,,Easter Week 2015 (2 to 5 April) and 2016 (24 to 27 March),10 Spanish town destinations,Twitter application programming interface (API) (2),Twitter application programming interface (API) (2),"10 destinations were selected based on the following three criteria: (1) the volume of Twitter activity between 16 March and 1 April 2015 (the days leading up to Easter Week), (2) type of destination (inland vs. coastal tourism), and (3) geographic location (north, south, east, and west), reflecting diversity.",,,content analysis (2),Two independent experts,"['event', 'tourist attraction', 'socialisation', 'commercial']",94%,classification,artificial neural networks (ANN) (15); multilayer perceptron artificial neural network (2),MLP ANN (3); RBF ANN (2),,,text mining (5),,,training set of seven DMOs and a test set of three DMOs,,,"0.7 and 0.8 for Models t1 and t2, respectively.",,,,,{},,,,,,,"ANN comprised an input layer with 10 predictors, a hidden layer with 4 neurons generated by the program, and an output layer with 1 outcome variable.","The dataset was divided into a training set of seven DMOs and a test set of three DMOs to validate the model. During the training phase, the backpropagation and gradient descent algorithms were used.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,root mean square error (RMSE); R2,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This study was limited by the scope of the variables provided by Twitter.', 'Although this study covers the majority of the formal accommodation offer (i.e. hotels), it overlooks other forms of formal accommodation.', 'Likewise, the study omits the non-formal offer because of a lack of specific information about the occupancy rate by type of tourism offer\n(i.e. formal or non-formal accommodation) over a specific period (i.e. Easter Week) in the chosen destinations.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,,,,
_Bin Rosley et al.-2024-From Urban heritage resources s.txt,From Urban heritage resources s,Bin Rosley; Noor Hanita Abdul Majid; Syahruddin Awang Iskandar; Muhammad Faris Abdullah; Mohd Sabrizaa Mohd Rosley,,2024,journal article,Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/rapt20,,https://doi.org/10.1080/10941665.2024.2379285,https://asean.org/book/asean-tourismstrategic-plan-2016-2025-2/,,1113 1132,Urban heritage (6),tangible (3); cultural (5); intangible (4),heritage buildings (14); historic buildings (6); heritage sites (4); urban heritage resources (5); historic urban areas (1),Outstanding Universal Values (OUV) (1); heritage authenticity (1); heritage integrity (1); heritage tourism (13); National Heritage Act of 2006 (1); heritage conservation (3); heritage planning (1); heritage information (1); heritage sites (2); heritage places (2); historical tourism (1); cultural heritage (4); preservation (4); conservation (3); historical presentation (1); tourism management (2); architectural preservation (1); architectural conservation (1); narrative development (1); visitors’ expectations (1); urban heritage management (1); built heritage resources (1); heritage buildings (1); heritage tourism destination (1),"historical signiﬁcance (3) - When assessing historic buildings, it is essential to consider various factors, including their distinctive architecture, aesthetics, historical signiﬁcance, documentation, archaeological value, economic impact, social implications, political context, spiritual signiﬁcance, and symbolism (Mohd-Isa et al., 2011).; archaeological value (2) - When assessing historic buildings, it is essential to consider various factors, including their distinctive architecture, aesthetics, historical signiﬁcance, documentation, archaeological value, economic impact, social implications, political context, spiritual signiﬁcance, and symbolism (Mohd-Isa et al., 2011).; economic impact (1) - When assessing historic buildings, it is essential to consider various factors, including their distinctive architecture, aesthetics, historical signiﬁcance, documentation, archaeological value, economic impact, social implications, political context, spiritual signiﬁcance, and symbolism (Mohd-Isa et al., 2011).; social implications (1) - When assessing historic buildings, it is essential to consider various factors, including their distinctive architecture, aesthetics, historical signiﬁcance, documentation, archaeological value, economic impact, social implications, political context, spiritual signiﬁcance, and symbolism (Mohd-Isa et al., 2011).; political context (1) - When assessing historic buildings, it is essential to consider various factors, including their distinctive architecture, aesthetics, historical signiﬁcance, documentation, archaeological value, economic impact, social implications, political context, spiritual signiﬁcance, and symbolism (Mohd-Isa et al., 2011).; spiritual signiﬁcance (1) - When assessing historic buildings, it is essential to consider various factors, including their distinctive architecture, aesthetics, historical signiﬁcance, documentation, archaeological value, economic impact, social implications, political context, spiritual signiﬁcance, and symbolism (Mohd-Isa et al., 2011).; symbolism (1) - When assessing historic buildings, it is essential to consider various factors, including their distinctive architecture, aesthetics, historical signiﬁcance, documentation, archaeological value, economic impact, social implications, political context, spiritual signiﬁcance, and symbolism (Mohd-Isa et al., 2011).; architectural value (1) - Despite their architectural value, royal structures attracted fewer tourists due to the absence of diversity, inclusivity, and activities and restrictive measures.; universal value (1) - They also possess a universal value signiﬁcant from historical, artistic, or scientiﬁc perspectives, as outlined in the National Heritage Act of 2006.","Social: Emotional (collective) – The study highlights how heritage sites can enhance a sense of pride and belonging, especially when they reflect a community's identity and history.; Political: Symbolic – The study mentions how heritage sites, particularly royal buildings, can symbolize power and authority, reflecting the historical and political context of the region.; Historical: Educational – The study emphasizes the importance of heritage sites in providing knowledge about the past, especially through their architecture and cultural significance.; Aesthetical: Artistic – The study discusses the aesthetic qualities of heritage buildings, noting how their design, craftsmanship, and materials contribute to their visual appeal and artistic value.; Age: Existential – The study touches on how heritage sites represent the memory and lives of past generations, offering a connection to history and a sense of continuity.",architectural preservation (1); architectural conservation (1),Johor Bahru; Melaka; Penang; Singapore; Istanbul,Malaysia (4); Asian countries (1); Southeast Asian countries (1); ASEAN countries (2); Europe (1),nineteenth century (4),COVID-19 pandemic (2),"Johor Bahru, Malaysia, has been overlooked for heritage tourism due to its industrial reputation.",(1) What are the tourists’ motivations for visiting heritage buildings across Johor Bahru? (2) What values inﬂuence tourists’ experience of perceiving heritage buildings? (3) What is the signiﬁcance of the identiﬁed heritage buildings in projecting Johor Bahru’s urban heritage resources?,Google Review (8); Tripadvisor (10); Google Map (1),"text (posts, captions, comments, reviews, hashtags) (1)",,,,likes (1); comments (1),usernames (1); Internet IP (1); email addresses (1),"2,369",,"2,369 comments",,,,,2021 to 2023,Johor Bahru,web crawlers,Python,,A series of data cleaning steps which were included in data pre-processing procedure were conducted for both Google Review and Tripadvisor data.,,manual,two human raters,[],,sentiment analysis (4); content analysis (10),deep learning techniques (1); natural language processing (NLP) (1),Chat GPT-3.5 (11); VADER (2),,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,precision (1); recall (1); F-score (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],dashboard analysis (1),[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['user privacy (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,industrial reputation; Anglophilia obsession; Modern Johor Sultanate; Malay administrative capital; word-of-mouth; open-source data; Big Data,,,
_Bogdanovych et al.-2011-Developing virtual heritage appl.txt,Developing Virtual Heritage Applications as,A. Bogdanovych,"UTS, Australia",2007,other,,ACM,,http://secondlife.com,,,cultural heritage (4),,ancient artifacts; ancient City of Pompeii; ancient city of Uruk; ancient Mesopotamia,virtual heritage (4); historically signiﬁcant sites; heritage site; heritage oriented; historical; historically authentic; archaeological excavations; historical accurate reconstruction,,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study aims to provide visitors with a more engaging experience by populating virtual heritage sites with avatars that behave similarly to the ancient citizens of the reconstructed places, fostering a sense of cultural identity and place attachment.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study aims to enhance the educational process of history students by providing them with a possibility to immerse into an accurate replica of the daily life of the ancient citizens of Uruk and gain quick understanding of the advance of technological and cultural development of ancient Sumerians.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study involves the 3D reconstruction of historically significant sites and ancient artifacts, aiming to recreate the visual and audio presence of these elements in a virtual world.'}",Preserving,City of Uruk; ancient City of Pompeii,South Korea; Iraq; Mesopotamia,3000 B.C.,,Existing crowd simulation algorithms are not suitable for modeling complex individual behaviors and role dependent agent interactions with other participants in the Virtual World.,To address this problem we sug- gest treating 3D Virtual Worlds as Normative Multiagent Systems and propose the Virtual Institutions Methodology to be used for design and deployment of Virtual Worlds that require complex interactions involv- ing both humans and autonomous agents.,Second Life (4),3D graphics,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[]