_Cao et al.-2022-Explicit quantification of coast.txt,Explicit quantification of coastal cultural ecosystem services: A novel approach based on the content and sentimental analysis of social media,Haojie Cao; Miao Wang; Shiliang Su; Mengjun Kang,"School of Resource and Environmental Sciences, Wuhan University, Wuhan, China; Beijing Key Laboratory of Urban Spatial Information Engineering, Beijing Institute of Surveying and Mapping, Beijing, China",2022,journal article,Ecological Indicators,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2022.108756,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2022.108756,,,,,coastal areas (6); cultural attractions (2); protected areas (2); city waterfront; cultural sites,cultural ecosystem services (CESs) (13); ecosystem integrity; viable economies; coastal ecological conservation policy; sustainable development; environmental protection; coastal protection; sustainable coastal management; coastal ecosystem management (2); coastal ecosystem exploitation; sustainable planning strategies; maintenance of ecosystem services; coastal management (2); coastal recreation,,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study mentions that coastal areas play an essential role in enhancing social cohesion among communities, indicating a discussion of collective emotional value related to cultural identity and sense of place attachment.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment – The study discusses the importance of coastal areas for tourism and recreation, indicating a discussion of entertainment value related to the role of these assets in the contemporary market, especially tourism.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Management – The study discusses the need for efficient countermeasures and an ecosystem service approach for coastal areas, indicating a discussion of management value related to the incorporation of the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study mentions the aesthetic experiences provided by coastal ecosystems, indicating a discussion of artistic value related to the original product of creativity and imagination.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': 'Spiritual – The study mentions the harmony between the building and its natural/artificial environment.'}",coastal management (2); coastal protection,Hong Kong (8); Victoria Harbor (5); Aberdeen Harbor; Tai O (2); Cheung Chau (2); Stanley; Clear Water Bay (2); Tolo Harbor (2); Repulse Bay; Po Toi Islands; Sai Kung; Lantau Island,China; Asia; Europe; North America; Oceania; South America,,,"Thus far, the explicit variation of cultural ecosystem services (CESs) and particularly the sentiments of CES-related ecotourism are not well understood.",(1) identify important CESs of coastal areas through content analysis of social media images and reveal the spatial distribution of CESs; (2) combine sentimental analysis of CESrelated photo metadata (user-generated textual content) into the coastal CESs assessments; (3) quantify the relationship between CESs and related sentiments with temporal and sociodemographic indicators; and (4) provide theoretical guidance for coastal ecosystem management and protection.,social media (7); Instagram (2); Twitter (2); Flickr (11); Weibo,image data (2); textual data (3); geotagged photographs (2); text (4); images (10); metadata (3); visual information (3),geotagged (4); geographic information (2); geolocation metadata,spatial distribution (4); spatial patterns (2); spatial heterogeneity (2),temporal factors (3); temporal patterns (2); visiting time; season,,photo title; description; tag; date data; user’s location; hometown; current locations,"160,629 geotagged photos",3599 users,,"160,629",,,"160,629",between 2004 and 2020,coastal areas of Hong Kong,web-crawler tool,Flickr Application Programming Interface (API),"those with missing attributes such as (1) hometown or current locations and (2) textual content including photo titles, descriptions, and tags were removed","translated all textual content into English. We removed trivial and less meaningful text, such as independent numerals, emoticons, and text referring to photographic information (e.g., image numbering or camera types). In particular, semantically meaningless stop words were discarded, such as ’the’, ’it’, or ’am’, and two widely used stop word lexicons were used for further initialization: the Natural Language Toolkit (https://www.nltk.org/) and Language Technology Platform (https://ltp.ai/docs/index.html).",labels with a score of 0.6 or higher,automated,five professors of ecology,"['recreational', 'aesthetic', 'amenity', 'knowledge', 'naturalistic', 'spiritual']",Cohen’s kappa coefficient,content analysis (4); sentimental analysis (3); natural language processing; regression analysis (2); multiple comparisons analysis (2); object detection,machine learning (4),Google Cloud Vision API; Google Cloud Natural Language API,,,,images; text,,,,,,,,,,{},,cloud computing,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,Cohen’s kappa coefficient; accuracy,['93% overall classification agreement'],[],[],Kruskal–Wallis test; Dunn’s post-hoc test; logistic regression; multivariate regression,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['hotspots of CESs are primarily concentrated in waterfront areas of Victoria Harbor, Aberdeen Harbor, Tai O, Cheung Chau, Stan\xadley, and Clear Water Bay', 'high concentrations of CESs tend to be more spatially concen\xadtrated around both cultural attractions and protected areas', 'night-time travel may be driving positive sentiment']",[],[],['Over 60% of the CES-related photographs and textual content show positive and neutral views of coastal areas in Hong Kong.'],['visitors of different origins differ in their cul\xadtural preferences of coastal ecosystems as well as the spatial and temporal characteristics of their visitation patterns.'],[],[],"['People’s demand for coastal ame\xadnities should be integrated into the policy discussion and into decisionmaking processes to yield better-informed strategies for the manage\xadment of natural capital.', 'govern\xadments should recognize the effects of a vibrant, accessible, and sus\xadtainable waterfront to provide seaside space for all residents and tourists.']",['Our paper takes advantage of pluralistic approaches that combine mapping and statistics to fully analyze differences in how CESs are delivered to diverse groups of visitors drawn from social media activities.'],[],[],"['some subjective judgment is unavoidable in the development of keywords and associations with specific CESs.', 'geotagged photographs uploaded to social media are uneven in distri\xadbution and limited in quantity compared to the huge tourist flows of Hong Kong.', 'digital divides of large-scale studies interfere with research on where ecosystem services actually originate and who is interviewed on social media']",['The results are presented in aggregated form to ensure privacy.'],['digital divides of large-scale studies interfere with research on where ecosystem services actually originate and who is interviewed on social media'],[],[],[],[],[],"['some subjective judgment is unavoidable in the development of keywords and associations with specific CESs.', 'geotagged photographs uploaded to social media are uneven in distri\xadbution and limited in quantity compared to the huge tourist flows of Hong Kong.', 'digital divides of large-scale studies interfere with research on where ecosystem services actually originate and who is interviewed on social media']",,,,,R 4.0.3,Natural Language Toolkit; Language Technology Platform,,blue ecotourism; urban recreations; urban residence structures; coastal biome; sacred landscape; religious tourism; secular pilgrimage; racial and ethnic differences; Application Programming Interface; photo-user-days (PUD); photographic intensity; Arc GIS 10.4; National GeospatialIntelligence Agency; off-the-shelf technologies; semantic image segmentation; latent features; stop word lexicons; sentiment score; magnitude score; mean sentiment rating; mean magnitude scores; nonparametric test; Bonferroni adjustment; pairwise comparisons; nonzero dominance; viewshed,,,
_Cao et al.-2023-Sentiment of Chinese Tourists to.txt,Sentiment of Chinese Tourists to Heritage Sites: A Deep Learning Approach,"Cao, Z.; Xu, H.; Teo, B.S.-X.",,2023,journal article,,,,https://news.bjd.com.cn/2023/01/11/10297412.shtml,,,heritage tourism,,heritage sites; scenic spots; World Heritage City,heritage tourism; heritage sites; World Heritage City,,Historic-Artistic; Aesthetical; Social; Emotional (collective),sustainable development of tourism for the heritage site; heritage protection,Melaka; Stadthuys; Melaka River; St. Paul’s Church; Port Santiago; Jonker Street; Sultanate Palace; Maritime Museum; Dutch Square; Strait Mosque; Uveda; Baessa; Summer Palace in Beijing; Jeju island; Terracotta Warriors of Qin Shihuang; Yin Ruins; Paris; Macau; World Heritage Goreme National Park; Cappadocia Rocks,China; Malaysia; Indonesia; Hong Kong; Australia; Spain,,COVID-19 infection,"Therefore, we must gain a better understanding of tourists’ experiences and behavioral intentions at heritage sites [6].","Therefore, the purpose of this study was to analyze online travel reviews using a deep learning approach to investigate Chinese tourists’ perceptions of the heritage site in Melaka, to reveal the emotional characteristics of Chinese outbound tourists and the factors inﬂuencing positive and negative emotions.",social networks; Facebook; travel advisors; Twitter; Trip Advisor; tourism websites; Qunar,online text; online comments; review texts,,spatio-temporal activities,,,,"17,555",,,,,,,2015 to 2019,Melaka,web crawler technology,Python,,"Python package “jieba” was used for Chinese word separation considering the accuracy and convenience of word separation. Third, Python software was used to remove invalid characters, thereby rendering the dataset clean and tidy.",irrelevant and ambiguous data were removed,,,"['1', '0', '2']",,sentiment analysis; text sentiment classiﬁcation,deep learning; neural networks; convolutional neural networks (CNN); recurrent neural networks (RNN); long shortterm memory (LSTM) networks; Transformer model,BERT; Char CNN; LSTM; Bi LSTM,pre-trained BERT model,word2vec,,text,,80%/20%,,,0.01,,,,,"{'vector dimension': '768', 'sentence length': '300', 'activation function': 'Softmax'}",,,,,,,"The Encoder module in the bi-directional Transformer is used to build the model, and the input text information is extracted by this layer, which discards the circular structure of traditional NLP processing methods, such as RNN, LSTM, and other such models, and effectively solves the problem that the model cannot be processed in parallel and the long-term dependence of the text.","The analytical process is divided into ﬁve steps: (1) data collection, (2) text preprocessing, (3) building the training set, (4) sentiment analysis based on the BERT model, and (5) precision inspection and analysis.",yes,['semantic network'],high-frequency words in tourists’ comments,closeness,,,,co-occurrence analysis on high-frequency words,,Gephi,"capture the connections between the most frequently mentioned words, and thus the content and structure of the many travel reviews related to tourists’ emotions.",Accuracy; Precision; Recall; F1-Score,['predictive accuracy of the BERT-based deep learning approach was 92.78%'],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,Char CNN; LSTM; Bi LSTM,[],,"['architecture', 'history', 'heritage sites', 'landscapes']",[],[],"['Chinese tourists’ emotional perceptions of Melaka were positive, and that they were very interested in heritage sites.']",[],[],[],"['Heritage sites need to strengthen their management and supervision, such as posting peak hours on the website.', 'the government and related departments need to deeply explore the architectural culture and intangible cultural heritage, customs, and other cultural resources contained in multicultural integration, and accurately and deeply reﬁne and publicize them.', 'Enhancing Chinese tourists’ experiences and perceptions of Melaka’s unique architectural culture and historical stories will further enhance Chinese tourists’ curiosity and yearning for foreign culture.', 'Cultural identity and local attachment are essential factors for tourists’ positive feelings.', 'Through elements such as historical monuments, unique natural landscapes, and colorful folk activities, the cultural and spiritual connotations of heritage sites are fully integrated into tourism programs, enhancing the cultural atmosphere of heritage sites, which helps to stimulate tourists’ cultural identity and local attachment, and strengthen the emotional connection between tourists and the heritage site.']","['provides a valuable perspective for tourism perception research using BERT based on deep learning methods, which can reveal critical emotional characteristics and explain tourists’ positive and negative views of a destination.', 'the BERT-based deep learning approach can serve as an interface between big data analysis and tourism management theory, which can help enrich our theoretical knowledge about tourists’ perceptions and extend the current perception theory.']",[],[],"['this study was based on user-generated online content, which may lead to bias and subjectivity in comment content, as users are more often from younger groups', 'the model used for text analysis was unable to identify sarcasm, jokes, or hyperbole; these sentiments were manually identiﬁed.', 'the study utilized comments posted between 2015 and 2019; future studies could compare these data with data from previous years.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Not applicable.,,,,Keras; Sklearn,,vocabulary ﬁltering; co-occurrence analysis; semantic clustering technology; online travel reviews; tourism destination services; GIS-based mobile apps; spatio-temporal activities; congestion problems; Destination sustainability; ﬂow control; overtourism; architectural culture; intangible cultural heritage; customs; cultural resources; multicultural integration; cultural identity; local attachment; historical monuments; natural landscapes; folk activities; cultural and spiritual connotations; cultural atmosphere; emotional connection; Cultural performance; Baba Nyonya; Chetti; multi-ethnic lineages; experiential projects; intangible cultural experience halls; emotional exchanges; ambassadors for the intangible cultural heritage; cultural ecology; heritage resources; heritage protection perspective; conservation agencies; mass tourism activities; expanding experience; personal consumption ability; tourism development; World Heritage Sites; livelihood; technological innovation; adaptation of heritage tourism; artiﬁcial intelligence; robots; classic behavioral methods; complete cognition of tourists,,,
_Cao et al.-2024-Unraveling the relationship betw.txt,R E S E A R C H A R T I C L E ABSTRACT:,,,,journal article,Transactions in GIS,,https://doi.org/10.1111/tgis.13175,https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/tgis.13175,,1065–1089,,,coastal areas (8); coastal landscapes (11); coastal environment (1); coastal ecosystem services (2); coastal destination (1); coastal areas of Hong Kong (2),sustainable development (2); conservation (4); sustainable management (2); ecological (1); ecosystem (2); sustainability (4); ecological concerns (1); natural resources (1); regional ecological protection (1),,"{'SOCIAL': 'The study mentions the importance of understanding and appreciation of the various services provided by natural landscapes, which are affected by environmental beliefs and ecological concerns. This relates to the emotional connection with coastal areas.'}; {'ECONOMIC': 'The study discusses the importance of coastal areas for tourism and related economic activities, which aligns with the entertainment value of heritage assets in the contemporary market.'}; {'AESTHETICAL': 'The study emphasizes the importance of visual aesthetics and emotional resonance in coastal landscapes, aligning with the artistic and conceptual values of heritage assets.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL': 'The study highlights the importance of natural elements, such as the sky and greenery landscapes, in positively affecting coastal landscape perceptions, which aligns with the essential value of ecological ideologies in design and construction.'}",coastal conservation policies (1); coastal planning (4); coastal management (1); ecological protection (1),Hong Kong,Hong Kong; China; Northern Norway; northern Wisconsin in USA,,,"However, little is known about explicitly linking between the landscape characteristics and people's sen- timental preferences expressed in social media data.","The main objective of this study was to explore the nonlinear and interaction effects of key factors that influenced senti- ments in the coastal areas of Hong Kong, considering both subjective landscape preferences and objective landscape patterns.",Flickr (4),text; images; videos; GPS locational data; mobile signal data; participatory mapping data; social media data,geotagged image data; coordinate information; user-generated location information,spatial distribution,photo timestamps,,image ID; user ID; coordinate information; titles; descriptions; tags; photo timestamps; user-generated location information,"280,137 geotagged images as Dataset 1",6879 users,,"280,137",,,,from 2004 to 2022,coastal areas at Hong Kong,We crawled geotagged image data and associated metadata using its application programming interface (API),application programming interface (API),,"Subsequently, we excluded images from the collected dataset that lacked the essential attrib- ute of textual content, including titles, descriptions, or tags.",,machine-generated classes,,[],,classification; topic modeling; sentiment analysis; semantic segmentation,Transformer models; machine learning,Swin Transformer model; Word2vec model; random forest classifier; k-means clustering algorithm; SHapley Additive ex Planations model (SHAP),This model was pretrained by the ADE20K dataset.,word embedding,,textual content; visual content of images; objective landscape patterns,,80% of the Dataset 2 for training and the remaining 20% for testing,,,,,,,,{},k-­nearest neighbors (K-­NN); support vector machine (SVM); gradient boosting classifier,,,,,,,"First image the textual data and natural language possession (NLP) methods. We then computed various visual landscape ob- jects' proportion in each image using a semantic segmentation model. To further analyze the subjective landscape preferences, we extracted the visual object labels and divided UC and IC based on this label corpus and a k-­means clustering algorithms with Word2vec model. To quantify the objective landscape patterns, four types of explana- tory variables were chosen to represent the physical environment of the location of photographic capture. Thirdly, a random forest classification model was employed to predict the sentiment polarity as the dependent variable, with landscape factors serving as independent variables. Finally, SHapley Additive ex Planations model (SHAP) was further utilized to apportion the global as well as the local relative importance of coastal landscape factors and their locality-­specific interactions. Thus, suggestions for spatial planning were formulated.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; precision; recall score,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['spatial planning'],[],['This article demonstrated a novel approach to deciphering the influence of coastal landscape characteristics on human sentiments that integrated social media data with interpretable machine learning methods.'],[],[],"['digital divides can present challenges in researching the actual origin of landscape perceptions, sentimental responses, and the inclusivity of representation from a broader population through social media platforms', 'the exploratory variables were derived from a specific context in a coastal metropolis of Hong Kong, and a more generalized set of indicators can be formulated by exploring other coastal areas that vary in landscape patterns and other socioeconomic contexts.', 'the findings can only uncover the empirical association between coastal landscape characteristics and sentiments rather than define a causal relationship.']",['The presented results are aggregated to safeguard privacy.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.,,,Both the random forest and the SHAP were implemented using the Python language.,scikit-learn package in Python; Gensim library,,sentimental preferences; sentimental states; sentimental polarities; sentimental well-being; subjective well-being; positive sentiments; negative sentiments; neutral 