 Spain (2),,,"Social media data analyses in the context of CES can be time consuming and costly, particularly when based on the manual classification of images or texts shared by people.",In this study we use freely available tools for the automated classification of CES-relevant nature and human elements from the content of crowdsourced social media images.,Flickr (5); Wikiloc (5); Instagram,images (21); geo-tagged images (1),,,time stamps (1),,georeferenced meta-data (1); tags (1); captions (1),1778 images were considered for Peneda-Gerˆes and 745 images for Sierra Nevada.,,,,,,,,Peneda-Gerˆes and Sierra Nevada,Data was collected from two social media platforms,Flickr’s Application Pro­gramming Interface (API) together with Python collection tools,"We considered only images taken outdoors in the wild (i.e., images inside people’s homes or other interior environments were not considered). Likewise, other irrelevant images, such as from advertisements, pamphlets and drawings were also excluded.",,,manually classified,one author (ASC) and was independently verified by two other authors (RML and ASV).,"['“Natural” or “Human”', '“Species”', '“Landscape”', '“Nature”', '“Human activities”', '“Human structures”', '“Posing”']","Inter-annotator agreement initially ranged between 0.58% and 88% (depending on the class). After evaluation of mismatched classifi­ cations, another classification round was done for another independent test set, reaching ~100% for all classes.",classification (14),Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) (7); Very Deep Convolutional Network (VGG16); Residual Neural Network (Res Net152),VGG16 (12); Res Net152 (14); Alex Net,pre-training the networks over large image datasets and then re-training them with the Peneda-Gerˆes data,,,images (10),,90% of the data of the 4 subsets was used for training the model. The remaining 10% was used for model validation,binary cross en­tropy (1),ADAM algorithm (1),10-6 and 10-4,10,100,,rotation (from 0 to 40◦); zoom; horizontal flip; width shift; height shift (range of 0.2),{},,,,,,,"three fully connected layers in VGG16 and one fully connected layer on Res Net152 were re-trained (fine-tuned) using our training dataset (Seresinhe et al., 2017). Moreover, an additional dense layer with 128 units and a rectifier linear unit activation function was included before the output layer","Training a CNN requires a training and a validation dataset. The training dataset is the subset of images that the neural network will use to learn how to classify the input images into different classes (in our case: L1 and L2), by adapting the weights in its layers. The validation dataset is an independent subset of images used to estimate the error of the model during training (accuracy and loss) and to assess the learning progress of the neural network.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (ACC) (3); sensitivity (recall or True Positive Rate: TPR) (2); specificity (True Negative Rate: TNR) (2); F1-score (F1) (4),[],[],[],paired samples t-test (1),,[],[],,[],[],[],k-fold cross-validation (2),,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['can provide meaningful insights on visitors’ preferences, as well as be very useful for the management of these protected areas']",[],[],[],[],"['data protected by social media users’ rights was not considered and public data containing sensitive and personal information (including images with recognizable faces or structures)\nwere treated as confidential or (pseudo-)anonymized', 'Images containing private data were anonymized before being used for scientific pre\xadsentations or other public events', 'Personal information about the user, such as name, ID (identification), age and location, was not extracted and/or considered.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],All code used in model training and test is available at: https://gith ub.com/anasccardoso/Deep-learning-for-cultural-ecosystem-services.,"The dataset of “Sierra Nevada” is available at https://figshare.com/arti cles/Dataset_SN_csv/8943509/2, while t