_Chen et al.-2024-Mining Social Media Data to Capt.txt,Mining Social Media Data to Capture Urban Park Visitors’ Perception of Cultural Ecosystem Services and Landscape Factors,"Chen, Y.; Hong, C.; Yang, Y.; Li, J.; Wang, Y.; Zheng, T.; Zhang, Y.; Shao, F.",,2024,journal article,Forests,MDPI,doi.org/10.3390/f15010213,https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/ 4.0/,,,,,urban parks (17); urban green space (1),cultural heritage (7); historical sites (1),,,environmental conservation (1); planning management (1),Hangzhou (7); Yangtze River Delta (1); Zhejiang Province (1); Xihu District (1); Shangcheng District (1); Binjiang District (1); Gongshu District (1); West Lake Scenic Area (2); Prince Bay Park (1); White Pagoda Park (2); Tongjian Lake Park (1); Eight Diagrams Field Heritage Park (1); Hangzhou Zoo (2); Flower Pool Park (1); Quyuan Garden (1); Hupao Park (1); Hang Zhou Botanical Park (1); Youth & Children’s Park (1); Agoda of Six Harmonies Park (1); Xishan Forest Park (1); Wuchao Mountain Forest Park (1); The Xixi National Westland Park (1); Chengdong Park (1); Orioles Singing in the Willow (1); Bachelor Park (1); Chengbei Sports Park (1); Jiangyangfan Ecological Park (1); Long bridge Park (1); Lakeside Park (1); Yongjin Park (1); Grand Canal Asian Games Park (1); Banshan Forest Park (1); Riverside Park (1),China (3); Europe (1); Germany (1); Canada (1),Southern Song culture (1),Asian Games (1),"Previous studies on CESs and landscape factors in urban parks have primarily focused on a small scale, utilizing offline methods such as face-to-face interviews or participatory mapping [28,29]. These studies often employ manual visual interpretation methods [26,30], which are time-consuming, suitable for small amounts of data, and subject to the inherent subjective biases of researchers [9].",The research aimed to address the following issues: (1) understanding the CES categories of urban parks as well as the satisfaction and expectations of park visitors; (2) effectively extracting the landscape factor preferences of park visitors; and (3) exploring the impact of internal correlations and landscape heterogeneity within CESs.,Weibo (10); Twitter (1); Instagram (1); Sina Weibo (2),text (10); images (10); photos (20); videos (2); metadata (5),,,time range (1),,user gender (1),"62,450 metadata items (1)",over 500 million users (1),"17,266 analyzable data points (1)","17,266 analyzable data points (1)",,,,1 January 2018 to 4 April 2023 (1),25 urban parks in Hangzhou (1),used the Python programming language to obtain data from the Sina Weibo application programming interface (API) (1),Sina Weibo application programming interface (API) (1),"identified and randomly selected User–Day (PUD) photos, i.e., the unique combination of users and dates, to avoid excessive representation of some active users (1)","We used the Python programming language and Excel statistical software to remove news, advertisements, and data without actual content (1)",deleted metadata that did not publish photos (1),manually annotated (2),a coding team of 10 professionals (1),"['39 categories (1)', 'herbaceous flowers (4)', 'arbors (5)', 'lawns (2)', 'lakes (3)', 'birds (3)', 'identification facilities (3)']",recoded them multiple times until we reached a consensus (1),target detection (2); sentiment analysis (3); text classification (1),machine learning (3); computer vision (3); Natural Language Processing (NLP) (4); YOLO (You Only Look Once) series (2); neural network (1),YOLO v5 (8); YOLO v4 (1); SD (1); Mobile Net (1),,,,Weibo comments (1); Weibo photos (2),,3:1 (1),,,,,200 iterations (1),,,{},,,,,,,"The YOLO model consists of four parts: the input end, the backbone (primary network), the neck network, and prediction (output end) (1)","The target recognition process was as follows: (1) The preparation of a photo training set for landscape factor recognition. We randomly selected 4300 photos from the preprocessed photo data and manually annotated them using Labelimg 1.8.5 annotation software. The landscape labels included 39 categories, such as herbaceous flowers, arbors, lawns, lakes, birds, and identification facilities. These preprocessed photos were randomly divided into training and validation sets in a ratio of 3:1. (2) The dataset was trained for 200 iterations, and a balance was reached at 120 iterations.",yes,['social network (1)'],users (1),,,,,,Apriori algorithm (3),,,precision (3); recall (3); m AP@0.5 (2); m AP@[0.5 = 0.95] (2); accuracy (3),[],[],[],Monte Carlo post hoc chi-square tests (1); Spearman correlation analysis (1),,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],confusion matrix (2),[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This study provides a new perspective for evaluating the framework of cultural ecosystems in urban parks, contributing to the coordinated development of public perception and planning management (1)']",[],[],"['limitations related to online data (1)', 'Uploaded data content is influenced by visitor characteristics like education level, gender, income, and visit frequency (1)', 'Social media data mainly reflect the opinions of younger users who publish comments, making it challenging to study the perceptions of users like children and the elderly who do not often comment (1)']",['privacy reasons (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author. The data are not publicly available due to privacy reasons (1),,,Anaconda-Py 3.10 environment (1),Python (10); Excel (2); SPSS (3); IBM SPSS Modeler (3),,landscape heterogeneity (2); landscape factors (25); cultural ecosystem services (CESs) (32); public perception (3); machine learning (1); natural environment (3); ecological systems (1); ecosystem services (5); biodiversity (2); green and blue areas (1); environmental conservation (1); big data (1); landscape features (2); symbolic meanings (1); urban ecosystems (2); land cover types (1); green spaces (1); natural landscapes (1); artificial landscapes (2); water bodi