_Chen et al.-2025-Multi-perspective perception of.txt,Multi-perspective perception of city image in Shanghai via massive short videos,Minxin Chen; Zhen Wei; Kai Cao,"School of Geographic Sciences, East China Normal University, Shanghai, China; Key Laboratory of Geographic Information Science (Ministry of Education), East China Normal University, Shanghai, China; School of Urban Planning and Design, Peking University, Shenzhen, China",2025,journal article,International Journal of Applied Earth Observation and Geoinformation,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jag.2025.104844,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jag.2025.104844,,,,,,,,,,Shanghai,Shanghai; China,,COVID-19 pandemic,"existing studies remain dominated by traditional media analysis, with insufficient examination of how short video platforms represent the spatial, landscape, social, and emotional dimensions of a city’s image","This study proposed an integrated approach combining deep learning and short video analysis to understand city image. This approach synthesized the core di­mensions of city image, including spatial, landscape, social, and emotional factors, offering a comprehensive view of urban perception.",Douyin; Tik Tok; Weibo,videos; text; audio; images,geo-tagging features,spatial density map,timestamps,likes; shares,locations; descriptions,"4,013 raw short videos were collected. Through filtering and preprocessing to remove irrelevant or redundant content, the dataset was refined to 2,180 highquality videos for further analysis.",962 million short video users in China,,"9,255 unique images","4,013 raw short videos",,,January 2019 to September 2022,Shanghai,web scraping tools,,"content extraction was conducted on multiple dates, creating a more comprehensive dataset.","Videos unrelated to Shanghai’s city image, such as breaking news, advertisements, celebrity content, and promotional materials, were excluded. Redundant content, including multiple up­loads by the same user, and videos exceeding five minutes were also removed to align with the study’s focus on short videos.","Videos unrelated to Shanghai’s city image, such as breaking news, advertisements, celebrity content, and promotional materials, were excluded. Redundant content, including multiple up­loads by the same user, and videos exceeding five minutes were also removed to align with the study’s focus on short videos.",manual,,[],,semantic segmentation; clustering; sentiment analysis; pattern recognition; feature extraction,deep learning,U-Net; Snow NLP; Qwen Model; GPT-4 V; Gemini 1.5,"trained on labeled datasets, including Cityscapes for outdoor scenes and the MIT Indoor Scenes dataset for indoor environments",,,video frames; text; audio,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,"The model’s symmetric encoder-decoder structure enables precise localization while preserving detailed spatial information, making it particularly suitable for complex urban scenes.",,,[],,,,,,,,,,Pixel Accuracy (PA); Intersection over Union (Io U); Precision; Recall; F1-score; Hamming distance,"['mean Io U of 0.69', 'pixel accuracy of 76 %']",[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],spatial density map; Heatmap,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['the analysis relied solely on data from a single short video platform.', 'the study focused exclusively on Shanghai, which may limit the generalizability of the findings.']","['Ethical standards and privacy regulations were strictly followed, including anonymizing personal information and ensuring all data was used exclusively for academic research.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The authors do not have permission to share data.,,,,Python,,,,,
_Chen et al.-2025-Unlocking visitor experiences in.txt,Unlocking visitor experiences in cultural heritage sites: an AI-driven framework for sentiment and spatial perception analysis,W. Chen; K. Zhou; B. Hu; Y. Yang; J. Peng; Y. J. Yang; Y. Xu; D. Zhang; R. Zhang,,2025,journal article,npj Herit. Sci.,Springer Nature,https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-025-02014-0,,,,Cultural Heritage (2); Urban Heritage (1),tangible (1); intangible (2),Ciqikou Ancient Town (4); Ciqikou Historic and Cultural Street (1); historic districts (1); historic urban sites (1); built heritage (1); historic urban sites (1); heritage sites (2),ABSA (2); Historic Urban Landscape (HUL) (2); cultural atmosphere (2); historic value (1); cultural values (1); tangible heritage attributes (1); urban conservation plans (1); heritage management (6); heritage sites (2); heritage attributes (1); heritage values (1); heritage buildings (1); heritage resources (1); heritage preservation (2); cultural assets (1); cultural heritage (3); Authenticity of Architectural Heritage (4); Integrity of Architectural Heritage (3); Perception of Intangible Cultural Heritage & Traditional Customs (3); Cultural Heritage Preservation & Protection Measures (1); sustainable heritage management (2); experiential conservation (2); physical conservation (2),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': ""Emotional (collective) – The study discusses the importance of architectural style, cultural atmosphere, and historic value in influencing visitors' emotional responses, indicating a focus on cultural identity and sense of place attachment.""}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Historic-Artistic – The study analyzes visitor sentiment towards architectural authenticity and integrity, suggesting an appreciation for the historical and artistic significance of the heritage site.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study examines visitor perceptions of spatial layout, architectural style, and visual appeal, indicating a consideration of the artistic and aesthetic qualities of the heritage site.'}",urban conservation (1); urban planning (1),Chongqing (1); George Town (1); Malacca (1),China (2); Chinese (1); Malaysian (1); Hunan Province (1); Bucharest (1); Bangla (1); Marathi (1); New Zealand (1); Suzhou (1),,Covid-19 pandemic (1),This study integrates cultural and historical contexts into the analysis of visitor perceptions by focusing on the interplay of visitor sentiment and spatial perceptions.,"The proposed BO-DXGBoost model produces two key outputs: (1) a multi-label classiﬁcation vector indicating all relevant aspects in a given review, and (2) a sentiment labelindicating the overall emotional polarity of the review.",,text (1); social media data (4); user-generated reviews (1); visitor surveys (2),,,Time series analysis (7); seasonal changes (2); seasonal/event-driven sentiment trends (1); temporal patterns (1),,,,,,,,,,,user-generated reviews related to Ciqikou Ancient Town,collected by the authors,,,"data preprocessing, including sentiment classiﬁcation and SHAP interpretability analysis.",,annotated manually,,"['Each review is labeled with one or more aspect categories from a hierarchical framework of 26 themes, and one sentiment score on a ﬁve-point scale ranging from “Negative” to “Positive.”']",,multi-label classification (2); sentiment analysis (13); sentiment classiﬁcation (5); emotion recognition (1),Transformers (1),XGBoost (13); BO-DXGBoost model (4); RF-SMOTE (9); ADASYN (7),,TF–IDF (2); Word2vec (1),,text (2),,,,,"0.292477463040 4986, ‘max_depth’: 8.827098485602953, ‘min_child_weight’: 2.911051996 1044856, ‘n_estimators’: 95.45624180177515",,,,,"{'gamma': '0.10292247147901223', 'learning_rate': '0.292477463040\n4986', 'max_depth': '8.827098485602953', 'min_child_weight': '2.911051996\n1044856', 'n_estimators': '95.45624180177515'}",,,,,,,,Hyperparameter optimization was performed on the two sub-models using Bayesian Optimization to enhance their predictive performance.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,Accuracy (7); Precision (10); Recall (10); F1 Score (10); mean squared error (MSE) (2); Pearson correlation coefﬁcient (2); RMSE (1),"['highest accuracy of 0.744 and F1 score of 0.745 (Table 3).', 'highest accuracy of 0.917, alongwithastrongprecisionscoreof\n0.799 (Table 4).']","['The macro-averaged precision was relatively low (0.62), likely due to the limited data samples for certain categories or the imbalanced distribution of categories.', 'the macro-averaged and weighted average scores were 0.94 and 0.93, respectively (Table 6).']",[],"Reliability was good (Cronbach’s α = 0.912 overall; all subscales α > 0.85), and validity was supported by a KMO of 0.907 and a signiﬁcant Bartlett’s test (p < 0 001) with exploratory factor analysis; r = 0.807, p < 0.001; r = 0.929, p < 0.001; r = 0.939, p < 0.001; r = 0.738, p < 0.001; r = −0.482,p < 0.001; r = −0.449, p < 0.001; r = −0.603, p < 0.001; r = −0.376, p < 0.001; r = −0.734, p < 0.001",,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],confusion matrix (2); scatter and bar chart (2); Heatmap (1); Barchart (1),"['Positive sentiment is prevalent in categories such as Art & Creative\nExperiences, Spatial Layout & Architectural Style, Perception of Cultural\nDiversity & Inclusiveness, and Leisure & Entertainment Activities.', 'Visitors often cited issues of overcrowding, inadequate maintenance, and the detrimental effects of modern renovations on the\nAuthenticity of Architectural Heritage.', 'The over-commercialization of the town—manifested through overpriced services, inconsistent product quality, and the replacement of traditional shops with chain stores—was a recurring theme, with many tourists expressing concerns that it diluted the town’s historical and cultural ambience.']",[],"['Visitors often cited issues of overcrowding, inadequate maintenance, and the detrimental effects of modern renovations on the\nAuthenticity of Architectural Heritage.', 'The over-commercialization of the town—manifested through overpriced services, inconsistent product quality, and the replacement of traditional shops with chain stores—was a recurring theme, with many tourists expressing concerns that it diluted the town’s historical and cultural ambience.']",[],[],[],"['inform resource allocation or interpretive programming at historic urban sites.', 'reﬁne marketing strategies and on-site interventions to enhance visitor satisfaction and protect heritage values.']","['pinpoint speciﬁc aspects of a site—such as architectural style, accessibility, or visitor services—that inﬂuence satisfaction']",['integration of Bayesian Optimization with XGBoost’s cascading multi-task architecture presents an innovative methodology for sentiment analysis.'],[],['shift from a traditional focus on physical conservation to experiential conservation emphasizes the emotional and cultural signiﬁcance of heritage sites.'],"['One signiﬁcant limitation is the biases inherent in social media data, where only a subset of visitors who choose to express their opinions online are represented.', 'Another limitation pertains to the applicability of the model in different cultural and linguistic contexts.', 'Lastly,thedivisionof spatial satisfaction dimensions used in this study, which includes 26\nheritage-speciﬁc attributes, may not be exhaustive.']",[],['Inherent Biases in Social Media Data'],[],[],[],[],['Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-No Derivatives 4.0 International License'],"['Inherent Biases in Social Media Data', 'Applicability in Cross-Cultural Contexts', 'Completenessof Spatial Satisfaction Dimensions']",The code used in this study is available upon reasonable request from the corresponding author.,"The datasets generated during the current study were collected by the authors and are not publicly available, but they can be obtained from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.",,,,,,visitor perceptions (4); spatial perceptions (3); visitor sentiment (4); sentiment volatility (1); experiential conservation (2); physical conservation (2); SHAP analysis (