_Chen et al.-2025-Using geo-data and social media.txt,Using geo-data and social media images to explore the supply and demand of cultural ecosystem services for terraces in China,Song Chen; Xiyue Wang; Tianming Liu; Mingwei Xie; Qing Lin,"School of Landscape Architecture, Beijing Forestry University, Beijing, China; The Bartlett Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis, University College London, London, UK",2025,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier B V,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2025.101778,www.elsevier.com/locate/ecoser,,,Cultural Heritage (6),tangible; intangible; cultural,terraces (30); cultural landscapes (3); historic agricultural landscapes; traditional farming civilization; historic cultural towns; traditional villages; agricultural heritage sites,cultural ecosystem services (CES) (10); World Cultural Heritage sites; Globally Important Agricultural Heritage Systems (GIAHS) (4); intangible cultural heritage (2); cultural heritage value; cultural heritage resources; cultural protection mechanisms; built heritage; cultural heritage; cultural tourism industry; cultural damage,,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) - The study discusses the cultural identity and sense of place attachment associated with terraces, particularly in regions like Zhejiang-Fujian Hills and Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau, indicating a collective emotional value.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Historic-Artistic - The paper mentions ancient terraces and their unique natural landscapes, settlement patterns, and intangible cultural heritage, suggesting a historic-artistic value due to their representation of historical and artistic movements.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic - The study focuses on the aesthetic services provided by terraces, particularly the scenic attractiveness of paddy fields and the visual appeal of terrace shapes, indicating an artistic value related to creativity and imagination.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment - The paper discusses the tourism potential of terraces and their role as tourist destinations, indicating an entertainment value related to the contemporary market and tourism.'}",planning (3); management (4); protection (4); conservation (4); terrace protection zones; terrace protection system,Ziquejie Terraces; Longji Rice Terraces; Honghe Hani Terraces; Jiabang Terraces; Tang’an Terraces; Yunhe Terraces; Huangling Terraces; Jiangling Terraces,China (14); Southeast Asia; Loess Plateau; Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau (4); southeastern hills; East Asia; Spain; Zhejiang-Fujian Hills (3); Fujian; Jiangxi; Hunan; Guangxi; Guizhou (2); Yunnan (3); Chongqing (2); Gansu (2); Shanxi; Hebei (2); Europe; Japan; Guangdong; Shaanxi; Hainan; Beijing (3); Africa,ancient,,"there is still a lack of a comprehensive and effective large-scale CES assessment framework for terraces, with most studies focusing on urban parks and scenic areas.","(1) How to build a complete framework to integrate diverse geo-data to effectively evaluate the TCES supply? (2) Can an automatic image classification model for terrace be generated, and can the correspondence between image and CES be evaluated to support TCES demand evaluation? (3) Is there an imbalance in the supply and demand of TCES in China, and is there a spatial autocorrelation between them?",Weibo (3); Sina Weibo (2),geotagged social media images (7); photographs; geo-data; population data; raster data; vector point data,geotagged; geographic location; latitude and longitude coordinates,spatial autocorrelation; spatial clustering; spatial dispersion; geospatial distribution,timeframe; time,,location names; latitude and longitude coordinates,"55,616 images (3)",588 million active monthly users,,"55,616 images (3)",,,"55,616 geotagged images",July 2022 to June 2024,mainland China,collected using Weibo’s cookie and Python collection tools,Weibo’s cookie and Python collection tools,"The search keyword used was “terraces,” and the timeframe was set from July 2022 to June 2024","images of poor quality (e.g., those synthesized from multiple images, cluttered, or very blurry) were removed, as well as irrelevant images such as advertisements, paintings, or text.","images of poor quality (e.g., those synthesized from multiple images, cluttered, or very blurry) were removed, as well as irrelevant images such as advertisements, paintings, or text.",manually annotated,,"['landscape', 'species', 'structures', 'indoor', 'food', 'activities', 'posing']",,image classification (4),Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) (5),Efficient Net-B3 model (4); Res Net (3); VGG (3); Res Net152; VGG16,Image Net pre-trained weights (3),,,images,,"64 %, 16 %, and 20 %",,Adam optimizer,1 × 10−5,,10 epochs,L2 regularization factor,rotation (between −10◦to 10◦); brightness adjustment (within a range of 0.1),{},,Google Colab,,,,,,"All model training procedures were executed in Google Colab (https://colab.research.google.com) using the Py Torch deep learning framework (https://pytorch.org). We used the Efficient Net-B3 model (Tan, 2019), a highly efficient CNN architecture developed by Google team. At the same time, we employed transfer learning by leveraging Image Net pre-trained weights (Krizhevsky et al., 2012). To adapt the model to our classification task, we replaced the final fully connected layer with a new one featuring seven output categories (landscape, species, structures, indoor, food, activities, and posing). We set the training to run for 10 epochs, using the Adam optimizer To prevent overfitting we tuned the learning rate and L2 regularization factor iteratively to obtain the best model. Addition­ally, we implemented a Step LR learning rate scheduler to reduce the learning rate to 0.1 times its original value every 5 epochs.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,overall accuracy (2); precision (3); recall (3); F1-score (3),['0.9210 accuracy'],[],[],"chi-square test (χ2 = 266.306, p < 0.001); Logistic regression",,[],[],,[],[],[],"the dataset was split into training, validation, and test sets with a ratio of 64 %, 16 %, and 20 %, respectively.",,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['This study also demonstrates that transfer learning can significantly improve image classifi\xadcation in CES research by producing more robust and reliable CNN models.'],[],[],"['variability in data availability and quality', 'bias in social media user demographics', 'subjectivity in image interpretation', 'geographic inaccuracies in geotagging']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],available at https://github.com/chen7092/Deep-learning-for-cultural-ecosystemservices-of-terraces,available at https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.28813259.v2,,https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.28813259.v2,Py Torch deep learning framework,PyTorch,,agro-ecosystem (3); nature-culture-coupled ecosystem service; paddy fields; drylands; biocultural diversity; Step LR learning rate scheduler; cookie; Python; shapefile point elements; queen contiguity weight matrix; permutation test; bivariate LISA clusters; megaregions,,,
