_Demessance et al.-2021-Hidden Markov Model to Predict T.txt,Hidden Markov Model to Predict Tourists Visited,,,2021,conference paper,2021 22nd IEEE International Conference on Mobile Data Management (MDM),IEEE,10.1109/MDM52706.2021.00041,,,,,,,tourist behavior (3),,,,Paris (4); capital city of France,"France; Taiwan; Hong Kong; United States; Germany; Beijing, China (2); Wuhan, China; Jeju Island, South Korea; Philip Island, Australia",,,Predicting the next movement of tourists plays a key role in tourism marketing to understand demand and improve decision support.,"In this paper, we propose a method to understand and to learn tourists’ movements based on social network data analysis to predict future movements.",Tripadvisor (2); Booking; Facebook; Instagram; Flickr (3),photos (3); reviews (3); recommendations; videos; geolocated data (2); geo-localized and temporal data information; temporal sequences of places,"geographical information; geolocated data; geo-tagged photos (2); coordinates (lat, long)",,time series (2); time series analysis; timeline; chronological order; temporal sequences,,nationality; age,1′063′447 reviews,,,,,,,from 2013 to 2019,Paris,data extracted from Tripadvisor,,,"After data processing, we extract from 1′063′447 reviews 11′471 sequences of at least 2 items.",sequences of only 1 item are removed because they do not show the movement of a tourist.,,,[],,prediction (10),neural networks (2); deep learning (3); machine learning (7); Hidden Markov Model (HMM) (11); Stochastic Automaton,,,,,temporal sequences; geo-localized and temporal data information,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,The method relies on a machine learning grammatical inference algorithm. A major contribution in this paper is to adapt the grammatical inference algorithm to the context of big data. Our method produces a hidden Markov model representing the movements of a group of tourists. The hidden Markov model is ﬂexible and editable with new data.,yes,"['social networks (2)', 'weighted oriented graph', 'directed and rooted hierarchical tree']",nodes and arcs weighted by probabilities,An arc between two nodes represents the probability of reaching the ﬁnal node as the next element in a sequence.,599 nodes,556,,"The learning process, named Grammatical Inference, consists of inducing rules, called Grammatical Rules, from the sequence set. From these, a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) is built. An HMM is a graph with nodes and arcs weighted by probabilities.",Viterbi algorithm; Baum-Welch algorithm,,to represent all tourists’ movements in a data set,Absolute Percent Error (APE) (4),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['tourism marketing'],[],"['A method to establish sequences representing a unique stay of a tourist from a data set', 'A new method of grammatical inference for processing very large data set', 'A ﬂexible and relevant decision-making tool to represent all tourists’ movements in a data set.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],complete source code and detail of results and ﬁgures are provided in a Git Hub folder,,,https://github.com/Theo Demessance/Hidden-Markov-Model-for-Tourism,,,,grammatical inference (3); tourist’s stay (2); tourist movements (4); tourist behavior (2); tourist next visits (2); tourist arrivals; Points of Interest,,,
_Deng and Li-2018-Feeling a destination through th.txt,Feeling a destination through the “right” photos: A machine learning,Ning Deng; Xiang (Robert) Li,"Department of Tourism Ecommerce and Marketing, School of Tourism Management, Beijing International Studies University, Beijing, 100024, PR China; School of Tourism and Hospitality Management, Temple University, Philadelphia, PA 19121, USA",2017,journal article,Tourism Management,Elsevier Ltd.,https://doi org/10 1016/j tourman 201709 010,www.elsevier.com/locate/tourman,,,,,destination,destination image (TDI) (6); destination marketing; user-generated content (UGC) (3); cognitive image (7); affective image (9),,,,New York City (NYC) (7); Brooklyn; Queens; Manhattan; The Bronx; Staten Island,PR China; USA; North America; New York State; Korea; China; Samoan; Portugal; Australia; New Zealand,,,"Currently, efﬁciently selecting appropriate photos for destination promotion remains a major challenge for DMOs, a problem closely related to the discrepancy between projected and received destination images.",This project proposes and implements a machine learning-based model to assist DMOs with photo content selection.,Flickr (13); Instagram,photos (17); metadata (8); comments (12); text,"location coordinates (i.e., the latitude and longitude where a photo was taken)",,"time information (i.e., when a photo was taken)",,title; tags; description; device type; photo ID,"over 20,000",,,99.2 million,0.8 million,,"48,366,323",from 2004 to 2014,NYC,download comments for a speciﬁc photo,python-based Flickr API,photos taken by foreign visitors whose self-reported residences were outside of the United States,ﬁltered the metadata in YFCC 100M by the preset geographic boundary,,manual,human judges,"['“relevant”', '“not sure”', '“irrelevant”']",,classification,,naïve Bayes classiﬁer (3),,,,metadata; comments,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,mean average precision (MAP) (3); normalized discounted cumulative gain (n DCG) (5); average precision,"['The mean value of the average precision of the top 10 VAC was 0.3835, which decreased to 0.2955 over the entire set of VAC.']",[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['photo selection'],[],"['This study utilized a data mining method based on millions of social network photos and comments.', 'this study marks the ﬁrst attempt to adopt a machine learning model for photo-based destination promotion.']",[],[],['the experiment was probably effected by sparseness of comments'],['All human faces in the images have been obscured to protect subjects’ privacy.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,Yahoo Flickr Creative Commons 100M” (YFCC 100M); Textblob; Senti Word Net; Carto; ARC GIS,,,
_Deng-2024-Discovering Perceived Images of.txt,Discovering Perceived Images,H. Deng,"Heritage, Anthropology and Technologies Group, College of Humanities, EPFL, Lausanne, Switzerland",,journal article,,,,,,,industrial heritage (9); cultural heritage (6),intangible; cultural; historic; listed,industrial heritage sites (2); industrial heritage transformation (4); industrial infrastructure; historical buildings; historic relics,collective memory (2); cultural heritage values; heritage values; UNESCO World Heritage site; industrial esthetic; historic relics,,"{'Social': 'Emotional (collective) – The study mentions emotional attachment to places and shared memory, indicating a sense of cultural identity and communal value associated with the industrial heritage sites.'}; {'Historic': 'Symbolic – The study discusses the role of industrial heritage in connecting past memories, suggesting an association with important past events and the history of industrial production.'}; {'Aesthetical': 'Artistic – The study explores the esthetic preference for “industrial style” and the visual impact of industrial objects, indicating an appreciation for the artistic qualities of industrial heritage.'}",heritage transformation (2); urban transformation (2); reuse (2); renovation (2),Shougang Steel Factories in Beijing; Shougang Park; Parco Dora in Turin; Cockatoo Island in Sydney,Beijing; China; Turin; Italy; Sydney; New South Wales; Australia,,Beijing 2022 Olympic Winter Games,designers and decision-makers face the challenge of determining which features and elements of industrial heritage are more perceived and preferred by the gen- eral public and will shape the future character of the site,"(1) Before and after the transformation triggered by mega-events, what are the changes and consistency in the perceived images? (2) In the three cases with different cultural backgrounds and similar physical environments, are there common highly perceived images, and if so, what are they? What are the characteristics of the scene or formal composition? To what extent are they perceived?",Flickr (3); Instagram (2); Weibo (2),photograph; image content; text; geolocation; timestamp; social network structure; multimodal datasets,geotags,spatial distribution,,,geotags; comments; public user profiles,around 3000 user-generated photographs for each case,,,,,,,from pre-event to post-event,"Shougang Park, Parco Dora, Cockatoo Island","collected through Python by invoking Flickr API, Instagram Basic Display API, and Weibo (微博) API","Flickr API, Instagram Basic Display API, and Weibo (微博) API","photographs are collected under several criteria: (a) the photographs are uploaded with the location tag of the case; (b) the photographs are uploaded by ordinary users instead of regular media or official account; (c) the selfies or portraits without enough environmental content will be excluded from the dataset; (d) the time when the first mega-event was held is used as the dividing point of data, in order to compare the changes between pre-event and on/post-event period",excluding selfies or portraits without enough environmental content,photographs uploaded with the location tag of the case; photographs uploaded by ordinary users instead of regular media or official account; selfies or portraits without enough environmental content; time when the first mega-event was held,manual labeling of each clustered photograph group based on the spatial features,,"['architectural elements', 'industrial elements', 'cultural elements', 'consumption elements', 'landscape elements']",,similarity clustering; image classification; content recognition; semantic segmentation,convolutional neural networks (CNN); deep learning algorithms; unsupervised learning algorithms; machine learning algorithms,Res Net; SENet; WRN; VGG19; K-means clustering,pre-trained model,,,visual content of photographs,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['macro scale-skyline with landmarks', 'medium scale-special geometric form and iconic objects', 'micro scale-machine and cultural symbols']",[],[],[],[],[],"['can be utilized by the designer and decisionmaker of industrial heritage renewal projects, aiming to establish a closer perceptual attachment between visitors and the physical space and connect on-site experience with past memories']","['Spatial features with higher/lower perceptions can be extracted and evaluated quantitatively, which allows decision-makers to understand the use of industrial heritage sites and to plan the future use of the sites in a targeted way: applying higher perceived spatial features to similar types of industrial heritage transformation projects in the future; or improving forgotten and neglected sites.']",['methods and computer script constructed by this research can be applied to more cases and groups of people in future research to further reveal “what” in space can be more perceived and preferred by people and quantitative measure the degree of perception'],['mega-events and the urban transformation triggered by them could play a role in changing the image of the industrial heritage site'],[],['there is no further distinction between the characteristics of photograph uploaders'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['The limitation of this research is that there is no further distinction between the characteristics of photograph uploaders.'],,The data used for this research are from public photograph-sharing platforms,,,,Python,,mega-event; user-generated photographs (UGPs); place attachment; bottom-up perspective; spatial experience; industrial style; crowdsourced knowledge,,,This research was funded by the SNSF project funding “Uses of Cultural Heritage at the Beijing Winter Olympic Games of 2022” (2020-2024).
_Depietri et al.-2025-Leveraging AI and social media f.txt,Leveraging AI and social media for actionable insights for nature park management,Yaella Depietri; Andrea Ghermandi; Liat Hadar; Daniel E. Orenstein,"Natural Resources and Environmental Research Center, University of Haifa, 199 Aba Khoushy Ave., Mount Carmel, Haifa, 3498838, Israel; Faculty of Architecture and Town Planning, Technion – Israel Institute of Technology, Haifa, 3200003, Israel; School of Environmental Sciences, University of Haifa, 199 Aba Khoushy Ave., Mount Carmel, Haifa, 3498838, Israel; Scientific Research Department, Ramat Hanadiv, P.O.B 325, Zikhron Ya’akov, 3095202, Israel",2025,journal article,Journal of Outdoor Recreation and Tourism,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jort.2025.100923,www.elsevier.com/locate/jort,,,,,Ramat Hanadiv Nature Park (2); Memorial Garden (4),,,,,Ramat Hanadiv Nature Park; Ramat Hanadiv Memorial Garden,Israel; South Africa; Nanjing; China; Wuhan; Singapore; Europe; New York City,,,,"1. Explore the potential of novel AI tools, including those based on GLLMs, for the analysis of emotions expressed in user-generated social media texts posted by park visitors, to support management purposes. 2. Combine information about the content of social media photographs derived from computer vision with socio-demographic user infor­ mation to characterize the interests of different segments of the population of park visitors. 3. Assess the potential application of the results obtained with these methods in collaboration with park managers.",social media platforms (10); Facebook (2); Instagram (4); Twitter (now X) (2); Google Reviews; Panoramio; Sina Weibo,texts (5); photographs (12); social media data (14); user-generated content (2); metadata (5),geolocated posts,geographical distribution problems; spatial distribution,temporal coverage; temporal distribution; temporal patterns; daily peak,,user’s first name; home location; user ID; date,9835 texts,,,"14,047 photographs",