 learning (DL) (2); deep neural networks (1); residual networks (1),Mobile Net V3 (10); Inception V3 (10); Inception Res Net V2 (9); Alex Net (1); Res Net (2); Goog Le Net (1); Monu Net (1),pre-trained on Image Net dataset (3),,,images (1),,80% image data as a training set and 20% image data as a validation set (1),,Gradient descent optimizers (1),0.005 (1),32 (1),200 (1),dropout (1),,{'dropout': '0.2'},,Intel(R) Xeon(R) Gold 5218R CPU @ 2.10GHz (1),NVIDIA Ge Force RTX 2080 Ti (1),,,an hour to several hours training time (1),"CNN model is composed of different components (convolutional, pooling layer, fully connected layers and activation functions) (1)","All CNN models were trained on Intel(R) Xeon(R) Gold 5218R CPU @ 2.10GHz and NVIDIA Ge Force RTX 2080 Ti, typically taking an hour to several hours training time. Learning rates of 0.005 were used for the Gradient descent optimizers, with a training batch size of 32 and a dropout of 0.2 added. Default input image sizes for the models tested ranged from 299x299 pixels for Inception V3 and Inception- Res Net V2 models, to 224x224 pixels for Mobile Net V3 but this study used larger input image sizes as outlined below. All models in this study were trained for 200 epochs using Tensor Flow 2.4.1 and Keras 2.4.0. For transfer learning, we used the Image Net dataset pre-trained weight. And we make the experiment with and without ﬁne-tuning.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (7); metrics (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['identify the Algerian monuments by using image recognition approaches (1)'],[],['Alg Heritage dataset helps to identify the Algerian monuments by using image recognition approaches (1)'],['This research work proposed a new dataset about Algerian architectural monuments heritage mainly conducted on the 90 most famous monuments (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Alg Heritage dataset is available at https://bit.ly/3O38FOe (1),,,Tensor Flow 2.4.1 and Keras 2.4.0 (1),TensorFlow (3),,,,,
_Do and Kim-2020-An assessment of the aesthetic v.txt,An assessment of the aesthetic value of protected wetlands based on a photo content and its metadata,Yuno Do; Ji Yoon Kim,"Department of Biological Sciences, Kongju National University, Gongju 32588, Republic of Korea; Center for Climate Change Adaptation, National Institute for Environmental Studies, 305-8506, Japan",2020,journal article,Ecological Engineering,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoleng.2020.105816,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoleng.2020.105816,,,,protected wetland,wetlands; Ramsar wetlands; Upo Wetlands; Suncheon Bay,aesthetic value (7); protected wetland areas (1); Wetlands of International Importance (1),,"{'Aesthetical Values': 'The study analyzes the aesthetic value of wetlands based on photo content and metadata, focusing on visual factors like scenery, fog, and sunsets. The research also considers color preferences in photos, linking them to emotional responses.'}; {'Ecological Values': 'The study mentions the ecological function and vulnerability of wetlands, as well as the importance of biodiversity. It also discusses the need to balance tourism with the protection of wildlife habitats.'}; {'Social Values': 'The study explores how aesthetically attractive wetlands create social motivation to preserve them. It also discusses the importance of understanding the needs of visitors and residents in order to make ecotourism sustainable.'}; {'Economic Values': 'The study mentions the economic benefits derived from wetlands through ecotourism. It also discusses the potential for increasing the monetary value of conservation through flagship species.'}",managing wetlands (1); heritage conservation (1),Upo Wetlands; Suncheon Bay,South Korea (3); Changnyeong County; Suncheon City,,Ramsar COP event in 2008,"Quantitative aesthetic value assessments continue to be diﬃcult because aesthetic value depends on personal factors such as age, gender, experiences, and ecological awareness, as well as visual factors such as the appearance and type of the environment.","In this study, we analyze the content and metadata of the photos taken in Ramsar wetlands in South Korea to assess their aesthetic value.",social media platforms (2); Flickr (1),image contents (4); metadata (7); text content (2); comments (2); timestamps (3); geotags (3),geotags (3),density,timestamps (3),,,69 and 287 geotagged photos,,,,,,,between 1 January 2007 and 31 December 2018,Upo Wetlands and Suncheon Bay,collected,Flickr application programming interface (API),We screened out duplicate photos as well as photos that were not taken inside the wetland areas.,,,,,[],,object detection (2),deep learning (3),Amazon Rekognition,,,,image contents,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,conﬁdence score,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,ecotourism (3); visitor satisfaction (2); natural site (1); natural environment (1); ecological function (1); ecological awareness (1); social media data (2); sentiment analysis (1); Ramsar sited (1); tourism attractions (2); scenery (4); fog (3); sunsets (3); wildlife habitats (2); biodiversity (3); ecological aesthetics (1); endangered species (2); panoramic view (1); ecosystem assessment (2); nutrient cycling (1); supporting biodiversity (1); social-economic beneﬁts (1); ecological landscaping (1); web-data (1),,,
_Dritsas et al.-2021-Aspect-Based Community Detection.txt,Aspect-Based Community Detection of,social network,,2021,conference paper,"2021 12th International Conference on Information, Intelligence, Systems & Applications (IISA)",IEEE,10.1109/IISA52424.2021.9555549,http://trumpet.di.ionio.gr/demo/,,,Cultural Heritage (2),cultural,museums (1); monuments (1); urban heritage sites (1),cultural heritage management (2); cultural inheritance (1),,,,,Greece (1); Ionian Islands (1); Greek (1),,,Massive cultural content management is important because reviews can be an- alyzed for extracting signiﬁcant representations.,"In this study, an aspect mining method of a cultural heritage approach by incorporating big data methods, is proposed. We propose the combination of a community detection algorithm, i.e., the Parallel Structural Clustering Algorithm for Networks (PSCAN), with topic modelling methods, i.e., the Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA), for performing large-scale data analysis in Twitter.",Twitter (13),text (1),,,,,,"5, 000",,,,,,,01/02/2021 to 28/02/2021,,crawling,Twitter Streaming API (2),keyword search query,"Numerous pre-processing techniques were implemented during the mining strategy [3], [5]. These steps include the utilization of regular expressions to remove, for example unnecessary URLs or the representation of emoticons with their equivalent form, e.g., lol as laugh out loud. The removal of punctuation marks and stop-words is another important step. Also, the lemmatization and tokenization processes were employed for removing complex sufﬁxes and retrieving the lexical form of each individual term.",only kept tweets posted in English language,,students of the University of Patras,[],,topic modelling (6); clustering (3); community detection (7); aspect mining (2),Topic Models,LDA (8),,,,,,,,,,,,,,"{'α': 'hyper-parameters for the document-topic', 'β': 'hyper-parameters for the topic-word distributions'}",,,,,,,,,yes,"['social network (6)', 'user graph (1)']",users,“follow” relationship between two users,,,,fetches the Twitter users along with their followers and subsequently connects users that “follow” each other or have a common follower,PSCAN (4),,community detection,accuracy (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,"Manjaro Linux, Kernel 4.14 LTS with Apache Spark 2.2.1, Scala 2.11, Mongo DB 3.6 and ML.Sparkling library",Apache Spark (4); Graph X (2),,cultural context (1); cultural content (1),,,
_Díaz-Pacheco et al.-2024-Quantifying differences between.txt,Quantifying differences between destination images: a deep learning approach,Ángel Díaz-Pacheco; Rafael Guerrero-Rodríguez; Miguel Á. Álvarez-Carmona; Ansel Y. Rodríguez-González; Ramón Aranda,"Departamento de ingeniería Electrónica, Campus Irapuato‑Salamanca, Universidad de Guanajuato, Carretera Salamanca ‑ Valle de Santiago km 3.5 Comunidad de Palo Blanco 36787, Salamanca, Guanajuato, Mexico; División de Ciencias Econímico Administrativas, Universidad de Guanajuato, El Establo, 36000 Marfil, Guanajuato, Mexico; Centro de Investigación en Matemáticas, Unidad Monterrey, Km. 10 Autopista al Aeropuerto, Parque de Investigación e Innovación Tecnológica (PIIT), Av. Alianza Centro 502, 66628 Apodaca, Mexico; Centro de Investigación Científica y de Educación Superior de Ensenada, Andador 10, No. 110, Ciudad del Conocimiento, 63173 Tepic, Mexico; Centro de Investigación en Matemáticas, Unidad Mérida, Parque Científico y Tecnológico de Yucatán (PCTY), Carretera Sierra Papacal‑Chuburna Puerto Km 5, 97302 Merida, Mexico; Consejo Nacional de Humanidades, Ciencias y Tecnologías (CONAHCy T, Av. Insurgentes Sur 1582, 03940 Ciudad de México, Mexico",2024,journal article,,Springer Nature,,,,,,,,,,,,Acapulco (4); Guadalajara (6); Mexico City (13); Riviera Maya (13),Mexico (12); Taiwan (1),,,"However, it remains unclear to what extent these efforts work to effectively construct the desired image and influence visitors’ behavior.","For this reason, the main purpose of this paper is to make a comparison between projected destination images by DMOs and perceived images using the photos posted by both within the digital social platform known as Instagram.",Instagram (21); Facebook (1); You Tube (2); Tik Tok (1); Google Images (1),photos (10); images (23); video (2); texts (2); audio (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Phantom Buster (3),,,,,,[],,classification (4); scene recognition (7); object recognition (3),deep learning (10); convolutional