_Egarter Vigl et al.-2021-Harnessing artificial intelligen.txt,Harnessing artificial intelligence technology and social media data to support Cultural Ecosystem Service assessments,Lukas Egarter Vigl; Thomas Marsoner; Valentina Giombini; Erich Tasser; Daniel Depellegrin,,2021,journal article,People Nat,,https://doi.org/10.1002/pan3.10199,,,673–685,cultural heritage (3); UNESCO world heritage (2),,cultural Alpine landscapes (1); historic places (1),UNESCO world heritage (2); cultural heritage (3); cultural landscapes (1),,"Social values: Emotional (collective) – The study uses social media data to understand the relationship between people and nature, reflecting a sense of place attachment and communal value related to the Dolomites.; Aesthetical values: Artistic – The study analyzes visual-sensory landscape values, indicating an appreciation for the artistic qualities of the landscape.; Economic values: Entertainment – The study identifies CES benefiting hotspots in areas with high tourism development, suggesting the role of the landscape in tourism and entertainment.",,UNESCO world heritage Dolomites,Italy; northern Italy,,,"Scientists, however, still often struggle to understand how landscape character- istics contribute to deliver these intangible benefits, largely because it is hard to navigate how people value nature, and because there is a lack in methods that accommodate both comprehensive and time-­efficient evaluations.","In particular, we show (a) how a text mining algorithm can be used to semantically group artificially generated tags into distinct CES categories, (b) how the geographic information embedded in social media photographs can be used by Maximum Entropy modelling to identify variables that best explain users' pref- erence patterns and (c) how AI and social media data can be com- bined and applied to support CES assessment and environmental management.",Flickr (7); Panoramio (1); Instagram (1); Twitter (1); Weibo (1),photograph content (2); photographs' spatial distribution (1); densities (1); natural language tags (3); metadata information (1),geographic information (2); geolocated photographs (1); locational information (1); coordinates (1),spatial patterns (2); spatial distribution (1); densities (1); spatial dataset (1); spatial pattern (1),time/ date information (1); temporal coverage (1); temporal understanding (1),,metadata information (1); time/ date information (1); locational information (1); coordinates (1),"~32,000 images (2)","1,834 users uploaded only a single photograph",,"~32,000 images (2)",,,"106,190 images were downloaded, and, after data pre-­processing (i.e. removing duplicates or not geolo- cated images)",2005 to 2018,"95 municipalities located in northern Italy, including the UNESCO world heritage Dolomites, and extends over approximately 6,000 km2 of land",accessed and collected via the publicly available Flickr API,Flickr API (2),selecting randomly only one photograph per user,removing duplicates or not geolo- cated images,,manual classification by a group of instructed experts (n = 9),group of instructed experts (n = 9) living and working in the study region,['one or more CES classes'],"Their Fleiss' Kappa value (indicating the inter-­annotator agreement) was 0.44, which can be considered fair",classifying images (1); identifying objects (1); labelling them with natural language tags (1); text mining (3); semantic analyses (1); image recognition (5),deep learning (1); machine learning (2); Maximum Entropy modelling (2),Clarifai (2); Maxent model (2),We applied their default pre-­ trained general model (version 1.3),,"edge, curve and pattern recognition (1)",image content (2); natural language (2); environmental grids (predictor variables) (1); occurrence of given observation points (presence data) (1); natural geographic variables (1); human variables (1),,10-­fold cross-­validation for training and testing the model results,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,"The AI engines took approximately 15 hr to screen and classify all 100,000 images to produce 20 tags and analyse the semantic associations between tags.",,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (3); precision (2); recall (2); F-­measure (2); ROC curve (1); AUC value (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],10-­fold cross-­validation,,[],confusion matrix (2); ROC curve (1),[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['flexible, replicable and transferable approach that expands the ability of stakeholders to make sound land management decisions']",[],[],"['AI is intrinsically limited in addressing very subjective and complex human values, views and believes, such as relational or spiritual CESs.']","['public data must not be used to compromise privacy, ano- nymity and trust expectations of individuals and user groups']","['the distribution of Flickr users is skewed over educational levels, wealth, geographical provenance, age and gender groups']",[],[],[],[],[],"['the distribution of Flickr users is skewed over educational levels, wealth, geographical provenance, age and gender groups']","Both, the image recognition engine and the semantic algorithm can be accessed through their API Python Clients accessible at https://github.com/Clari fai/clari fai-­pytho n-­grpc/ and at https://github.com/Seman tria/seman tria-­ sdk respectively.","A static URL list of all considered Flickr photographs, a summary table of the image recognition and semantic grouping analysis, and the detailed results of the expert classification procedure are freely available at the Dryad Digital Repository https://doi. org/10.5061/dryad.kwh70 rz33 (Egarter Vigl et al., 2021).",,https://doi. org/10.5061/dryad.kwh70 rz33,,,,Cultural Ecosystem Services; CES; aesthetic value; outdoor recreation; cultural heritage; symbolic species; relational values,,,
_Elayyan and Geril-2017-Expert system based opportunisti.txt,Expert system based opportunistic mobile social network for the historical,,,2017,other,,,,,,,Cultural Heritage,tangible; intangible; cultural,historical places (11); historic sites (2); ancient building (1); archaeological sites (2); historic sites (2); damaged monuments (1),historic (4); historical (10); virtual heritage (4); cultural heritage (5); archaeological (3); historic sites (2); historical sites (3); preserving (1); preservation (2); reconstruction (3); culture (4); cultural (6); heritage (10); monuments (2); antiquities (1); cultural cleansing programs (1); threatened sites (1); historical artefacts (1); historical artefacts (1); cultural significance (1); cultural value (1),,"{'SOCIAL': {'Spiritual': ""The paper discusses the importance of historic environments to a country's cultural identity and sense of place, suggesting a spiritual value related to beliefs and traditions.""}}; {'SOCIAL': {'Emotional (collective)': 'The paper mentions that people are still heart-linked with historic places all over the world, even during war, indicating a sense of place attachment and communal value.'}}; {'ECONOMIC': {'Use': 'The paper states that historic environments contribute to the economy and support job opportunities, highlighting the function and utility of these assets.'}}; {'POLITICAL': {'Educational': ""The paper suggests that historical places are reminders of a city's culture and complexity, implying an educational role in understanding the past.""}}; {'HISTORIC': {'Historic-Artistic': 'The paper refers to the magnificence of ancient buildings and the history forged over centuries, indicating a rare and unique example of historical movements.'}}; {'AESTHETICAL': {'Artistic': 'The paper mentions the magnificence of ancient buildings, suggesting an original product of creativity and imagination.'}}; {'AGE': {'Existential': ""The paper discusses the importance of preserving historical places as reminders of a city's culture and complexity, representing memory and the lives of past generations.""}}",restoration (1); preserving (1); reconstruction (3),"Aleppo city (1); Sphinx in Egypt (1); Machu Picchu in Peru (1); Lucerne, Switzerland (1)",Scotland (1); Middle East (2); Syria (1); Australian (1); United Arab Emirates (UAE) (2); Switzerland (1); London (1); Egypt (1); Peru (1); Russia (1),,wars (3),"The historic environment is one of our most precious assets. It contributes to our economy, to our cultural identity and to our sense of place. Without it, any country would not be that special country.","The project is aimed to motivate people all around the world to upload their photos of Aleppo city before and after the war, using their mobiles with the new technology of 360 panorama tic photos as well as short movies from millions of people.",Twitter (1); Face book (1); Whats App (1),photos (13); 2d / 3d (1); panoramic ones (1); short movies (1),,,,comments (1); sharing (4),,,,,,,,,,,"upload their photos of Aleppo city before and after the war, using their mobiles with the new technology of 360 panorama tic photos as well as short movies from millions of people.",,,,,,,[],,classification (3); location recognition (2); image localization (1),Neural networks (2),SIFT (2),,,,image features (4),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,"['social network (10)', 'mobile ad hoc networks (1)']",,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['SMART Tourism (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,Opportunistic computing (4); Recommender systems (3); Mobile data offloading (2); Data mining (3); Image processing (4); Artificial Intelligence (2); Internet of Things (IoT) (5); Cloud Computing (2); GIS based Visualization (1); Expert system (7); 3D model (1); virtual tours (2); virtual visit (1); virtual life (2); avatars (1); panoramas (4); 360 virtual visit (1); 360 panorama tic photos (1); 360 tour (1),,,
_Erskine et al.-2021-Marine Protected Areas provide m.txt,Marine Protected Areas provide more cultural ecosystem services than other adjacent coastal areas,Emily Erskine; Rosie Baillie; David Lusseau,"School of Biological Sciences, University of Aberdeen, Aberdeen, UK; National Institute of Aquatic Resources, Technical University of Denmark, Kongens Lyngby, Denmark",2021,journal article,One Earth,Elsevier Inc.,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.oneear.2021.07.014,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.oneear.2021.07.014,,11,,,,cultural ecosystem services (CESs) (11); cultural identity (1),,"{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study discusses how cultural ecosystem services (CESs) provide people with a sense of place and belonging, which is directly related to cultural identity and a sense of place attachment.'}; {'value': 'Social', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions that cultural ecosystem services (CESs) provide people with a sense of place and belonging, improve mental and physical well-being, and are important for cultural identity.'}; {'value': 'Economic', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses how marine protected areas (MPAs) can offer new economic opportunities to affected communities and how tourism can benefit communities via poverty alleviation, development of infrastructure, and job market diversification and increased well-being.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions that cultural ecosystem services (CESs) are intangible benefits provided by ecosystems, such as aesthetic experiences.'}",heritage conservation (1),,,,SARS - CoV - 2 pandemic (3),"Marine biodiversity loss is a global threat for the integrity of ecosystem services. Marine protected areas (MPAs) are commonly used for biodiversity conservation to displace anthropogenic threats, but by doing so they can impact coastal community livelihoods. They are often assumed to confer added cultural ecosystem services (CESs) to affected communities, offering new economic opportunities, but we currently lack a global estimate of MPA CES provision.","Here, we used a paired design to compare the intensity with which CESs from all MPAs worldwide and adjacent control areas were utilized over the past 10 years by using geo - referenced Flickr photos and their user - generated descriptions.",Flickr (22),photos (20); text (descriptions) (5); geotagged posts (1),geo - referenced (2),,year (3),likes (faves) (4); comments (4),user ID (2),126 million photos,,,,,,,"January 1, 2010, and December 31, 2019 (4)",all MPAs worldwide and adjacent control areas,sampling photos posted on Flickr,Flickr API (2),paired design to compare MPAs worldwide to adjacent control areas,all descriptions written in languages other than English were removed along with punctuation and HTML links. We also replaced emoticons and emojis with word equivalents using textclean 88 in R 3.6.3. Words were stemmed and infrequent words (encountered fewer than 10 times in the whole corpus) were removed,Coastal areas that were partially terrestrial were not discarded.,,,[],,topic modeling (2); sentiment analysis (2),,astructural topic model (stm in R) (2),,,,free text associated with the photo title and the photo description,,,,,,,100,,,{},,,,,,,,We developed astructural topic model (implemented using stm 89 in R 3.6.3) using the free text associated with the photo title and the photo description to estimate topics mentioned in this text corpus. Treatment (association of the photo with an MPA or a control quadrat) was retained as a ﬁxed effect to estimate the relationship between topic prevalence and protection status.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Incorporating user - generated content in future MPA tourism and recreation assessments and management strategies could make evaluating their CES beneﬁts easier and more unambiguous.', 'Managers could use differences in the numbers of photos posted to various online platforms as an indirect measure of CES provision to manage nature tourism.', 'Managers and communities could take advantage of visitors’ positive sentiments toward nature and MPAs and identify the CES assets in their MPA that can be used in social media to encourage behaviors and activities that help conserve biodiversity and discourage those that are environmentally harmful.']",[],[],[],"['Overdispersion in our hurdle model of photo count means that these results must be treated with caution for very small MPAs (< 0.1 km 2 ).', 'Data from social media are not without biases. People’s access to the internet at home and on holiday affects what can be posted online and by whom.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Code is available at https: / / github.com / dlusseau / MPA_ﬂickr,Data are available at Mendeley Data (https: / / doi.org / 10.17632 / dmk97w5vrr.1).,,https://doi.org/10.17632/dmk97w5vrr.1,R 3.6.3,,,Marine protected areas (MPAs); nature and wildlife; coastal communities; tourism and recreation; ecosystem services; natural assets; social media; Flickr; geo - referenced photos; user - generated descriptions; positive sentiments; visitor preferences; management strategies; biodiversity conservation; environmental quality; environmental attachment; pro - environmental behaviors; hashtags; geolocation tagging; monitoring programs; management strategies; online content; biodiversity; SARS - CoV - 2 pandemic; public health interventions,,,
_Feng et al.-2024-Evaluating the Culture and Touri.txt,Original Article ABSTRACT:,,,,journal article,,,,,,,Cultural Heritage,cultural,Grand Canal National Cultural Park (9); Cultural Landscape (1),UNESCO World Heritage (1); cultural heritage (6); traditional culture (2),,"{'SOCIAL': 'Emotional (collective) – The study emphasizes the importance of cultural identity and pride associated with the Grand Canal National Cultural Park, suggesting a strong sense of place attachment and communal value for visitors.'}; {'ECONOMIC': 'Entertainment – The study focuses on enhancing visitor experiences and optimizing resource allocation, indicating a recognition of the role the Grand Canal National Cultural Park plays in the contemporary tourism market.'}; {'POLITICAL': 'Educational – The study aims to contribute to the knowledge that will be useful in helping policymakers and cultural managers devise sustainable and enriching experiences for visitors, highlighting the educational role of heritage assets.'}; {'HISTORIC': ""Educational – The study highlights the Grand Canal's historical importance and its potential use as a tourism resource, emphasizing the potential of the asset to provide knowledge about the past.""}; {'AESTHETICAL': 'Artistic – The Grand Canal, a masterpiece of technology and an enduring testimony, reflects original creativity and imagination.'}; {'SCIENTIFIC': 'Technological – The Grand Canal, a masterpiece of technology, showcases outstanding skill in techniques or materials.'}; {'AGE': 'Existential – The Grand Canal represents memory and the lives of past generations.'}",heritage site management (1); cultural preservation (3); heritage sites (2),Grand Canal National Cultural Park (17),China (2); Chinese (1); Yangtze Rivers (1); Yellow (1); Northern Guangxi (1),,,"The successful combination of these aspects demands expertise in various issues, including those relating to visitor demand, cultural concerns, and economic aspects. This hinders the ability to capture the full complexity and dynamics involved in such integrations.",This paper aims to evaluate the culture-tourism integration model of the Grand Canal National Cultural Park with machine learning algorithms [8].,Weibo (1); Trip Advisor (1); Twitter (1); Facebook (1); Instagram (1),visitor reviews (2); ratings (2); comments (3),,,seasonal fluctuations (1); seasonal patterns (1),likes (1); shares (1); comments (1),demographic elements (1); demographic data (1),,,,,,,,,,"Data shall be obtained from multiple sources, such as visitor surveys, social media platforms, and official records from the park.",Advanced web scraping tools will be used to collect social media data,,The survey questionnaires need to be analyzed for incomplete or contradictory records and removed to ensure this set of data is accurate and valid.,"Selection criteria for historical records, such as period and geographic focus, should be made available and justified regarding relevance to research objectives.",,,"['positive', 'neutral', 'negative']",,prediction (6); sentiment analysis (3); clustering (2); dimensionality reduction,machine learning (16); ensemble learning (3); supervised learning (1),Random Forests (10); Decision Trees (14); SVMs (10); Logistic Regression (6); PCA (2); k-means (2),,,,demographic variables; visit frequency; satisfaction scores; sentiment scores; keyword frequency; engagement metrics; visitor trends; seasonal patterns,,80-20 ratio,,,,,,,,"{'n_estimators': 'optimized to prevent overfitting', 'max features': 'optimized to prevent overfitting', 'kernel type': '(linear and radial basis function)', 'regularization parameter C': 'manages the trade-off between margin and misclassification error', 'maximum depth': 'for the prevention of overfitting and thereby controlling model complexity'}",Logistic Regression (6),,,,,,,"data collection, preprocessing, feature engineering, model selection, training, and validation",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (14); precision (13); recall (13); F1-score (15),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],k-fold cross-validation (2),Logistic Regression (6),[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['strict protocols will ensure the anonymization of information gathered, hence protecting the anonymity and privacy of the respondents']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,Visitor satisfaction (6); Predictive analytics (1); Visitor behaviour (3); Visitor preferences (8); Visitor experiences (6); Cultural tourism (8); Machine Learning (12); NLP (2); ensemble methods (3); hyperparameter tuning (2); feature engineering (4); data-driven (4),,,
_Ferreira et al.-2016-Beyond Sights Large Scale Study.txt,Beyond Sights: Large Scale Study of Tourists’ Behavior Using Foursquare Data,,,,other,,,,,,,,,,,,,,London; New York; Rio de Janeiro; Tokyo; Statue of Liberty; Christ the Redeemer (statue); St Paul’s Cathedral; Sega Akihabara; Times Square; Yankee Stadium; Union Square Park; Washington Square Park; Madison Square Garden; Grand Central Terminal; New York International Auto Show; Bryant Park; John F. Kennedy International Airport; New York Penn Station; Shopping Rio Sul; Santos Dumont; Galea˜o; Marina Palace hotel; UNIGRANRIO university; Starbucks,London; New York; Rio de Janeiro; Tokyo; Brazil; New York City; Manhattan,,Rio’s carnival,"In this work, we show how we can use the signals given by Foursquare users, the so-called check-ins, to better understand the behavior of tourists that would be hard using using traditional methods, such as surveys.",We perform a spatio-temporal study of properties of the behavior of these two classes of users (tourists and residents).,Foursquare; Twitter,check-ins; geographic coordinate (latitude and longitude),geographic coordinate (latitude and longitude),Spatial patterns,Temporal patterns,,check-in ID; user ID; time; category; subcategory,,,,,,,,one week of April 2012,"London, New York, Rio de Janeiro and Tokyo",indirectly collected check-ins from Foursquare using the Twitter service,"Twitter service, which provides a streaming API",,"Cities close to the analyzed ones are treated as a single city. Data from users who did not report a valid city name were disregarded. In addition, we removed the check-ins from users who did not report any city.",,automatic method for analyzing these names,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,[],nodes vi ∈V are speciﬁc venues in the city in a certain time,"a directed edge (i, j) exists from node vi to vj if at some point in time an user performed a check-in at a venue vj after performing a check-in in vi",,,degree centrality,,,,study spatio-temp