_Foronda-Robles et al.-2025-The sustainable management of ov.txt,The sustainable management of overtourism via user content☆,Concepci´on Foronda-Robles; Luis Galindo-P´erez-de-Azpillaga; Pablo Armario-P´erez,"Instituto Andaluz de Investigaci´on e Innovaci´on en Turismo (IATUR), Universidad de Sevilla, Av/ San Francisco Javier, S/N, 41018 Seville, Spain; Departamento de Geografía Humana, Universidad de Sevilla, Instituto Andaluz de Investigaci´on e Innovaci´on en Turismo (IATUR), C/ María de Padilla S/N, 41004 Seville, Spain; Instituto Andaluz de Investigaci´on e Innovaci´on en Turismo (IATUR), Universidad de M´alaga, Blvr. Louis Pasteur, 29, Jardín bot´anico Campus Teatinos, 29071 Malaga, Spain",2025,journal article,Annals of Tourism Research Empirical Insights,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.annale.2025.100184,https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/annals-of-tourism-research-empirical-insights,,,,,tourist attractions (7); Alhambra Palace; Generalife Gardens,local heritage (1); sustainability (10); sustainable management (2); sustainable tourism (3); heritage resources (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective): The study mentions cultural authenticity and positive resident interactions boosting satisfaction, while hostility or perceived invasion can lead to tourismphobia, causing friction between visitors and hosts. This indicates a discussion of cultural identity and sense of place attachment.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': ""Use: The paper discusses the function and utility of tourist attractions, and how overtourism raises prices, limiting access for residents and tourists, generating dissatisfaction. This indicates a discussion of the asset's function and utility.""}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Management: The paper discusses the need for sustainable management strategies, including destination diversification, improved transport networks, and access control, to mitigate highly touristified environments. This indicates a discussion of the incorporation of the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational: The paper mentions the Alhambra Palace and the Generalife Gardens, which have the potential to provide knowledge about the past. This indicates a discussion of the educational role of heritage assets.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic: The paper mentions the Alhambra Palace and the Generalife Gardens, which are original products of creativity and imagination. This indicates a discussion of the artistic value of heritage assets.'}",sustainable management strat­egies (1); destination diversification (2); access control (2); sustainable tourism management (1),Granada; Spain; Alhambra Palace; Generalife Gardens,Granada; Spain; Andalusia; USA; France; Italy; Germany,,,"Therefore, it is essential to analyse how overtourism affects tourist satisfaction and its implications for sustainable tourism management.","This article explores how overtourism influences the tourist experi­ ence and its expression in online reviews of Granada’s attractions. The study evaluates user satisfaction and its spatial significance using the BERT model (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Trans­ formers), AI indicators and multivariate analysis. It offers a novel perspective on digital feedback and proposes strategies for harmonising growth with sustainability in destination management.",Trip Advisor (12); Lonely Planet (1); Google Maps (1); X (1); Instagram (1),text (2); comments (7); online reviews (12); geolocalisation data (1),latitude and longitude values (1),,,,date of the review (1); numerical score (1); number of comments (1),"42,778 comments",,,,,,,May and June 2023,Granada,web scraping techniques (2),"Python (2), Apache Airflow (2), Scrapy framework (1), Selenium (1), APIs of open data services (1)","low activity periods on the target website, typically at night or in the early morning (1)","tokenisation divided sentences into segments (1). Using the Google Wordpiece algorithm (1), all letters were converted to lower case to normalise the text and avoid redundancies (1), followed by the removal of stop words, such as connectors and articles (1)","Comments were refined to retain only relevant texts (1). In a subsequent review, comments that could not be processed automatically were included (1)",manually classified comments (2),,[],,sentiment analysis (3); classification (1),Transformer models (1),BERT (7),,,,,,350 manually classified comments (1) validated with 50 manually labelled comments (1),,,0.00002 (1),1 (1),5 (1),weight_decay = 0.01 (1),,{},,,,,,,,"This model (Model ID: 12) learns in a controlled manner (learning rate = 0.00002), processes one datum at a time (batch size = 1) and repeats training five times (epoch = 5), with regularisation (weight_decay = 0.01) to avoid overfitting.",yes,"['social network (2)', 'two-mode network (1)']","tourist attractions (Node 1) (1), Economic Cost, Environmental Degradation and Social Congestion (Node 2) (1)",interconnection between the nodes (1),,,centrality (1); density (2),adjacency matrix (1),,Net Draw program (1),"visualises the interconnection between the nodes corresponding to Granada’s tourist attractions (Node 1) and the KDs, that is, Economic Cost, Environmental Degradation and Social Congestion (Node 2).",Pearson correlation (2); squared cosines (3); silhouette scores (3),"['The User_AI and User_opinion variables present a correlation coef\xad\nficient of 0.831, which indicates a strong positive correlation (1).', 'the KD-TAC variable has a notable correlation to F2, with a squared cosine of 0.633 (1)']",[],[],,,[],[],"The classification error rate for both positive and negative comments was 26.32 %, outperforming the other models.",[],[],[],,,[],heat maps (2),[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['improving key aspects of tourism such as customer support, cleanliness and the quality of the activities offered (1).', 'the implementation of regulatory measures such as limiting access to destinations and the use of flow management tech\xad\nnologies can help control congestion in areas affected by overtourism and thus improve the tourist experience (1).', 'the improvement of public transport systems and the creation of infrastructures that support a greater number of visitors reduce the economic and environmental impact (1).', 'the promotion of diversification to encompass other less well known and centralised at\xad\ntractions enables a more equitable tourist flow redistribution (1).']","['combining emotion analysis, multivariate analysis and geospatial tools, offers an approach that is applicable to other tourist destinations (1).']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,Power BI (1),,UGC (4); AI (5); KDs (14),,,
_Foroughi et al.-2023-Capturing public voices The rol.txt,Capturing public voices The role of social media in heritage management,Mahda Foroughi; Bruno de Andrade; Ana Pereira Roders,"Department of Architectural Engineering and Technology, Faculty of Architecture and Built Environment, Technical University of Delft, the Netherlands",2023,journal article,Habitat International,Elsevier Ltd,10.1016/j.habitatint.2023.102934,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.habitatint.2023.102934,,,Cultural Heritage (2); World Heritage (2),built,built heritage (2); heritage properties (2); Historic City of Yazd (2); windcatchers (14); historical homes,cultural significance (7); UNESCO (4); values (14); attributes (13); heritage management (4); heritage planning (3); conservation (3); historic (7); Outstanding Universal Value,,"{'value': 'Social', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the importance of public participation and stakeholder involvement in heritage planning, emphasizing inclusivity, transparency, and collaboration, which aligns with the definition of social values.'}; {'value': 'Economic', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions optimizing economic potential through responsible tourism and ensuring the benefits of tourism reach the community, indicating a discussion of economic values related to heritage.'}; {'value': 'Political', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the use of social media to capture and decode public voices, which can dynamize the dominant planning power structure, suggesting a discussion of political values related to heritage planning.'}; {'value': 'Historic', 'reasoning': 'The paper refers to the Historic City of Yazd and the historical significance of windcatchers, indicating a discussion of historic values related to heritage.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions windcatchers as distinctive architectural features visible from all corners of the historic city, acting as landmarks within the urban landscape, suggesting a discussion of aesthetical values related to heritage.'}; {'value': 'Age', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions the age value is recognized as the most important value by all interest groups, indicating a discussion of age values related to heritage.'}; {'value': 'Ecological', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions windcatchers have been utilized for centuries to provide natural ventilation in buildings by cleverly utilizing wind flow, indicating a discussion of ecological values related to heritage.'}; {'value': 'Scientific', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions smart technologies (e.g., artificial intelligence) and techniques (e.g., Natural Language Processing) provide opportunities to capture and decode public voices, indicating a discussion of scientific values related to heritage.'}",heritage management (3); heritage planning (4); conservation (2); preservation (2),"city of Yazd, Iran (2); Yazd Historic City; Aghazadeh mansion in Abarkuh, Yazd; Dolat Abad Garden in Yazd",the Netherlands; Iran (6); United Arab Emirates (UAE) (2); Dubai; Persia,Qajar Dynasty,COVID-19 virus spread widely around the world,"Still, the application of artificial intelligence (AI) methods to analyze social media data for heritage management is seldom explored.",This paper investigates the potentials of short texts (sentences and hashtags) shared through social media as a data source and artificial intelligence methods for data analysis for revealing the cultural significance (values and attributes) of built heritage.,social media (13); social media platforms (3); Instagram (7); Twitter (5),short texts (sentences and hashtags) (2); textual data,,,time; year,,username; post content; time; users’ biographies,"23,899 posts",,"3346 sentences and 12,646 unique hashtags",Only a few photographs are used in this paper to clarify some aspects of the case study or to support an argument developed in the text.,,,,the last ten-years,city of Yazd,automatically mined,Web Harvey software,,"All the posts were translated to English using Google API to facilitate the analysis process. The gathered posts which do not mention the words “windcatcher” and “Yazd”, in their variations, were excluded.",,automatic quantitative content analysis and qualitative categorical analysis,,"['values', 'attributes', 'sentiments']",,sentiment analysis; multi-label text classification,BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) model,BERT,pre-trained transformer model,Word embedding,,text,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,The embedding fed into the gated recurrent unit (GRU) model to predict sentiment.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; precision; recall; F1 score matrices; F-measure,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['heritage management processes'],[],"['methodological process developed, which can be applied to other heritage management case studies and different scales.']",[],[],[],"['this research considers the ethical issues by only processing the hashtags and com\xad\nments expressing heritage values and attributes and not storing any sensitive personal data.', 'personal data is not disclosed at any stage of the research, and the users’ identities will remain anonymous unless permission is asked.', 'to make the data untraceable, the usernames were changed (e.g., user1, user2, etc.) and posts were rephrased.']",[],[],[],['Instagram’s terms of service which forbids automated retrieval of photographs.'],[],[],[],"The model has been archived in the Github repository (Foroughi, 2023).",,,,"Data analysis and modeling are conducted using Google Colaboratory (Colab), an online platform for collaborative coding and computation.",Python libraries; Numpy; Pandas; transformers library,,,,,
_Foroughi et al.-2025-In Praise of Diversity in Partic.txt,In Praise of Diversity in Participatory Heritage Planning: An AI-Supported Approach to Uncover Cultural Significance,Mahda Foroughi; Tong Wang; Ana Pereira Roders,TU Delft; TU Delft; TU Delft,2025,journal article,,,,https://data.4tu.nl/datasets/5e55cf64‐7912‐4b07‐8b8e‐c2afb067c3e7/2,,,Cultural Heritage (2),built,heritage property (1); built heritage (3); city (2); windcatchers (22); historic city (2); urban landscape (1),cultural significance (14); values (24); attributes (25); outstanding universal value (OUV) (2); UNESCO (9); World Heritage List (2); heritage planning (18); heritage properties (2); conservation (2); nomination (1); inscription (2); historic urban landscape (1),,"{'SOCIAL': 'The study mentions the social values associated with windcatchers, indicating a collective emotional connection and cultural identity related to these structures. The windcatchers contribute to the sense of place and communal value for the community.'}; {'ECONOMIC': 'The study discusses the economic values of windcatchers, particularly their use in providing thermal comfort and natural ventilation, which can reduce energy consumption. This highlights the utility and function of windcatchers in contemporary markets.'}; {'POLITICAL': 'The study implicitly discusses the management and symbolic values of windcatchers, as they are incorporated into heritage planning and legislative frameworks. The windcatchers serve as emblematic expressions of cultural awareness and are used in political strategies for disseminating cultural heritage.'}; {'HISTORIC': 'The study discusses the historic values of windcatchers, particularly their potential to provide knowledge about the past and their association with traditional architectural and engineering practices. The windcatchers are considered rare and unique examples of historical and conceptual indicators.'}; {'AESTHETICAL': 'The study implicitly discusses the artistic and notable values of windcatchers, as they are recognized as unique building elements that contribute to the urban landscape. The windcatchers are considered materializations of conceptual intentions and authentic examples of architectural history.'}; {'SCIENTIFIC': 'The study discusses the technological and conceptual values of windcatchers, as they represent outstanding skill in techniques and materials. The windcatchers are integral materializations of concept-driven design and original results of human labor and craftsmanship.'}; {'AGE': 'The study discusses the existential and workmanship values of windcatchers, as they represent memory and the lives of past generations. The windcatchers have craftsmanship value linked to the production period and represent marks of time visible on forms, components, and materials.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL': 'The study discusses the essential and spiritual values of windcatchers, as they represent harmony between the building and its natural environment. The windcatchers contribute to ecological ideologies in the design and construction and can be reused, reprocessed, or recycled.'}",heritage planning (10); heritage management (2); urban conservation (1),"Yazd, Iran (6); city of Yazd (4)","Iran (4); Australia (2); United Kingdom (1); North Africa (1); West Asia (1); Persian Gulf (1); China (2); Taipei City, Taiwan (1); Lisbon, Portugal (1); United States (1); Indonesia (1); Sweden (1); Honduras (1)",ancient times (1),,"Effectively involving diverse stakeholders and capturing the cultural significance they attribute to heritage remains a challenge, particularly when dealing with extensive datasets and multiple stakeholders.","This research aims to investigate the potential of artificial intelligence (AI) models (e.g., multi‐label text classification analysis) in analyzing available unstructured textual data from multiple sources (e.g., policy documents, literature, and social media), to reveal values and attributes conveyed to built heritage by different stakeholders, to build a foundation to align various values for making integrated decisions.",social media platforms (2); social media (10); Instagram (5); Twitter (5); Facebook (2); Linked In (1); Weibo (1),text (2); images (1); URLs (1),,,publication time (1),,usernames (2); post content (1); users’ biographies (1),"23,899 posts (1)",,,,,,,,,automatically mined (1),Web Harvy (2),,"All variations of “windcatcher” and “Yazd” were normalized to “windcatcher” and “Yazd” (both in Persian and English, e.g., “Yazd,” “yazd,” “.)”ﯾﺰد Moreover, unnecessary data including stop words, references, punctuation marks, and website links were removed.",only processing hashtags and comments related to heritage values and attributes (1),,,[],,text classification (3); multi‐label text classification (2); natural language processing (2),transformers (1),BERT (4),pre‐trained language model (1),,,text (2),,,,,,,,,,{},,Google Colaboratory (Colab) (1),,,,,bidirectional approach to language understanding and generation (1),,,['semantic network analysis (1)'],,,,,,matrix construction (1),,,,accuracy (2); precision (1); recall (1); F1 score metrics (1); F‐measure (1),"['accuracy: 94% (1)', 'precision value: 77% (1)', 'F‐measure: 76% (1)']",[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Such methodologies using AI and available data sources (e.g., social media) can provide necessary information for heritage managers to enhance legislative frameworks.']","['This work introduces an innovative methodology for heritage planning that provides deeper insights into the perceptions of policymakers, experts, and users on the heritage values of windcatchers in Yazd, Iran, using social media data, academic literature, and policy documents.']",[],[],"['AI may struggle with contextual understanding, potentially missing nuances in the history and culture of heritage sites that human experts can capture.', 'Furthermore, biases in training data can lead to skewed representations of cultural aspects.', 'Additionally, the complexity of AI algorithms can hinder transparency and accountability, making it difficult to explain decision‐making processes to policymakers and potentially eroding trust in the technology.', 'AI relying on available data sources including social media data may not fully represent all stakeholder perspectives or demographic information.', 'The methodology’s limitations include a focus on official policy documents, academic literature, and social media, potentially overlooking other policymaker, expert, and user groups.']","['Ethical considerations were prioritized by only processing hashtags and comments related to heritage values and attributes, without storing any sensitive personal data.', 'Personal information was not disclosed at any stage of the research, and users’ identities were kept anonymous unless explicit permission was obtained.', 'To further ensure anonymity, usernames were altered (e.g., user1, user2) and posts were rephrased to make the data untraceable.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The data are available at https://data.4tu.nl/datasets/5e55cf64‐7912‐4b07‐8b8e‐c2afb067c3e7/2,,https://data.4tu.nl/datasets/5e55cf64‐7912‐4b07‐8b8e‐c2afb067c3e7/2,,Python (1); Numpy (1); Pandas (1),,,,,
_Franceschi-2021-China as an International Travel.txt,China as an International Travel Destination: A Corpus-Based Analysis of the Tourist Gaze in Travel Blogs,Valeria Franceschi,Department of Foreign Languages and Literatures of the University of Verona,2021,journal article,,,,https://news.cgtn.com/news/2020-02-29/Chinese-tourists-made-169-million-outbound-trips-in2019-report-Ot IYWs Zm OQ/index.html,,,,,historical heritage sites (1),historical sites (1); cultural heritage (1),,"{'value': 'Social - Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study discusses how tourists seek experiences that align with their pre-existing images of China, suggesting a desire for cultural identity and a sense of place attachment related to the destination.'}; {'value': 'Historic - Symbolic', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions the importance of historical sites and cultural landmarks in shaping tourist perceptions of China, indicating an association with important past events and cultural heritage.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical - Artistic', 'reasoning': 'The study highlights the visual aspects of tourism, with tourists seeking visually extraordinary scenes and emphasizing the exoticized Other in their travel narratives.'}",,China; Beijing; Shanghai; Hong Kong; Xi’an; Great Wall,China; Hong Kong; Macao; Taiwan; Beijing; Shanghai; Guangzhou; Xi'an; Hutong,Ming,Sars-Cov-2 pandemic,This paper aims at contributing to existing research on China as an international travel destination by investigating the way visitors perceive and describe China and its people.,"This paper aims at investigating the tourist gaze on China and its population as emerging from travel blogs and travel guides produced by non-professional internet users. Specifically, it wants to determine how popular Chinese destinations and local people are perceived through the eyes of international tourists.",Facebook; You Tube; Pinterest; Trip Advisor,text,,,,,,333,,,,,,,,,text mining,Boot Cat (Baroni and Bernardini 2004),"inputting a number of keywords, or ‘seeds,’ which are then combined into sequences— ‘tuples’—of different length",the output was manually sifted to exclude texts irrelevant to the scope of this study,"Such URLs include social media platforms and media-sharing websites (e.g. Facebook, You Tube, Pinterest, Trip Advisor), tour operators, travel agencies, individual travel agents, and DMOs (Destination Marketing Organization).",manual,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,"Results indicate that visitors have an overall positive perception of China and its people but describe the country in contrasting terms, as seen in previous studies on this destination. Tourists appear to look for elements signifying authenticity, such as historical heritage sites and locals engaging in stereotypical activities.",[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['juxtaposition of ‘old’ and ‘new’ China is recurrent in the data, alongside a certain nostalgic feeling for a more traditional China']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['As the American spelling of\n‘traveling’ was used as a search term, the corpus is expected to include mainly texts written by either American bloggers or international bloggers favoring American spelling conventions, which may be seen as a limitation in this study.', 'Another limitation of web-mined corpora is the lack of ethnographic information about the bloggers.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,tourism discourse; collocation analysis; content analysis; tourist gaze; tourism in China; electronic Word-of-Mouth (e Wo M); Word-of-Mouse,,,
_Freeman and Zarandona-2021-Digital spectres the Notre-Dame.txt,Digital spectres: the Notre-Dame effect and the mediatization of World Heritage,Cristina Garduño Freeman; José Antonio González Zarandona,ACAHUCH at the University of Melbourne; Centro de Investigacion,2021,journal article,Journal of Heritage Studies,Taylor & Francis,https://doi.org/10.1080/13527258.2021.1950029,https://doi.org/10.1080/13527258.2021.1950029,,1264–1277,World Heritage (7),,"World Heritage sites (3); Notre-Dame Cathedral (1); Palmyra, Syria (6); archaeological site of Palmyra (2); Notre-Dame in Paris (1); Notre-Dame gothic cathedral (1)",World Heritage (10); cultural (16); natural sites (1); preservation (1); archaeological (3); historical significance (1); outstanding universal value (1); heritage sites (10); World Heritage List (2); conservation (1); cultural heritage (11),,"Social: Emotional (collective) – The authors discuss how the destruction of heritage sites like Notre-Dame and Palmyra evokes a sense of loss and communal grief, impacting cultural identity and sense of place attachment.; Political: Symbolic – The paper highlights how heritage sites like Notre-Dame and Palmyra serve as symbols of civilization, national identity, and cultural values, making their destruction a politically charged event.; Historic: Historic-Artistic – The paper mentions the historical and artistic significance of Notre-Dame and Palmyra, emphasizing their role as examples of historical movements and artistic achievements.; Aesthetical: Artistic – The paper implicitly discusses the artistic value of Notre-Dame and Palmyra by highlighting their architectural and artistic features, such as the gothic style of Notre-Dame and the multicultural cosmos of Palmyra.",preservation (1),Palmyra; Syria; Notre-Dame; France; Paris,Syria (6); France (2); Middle East (1); Europe (2); Iraq (1); Western nations (1); United States (3); United Kingdom (2); Germany (2); Italy (1); Spain (2); Australia (2); Sweden (1); China (1),Middle Ages,fire at Notre-Dame Cathedral,But the complexities and nuances for World Heritage in the new digital paradigm are not yet fully understood.,"Here we consider questions raised by the digital specter generated by search queries on Google in response to two significant losses: the fire at Notre-Dame Cathedral, and the destruction of Palmyra, Syria.",social media (5); Twitter (1); Facebook (1); Instagram (1); Reddit (1); You Tube (2),images (2); video (1); text (1),global positioning systems (GPS) (1),,,,device type (1); location information (1),,,,,,,,June 2015 to July 2019,global,data retrieved from Google Ads Keyword Planner,Google Ads Keyword Planner (3),,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,click through rate’ (CTR) (2),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,digital specter (2); digital colonialism (3); digital heritage (1); digital age (2); digital platforms (2); digital realm (3); digital iconomy (1); digital reconstructions (1); digital diffusion (1); digital spectres (2); search queries (6); search terms (3); search culture (3); search engine (4); search volumes (4); keywords (10); Google Ads (7); Google Search (2); Page Rank™ (2); Google Ads policies (1); UNESCO (5); iconoclasm (3); mediatisation (8); algorithmic (2); algorithms (3); algorithmic structure (1); targeted marketing (2); advertising revenue (2); advertising campaigns (4); advertising network (3); Google (27); World Heritage sites (3); World Heritage (10); cultural heritage (11),,,
_Fu and Li-2024-Investigation of Visual Language.txt,Investigation of Visual Language Landscape of Tourist,L. Fu; Q. Li,,2023,journal article,,,,,,17,,,visual language landscape of tourist attractions (5),language landscape (24); visual language (3); Chinese symbolic artifacts,,,,,"Hazelton, Pennsylvania; Malaysia; Coventry, England; China; North Sumatera Province; Tehran-Qom; Arabian Gulf; Manama, Kingdom of Bahrain; Cozumel; Seoul; Guangzhou, China; Israeli",,,"Therefore, it is necessary to study the visual language landscape of tourist attractions from other dimensions.","This article would investigate and analyze the visual language landscape of tourist attractions from a multimodal perspective, and lay a foundation for the theoretical direction and program design for the subsequent development of the visual language landscape of tourist attractions.",Flickr,images; social media tags; GPS tags; user comments,GPS tags,,,,user tags; albums; photography parameters,"269,648",,,"269,648",,,,,,collected from the image sharing website Flickr,,,,,,,[],,keyword description; natural language description; object detection; semantic segmentation,convolutional neural network (CNN); Recurrent Neural Network (RNN),CNN-RNN; LSTM,pretrained CNN network with the classification part removed,word vector,,images; social media tags; user tags,,,correlation entropy; relative entropy loss,,,,,,,{},CNN +Logistic; CNN +Softmax; CNN +WRAP; RIA; Tag Neighbor; SINN; MSRCap; m RNN; NICv2; V2L; ATT; NIC-F; NIC-deeply,,,,,,The CNN-RNN model consists of two parts: a visual encoder and a sequence decoder.,"The CNN-RNN model with semantic regularization can be trained in stages, including pretraining of the CNN and RNN, respectively, and end-to-end fine-tuning of both.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,precision; recall; F1 value; AUC; overall precision (O-P); overall recall (O-R); overall F1 value (O-F1); class precision (C-P); class recall (C-R); class F1 value (C-F1); CIDEr; METEOR; ROUGE; BLEU-4,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This article optimized the visual language landscape of tourist attractions with the help of multimodal horizons, which promoted the development and innovation of the tourism industry.']",[],[],"['The limitations of this article are mainly reflected in the experimental part; specifically, the sample selection is not enough, and there are also some limitations in the perspective of analysis, which makes the universality of this research conclusion need to be further verified.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,This article does not cover data research. No data were used to support this study.,,,,,,linguistic landscape (2); biometric algorithms; multimodal theory; multimode; heterogeneous modal data; geosemiotics; CNN-RNN model; semantic regularization,,,
_Fu et al.-2025-Designing a novel technique for.txt,of tourism informatization in scenic spots,Li Fu; Yao Yi; Lina Liu; Ran Chen,,2025,journal article,PeerJ Computer Science,PeerJ,10.7717/peerj-cs.2807,http://dx.doi.org/10.7717/ peerj-cs.2807#supplemental-information,,33,,,,,,,,,China,,,"Although there is a wealth of user-generated content, most studies currently concentrate on individual applications of big data and sentiment analysis in tourism. They frequently neglect the broader implications for real-time data informatization and decision-making at tourist sites.","This study addresses these challenges by utilizing bidirectional encoder representations from transformers (BERT) for sentiment analysis alongside Hadoop for efﬁcient big data processing. By integrating these technologies, we deliver a robust framework for the real-time assessment of tourist sentiments, providing actionable insights for managing scenic spots and improving overall informatization.",Trip Advisor; Twitter,text,,,,likes; reviews; ratings,username; posting device; timestamp fields; language; location tags; description fields,"25,481 instances from Twitter and 12,211 instances from Trip Advisor","1,779 unique visitors",,,,,,January to December,China,"scraping, API",Twitter API,"gathering posts, reviews, and tweets that referenced speciﬁc tourist attractions across China, such as zoos, parks, mountains, and historical sites","removal of irrelevant and duplicated data, handling missing values by inserting 0 s in the place of missing values, and standardizing data that is to convert all the data for instance into a standard format",,,,"['positive', 'negative', 'neutral']",,sentiment analysis; classification,Transformer models,BERT,pre-trained model,word2vec model,term frequency-inverse document frequency (TF-IDF),text tokens,,80/20,cross-entropy loss,,,8,5,,,{},multinomial naïve Bayes (MNB); k-nearest neighbors (KNN); random forest (RF),Google Colab,,,,,,"The BERT is used to computes the contextual embedding from the text. A linear classiﬁer with a cross-entropy loss function is then applied on top of BERT to classify the sentiment as negative, positive, and neutral.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; precision; recall; F1-score; ROC-AUC curve; confusion matrix; precision-recall curve,['average accuracy of 83.53%'],"['The value of the precision for both negative and neutral is 0.80 while 0.92 for the positive.', 'the recall value for negative and positive is 80 while 0.89 for the natural.', 'The value of the F1-score for negative is 0.80, for neutral it is 0.84, and for positive it is 0.85.']",[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,MNB; LR; KNN; RF; VC; XGB; SC,[],ROC-AUC curve; confusion matrix; precision-recall curve,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['tourism destination management', 'improve tourist hospitality', 'contribute to sustainable tourism practices']",[],"['This study is unique and innovative in different ways; ﬁrst, we considered the whole of China as your study area, secondly, the study uses big data analytics in combination with a start-of-the art technique for the sentiment analysis that is BERT and linear classiﬁer.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],The raw data and code are available in the Supplemental Files.,The raw data and code are available in the Supplemental Files.,,,,Hadoop,,,,,
_Fu et al.-2025-Evaluating Perceived Cultural Ec.txt,"Evaluating Perceived Cultural Ecosystem Services in Urban Green Spaces Using Big Data and Machine Learning: Insights from Fragrance Hill Park in Beijing, China",L. Fu; H. Fu; C. Xiong,,2025,journal article,Sustainability,"MDPI, Basel, Switzerland",https://doi.org/10.3390/su17041725,https://doi.org/10.3390/su17041725,,,Cultural Heritage (2),cultural; natural,urban green spaces (5); cultural landscapes (6); historic sites; Fragrance Hill Park (9); Fragrant Temple (Xiangshan Temple); Bright Temple (Zhao Temple),Cultural Significance; cultural ecosystem services (19); sustainable development (3); cultural heritage (4); traditional architecture (2); historical relics; ecological benefits; recreational opportunities; UNESCO; natural landscape (2); cultural landscape (7); heritage sites; urban parks (2); natural preservation; cultural offerings,,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study identifies that users find solace and enjoyment in the natural surroundings while exploring Fragrance Hill, which contributes to their cultural identity and sense of place attachment.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': ""Educational – The study mentions that the park's history dates back to the Tang Dynasty and includes structures built during the Qing Dynasty, indicating its potential to provide knowledge about the past.""}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': ""Artistic – The study highlights the aesthetic appreciation of the park's natural scenery, particularly its renowned maple trees in the fall, indicating its artistic value.""}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment – The study mentions that the park hosts the annual Red Leaves Festival, drawing large crowds, indicating its role in the contemporary market, especially tourism.'}",conservation; management (5); sustainable urban development (2); sustainable decision making; heritage sites,Fragrance Hill Park; Beijing; Moscow; Xi’an; Murcia; Berlin; Las Vegas; Pieniny Mountains; southern Poland; Kowloon Walled City Park in Hong Kong,Beijing; China (6); Global South; Russia (2); Moscow; Xi’an; Spain; Murcia; Europe; USA; Haidian District; Western Hills; Taiwan; Las Vegas; Pieniny Mountains; southern Poland; Hungary; Northeast China; Hong Kong,Tang Dynasty (2),Red Leaves Festival (3),"However, there remains a gap in leveraging big data and unsupervised machine learning to comprehensively evaluate perceived CESs.",(1) What cultural ecosystem services do users perceive in urban green spaces? (2) How do users evaluate these cultural ecosystem services? (3) Which cultural ecosystem services should park managers prioritize for improvement?,Trip Advisor (2); Ctrip (2); Meituan (5); Meituan.com; Weibo,text (2); user-generated content (UGC) (10); online reviews (4); social media photo,,,,,,"20,087",470 million,"20,087",,,,,2010 to 2020,Fragrance Hill Park in Beijing,Python 3.5 crawl,Python 3.5,"filtered out duplicate entries and irrelevant data, including those with empty content, advertisements, and other non-contributory content","Chinese word segmentation, the elimination of stop words, both high and low-frequency words, as well as the consolidation of synonyms","duplicate entries and irrelevant data, including those with empty content, advertisements, and other non-contributory content",,,[],,topic modeling (2); sentiment analysis (2); classification (4),unsupervised machine learning (6),LDA (11),,,word frequency analysis (2),text; online reviews,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"This study used the gensim toolkit of Python to conduct the LDA model. Determining the optimal number of LDA dimensions is a crucial step. Following the previous studies, we adopted a method based on coherence and perplexity metrics",no,[],,,,,,,,,,coherence; perplexity; word frequency; sentiment analysis,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],line graph,"['ten dimensions of cultural ecosystem services', 'five CES-related personal well-being', 'four CES-related public well-being dimensions']",[],[],[],[],[],"['improving the management of urban green spaces', 'crafting targeted marketing strategies and tourism experiences that enhance user satisfaction and promote sustainable tourism']","['park managers can focus on developing and expanding recreational facilities such as hiking trails and themed cultural events', 'investments should be made into aesthetic improvements by preserving the park’s natural beauty, enhancing visual experiences, and promoting cultural heritage']","['integrates the IPA framework with the LDA method, addressing the principles of the Data-Intensive Scientific Discovery Paradigm']","['identified ten types of CESs, including five related to personal well-being, four to public well-being, and one bridging both categories, reflecting public perceptions of these ten CES types']",[],"['the CES dimensions are based on a single dataset at a specific point in time, which may not fully capture evolving user perceptions', 'the reliance on Meituan.com introduces platform bias, as its user base may not represent diverse demographics', 'the absence of user demographic information limits the ability to differentiate CES perceptions among groups based on factors like age or socioeconomic status', 'the use of big data and machine learning may raise ethical concerns, including data privacy, informed consent, and algorithmic bias']",[],"['platform bias', 'user base may not represent diverse demographics']",[],[],[],[],[],"['ethical concerns, including data privacy, informed consent, and algorithmic bias']",Data supporting conclusions of this article will be made available by authors on request.,Data supporting conclusions of this article will be made available by authors on request.,,,gensim toolkit of Python,Python; Matplotlib,,"well-being (10); ecosystem services (ESs) (5); urban environment (2); quality of life (2); natural environment (4); cultural identity; physical health (4); mental health (3); social norms; cultural values (2); Destination Marketing Organizati