orithmic bias",,,
_Fudholi et al.-2023-BERT-based tourism named entity.txt,BERT-based tourism named entity recognition for tourism destination recommendation,Dhomas Hatta Fudholi; Annisa Zahra; Septia Rani; Sheila Nurul Huda; Irving Vitra Paputungan; Zainudin Zukhri,,2022,other,,,10.7717/peerjcs.1731/fig-1,http://dx.doi.org/10.7717/ peerj-cs.1731#supplemental-information,,,World Heritage (2),cultural (4),world heritage sites (1); historical buildings (1); places such as monuments (1); temples (1); castles (2); cathedrals (2); churches (1); tourist attractions (14); tourist destination (5); tourist places (2),heritage (7); cultural (4); historical (2); ancient (1),,"{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study aims to provide tourist attraction recommendations, which can enhance cultural identity and pride by enabling travelers to explore and appreciate different cultures and historical sites.'}; {'value': 'Entertainment', 'reasoning': 'The study focuses on developing a recommendation engine for tourist destinations, directly contributing to the entertainment value by helping travelers discover and plan visits to various attractions.'}; {'value': 'Educational', 'reasoning': 'The study enhances the potential of heritage assets to provide knowledge about the past by recommending historical and cultural sites, thus serving an educational role.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical', 'reasoning': 'The study recommends places with natural scenery, such as rivers, waterfalls, mountains, and beaches, which are open to the public, thus highlighting the artistic and notable aspects of these locations.'}; {'value': 'Existential', 'reasoning': 'The study focuses on heritage sites that have been around for many years, representing memory and the lives of past generations through historical, cultural, and ancient landmarks.'}",,Kyoto (3); London (3); Lisbon (3); Colorado (4); Bali (1); Jeju (1); Thailand (2); Moroccan (2); Mongolian (3),United States (1); India (1); Korea (1); Moroccan (2); Mongolian (3); Thailand (2); Jeju (2),,COVID19 epidemic (1),"making a decision can be difficult and time-consuming, especially when visiting new regions or traveling alone. Furthermore, the abundance of sources frequently leads to new issues. It is understandable that carefully analyzing all sources to gather useful information can be overwhelming.","A BERT-based NER model is proposed in this article to support tourism destination recommendations from social media posts. As there is still no similar research that we found, this research becomes one of the pioneers in using BERT-based NER to be the core of recommendation engine. This research is also conducted to see BERT’s performance for NER modeling on only English tourism dataset.",Instagram (1); Facebook (2); Twitter (2); Trip Advisor (2); Traveloka (2); Hotels.com (2),text (1),,,,,,92 articles,40 people,"8,500 sentences",,,,,,,crawling the web,Google search engine,,"removal of emoticons and URLs, as well as tokenization",,manually,the author,"['Natural', 'Purpose', 'Heritage']",,named entity recognition (15); token classification (1),Transformer models (1),BERT (24); BERT-base-cased (3); BERT-base-uncased (2); Ko BERT (2); Ro BERTa (2),pre-trained (3),,,text tokens (1),,80:20,,Adam (2),0.00001 (3),4 (3),3 (3),,,{},CRF (6); Bi LSTM-CRF (3); naive Bayes classifier (NBC) (1); K-nearest neighbor (KNN) (1),,,,,,"The BERT-base-cased model has a linear layer on top of the output of the hidden states, and this layer functions as a token classification layer.",The pre-training was accomplished by simultaneously conditioning all model layers on the right and left contexts.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,F1-score (15); precision (3); recall (3); accuracy (1),['average F1-score of 0.80 (2)'],"['HERITAGE 0.81 0.81 0.81 521', 'NATURAL 0.78 0.85 0.81 796', 'PURPOSE 0.74 0.80 0.77 722']",[],,"With the average interest level higher than 4 on a scale of 1 to 5, the output from NER in those source articles can be considered a good recommendation.",[],[],,[],[],[],holdout method (1),,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['travel location recommendation system (1)'],[],[],[],[],"['This study has not yet considered whether the article sources have positive or negative reviews of the tourist destinations mentioned in them.', 'Another limitation is that some article sources may write similar tourist location names in different writing.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"The modeling code, implementation code, and data are available in the Supplemental Files.","The modeling code, implementation code, and data are available in the Supplemental Files.",,http://dx.doi.org/10.7717/ peerj-cs.1731#supplemental-information,,,,tourism entities (1); tourism categories (3); tourist attraction categories (3); tourist destination categories (1); tourism domain (5); tourism industry (1); tourism NER (4); tourism datasets (2); travel blogs (1); travel articles (2); travel bloggers (1); travel recommendations (1); travel reviews (1); traveling destination recommendation system (1); vacation photographs (1); vacation recommendations (1); vacation destination (1); worldofwanderlust.com (1); touropia.com (1); tourmyindia.com (1); vaticantour.com (1); myguidezimbabwe.com (1); daytours4u.com (1); Traveloka.com (1); Trip.com (1); Mafengwo app (1),,,
_Fukui and Ohe-2020-Assessing the role of social med.txt,Article ABSTRACT:,Miho Fukui; Yasuo Ohe,"Department of Food and Resource Economics, Chiba University in Japan; Department of Food and Resource Economics, Chiba University in Japan",2020,journal article,,,,https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7361-8600,,,Cultural Heritage (2); natural heritage (1),,rural areas (4); cultural resources (2); natural heritage (1); Hiraizumi Chusonji Temple (1),World Cultural Heritage site (1); cultural heritage (2); natural heritage (1),,,restoration (4),Iwate prefecture (4); Tohoku region (1); L’Aquilla Italy (1); Nepal (2); Pompei (1),Japan (5); eastern Japan (1); Iwate (14); Miyagi (2); Fukushima (3); Tohoku region (1); Central Italy (1); Hong Kong (1); United States (1); Central America (1); Thailand (2); Vietnam (1); China (1); Kenya (1); Italy (1); Nepal (2),,Great East Japan Earthquake (2),"there has been little investigation on how to effectively mobilize this newly emerging tool to promote tourism, especially in rural areas.","The aims of this article are twofold. In the first aim, we examine the role of Twitter among social media for tourism promotion in connection with the recovery of tourism after a natural disaster. The second aim focuses on the recovery of tourism in a rural area affected by the huge earthquake and tsunami, Magnitude 9.0, which hit eastern Japan in March 2011.",Twitter (5),text (4),,,time-series,,,,,,,,,,January 2010 to March 2013,Iwate prefecture,Data were collected using official statistics on incoming numbers of ordinary tourists and volunteer tourists.,twilog (1),,,,classified tweets by month for text mining,,[],,text mining (4),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['profiles of volunteer tourists were not touched upon such as by providing information on gender, age, income and where they lived, nor was it examined how those people who behaved as ordinary tourists in normal times became volunteer tourists when the disaster happened.', 'the technical issue of the seasonal unit root, which was not tested exactly in this article although seasonal adjustments were made.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,official statistics,,,,R,,restoration tourism (3); dark tourism (5); volunteer tourism (14); ordinary tourism (12); conventional tourism (4); leisure tourists (4); business-oriented tourists (3); restoration workers (2),,,
_Furini et al.-2018-Towards Tweet Content Suggestion.txt,Towards Tweet Content Suggestions,M. Furini; F. Mandreoli; R. Martoglia; M. Montangero,,2018,conference paper,"Goodtechs 18, November 28–30, 2018, Bologna, Italy",,,,,,Cultural Heritage (CH) (3),,,,,,,,Italy; Europe; U.S.; China,,Museum Week event organized by Twitter,"Motivated by the need to support effective communication and marketing campaigns within the CH domain, our goal is to design and develop an innovative dashboard that will guarantee a continuously-updated analysis of the Twitter account and that will provide media managers with effective content suggestions for writing successful tweets.","Through a content-based predictive approach, we aim to: i) foresee the popularity of the tweet the media manager is writing, and ii) give insights on the content features that most influenced the prediction (e.g., “use of the word conversation has not brought benefits 7 times out of 10"").",Twitter (10); Sina Weibo,text (4); images (3); video (2); multimedia,,,,likes (7); retweets (2); followers (4); comments,username; account ID; source,"19,527 tweets (2)",25 well known world spread art museums,,,,,,at the time we were observing data,,analyzed around 800 tweets authored by each of the 25 museums,,We selected 25 well known world spread art museums and clustered them according to the number of followers of their Twitter account.,,we concentrate only on those features that are proper of the tweet and do not depend on the context around the sender.,automatic message classification,,"['GOOD', 'BAD']",,classification (6); prediction (10); sentiment analysis (3),machine learning (4),Naïve Bayes (4); Decision Tree (2); Max Entropy (2),,,,content features (10); source information (3); numerical features; tweet vocabulary,,4:5 and 1:5,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (14); percentage of correct classifications,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['privacy issues'],[],[],[],[],[]