_García-Esparza and Altaba-2023-Identifying habitation patterns.txt,Identifying habitation patterns in world heritage areas through social media and open data,Juan A. García-Esparza; Francesc Altaba,"School of Technology and Environmental Sciences, Universitat Jaume I, ESTCE. Avda. Vicent Sos Baynat, s/n. 12006, Spain",2023,journal article,Urban Research & Practice,Taylor & Francis Group,https://doi.org/10.1080/02723638.2022.2140971,www.tandfonline.com/journals/rurb20,,2280 2292,World Heritage (WH) (10),historic,historic districts (2); historic city (2); historic environment (1); historic sites (2); historic cities (4); historic town (1); historic places (1); historic area (1),"World Heritage (WH) (10); cultural (11); cultural heritage protection (1); heritage conservation (1); historic (21); intangible aspects (1); sustainable development (4); conservation (4); enhancement (2); protection (2); values (4); nominated (2); historic centers (1); urban conservation (1); cultural ecosystem (1); historic cities, towns and villages (1); governance (2); management (5); climate change (2); digital data analysis (2); protected areas (3); urban protected areas (1); heritage-led models (1); historical value (1); heritage sites (1)","social values (2) When Foursquare users express interest in certain activities, gastronomy, tourism, sightseeing, trips, festivals or entertainment, locals also associate the physical environment with social, leisure, and economic values.; economic values (1) When Foursquare users express interest in certain activities, gastronomy, tourism, sightseeing, trips, festivals or entertainment, locals also associate the physical environment with social, leisure, and economic values.; architectural values (2) Data demonstrate that today the contemporary historical area does not only host architectural values, but also social ones linked to leisure and recreation.; historical values (1) Speciﬁcally, the ﬁrst tendency in results reveals that historical heritage, cultural representations, and activities are assets of great importance to visitors; locals or foreigners.; esthetic aspects (1) Nonetheless, while a ﬁrst tendency links the urban layout and architecture to esthetic aspects, the second dissociates culture and consumption from other aspects of the city, establishing a semantic and spatial separation from the surroundings.","Social (collective): The study discusses how WH nominations influence the visual nature of WH areas and how locals associate the physical environment with social, leisure, and economic values, indicating a sense of place attachment and communal value.; Economic (use): The research analyzes the potential contribution of online data and user-generated content for understanding contemporary socio-cultural trends, suggesting the function and utility of heritage sites in the modern context.; Political (management): The study emphasizes the need to incorporate social and environmental spheres of development to avoid inequalities between districts, indicating the incorporation of heritage into political strategies and policies.; Aesthetic (artistic): The study links the urban layout and architecture to esthetic aspects, highlighting the artistic value of heritage sites.; Historic (educational): The study focuses on understanding the contemporary socio-cultural trends in historic cities, indicating the potential of these sites to provide knowledge about the past in the future.",cultural heritage management (1); heritage conservation (2); urban conservation (1); governance (2); management (5); safeguarding (2); conservation policies (1),Ávila (3); Cuenca (4); Salamanca (3); Segovia (4); Toledo (4),Spanish (1); Spain (1); European (1),late twentieth century (1),,Contemporary habitation patterns in historic districts require technology to understand parallel realities in protected areas.,"The ﬁrst objective of this research analyses the potential contribution of online data and user-generated content for understanding the contemporary socio-cultural trends in ﬁve medium-sized cities in Spain: Ávila, Cuenca, Salamanca, Segovia, and Toledo. In line with the second objective, the similarity of these cities beneﬁts the comparison of data about location, intensity and dispersion of activities and services.",social media (7); Foursquare (6); Google Maps (5); social networks (1),social media (7); open data sources (1); online data (4); user-generated content (1); datasets (3); surveys (1); mobile apps (1); cartographic data (2); cadastre data (3); online applications (2); qualitative data (2),locative social media (1); geolocated points (1),hotspots (10); coldspots (12); spatial implications (1); point clouds (1); location (3); intensity (1); dispersion (2),contemporary (6); contemporary patterns (1); contemporary socio-cultural trends (1); contemporary social interaction (1); contemporary urban issues (1); contemporary forms of valuation (1),visitors (6); users (4); stakeholders (3); inhabitants (2); residents (2); citizens (2); locals (4); foreigners (1),location (3); age data (1); non-primary residence (3); population density (1); morphology (2); accesses (3); services (10); activities (11); uses of space (3); cadastral data (1),,,,,,,,,"ﬁve medium-sized cities in Spain: Ávila, Cuenca, Salamanca, Segovia, and Toledo",mixed-source methods (2),,,,,,,[],,,Machine Learning (ML) (4),,,,,qualitative data (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,['social networks (1)'],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Governance and management must incorporate the social and environmental spheres of development to avoid inequalities between districts and, particularly, to boost measures that aﬀect the quality of life in the entire WH area.']","['The digital method, that helps analyze and interpret data retrieved from cadastre and online applications has proved reliable and provides clear outputs to identify patterns in the ﬁve WH areas.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,