_Ge et al.-2025-Leveraging deep learning for des.txt,Leveraging deep learning for design,S.G.; Q.Q.; Y.Z.; S.L.; H.C.; X.R.; P.C.; T.Z.,Jinan University (Shenzhen Campus),2025,journal article,https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-025-02004-2,Springer Nature,https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-025-02004-2,http://www.nature.com/reprints,,,heritage tourism (2),,Chinese heritage tourism sites (1); historical events (5); revolutionary destinations (1); dark tourism destinations (1); red tourism sites (1); heritage sites (3),heritage value (1); heritage sustainability (1); heritage destinations (1); heritage tourism (3),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study analyzes sites associated with the Chinese revolution, which evoke cultural identity, motivation, and pride related to the Communist Party’s struggle against Japanese invaders and the Nationalist Party.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study focuses on historical events and sites from 1927 to 1948, aiming to provide knowledge about the past and the Chinese revolution.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study analyzes the esthetics of images captured by travelers at heritage sites, considering attributes like color harmony, content, and light.'}",,"Nanchang Uprising (NCU) (2); Jinggangshan (JGS) (8); September 18th Incident (918) (8); Zunyi Meeting (the Long March, LM) (7); Nanjing Massacre (NJM) (9); Unit 731 of Manchuria (731) (9); Liaoshen Campaign (LSC) (9); Crossing-the-Yangtze Campaign (CYC) (7)",China (4); Chinese (4); Manchuria (1),1927 to 1948 (3),Chinese revolution (1),research on the development of ambiguous viewpoints about chromatic perceptions of tourists’ visual experiences during the revolutionary period has been scarce. The identiﬁcation of conﬂict and consistency among several destinations is still to be determined.,"This study identiﬁes eight Chinese heritage tourism sites associated with historical events from 1927 to 1948, evaluates 16,089 UGPs sourced from social media platforms, and analyzes the similarities and contrasts in color, objects, sentiment, and esthetics among these photographs. A qualitative evaluation of tourists’ chromatic perception based on dark-red dominance is ﬁnalized for each destination.",Dianping.com (2); Instagram (1); Flickr (1); Trip Advisor (1); Mafengwo.cn (1),photos (3); images (5); user-generated photos (UGPs) (3),,,,,,"16,089 photos",,,"16,089",,,,between 1927 and 1948,eight regional places,web crawler,Python,"To achieve a balanced dataset, 2228 photographs were amassed from each of the eight locations. This number was determined based on the minimal number of photographs across eight places (speciﬁcally, the CYC). Photographs from any destination exceeding this quantity were selected at random.","Prior to further analysis, the watermark was removed from the photographs by clipping 15% of the pixel areas at the bottom of the image.","Some photographs were omitted from further analysis due to challenges in identiﬁcation, predominance of human close-ups, or irrelevance to the event.",,,[],,semantic segmentation (1); object detection (2); sentiment analysis (3); esthetics evaluation (3); label detection (1); clustering (1),deep learning (2),Deep Sentibank (1); Vader Sentiment models (1); Google Cloud Vision (1); K-Means clustering (1),,,,RGB values (1); pixel (2),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"Given that data mining and deep learning approaches can enhance the accuracy of semantic segmentation and yield more precise geographic features30, this studyincorporated color analysis, object detection, sentiment analysis, and esthetics evaluation toanalyzeimages capturedby travelers for multiple destinations.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (1); Welch's F values (1),[],[],[],p < 0.01 (1),A qualitative evaluation of tourists’ chromatic perception based on dark-red dominance is ﬁnalized for each destination.,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],word cloud (1),[],[],[],[],[],[],"['educational services (1)', 'experiential marketing (1)']",['managing cultural heritage (1)'],"['integrated framework employing several deep learning approaches to independently identify cognitive, affective, and overall perspectives in the study of cultural heritage, highlighting how to use innovative methodologies and evaluation methods to measure interactions between human societies and theirenvironments (1)']",[],[],"['the absence of a universally recognized scale for assessing chromatic perceptions at tourist sites prompted our research to analyze UGPs to develop measurement items by four variables, thereby constraining the generalizability of the ﬁndings (1)', 'we did not distinguish the UGPs based on creators from diverse backgrounds (1)', 'considerable debate exists concerning whether disaster sitesareperceivedasdarktourismdestinationsbylocalresidents,akintored tourism sites (1)', 'this study does not provide a quantitative assessment to calculate the dark-red intensity beyond dark-red dominance on the chromatic perception (1)']",[],[],['Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-No Derivatives 4.0 International License (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],developed the code in Python,the following websites (accessed on 12 February 2025) offered the data that can be found to support the research,,https://www.dianping. com/shop/l3D2l6pzs Q0l3MA8/photos https://www.dianping.com/shop/ l3MUFQsa Eg Tshzo6/photos https://www.dianping.com/shop/l3S xo AUm P8b70Eu X/photos https://www.dianping.com/shop/G3d W Ai IHkf DOPW1J/photos https://www.dianping.com/shop/l8X8DO7 dci FHla A0/photos https://www.dianping.com/shop/l5k4s9t Yn2Se3p2N/ photos https://www.dianping.com/shop/la8dw Ssc Fnu XBf KF/photos https://www.dianping.com/shop/k52BVc MSPhi Ozxp P/photos.,,Python,,dark tourism (13); red tourism (13); dark-red dominance (5); chromatic perceptions (7); visual experiences (10); tourists’ visual experiences (4); UGPs (3); aesthetic (1); affective (2); cognitive (3); overall DI (2),,,"This study was funded by the Jinan University Shenzhen Campus Funding Program