_Ginzarly and Srour-2022-Cultural heritage through the le.txt,Cultural heritage through the lens of COVID-19,Manal Ginzarly; F. Jordan Srour,"Graduate Studies and Research, Lebanese American University, Beirut, Lebanon; Adnan Kassar School of Business, Lebanese American University, Beirut, Lebanon",2021,journal article,Poetics,Elsevier B.V.,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.poetic.2021.101622,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.poetic.2021.101622,,,Cultural heritage (13); World Heritage (WH) (3),tangible (4); intangible (3),cultural heritage content (4); World Heritage properties (2); historical monuments (1); heritage asset (2); heritage places (2); historic village (1),conservation (2); UNESCO (8); social value (5); cultural values (7); heritage values (4); digital heritage (3); heritage attributes (1); heritage practices (3); digitalization of cultural heritage (3); digitization of tangible and intangible heritage assets (1); digital engagement with heritage (1); value-based approach to heritage (1); management of cultural heritage (2); online heritage discourse (2); digital heritage initiatives (1); digital resilience (2); heritage activism (1); virtual heritage (1); cultural heritage sector (1); safeguard WH properties (1); sustainable tourism (1); heritage narratives (1); heritage discourse (1); heritage sites (2); cultural treasures (1); digital transformation (2),"social value: Jones (2017) argued that while experts tend to focus on intrinsic historic and scientific values, the public is primarily concerned with social value.; aesthetic (1); social (4); historic (2); age (1); economic (1); political (1); scientific (2); ecological values (1)","Social (Emotional collective): The study highlights the importance of cultural heritage in fostering a sense of community, identity, and shared experiences during the COVID-19 pandemic, indicating a strong emotional connection to heritage.; Social (Spiritual): The research emphasizes the role of cultural heritage as a source of inspiration, resilience, and artistic innovation, suggesting a spiritual or uplifting value derived from engaging with culture.; Economic (Entertainment): The paper discusses the use of online platforms to provide virtual visits and tours of heritage sites, indicating the entertainment value of heritage in a digital context.; Political (Educational): The study mentions the educational role of cultural heritage in sustaining the educational needs of diverse communities during mass confinement, suggesting a political value related to education and cultural awareness.; Aesthetic (Artistic): The study analyzes the cognitive and affective dimensions of Instagram posts, including terms related to art and cultural expressions, indicating an artistic value associated with heritage.; Historic (Symbolic): The research explores how sharing cultural heritage content on social media can contribute to building narratives about collective meaning, identities, and experiences, suggesting a symbolic value related to historical and cultural identity.",conservation (2); management (3); promote (2); safeguard (1),,"Rome (1); Russia (1); Kenya (1); Liria, Spain (1); Italy (5); USA (3); France (2); Haiti (2); Indonesia (3); Mexico (2); Sweden (1); Germany (4); Iran (4); Armenia (1); India (1); Brazil (1); Portugal (1); African countries (1); Eastern Europe (1); Russia (1); Arab States (1); Western Pacific (1); Spain (2); Wales (1)",18th-century (1),COVID-19 pandemic (12),This study examines the discourse emerging from cultural heritage content shared online during the COVID-19 pandemic.,It aims to understand the different affective and cognitive dynamics that are associated with the online sharing of cultural heritage in difficult times.,Instagram (11); Facebook (2),text (2); images (4); metadata (3); photos (17),geographical location (2); geographical reference (1),geographical distribution (1),,,date (1); hashtags (5); users’ names (1),"12,138 posts (2020) and 705 posts (2019)",,,"4465 photos on Instagram for #Share Our Heritage and 7673 photos for #Share Culture for a total of 12,138 posts. We also collected data for the period 9 April-12 August 2019, extracting a dataset of 75 photos on Instagram for #Share Our Heritage and 630 photos for #Share Culture for a total of 705 posts.",,,,9 April-12 August 2020 and 9 April-12 August 2019,140 countries (2020) and 51 countries (2019),web data crawling,Webharvy (1),,"removing repeated posts, stop words, mentions, URLs, emojis, numbers, and punctuations",,,,[],,topic modeling (3); sentiment analysis (3); unsupervised classification (1),,Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) (4),,,,textual data (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,['network (1)'],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['sharing valued World Heritage tangible assets like castles, cathedrals, and other historical monuments', 'cultural diversity and resilience in times of COVID-19', 'fulfilling cultural needs while in quarantine — centered on visiting museums virtually from home, sharing content, and looking back at history to have hope and be inspired', 'remembering from home the places visited before the lockdown by sharing photos of past visits or through online tourism and exhibitions of heritage sites', 'staying healthy and activities during lockdown including dancing, painting, and home yoga', 'positive vibes and the imagination of a better future after COVID-19', 'family time']",[],"['social value of heritage through terms like resilience, creativity, empowerment, togetherness, union, love, experience, as well as memory, nostalgia for the past, and hope for the future']","['positive emotional expressions', 'dominant sentiment is trust followed by joy and anticipation']",[],[],"['heritage virtual tours can be designed to meld both normative and co-created heritage values', 'the physical management of heritage sites can be enhanced by associating the digitally manifest meanings to effectively communicate cultural values to audiences through interactive cognitive-emotional experiences']",['The knowledge gained from this analysis can support the conservation and management of heritage which is dependent on ascribed cultural values representing the diversity of interpretations.'],"['The method employed in this work is transferable and can be employed to assess data from other social media platforms.', 'The use of LDA and sentiment analysis can contribute to the online discourse of cultural heritage during later phases of and after the pandemic.', 'Social media data analysis allows comprehensive documentation and interpretation of the co-construction processes of heritage values over time and across different platforms.']","['COVID-19 crisis was a catalyst for sharing heritage content online', 'digital platform provided a public virtual space that allowed greater participation and engagement in shaping the online heritage discourse']",['new theoretical concept of virtual heritage –\nheritage activities (e.g. dancing) and values surrounding those activities (e.g. resilience) that are fully co-created within the digital realm'],['digital gaps hindering participation'],"['maintain the anonymity of Instagram users, each was given a number prefixed by User', 'some of the Instagram users were contacted and they gave their consent to include their posted photos, and some wanted to include their names, in the results section']",[],['ethical approach to social media research is grounded on preventing harm to the research subjects and protecting their privacy'],[],['respecting the site users and adhering to the terms of service and privacy policy of the social media platform'],[],[],[],,,,,"R-Studio (R Core Team, 2020)",dplyr; textcat; maps; rworldmap; topicmodels; ldatuning; syuzhet,,#Share Our Heritage; #Share Culture; #traveltomorrow; #stayhome; #stayhomestaysafe; #museumweek; #museumsfromhome; #museumsforculture; #picoftheday; #emptymuseum; #travelphotography; #unescoheritage; #shareknowledge; #noheritagenofuture; #homeyoga; #publichealth; #culturaldiversity; folksonomies,,,
