"_Ginzarly et al.-2022-THE INTERPLAY OF CONTEXT, EXPERI.txt","The Interplay of Context, Experience, and Emotion at World Heritage Sites: a Qualitative and Machine Learning Approach",Manal Ginzarly; F. Jordan Srour; Ana Pereira Roders,University of Liège; Lebanese American University; Delft University of Technology,2021,journal article,Tourism Culture & Communication,,10.3727/109830421X16345418234065,https://www.researchgate.net/publication/355667472,,,World Heritage (6),cultural; natural; intangible; tangible (4),Byblos historic city (2); Roman temples of Baalbek (2); WHSs (World Heritage Sites) (10); heritage site (3); heritage place (4); heritage property (3); historic commercial area; archeological remains; heritage destination,OUV (3); Outstanding Universal Value; Cultural Significance; Historic/Aesthetical/Scientific Value; historic value (7); aesthetical value (7); economic value (3); cultural values (4); authenticity (7); historic Roman construction; national official statement of values; national organizations for WH management; WH protection and enhancement strategies; heritage tourism (2); heritage interpretation (2); heritage audiences; heritage experience (6); heritage values (2); heritage assets; tangible heritage (2); intangible attributes (2); sustainable conservation and management; historic urban fabric; cultural heritage (3); World Heritage value; cultural institutions; cultural values; historic Roman construction; official narratives; associative values (2); historic site; archaeological site,"aesthetical value - Refers to aesthetics that mobilize a sense of place or contribute to positive/negative experience because of the presence or absence of an aesthetical quality.; historic value - Refers to implicit interactions with the history of the heritage place.; social value - This social value refers to imaginations, connections to the past, meanings, and emotions involved in personal interactions with the heritage place.; economic value - This economic value refers to the cost of recreational amenities: restaurants, souvenir shops, or nearby hotels and heritage sites entrance fees.","Social (Emotional (collective)): The study highlights the importance of personal interaction with heritage sites, emphasizing the emotional connection and sense of place that visitors experience, contributing to their cultural identity and pride.; Historic (Symbolic): The study discusses how visitors engage in a dialogue with the past, reinforcing their sense of place and contributing to meaning-making, which aligns with the symbolic association of heritage with important past events.; Aesthetical (Artistic): The study emphasizes the beauty of heritage attributes and architectural details, as well as the natural landscape surrounding the heritage sites, which evokes an emotional response and personal engagement with the place.; Economic (Use): The study mentions the cost of recreational amenities, restaurants, souvenir shops, nearby hotels, and heritage site entrance fees, indicating the economic value derived from the function and utility of the heritage assets.",sustainable conservation and management,"Byblos; Baalbek; Angkor Wat; Istanbul; Petra, Jordan; Zuoying; Xi’an; Alentejo, Portugal; Macao’s Historic Center","Lebanon (2); Middle East; Mediterranean (2); Russia; China (2); Asia; Kenya; Nepal; Victoria Falls; Yunyang County (Chongqing, China)",Roman period (4),,,,Trip Advisor (7); Flickr (2); Instagram (2); social media platforms (2); social media (12); Twitter,reviews (12); embedded metadata (2); text (2); online reviews (3); UGC (User-Generated Content) (3); online heritage practices; online social interactions; textual data,,,,,title; time of visit; reviewer’s name; country of origin,A total of 1060 reviews were retrieved for Baalbek and 1170 reviews for Byblos.,,,,,,,Reviews for both locations were collected for the period between January 2015 and August 2019.,Byblos historic city and the Roman temples of Baalbek.,Web 