_Goldspiel et al.-2023-Snapshots of Nature-Based Recrea.txt,Snapshots of Nature-Based Recreation Across Rural Landscapes:,Harrison Goldspiel; Danielle Kuehn; Breton Barr; Joshua Badding,"Department of Wildlife, Fisheries, and Conservation Biology, University of Maine, Orono, ME, USA; Department of Environmental Biology, State University of New York College of Environmental Science and Forestry, Syracuse, NY, USA; Department of Forest and Natural Resources Management, State University of New York College of Environmental Science and Forestry, Syracuse, NY, USA",2023,journal article,Ecological Processes,Springer Nature,https://doi.org/10.1007/s00267022-01728-2,https://doi.org/10.1007/s00267022-01728-2,,,,cultural; natural,rural landscapes (1); Northern Forest (2); municipal parks (1); conservation easement properties (1); protected areas (2); public areas (5); private areas (4); rural areas (11); open spaces (1),cultural ecosystem services (7); social–ecological systems (1); conservation (3); protected areas (2); sustainable use (1),,"{'value': 'Social (Emotional (collective))', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the importance of nature-based recreation for local and regional economies and human livelihoods, suggesting a connection to cultural identity and a sense of place attachment within the communities that benefit from these services.'}; {'value': 'Economic (Use)', 'reasoning': 'The paper explicitly mentions the economic importance of nature-based tourism and recreation to rural communities, highlighting the function and utility of natural areas for generating revenue and supporting livelihoods.'}; {'value': 'Ecological (Essential)', 'reasoning': 'The paper emphasizes the role of cultural ecosystem services in fostering conservation of large landscapes, indicating an understanding of the essential presence of ecological ideologies in the design and construction of sustainable systems.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical (Notable)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses visitor preferences for different aesthetic and/or environmental features, suggesting that the aesthetic qualities of the landscape are important for attracting visitors and supporting recreation.'}",conservation (2); protecting (1); managing (1),Northern Forest Region (4); Maine (4); New Hampshire (6); Vermont (4); New York (3),Northern Forest Region (10); northeastern United States (2); Maine (4); New Hampshire (6); Vermont (4); New York (3); Germany (1); Mulde River basin (1); United States (1); South Korea (1); Australia (1); India (1); European Alps (1),,,Applications of such “big data” are often limited in either spatiotemporal or thematic scope.,"1. What are the predominant themes of crowdsourced photographs in large rural regions and how do visitors engage with cultural-ecosystem services across time and space? 2. How well do different landscape characteristics explain human engagement with nature in rural spaces and predict suitability for visitation in distinct geopolitical regions? 3. How relevant are rural geotagged photographs to parks, protected areas, and outdoor recreation, and how well do unsupervised image classiﬁcation algorithms track observed image content?",Flickr (12); Instagram (2); Facebook (1),images (17); geotagged photographs (4); metadata (5); text captions (2),geotagged (5); latitude (1); longitude (1),spatial hotspots (1); spatial patterns (5); spatial scales (2),temporal (4); time (2); daily (1); seasonal patterns (2); annual photography trends (1); annual variation (1); spatiotemporal patterns (3); time periods (1),,user ID (1); location of residence (1); personal image captions (1),"280,503 images",,,"280,503",,,,2012 and 2017,Northern Forest Region,"Public images were obtained from Flickr, a web-based image repository and social media platform.",Flickr’s Application Programmer Interface (API) (2),"randomly selected one photograph per user per day (i.e., PUD), stratiﬁed by image theme, to reduce biases by individual users on spatiotemporal patterns of visitation",,All publicly accessible images taken between 2012 and 2017 within the boundaries of the NFR were accessed.,automated,"two researchers, undergraduate students",[],92%,image classiﬁcation (4); clustering (3); predictive models (1),neural network (1); Deep Convolutional Neural Networks (1); random forests (5),Clarifai (8),,,,geographic variables (4); environmental variables (1); image tags (4),,70% and testing (30%),,,,,,,,"{'mtry': 'the number of variables randomly selected when growing each tree', 'sampsize': 'the number of images randomly sampled from the training data for each tree'}",,,,,,,,,yes,['image tag networks (1)'],image tags,co-occurrence,,,modularity (1),Monte Carlo network analysis,,,identify principal themes of visitation in the region and distinguish among different forms of culturalecosystem services,sensitivity (3); speciﬁcity (3); overall accuracy (2); confusion matrix (2); intraspeciﬁc accuracy (1),[],[],[],,The majority of sample images from protected areas (83% and 86% of photographs according to two independent researchers) were related to recreation.,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['landscape scenery was more prominently featured by people in public areas.', 'Photos of people, biota, human structures, and transportation were more common in private areas']",[],[],[],[],"['Daily and seasonal patterns in photography were broadly consistent throughout the region, whereas annual photography trends were more variable.']","['land-use planning (1)', 'support regional opportunities for recreation (1)', 'promote conservation (1)']",[],['Generating automated tags and clustering images based on common visual features can streamline a traditionally cumbersome process for describing and analyzing human–environment interactions from crowdsourced data.'],[],[],"['not all visitors are capturing their experiences with cameras and posting content on the Internet.', 'Uploading biases are difﬁcult to ascertain and should be studied with respect to different types of outdoor activities to better understand processes behind presence/absence point patterns and potentially create new opportunities for modeling visitor routes and itineraries over small and large regions.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],available in supplemental Git Hub repository at https://github.com/hgoldspiel/ﬂickr,Public images were obtained from Flickr,,,Python statistical software language (Van Rossum and Drake 2000),Shapely (v1.5.17); requests (v2.14.2); QGIS (v3.16.1); R statistical software; raster package; ranger package; caret package,,nature-based recreation (6); human-environment interactions (7); visitor engagement (4); geopolitical scales (4); landscape characteristics (2); rural spaces (1); outdoor recreation (3); image content (2); Clarifai-generated tags (2); geographic variables (3); environmental variables (1); cultural ecosystem services (CES) (11),,,
_Gomez et al.-2019-Learning from #Barcelona Instagr.txt,Learning from #Barcelona Instagram,,,2019,other,,Springer Nature Switzerland AG,,,,,,,Barcelona (17),,,,,Barcelona; Ciutat Vella; Eixample; La Barceloneta; El G`otic; Sant Pere; El Born; El Raval; Sant Mart´ı; El Poblenou; Diagonal Mar; Sants-Montjuic; Sants; Poble Sec; Sarri`a; Vallvidrera; Gr`acia; Vila de Gr`acia; Vallcarca; La Salut,Venezuela; United States; European,,,"Measuring the tourism overcrowding per neighborhood is not easy, since some areas receive high touristic interest but they don’t have hotels or tourism installations.",Our goal is to learn which visual elements appear in photos when people is posting about each neighborhood.,Instagram (13),images (10); captions (10); text (10),,,,,,around 1.3 million images,"335, 880 diﬀerent users",,"597, 766 image-captions pairs",,,,September and December of 2017,Barcelona,gathered a dataset of Instagram images related to Barcelona uploaded between September and December of 2017,Google’s language detection API,,"Users with many publications tend to be spam or commercial accounts. We blacklisted the users having more than 50 publications and discarded all their content. Images with short captions are not desirable since they usually do not provide enough information of the image to learn from. We discarded all the images accompanied with captions shorter than 3 words. Repeated images tend to be spam and should be discarded. Images containing other city names in their captions were also discarded, since they tend to be spam and to not provide information related to Barcelona. Images with captions in other languages than English, Spanish or Catalan were discarded since in this work we analyze publications related to those languages.",,,,[],,,Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) (7),Google Net (1),weights are initialized with an Image Net [9] pretrained network,word embedding methods (2); Word2Vec (14); Glo Ve (1); LSTM encodings (2),,images (5); captions (5),,"80% training set, 5% validation set and 15% retrieval set",,Stochastic Gradient Descent,0.001,120,25,weight decay of 2e −4,,{'margin': '0.4'},,,,,,,,"Img2Neigh Ctx is trained to minimize this loss, which maximizes the diﬀerence between the distances of the image with the positive and negative captions upon a certain margin.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['we show how state of the art multi-modal text and images techniques can be applied to learn from Instagram data.', 'we show how Instagram data related to a city can be used to do a per-neighborhood analysis obtaining very useful social and commercial information.', 'we propose a method to analyze tourism activity at a neighborhood level using only images and text.']","['we provide a new dataset, Insta Barcelona, formed by Instagram images related to Barcelona and its captions, and the code and models used to perform the subsequent experiments.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],code used in the project is available on https://github.com/gombru/insbcn,The dataset is available on https://gombru.github.io/2018/ 08/02/Insta Barcelona/.,,,,,,,,,
