_Gosal and Ziv-2020-Landscape aesthetics Spatial mo.txt,This is a repository copy of Landscape aesthetics: Spatial modelling and mapping using,,,,other,,,,,,,,,cultural landscape (2); natural landscape (1); rural landscape (1); historic features (1),heritage-based (1); natural heritage (1),,"{'Aesthetical Values': 'The paper discusses the aesthetic value of landscapes, particularly in relation to naturalness, complexity, and the presence of mountains and water bodies. This aligns with the artistic and notable aspects of aesthetic values, as the study seeks to quantify and map these qualities.'}; {'Social Values': 'The paper implicitly discusses emotional (collective) values by exploring how landscapes contribute to a sense of place attachment and cultural identity, particularly through the study of aesthetic preferences in the Yorkshire Dales National Park.'}; {'Economic Values': 'The paper implicitly discusses entertainment value by exploring how landscapes contribute to tourism. The study also mentions that tourism is important for the national park with 3.85 million visitors (including 0.52 million overnight visitors) totalling 5.06 million tourist days in 2017 alone, bringing in £263 million into the regional economy'}",,"Yorkshire Dales National Park; Ruissalo Island, Finland; Spanish national parks","England; UK; Europe; Basque Country, Spain; Nebraska; French Alps; Dutch; Netherlands; Germany; Switzerland; East Asians; Americans; Brazil; EU states",,,"Whilst this field has grown in socioecological research and public interest, it is still missing well developed quantitative and standardised techniques for assessment","(a) As the number of paired comparisons increases by a power of 2, how well can this technique be applied to larger datasets with partial coverage and the BT model? (b) Given that social media photos are likely to be ‘aesthetic’ (at least to the Flickr user who took and uploaded them), would including publicly available and systematically stratified European Land Use and Coverage Area frame Survey (LUCAS) survey photos (Ballin et al., 2018) give a reference set with a wider ‘aesthetic gradient’ on which we can train a predictive model? (c) Can machine learning be used to create a useable set of linguistic predictors for a regression analysis, allowing to extrapolate a BT model based on a subset of images to predict landscape aesthetics at the landscape scale in a given cultural context?",Flickr (4); Instagram (1); Panaromio (1),images (11); photos (13); text (1),geographic coordinates (1); geo-tagged photos (2),spatial modelling (2); mapping (4),date (1); seasons (1),favourite images (1),photographic ID (1); unique ID (1),"43,863 images",,,"43,863",,,,2009-2018,Yorkshire Dales National Park,downloaded,Flickr’s Application Programming Interface (API) using the Python ‘Scrapy’ package,stratified sample,Images were selected for the years 2009-2018 and geographically cropped to the boundary of the Yorkshire Dales National Park,"1,393 terms related to human activity or infrastructure, for example, ‘terrier’, ‘garden gnome’, or ‘coffee house’, used to filter the images, resulting in 22,615 images related to landscape with minimal human infrastructure",machine learning algorithm,,[],,regression analysis (2); machine learning (6),neural networks (2); deep learning (2),,pre-trained machine learning algorithm,,,content tags (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,['fully connected undirected ‘graph’'],vertices (images),edges (paired comparisons),,,,,,,,RMSE (1); R2 (2); Kendall’s tau-b (3),[],[],[],,"High scoring images can be seen to be exhibit more ‘complexity’, having a ‘hilly’ character, with the horizon not being complexly ‘horizontal’.",[],[],,[],[],[],8-fold cross-validation,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['the use of photographs from all seasons in this\nstudy was a limitation', 'By using only ten people in this study, similar in age and education level, with an interest in\ngeography as a subject, there was a potential limitation in not being representation of the general\npopulation']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,R (3),,landscape aesthetics (13); aesthetic value (16); paired-comparisons (10); natural language processing (2); spatial model (1); protected area (3); ecosystem services (3); Likert scale (8); scenic beauty (4); social media (8); machine learning (6); Bradley Terry (BT) model (11); LUCAS (7); GIS (2); Open Street Map (1); neural network (2); deep learning (2); Python (3); RStudio (1); Arc Map (1); Construct 3 (1); Wordnet (1); Scrapy (1); tm R package (1); wordnet R package (1); Bradley Terry2’ R package (1),,,
"_Gosal et al.-2019-Using social media, machine lear.txt","Using social media, machine learning and natural language",Gosal; Newton; Gillingham,,,other,,,,,,,,,,cultural services (4); cultural Ecosystem Services (CES) (3); cultural and natural heritage (1); protected area (PA) (1),,"{'SOCIAL': 'The study identifies different user groups based on their recreational activities and interests in the Camargue region, suggesting a focus on understanding the social and cultural identity associated with the area.'}; {'ECONOMIC': 'The study mentions the potential for birdwatching tourism to benefit the local economy, indicating a consideration of the economic value derived from recreational activities in the Camargue.'}; {'POLITICAL': 'The study aims to inform managers of Protected Areas (PAs) about the spatial pattern of different uses of the site, suggesting an interest in incorporating the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'AESTHETICAL': 'The study mentions that 87% of those surveyed in the Camargue enjoy and are interested in the observation of fauna (birds and other wildlife) in an aesthetically pleasing and accessible landscape.'}",protection (1); conservation (3),Camargue; Camargue region in 15 Southern France; Camargue Biosphere Reserve in the Rhône delta,"Southern France; Europe; Vermont, USA; North Carolina; Singapore; Finland; Poland; US; Middle Atlantic Coastal Plain; French Alps",,,"Cultural services, such as recreation, are the most challenging group of Ecosystem Services (ES) to 29 study, as it is evident from their low frequency of inclusion both in scientific studies (Feld et al., 30 2009) and national ecosystem assessments (Geijzendorffer et al., 2017).",(a) identify a typology of users of the Camargue PA; (b) map the 138 spatial and intra-annual pattern of use for each group of beneficiaries; (c) identify inter-annual and 139 long-term trends in visitations for each group; (d) identify use value for protected area managers of 140 the detected trends.,Flickr (14); Facebook (1); Twitter (2); Instagram (4); Panoramio (1),photos (11); geotagged images (3); videos (1); text (2),longitude and latitude (2),spatial (11); spatial distributions (2); spatial pattern (3); spatial scales (1); spatial and temporal dynamics (1); spatial and temporal flexible use (1); spatial distinction (1),temporal (6); date and time (1); intra-annual pattern (1); inter-annual (1); long-term trends (2); seasonal (8); yearly season (1),,longitude and latitude (1); date and time (1); user ID (1),"20,051 images",1292 users,,"20,051",,,,between 2007 and 2016,"the Camargue, France",retrieved images from the photo-sharing social media website Flickr using Python scripts,Flickr͛Ɛ Application Programming Interface (API).,"Images were not filtered, as this study does not purely look at the contribution of nature 185 to recreation.",,,Automatic annotation of descriptive terms for the image͛Ɛ content ǁŝƚŚ confidence scores,,[],,clustering (3); image analysis (3),neural network (1),Google Cloud Vision (4); Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA) (7); SOM (4),Google Cloud Vision API was used for ĂƵƚŽŵĂƚŝĐ ĂŶŶŽƚĂƚŝŽŶ ŽĨ ĚĞƐĐƌŝƉƚŝǀĞ ƚĞƌŵƐ ĨŽƌ ƚŚĞ ŝŵĂŐĞ͛Ɛ 182 content ǁŝƚŚ ĐŽŶĨŝĚĞŶĐĞ ƐĐŽƌĞƐ,,,image annotations (3),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['demonstrate a novel method combining social media, machine learning and natural language 127 processing, using a case study in the Camargue, France, which can be applied to other protected 128 areas.']",[],[],"['Flickr data is biased by factors that are subject to continuous change including the popularity of the 458 platform, user groups and geography (Sessions et al., 2016).', 'Not all visitors will 455 take and upload photos onto a social media platform, hence sampling bias is inherent in Flickr and 456 social media data (Levin, Lechner and Brown, 2017; Walden-Schreiner et al., 2018).']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,R (5),,,,,
_Grandi and Neri-2014-Sentiment analysis and city bran.txt,Sentiment Analysis and City Branding,Grandi; Neri,,,other,,,,,,,,,historic center (1); historic city center (2),well preserved historic center (1); City of Music by Unesco (1),,,,Bologna (20),Italy (3); Italian (4),,,,"This paper describes, on one hand, the characteristics of Sentiment Analysis and, on the other hand, the results of its application to an empirical research about the brand perception of the city of Bologna on the Web.",Social Media (7),text (3),,,,,,"Around 20,000 posts and blog contributions related to Bologna have been collected by focus crawling techniques.",,,,,,,between the end of 2012 and March 2013,,focus crawling techniques (1),,,,,,,[],,Sentiment Analysis (6); Opinion Mining (6); classification (2); clustering (1),Machine-Learning (2),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,['Network of Concepts (1)'],concepts (4),relations (3),,,,,,,,accuracy (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,city branding (10); place marketing (4); territorial marketing (1); brand image (1); brand perception (2); Sentiment Analysis (6); Opinion Mining (6); Knowledge Mining (3); Semantic Engine (1); Search Engine (1); Classification Engine (1); Concept Cloud (2); Semantic Role (2); Tag Cloud (1); Motor Valley (2); porticos (2),,,
_Grugni et al.-2022-USE OF OBJECT RECOGNITION AI IN.txt,USE OF OBJECT RECOGNITION AI IN COMMUNITY AND HERITAGE,F. GRUGNI; M. VOLTOLINA; T. CATTANEO,,2022,conference paper,"POST-CARBON, Proceedings of the 27th International Conference of the Association for Computer-Aided Architectural Design Research in Asia (CAADRIA) 2022, Volume 1, 717-726","the Association for Computer-Aided Architectural Design Research in Asia (CAADRIA), Hong Kong",,,,717-726,Cultural Heritage (2); Natural Heritage (1),,cities (1); neighbourhood (4); village (4); rural territory (2); castles (2); ancient churches (1),cultural heritage (3); natural heritage (2); sustainable development (4); sustainable tourism (2); preservation (2),,"{'value': 'Social – Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study aims to understand the relationship between a community and a place through the emotional, subjective point of view of its members, focusing on how the community lives and perceives the place.'}; {'value': 'Historic – Educational', 'reasoning': 'The study uses object recognition algorithms to identify and analyze elements related to historical heritage, such as castles and churches, which have the potential to provide knowledge about the past.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical – Artistic', 'reasoning': 'The study analyzes images from social media to understand the aesthetic and artistic elements that are valued by the community in different locations, such as architecture and natural landscapes.'}",heritage (4); preserving (1); promoting (2),"Siping Road, Shanghai, China; Moscopole, Albania; Oltrepò Pavese, Italy; Corvino San Quirico; Mornico Losana; Oliva Gessi; Torricella Verzate","Shanghai, China; Yangpu district; Albania; Korçë County; Italy; Pavia; United States of America",,,"In order to plan effective strategies for the sustainable development of individual communities, as prescribed by the United Nations’ Sustainable Development Goal 11, it is necessary for designers and policy makers to gain a deep awareness of the bond that connects people to their territory.","This research argues that AI-driven technologies, and specifically Object Recognition algorithms, can be effectively used for this purpose.",Instagram (7); Trip Advisor (1),images (11); geo-tagged images (3); metadata (2); text (1),geo-tag (5),,date range (1),,,"The number of pictures collected for each case study depends on the local context and the scale of the considered area: for the first two case studies (a neighbourhood and a village), 500 images have been selected, while for the third one (a larger-scale territory) the number increased to 1,200.",,,"500 (Siping Road), 500 (Moscopole), 1200 (Oltrepò Pavese)",,,,2021 (1),"Siping Road, Moscopole, Oltrepò Pavese","collected through a python script, based on their geo-tag and hashtags",python script (1),,,,,,[],,Object Recognition (8),,R-CNN (1); SSD model (1),pre-trained with millions of images,,SIFT (Scale-Invariant Feature Transform) system (1); Lowe method (1); Spatial Pyramid Planning strategy (1),images (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"the algorithm has been pre-trained with millions of images and has learnt to recognise around 10,000 different objects (“picturable nouns”). It has also been programmed to select, for each picture, only the five most prominent objects, so as to avoid giving irrelevant or redundant information as output.",yes,[],objects (2),frequency with which different objects appear in the image dataset,,,,,,,shows the frequency with which different objects appear in the image dataset,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['drafting strategies for the sustainable development of the local communities (2)'],[],[],[],[],"['The effectiveness of strategies based on AI-driven technologies always depends on the quality of input data.', 'As the method proposed in this study takes its dataset from social media, it is important to be aware that these platforms are used by certain demographic groups more than others.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['the method proposed in this research is not intended to be used alone, but rather as part of a wider strategy that also involves other tools and techniques.']",,,,,,,,genius loci (2); SDG 11 (3); Sustainable Development Goal 11 (1); Object Recognition algorithms (3); picturable nouns (1),,,
