_Gülçin and Yalçinkaya-2024-Correlating fluency theory-based.txt,Correlating fluency theory-based visual aesthetic preferences with landscape types and features using social media photographs and machine learning,Derya Gülçin; Nermin Merve Yalçınkaya,,2021,journal article,Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/tgsi20,,,www.tandfonline.com/journals/tgsi20,,,,cultural; natural; historical,historical sites (2); ancient cities of Herakleia and Latmos; Byzantine Yediler Monastery; historical areas of the islets in Lake Bafa; Byzantine archeological remains; ancient rock paintings,historical importance; historical sites; cultural value; historical areas; Byzantine archeological remains; cultural site; archeological sites; cultural heritage sites,,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study suggests that historical sites are important not just for their cultural value, but for their visual aesthetic value as well, indicating a sense of place attachment and communal value.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study analyzes visual aesthetic preferences in landscapes, suggesting an appreciation for the artistic qualities of natural and built environments.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study highlights the importance of historical sites and their visual aesthetic value, implying their potential to provide knowledge about the past.'}",conservation strategies,Lake Bafa; Lake Bafa Natural Park; Kapıkırı; Beşparmak Mountains; Latmos; Herakleia; Yediler Monastery; Kahve Asar Island; Ikiz Islands; Küçük Ikiz Island; Büyük Ikiz Island; Menet Island; Kapıkırı Island; Muğla; Aydın; Turkey,Turkey; Aegean Sea; district of Milas; province of Muğla; district of Söke; province of Aydın; southwest Turkey,pre-classical times,,"Understanding the visual aesthetic value which people attach to landscapes is of considerable importance, especially in the case of landscapes that have become endangered.",(1) How can people’s visual aesthetic liking of landscape be predicted? (2) How does visual aesthetic liking of landscape vary by type of landscape? (3) Which landscape features dominate in landscapes of high visual aesthetic value?,Flickr; Google Earth; Twitter; Instagram; Facebook,geotagged photographs; text; spatial maps,geotagged photographs; geolocations; latitude; longitude,spatial distribution,dates; years between 2004 and 2020; time of uploading,view counts,dates; hashtags; user identities; view counts; location (latitude and longitude); the dates when they were taken and uploaded; user identities,"6,091 geotagged photographs",106 different users,,,,,,2004 and 2020,"55,366.33 ha between the district of Milas in the province of Muğla and the district of Söke in the province of Aydın, in southwest Turkey",geotagged photographs collected from Flickr and Google Earth,Flickr API,"photographs showing the landscape were shared on social media from 2004 onwards, they were grouped in such a way as to cover the years between 2004 and 2020",Images that do not depict the landscape (personal photographs such as selfies) were excluded.,Images that do not depict the landscape (personal photographs such as selfies) were excluded.,machine-learning algorithm,,[],,image labeling,machine learning algorithm,,,,,images,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],"['historical sites are not just important for their cultural value, but also for their visual aesthetic value']",[],[],[],"['designing future landscapes', 'determining potential conservation strategies', 'landscape planning and development']",[],[],[],[],"['inaccessibility of data from widelyused social media platforms', 'lack of any assessment of the accuracy of the measure of visual aesthetic liking']",['privacy policies'],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['One of the limitations of this study, therefore, is the inaccessibility of data from widelyused social media platforms.', 'Another limitation of this study is the lack of any assessment of the accuracy of the measure of visual aesthetic liking.']",,There is no data related to this work.,,,R v. 4.0.3,,,visual aesthetic liking; Landscape Character Assessment; machine learning; visual aesthetic preference; viewshed analysis; fluency theory; image fluency; processing fluency; image fluency theory; Google Cloud Vision API; APIs; GIS; OLS; geotagged photographs; Euclidean Distance tool; Geoalgorithms tool of the QGIS 2.8; photo­searcher R library; ASTER GGDEM – Global Digital Elevation Model,,,
_Hacar et al.-2025-Where We Rate The Impact of Urb.txt,Where We Rate The Impact of Urb,Mihaela Popa; Ö.Ö.H.; M.H.; L.P.; F.G.,,2025,other,,,,,,,,,urban spaces (1),,,,,"Melbourne; Melbourne’s city center; Matera, Italy; Dublin",Türkiye; Spain; Canada; Austria,,,"While the existing research has extensively explored the effect of product and business attributes on consumer decisions, the spatial context has often been overlooked.","Specifically, we focus on three research objectives: Centrality Impact: Determine to what extent the centrality of a specific location (in particular, the street on which an experience occurs) affects whether an experience is rated positively or negatively. Pedestrian Flow Effect: Investigate how dynamic spatial data, including sensorderived pedestrian counts, affects the volume and sentiment of online reviews. Role of Spatial Attributes: Explore the contribution of various spatial factors in shaping digital consumer behavior.",Google Maps; Google; Yelp; Instagram; Flickr,text; geolocated reviews; ratings; photos; mobile data,geolocated reviews; geotagged photos,,,reviews; ratings,,,,,,,,,2022 and 2023,Melbourne’s city center,Google Maps API,Google Maps API,only venues with a minimum of 10 reviews were selected,,,,,[],,classification,random forest,,,,,pedestrian count; city dwelling distribution; street type; sinuosity; venue proximity,,80–20%,,,,,,,,"{'n_estimators': '100', 'criterion': 'Gini impurity', 'max_features': 'square root of the total features'}",,,,,,,,,yes,['street networks'],street segment,connectivity within the network,,,betweenness; closeness; degree; eigenvector; harmonic; Katz centralities; load; Page Rank,,,,assess the connectivity within the network,F1-score,['The model achieved a 65% F1-score for review volume classifications and a 62% for visitor score.'],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],80–20% split,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['assisting new EDO businesses in selecting optimal locations', 'providing existing EDO businesses with valuable insights for situation analysis']",[],['integrating dynamic pedestrian data and geometric street attributes into predictive modeling'],[],[],"['reliance on Google POI data, which excludes venues with fewer than 10 reviews', 'the use of digital footprints carries the risk of including fake or unreliable reviews']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,"Google POIs, including place type, review count, and ratings, can be extracted by Google Maps API. The OSM road data can be filtered and downloaded from https://download.bbbike.org/osm/bbbike/ (accessed on 1 December 2024). The Melbourne Open Data, including pedestrian count data and residential dwelling information, are available on https://data. melbourne.vic.gov.au/pages/home/ (accessed on 1 December 2024).",,https://download.bbbike.org/osm/bbbike/,,Network X Python Package,,digital footprints; urban dynamics; pedestrian density; centrality metrics; spatial analysis; urban street networks; urban mobility; urban spatial modeling; spatial predictors,,,
_Hadjiprocopis et al.-2014-Cloud-based 3D Reconstruction of.txt,Cloud-based 3D Reconstruction of Cultural Heritage,A.Hadjiprocopis; et al.,,2014,conference paper,EUROGRAPHICS Workshop on Graphics and Cultural Heritage,The Eurographics Association,,,,,Cultural Heritage (3),,historical monuments (2); historical sites (1),documentation of cultural heritage (1),,,,"Asinou Church, Cyprus (1); Archangelos Church, Pedoulas, Cyprus (1); Colisseum (3); Rome (2)",Cyprus (3); Rome (2),,,"One challenge in applying Sf M to images harvested from the web is that camera characteristics, resolution and content of these images may be uncertain.","Our main goal is to enable architects, archaeologists, but also the general public to reconstruct in 3D, views of historical monuments from images harvested from the web in a simple, robust and computationally efﬁcient manner on desktop or cluster computers or via a cloud-based ser- vice.",Flickr (3); Picasa (2),photographs (7); images (21); metadata (6),,,,,Exif tags (1),"1,090 images",,,"1,090",,,,,,Image Search and Retrieval Engine,API,,,,,,[],,,,SIFT (3),,,SIFT features (2),,,,,,,,,,,{},,single node comprising 2 Intel X5650@2.66GHz hexa-core CPU and 48GB RAM under Linux,,,48GB RAM,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,re-projection error (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['copyright (1)'],[],,,,,,,,3D point clouds (4); Structure from Motion (Sf M) (4); dense image matching techniques (1); Open Access Image Repositories (OAIR) (5); Digital Libraries (1); Virtual Museums (1),,,
_Haikal et al.-2022-Place embedding across cities in.txt,Place Embedding across Cities in,Christophe Haikal; Pegah Alizadeh; Christophe Rodrigues; Bi Chongke,"College of Intelligence and Computing, Tianjin University, China; Léonard De Vinci Pole Universitaire, Research Center, 92916 Paris La Défense, France",2022,conference paper,ACM SAC Conference (SAC’22),ACM,https://doi.org/10.1145/3477314.3506992,https://doi.org/10.1145/3477314.3506992,,8 pages,,,urban area(s) (2); touristic places,,,,,Lille; Bordeaux,France; China,,,"In the urban computing field, analysing human mobility patterns are used to understand tourists and residents behaviours, or urban areas functionalities.","we first propose a place embedding method based on users mobility trajectories in each city individually, second we learn a place translation function between two cities using the set of reviews written for each place.",Web-based Social Networks; Location-based Social Networks (Lb SN); Trip-Advisor; Yelp,text; reviews; mobility data; Geographical Data; GPS data,GPS data; place-latitude; place-longitude,spatial density maps; heatmaps,review-date,place-averagerating; place-number-of-review,place-id; place-name; place-url; place-tags,millions of reviews,"438 525 (Lille), 243 914 (Bordeaux)","1 059 335 (Lille), 555 022 (Bordeaux)",,,,,"2016 (Bordeaux), 2016, 2017 and 2018 (Lille)","Bordeaux Metropolitan (BM), Lille Metropolitan (LM)",extracted data,,"We keep 95% of each city trajectories, i.e. the trajectories of a length less than 3 and trajectories of long length are not taken into account.","stop words removal, stemming and lemmatisation",,,,[],,clustering; translation,Embedding-based models,word2vec; Place2vec,,word embedding; place embedding,Term Frequency - Inverse Document Frequency (tfidf ),users’ trajectories; reviews; tags,,40% of data for training the place translation function and 60% of data for evaluating the place translation performance,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,train the word2vec model on a set of place trajectories inside each city,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],PCA,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,human mobility patterns; urban areas functionalities; touristic attractions; urban strategies; mobility trajectories; place translation function; textual and semantic similarities; parallel places; machine translation matrix; Point of Interest (POI); power law distribution; Location-based Social Network (Lb SN); Trip-Advisor; Principal Component Analysis (PCA); Cosine similarity distance; Fuzzy Wuzzy ratio(FW); Levenshtein distance,,,
_Han et al.-2020-Extracting Representative Images.txt,Article ABSTRACT:,Qingyun Du; Shanshan Han; Fu Ren; Dawei Gui,,2020,journal article,,MDPI,,http://www.mdpi.com/2220-9964/9/2/81/s1,,,,,tourist attractions (14); places of interest (5); Summer Palace (3); Forbidden City (3); Olympic Park; Tiananmen Square; Capital Museum; Zoo; Zhongshan Park; Wangfujing (2); Ditan Park (2); CCTV Building; National Theater; Yuanmingyuan (2); Jingshan Park; Drum Tower; Old Summer Palace,,,,,Beijing (7),China,,,"After clustering, the challenge still exists for the task of extracting representative images within the geotagged base image data, because of the existence of noisy photos occupied by a large area proportion of humans and unrelated objects.","In this paper, we propose a framework containing an improved cluster method and multiple neural network models to extract representative images of tourist attractions.",Flickr (2),images / photos (14); geotagged posts (2),geotags,,,,,Flickr Creative Commons 100 Million Dataset,,,,,,,,Beijing (2),,,,We ﬁlter the dataset with an entropy-based method to remove certain photos uploaded by natives.,,,,[],,clustering (5); ranking,neural network models (2); deep ranking model (3); Multilayer Perceptron; convolutional models,Word2Vec (3); SSD model,pre-trained MLP,tag embeddings,,images,,,,,,,,,,{},DBSCAN (4); P-DBSCAN (5); TF-IDF-UF; random selection,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,"The quantitative and qualitative analysis, as well as the questionnaire results obtained from real-life tourists, demonstrate the eﬀectiveness of this framework.",[],[],,[],[],[],,DBSCAN (4); P-DBSCAN (5); TF-IDF-UF; random selection,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['tourism applications', 'tourist attraction recommendation', 'recommendation systems']",[],['improved cluster method'],[],[],"['even though noise images are ﬁltered prior to their importance ranking, some thematically-unrelated images remain.', 'This inﬂuences the ranking results, due to the diverse visual preferences of diﬀerent users.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,place-relevant tags (6); noisy photos (2); geotagged photos (4),,,
_Han et al.-2021-A Tourist Attraction Recommendat.txt,"A Tourist Attraction Recommendation Model Fusing Spatial, Temporal, and Visual Embeddings for Flickr-Geotagged Photos","Han, S.; Liu, C.; Chen, K.; Gui, D.; Du, Q.",,2021,journal article,ISPRS Int. J. Geo-Inf.,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/ ijgi10010020,https:// creativecommons.org/licenses/by/,,,,,tourist attractions (13),,,,,Tokyo (6),Japan,,,"However, few studies have comprehensively considered the content and contextual information that may inﬂu- ence the recommendation accuracy, especially tourist attractions’ visual content due to redundant and noisy geotagged photos; therefore, we propose a tourist attraction recommendation model for Flickr-geotagged photos which fuses spatial, temporal, and visual embeddings (STVE).","We propose a tourist attraction recommendation model fusing spatial, temporal, and visual embeddings (STVE) for geotagged photos.",Flickr (4),images; photos; geotagged photos (10); tags,coordinates; longitude and latitude,spatial distribution,time; time slots; time threshold,,user ID; capture time; longitude and latitude; user tags; ID of each geotagged photo,"145,397 photos",2750 users,,,,,,2018,Tokyo,,Yahoo Flickr Creative Commons 100 Million Dataset (YFCC100M),,"We ﬁltered two kinds of noisy photos before the photos are input into the deep ranking model: the photos whose content is mainly occupied by people are detected and removed by a single-shot multibox detector (SSD) model [41], and the photos that mainly displayed the objects are ﬁltered by Multilayer Perceptron pre-trained by the Caltech 101 dataset [42] and the Places2 dataset [43].",,,,[],,recommendation,Convolutional neural network,Word2Vec (5); SSD; Multilayer Perceptron,pre-trained,visual embeddings (10),,photos; location information; time; tags,,80%/20%,,,,,,,,{},User-based Collaborative Filtering (UCF); Bayesian Personalized Ranking-Matrix Factorization (BPR-MF); Factorizing Personalized Markov Chains with the localized regions (FPMC-LR); VBPR; Geo-Teaser,,,,,,,"The STVE model is built after some preprocessing steps, and it mainly consists of two parts: the embeddings of temporal and spatial constraint information and the embeddings of visual information.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,precision; recall; mean reciprocal rank (MRR); mean distance error (MDE),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,User-based Collaborative Filtering (UCF); Bayesian Personalized Ranking-Matrix Factorization (BPR-MF); Factorizing Personalized Markov Chains with the localized regions (FPMC-LR); VBPR; Geo-Teaser,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Given the CF-based models’ cold-start problems and the content-based models’ low accuracy problems, we propose a hybrid recommendation model for tourist attractions that fuses spatial, temporal, and visual embeddings (STVE).', 'We modify Skip-gram’s objective function to model the sequential factors in STVE, which takes advantage of Skip-gram’s characteristics that handle the sequential data well and is more in line with the actual tourist attraction recommendation scenario.', 'Given the problems that the noisy and redundant photos may exert a bad inﬂuence on the extraction of visual embeddings and the recommendation results, we propose a framework that can automatically remove the noisy and redundant photos and select representative images to extract visual embeddings of the tourist attractions for further use.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Publicly available datasets were analyzed in this study. This data can be found here: http://projects.dfki.uni-kl.de/yfcc100m/.,,http://projects.dfki.uni-kl.de/yfcc100m/,,,,geotagged photos; spatial embeddings; temporal embeddings; visual embeddings; matrix factorization; Bayesian Personalized Ranking; Skip-gram; tourist attraction recommendation; location recommendation,,,
_Hartmann et al.-2022-Automated motif identification.txt,Automated motif identification: Analysing Flickr images to identify popular viewpoints in Europe’s protected areas,Maximilian C. Hartmann; Olga Koblet; Manuel F. Baer; Ross S. Purves,"Department of Geography, University of Zurich, Winterthurerstrasse 190, Zurich, 8057, Zurich, Switzerland",2022,journal article,Journal of Outdoor Recreation and Tourism,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jort.2021.100479,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jort.2021.100479,,,,,,motifs (14); landscape (17); protected areas (6); cultural elements (2); cultural heritage (1),,,,Europe (6); Furka Pass (1); Le Mont-Saint-Michel (3); ¨Ascher-Wildkirchli’ in Switzerland (1),Europe (5); UK (1); Germany (2); France (1); Italy (1); Finnish (1),18th and 19th centuries (2),Covid pandemic (1),"What draws visitors to some locations in landscapes? Can we identify commonalities in what tourists photograph, appreciate and share using computational methods to analyse user-generated content?","Our aim is thus to provide an automated, scalable tool, capable of detecting existing motifs in large volumes of user-generated content (UGC).",Flickr (13); Instagram (1),images (15); georeferenced images (2); social media posts (2); tags (2),georeferenced images (2),spatial clusters (4); spatial distribution (1),timestamps (2),,unique user identifiers (1),2’146’176 (2),,,,,,,2002 and 2019 (1),European Natura 2000 sites (2),custom search script (1),Flickr Application Programming Interface (API) (1),,,filtered the enriched YFCC100M dataset to only include images within the Natura 2000 polygons (1),manual evaluation (1),,"['natural landscape elements (2)', 'cultural landscape elements (2)']",,clustering (6); image similarity (6),,HDBSCAN (5); SIFT (10); SURF (3); ORB (3),,,,raw image content (2),,,,,,,,,,{},,dedicated server (1),,,64 GB of RAM (1),3.2 days (1),,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,precision (2),['overall model precision of 87.4% (1)'],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,['tendency towards coastal regions (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],"['detection of existing and emerging landscape motifs has the potential to be a useful monitoring tool, both locally and across Europe (1)']",[],"['We developed a robust processing pipeline, capable of extracting motifs from millions of images, based on their locations, shared image features, and the number of users contributing to a motif. (1)']","['Our approach correctly identified 104 motifs in Natura 2000 areas, of which more than two thirds were related to cultural objects. (1)']",[],"['Key limitations of our work are the relatively small number of motifs identified after filtering, which also points to more general issues in using social media across very large areas outside of very popular or urban sites. (1)', 'Our results are subject to the same issues of representativeness and bias of all work with social media, but crucially our approach can be applied to any georeferenced images, and is thus transferable. (1)']",[],"['Social media is biased, and does not represent all elements of the population equally (Tufekci, 2014). (1)']",[],[],[],[],"['decision to licence images under a CC licence, incentivised by Flickr’s provision of free storage for very active users, is less likely to be made by occasional users with limited storage needs. (1)']",[],available from a Git Hub repository (github.com/Bell ador/Motif Detection) (1),"Since we used CC Flickr images, the images can also be reused, further aiding reproducibility (Nüst, Granell, Hofer, Konkol, Ostermann, Sileryte and Cerutti, 2018). (1)",,,,Open CV Python library (1),,user-generated content (UGC) (5); Yahoo-Flickr Creative Commons 100 Million’ (YFCC100M) dataset (2); Creative Commons (CC) licenses (3); Natura 2000 sites (10); Application Programming Interface (API) (1); Postgre SQL database (1); Post GIS extension (1); keypoints (3); scale invariant (2); image semantics (2),,,
_Hasnat and Hasan-2018-Identifying tourists and analyzi.txt,Identifying tourists and analyzing spatial patterns of their destinations from location-based social media data,T Md Mehedi Hasnat; Samiul Hasan,"Department of Civil, Environmental, and Construction Engineering, University of Central Florida, 12800 Pegasus Drive, Orlando, FL 32816, United States",2018,journal article,Transportation Research Part C,Elsevier Ltd.,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.trc.2018.09.006,www.elsevier.com/locate/trc,,38–54,,,,,,,,,Florida; USA; Central Florida; De-soto County; Volusia County,,,Traditional travel surveys generally observe resident travel behavior and rarely target tourists. Ubiquitous uses of social media platforms in smartphones have created a tremendous opportunity to gather digital traces of tourists at a large scale.,"In this paper, we present a framework on how to use location-based data from social media to gather and analyze travel behavior of tourists.",Twitter; Facebook; Instagram; Flickr,text; spatial (geotagged) and temporal (time-stamped) information,coordinates; latitude and longitude values,,time-stamped; time series analysis,,user proﬁle; user’s Twitter account proﬁle; Place,"635,787","66,919",,,,,"94,333","March 29, 2017 to April 24, 2017",Florida,Twitter data is collected using its Streaming API,"Twitter Streaming API, Twitter REST API",search interface for Florida,several ﬁltering steps to create a reliable sample,"users with at least two unique geo-tagged tweets are selected for further analysis, users with BOT scores equal to or less than 0.4",,,[],,classification; clustering,Decision Tree; K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN); Support Vector Machine (SVM),,,,,geographical coordinates,,70% training set and 30% test set,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; precision; recall; f-score; Caliński and Harabasz; Dunn; Davies and Bouldin; Silhouette Index,[],[],[],,"locations are found to be clustered around some of the famous tourist spots, reserved forests and wetlands, airport locations, and beaches in Florida.",[],[],,[],[],[],10-fold cross validation,,[],elbow plot; histogram,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['it shows how to collect reliable data on tourist travel behavior from social media'],[],[],"['In the process of BOT ﬁltering, it is possible that we might have excluded many individual user proﬁles along with the social BOTs as the ﬁltering process is not 100% accurate.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,travel behavior; destination choice; location-based social media; machine learning; classification; clustering; social media data; tourist travel behavior; spatial patterns; temporal patterns; geo-tagged tweets; social BOT; ensemble classification; k-fold cross validation; time series analysis; spatio-temporal densities,,,
_Hasnat et al.-2018-Understanding Tourist Destinatio.txt,Understanding Tourist Destination Choices from Geo-tagged Tweets,Md Mehedi Hasnat; Samiul Hasan,"Department of Civil, Environmental, and Construction Engineering, University of Central Florida, 12800 Pegasus Dr, Orlando, FL 32816",2018,other,Transportation Research Board 97th Annual Meeting,IEEE,,https://www.omnicoreagency.com/twitter-statistics/,,,,,,,,,,Florida,USA; Florida; Central Florida,,,"Cities with a significant number of tourists face traffic congestion due to tourism related travels. For such cities, conducting travel surveys is expensive as tourists are the most dynamic population segment whose size and travel choices change over time compared to residents.",The main objective of this study is to predict the next destination choice of tourists given the current location and other extracted features.,Twitter,text; geo-tagged; spatial; temporal,geo-tagged; latitude; longitude,,time-stamped,,user attributes; user activities; user posted locations; Twitter profile,"35,680 geo-tagged tweets",2438 user accounts,,,,,"35,680","March 29, 2017 to October 10, 2017",Central Florida region,streaming API,Twitter streaming API,filtered out a reliable sample and identified tourists using a data mining approach,We selected the tweets posted with at least 1 hour time difference.,BOT score and number of geotagged tweets,manually,,[],,classification,Conditional Random Field (CRF),,,,,tweet posted time (in hour of day); tweet posted day (in day of week); the type of current location visited by the tourists; an individual tourist’s trip day (i.e. 1st day or 2nd day or nth day of his/her visit in Florida),,70% of the data set for training and 30% for testing,,LBFGS or limited memory Broyden–Fletcher–Goldfarb–Shanno algorithm,,,,,,{},Gaussian HMM,,,,,,linear chain CRF,"To estimate the parameter ϴ of CRF the training data set containing state sequence y = y1, y2, ….., y T given an input sequence of feature x = x1, x2, ….., x T is given. In this study we assume all the tourists behave is a similar fashion, thereby creating a single sequence for the whole observation.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; precision; recall; f-score,"['accuracy, precision, recall and f-score of 65%, 64.7%, 64.9% and 64.8% respectively']",[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],70% of the data set for training and 30% for testing,Gaussian HMM,['Gaussian HMM which yielded a 15% accuracy in predicting the next location type'],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['This study presents a cost-effective way of collecting and analyzing longitudinal travel data of tourists.'],[],[],"['In this study, we assumed that all tourists have similar travel behavior patterns.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,,,,
_Hassan et al.-2023-Brand personality positioning of.txt,Brand personality positioning of world heritage sites: A text mining approach,M.A.E. Hassan; K. Zerva; S. Aulet,,2023,journal article,European Journal of Tourism Research,,https://doi.org/10.54055/ejtr.v35i.2923,https://doi.org/10.54055/ejtr.v35i.2923,,,World Heritage Sites (WHSs) (3); World Heritage (WH) (20); cultural heritage (4),cultural (14); natural (4); mixed (2),cultural attractions (13); heritage sites (3); historic cities (1),Outstanding Universal Values (OUVs) (4); Authenticity (2); Integrated (1); Protection and Management (2); Integrity (4); Criteria of Significance (1); heritage assets (2); heritage conservation (1); heritage aesthetics (3),aesthetic (1),"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study identifies brand personality categories associated with World Heritage Sites, which can contribute to a sense of cultural identity and pride for visitors.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study analyzes cultural heritage sites, which inherently have the potential to provide knowledge about the past.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': ""Artistic – The study identifies brand personality categories such as Sophistication, which are linked to the aesthetics of World Heritage Sites and visitors' evaluations of beauty.""}",heritage promotion (2); heritage brand equity (1); heritage conservation (1),,Asia and the Pacific (7); Europe (7); China (7); India (6); Japan (6); France (8); Germany (7); Italy (7); Spain (7),,,"World Heritage Sites (WHSs) are perceived as significant tourism brands. However, there is no in-depth analysis of the intangible meaning of World Heritage (WH) cultural attraction types from the visitor’s lens.","Thus, the current study’s primary aim is to identify the attributes of types of WH cultural attractions from the visitor’s lens for the first time. This helps define how visitors perceive particular WH cultural types, which may enable destination managers to target their promotional materials more effectively towards visitors.",Trip Advisor (13),text (1); visitor-generated reviews (7),,,,,,"9,971 visitor-generated reviews",,,,,,,May and August 2021,"WHSs located in Asia and Pacific and Europe two UNESCO areas containing the top seven countries with listed sites: China (37), India (30), Japan (19), France (39), Germany (44), Italy (50), and Spain (42).",extracted the identifications of attraction categories related to WHS data,Python’s ‘Beautiful Soap Library’,"Using Trip Advisor’s filter feature, only 402 URLs with the keywords ‘World Heritage’ or ‘UNESCO World Heritage’ from visitor-generated reviews in English were collected.","Nodes —text mining pre-processing features of Knime Software — were used to simplify the complexity of text data files (Denny & Spirling, 2017). These features include Punctuation, Numbers Erasers, and Stop-word Lists—which comprise the majority of meaningless words and eliminate any that were not likely to provide meaningful information for the meanings—. Lemmatization and Stemming were employed to shorten the terms to their original forms. These techniques also help combine words like beautiful, beauty, and beautifying. Case Converter node is used because the 222 WH category items were compared to the text in the units of analysis (textual data files) that were established for the study. Case convertors had to be used to ensure frequency accuracy in all textual data files. This ensured that all texts had the same upper or lowercase format. Frequency Filters were used that enabled the calculation of the frequencies of each item in a specific WH category across the entire unit of analysis.","Using Trip Advisor’s filter feature, only 402 URLs with the keywords ‘World Heritage’ or ‘UNESCO World Heritage’ from visitor-generated reviews in English were collected.",,,[],,text mining (3),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This study is limited by the fact that it only examined WHSs in selected seven countries, and the English-language Trip Advisor experience reviews.']",,,,,,,,WH 222-item personality dictionary (2); Big Five model (2); Brand Personality (BP) (7); Destination Personality (DP) (6); Four Thesaurus BP dictionaries (1); Knime Software (1); Antword Profiler (2); Correspondence Analysis (CA) (6),,,
_Hassan et al.-2023-Egypt Monuments Dataset version.txt,Egypt Monuments Dataset version 1:,ABSTRACT:,,2022,other,,,,https://github.com/mennatallahhassan/egypt- monuments-dataset,,,heritage sites (2),ancient,ancient Egyptian monuments (5); historical landmarks (4); ancient Temple; Head Sculpture,heritage sites (2); monument (6); monuments (13); historical landmarks (4); ancient Egyptian monuments (3); ancient Egyptian monument (2),,,,Luxor (3); Giza (7); Aswan (3); Cairo (10); Sohag; Beni Suef,Egypt (12); Delhi; India,Ancient,,deep learning (DL) depends largely on data availability and quality. The system’s performance is frequently more affected by the amount and quality of its training data than by its architecture and training specifics.,The Egypt Monuments Dataset v1 (EGYPT-v1) is introduced as a new scalable benchmark for fine-image classification (IC) and object recognition (OR) in the domain of ancient Egyptian monuments.,Youtube; Wikimedia Commons,images; videos,,,,,,over seven thousand images,,,over seven thousand images,,,,,Egypt,manually collecting images,,,,,Manual,,[],,fine-image classification; object recognition; image classification; instance-level recognition,deep network architectures,Res Net50; Inception V3; Le Net5,fine-tuned using pre-trained weights of Image Net,,,,,,,Adam,1e-05,,,,data augmentation techniques,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; Precision; Recall; F1-Score,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['practical applications in the fields of conservation and Egyptology'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],The images that comprise the dataset can be accessed on the Git Hub repository https://github.com/mennatallahhassan/egypt- monuments-dataset.,"The dataset, training instance labels, classification and recognition ground truth data, and metric computation code are accessible to the public.",,https://github.com/mennatallahhassan/egypt- monuments-dataset,,,,fine-grained tasks,,,
_Hassan et al.-2024-A Model for Brand Personality Wo.txt,A Model for Brand Personality Word Embedding: The Case of UNESCO World Heritage Sites,Mohamed Abdalla Elsayed Hassan; Konstantina Zerva; Silvia Aulet,,2024,journal article,Tourism Geographies,,https://doi.org/10.1177/00472875221147142,https://doi.org/10.1177/00472875221147142,,,World Heritage Site (WHS) (5); World Cultural and Natural Heritage (1),cultural (6); natural (2); mixed (1),World Heritage Sites (WHSs) (10); tourism destinations (3); tourism attractions (4); cathedral (1); Real Alcázar (1); Archivo General de Indias in Seville (1),brand personality (BP) (19); UNESCO (20); outstanding universal values (OUVs) (2); authenticity (4); integrity (4); protection (6); management (6); World Heritage (WH) (23); 1972 Convention Concerning the Protection of the World Cultural and Natural Heritage (1); WHS list (3); WHS status (2); WHS brand (2); WHS personality (5); WHS personality categories (6); WHS personality items (3); WH personality categories (10); WH personality distribution (2); WH cultural sites (1); WH concept (2); WH Convention (1); WH meanings (2); WH status (1),"valuable (2) Items such as “valuable,” and “significant” refer to how valuable WHSs are—this category also includes integrity items such as “integral” and “complete.”; universal (1) This refers to the “continental” and “universal” WHS identities and types, and is present extensively in the Brief Synthesis, and Protection and Management text files.; outstanding (2) In the Criteria Description, Description and Brief Synthesis text files, these items refer to the outstanding qualities and uniqueness of WHSs, such as “outstanding” and “extraordinary.”; economic (1) The perception of authenticity and integrity adds economic value and protection (Kim et al., 2018; Nian et al., 2019).","Social (Emotional (collective)) – The study explores the intangible meanings of World Heritage Sites (WHSs) and their potential influence on destination preferences, suggesting a connection to cultural identity and a sense of place attachment.; Aesthetical (Artistic) – The research identifies personality categories of WHSs, including 'Attractiveness,' which relates to the aesthetic qualities and originality of these sites, reflecting artistic value.; Historic (Symbolic) – The study mentions that countries try to include as many sites as possible on the WHS list, as this is seen as increasing their international pride and recognition, indicating a symbolic value related to historical significance.; Scientific (Conceptual) – The study uses a text-mining approach to extract WHS items and classify them based on their contextual similarity, employing natural language processing (NLP) and pre-trained language models, which aligns with the conceptual aspect of scientific values.",Protection (3); Management (6); heritage Conservation (1),,China (1); India (1); Japan (1); France (1); Germany (1); Italy (1); Spain (1); Asia and Pacific region (2); Europe (2); Iran (1); Catalunya (1),,Covid-19 pandemic (1),"Despite having received considerable academic attention, existing brand personality (BP) scales are difficult to generalize and likely need further development.","The aim of this study was to design a novel method for constructing context driven- BP categories through the use of a multi-disciplinary approach and advances in personality studies in psychology and natural language processing. The study has two aims. The first aim is to define the mechanisms by which WHS personality categories are constructed, given the fact that brands have cultural meanings and require a way of creating their personality categories. The second aim is to show how the personality categories of WHSs are perceived differently by experts and visitors to WHSs, since it has been suggested that personality categories may be perceived differently by those who communicate the symbolic meaning of brands and the receivers of those messages (Ranfagni et al., 2016).",Trip Advisor (2); Tripadvisor (11),text (1); digital text data (2); digital visitor reviews (2); digital data (1),,,,,,"9,920 individual reviews (1)",,"9,920 Tripadvisor reviews (1)",,,,,"accessed June 28, 2021 (2)","1,121 UNESCO World Heritage Sites (WHSs) (1)","texts describing 1,121 WHSs from the UNESCO WH Center website, (hereafter, the UNESCO corpus), and 9,920 Tripadvisor reviews of WHSs in seven countries (hereafter, the Tripadvisor corpus) were extracted",,"The sample was limited to reviews written in English, although visitors were from many countries, reducing segment bias.","To reduce the complexity of the texts and to conduct further analysis, text-mining preprocessing—an essential tool in text analysis (Denny & Spirling, 2018)—was employed to reduce the complexity of the data",English language filter (1),,,[],,text mining (5); machine learning (2); topic modeling (3); sentimental analysis (2); document classification (1); clustering (4),neural network language models (1); pre-trained language models (2); K-means methods (2),Word2Vec (1); Glo Ve (1); Fasttext (6); BERT (1),pre-trained (3),word embedding (6); vectors (5),,text (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,This article presents a new technique for identifying the personality categories of WHSs in three phases: (1) item generation; (2) item refinement; and (3) word embedding and clustering.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,cosine similarity (3); Sum of Squared Errors (SSE) (2); ANOVA (1),[],[],[],p < .005 (1); p > .05 (1),,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],confusion matrix (3),[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This study developed a three step approach to constructing WHS personality categories, aimed at overcoming the differences between the goals of academics, who want to create a general BP scale, and practitioners, who need to establish their own BP categories: generating items; refining items; and word embedding and clustering items.']",[],[],"['Limitations to this study included the amount of data available, which forced the authors to use the Fasttext pretrained language model.']",[],"['The sample was limited to reviews written in English, although visitors were from many countries, reducing segment bias.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],codes are available from the authors (3),,,,Knime Analytics Software 4.1.2 (1),,,,,,
_Hassan et al.-2024-Brand Personality Word Embedding.txt,Brand Personality Word Embedding,Mohamed Abdalla Elsayed Hassan; Konstantina Zerva; Silvia Aulet,University of Girona,2024,journal article,,,https://doi.org/10.1177/00472875221147142,https://doi.org/10.1177/00472875221147142,,,World Heritage Site (WHS) (7); UNESCO World Heritage Sites (WHSs) (2),cultural (6); natural (2); mixed (1),World Heritage Sites (WHSs) (3),world heritage (2); OUVs (1); outstanding universal values (1); 1972 Convention Concerning the Protection of the World Cultural and Natural Heritage (1); Authenticity (4); Integrity (5); Protection (6); Management (6); UNESCO (16); WHS (29),,"{'value': 'Social: Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study explores the intangible meanings of World Heritage Sites and their potential influence on destination preferences, suggesting a focus on cultural identity and sense of place attachment.'}; {'value': 'Economic: Entertainment', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the promotion of World Heritage Sites as tourism destinations and their role in attracting visitors, indicating a consideration of the entertainment value of these sites.'}; {'value': 'Political: Symbolic', 'reasoning': 'The text mentions that countries try to include as many sites as possible on the WHS list, since this is seen as increasing their international pride and recognition, indicating a symbolic value.'}; {'value': 'Historic: Historic-Artistic', 'reasoning': 'The study focuses on identifying personality categories of World Heritage Sites, which inherently involves considering their historical and artistic significance as rare or unique examples.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical: Artistic', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the attractiveness of World Heritage Sites and their authenticity, suggesting a consideration of their artistic value as original products of creativity and imagination.'}; {'value': 'Scientific: Workmanship', 'reasoning': 'The study mentions the importance of protecting and preserving World Heritage Sites, implying a consideration of the workmanship involved in their creation and maintenance.'}; {'value': 'Age: Existential', 'reasoning': 'The paper explores the intangible associations with World Heritage status, which can represent memory and the lives of past generations.'}",Protection (2); Management (2),,Asia (2); Pacific (2); Europe (3); China (2); India (1); Japan (1); France (1); Germany (1); Italy (1); Spain (1); Iran (1); Catalunya (1),,,"Despite having received considerable academic attention, existing brand personality (BP) scales are difficult to generalize and likely need further development.","The aim of this study was to design a novel method for constructing context driven- BP categories through the use of a multi-disciplinary approach and advances in personality studies in psychology and natural language processing. Based on a textual analysis that relies on devising hypotheses of psycholexical representation and the distributed representation of words, the aforementioned method was employed to explore World Heritage Site (WHS) personalities using information from all 1,121 UNESCO World Heritage Sites (WHSs) and 9,920 user-generated reviews on Trip Advisor.",Tripadvisor (8),text (10),,,,,,"9,920",,"9,920 individual reviews",,,,,"accessed June 28, 2021",seven countries,extracted,keyword search engine,"reviews specific to WHSs were therefore collected from seven countries, since the main objective was to collect as many reviews relevant to WH personality as possible.",text-mining preprocessing,English language filter,,,[],,text mining (4); machine learning (3); topic modeling (3); document classification (1); sentimental analysis (2); clustering (6); categorization (2),pre-trained language models (4); neural network language models (1),Word2Vec (1); Glo Ve (1); Fasttext (6); BERT (1),pre-trained (3),word embedding (7); vectors (5),,text (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,This article presents a new technique for identifying the personality categories of WHSs in three phases: (1) item generation; (2) item refinement; and (3) word embedding and clustering.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,cosine similarity (4); Sum of Squared Errors (SSE) (2); ANOVA (1),[],[],[],p < .005 (1); p > .05 (1),,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],confusion matrix (3),[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This study developed a three step approach to constructing WHS personality categories, aimed at overcoming the differences between the goals of academics, who want to create a general BP scale, and practitioners, who need to establish their own BP categories: generating items; refining items; and word embedding and clustering items.']",[],[],"['Limitations to this study included the amount of data available, which forced the authors to use the Fasttext pretrained language model.']",[],"['The sample was limited to reviews written in English, although visitors were from many countries, reducing segment bias.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],codes are available from the authors,,,,Knime Analytics Software 4.1.2,,,digital text data (2); digital visitor reviews (2); digital textual data (1); psycholexical hypothesis (2); text mining (5); natural language processing (NLP) (3); word embedding (5); brand personality (BP) (25); destination personality (DP) (10); outstanding universal values (OUVs) (2),,,
_Hausmann et al.-2020-Understanding sentiment of natio.txt,Understanding sentiment of national park visitors from social media data,Anna Hausmann; Tuuli Toivonen; Christian Fink; Valerie Härkönen; Ritwik Kulkarni; Henrikki Tenkanen; Enrico Di Minin,Helsinki Institute of Sustainability Science (HELSUS); University of Helsinki,2020,journal article,People Nat,,https://doi.org/10.1002/pan3.10130,https://besjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/pan3.10130,,750–760,,natural,national parks (18),natural heritage (1); cultural ecosystem services (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'The study highlights the societal role of national parks in providing opportunities for positive connections with nature, suggesting a discussion of emotional (collective) values related to cultural identity and sense of place attachment.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'The study mentions ecotourism and conservation marketing strategies, indicating a discussion of entertainment value related to the role of national parks in the contemporary tourism market.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'The study discusses the role of national parks in promoting socio-political support for conservation, suggesting a discussion of management value related to the incorporation of the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'The study mentions the appreciation of nature and landscapes, suggesting a discussion of artistic values related to the original product of creativity and imagination.'}",conservation (10),Kruger NP; Addo Elephant NP; Table Mountain NP; Garden Route NP,South Africa (6),colonial time (1),,"Therefore, it is crucial to assess how people perceive national parks in order to enhance socio-political support for conservation.","In this study, we assessed the sentiment and the discourse shared by visitors on social media to understand how they perceive national parks, and what they value during recreational experiences.",Instagram (7),text (4); images (2),geolocated (2),,,,,"33,213",,,,,,,between 2013 and 2016 (2),"Kruger, Addo Elephant, Table Mountain and Garden Route National Parks (NPs), in South Africa","We used Instagram's public Application Programming Interface (API) for accessing 1 week/month sample of posts, which were geolocated within the national parks borders, between June 2013 and February 2016.",Instagram's public Application Programming Interface (API),1 week/month sample of posts,"we used the packages tm (Feinerer & Hornik, 2018) and tidytext (Silge & Robinson, 2016) for cleaning the text by keeping only alpha-numeric characters and removing irrelevant features, such as links and user- names embedded in the text, all special characters and English stop words based on a pre-defined list of most common words in English, for example, pronouns.",identifying posts in English language,manually (2),,"['positive', 'negative', 'neutral']",,sentiment analysis (6); topic modelling (2); classification (3),,Latent Dirichlet Allocation (2),,fast Text framework (1),,text (3),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,F1-score (2); precision (2); recall (2),"['automatic sentiment classification was highly accurate with F score = 0.81 (precision = 0.88, recall = 0.75).']",[],[],ANOVA (α = 0.05) and Tukey post hoc tests; Chi-square (χ2) test,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],"['Overall, we found that the polarity of visitors’ sentiment on social media was positive, and was mostly expressing emotions such as joy, anticipation, trust and surprise, with only a small occurrence of posts with negative feelings.']",[],[],"['social media data may help inform ecotourism and conservation marketing strat- egies.', 'the methods applied in this study can be used by managers of national parks to understand the tourism profile of parks, according to physical and psychological dimensions symboliz- ical support for national parks and their long-term conservation ef- fectiveness']",[],[],[],[],"['the nature of the data involves limitations, including biases related to geograph- ical, population and visitation representativeness.', 'social media text often includes abbreviations, slang, emo- jis and hashtags combining multiple words. This short and unconven- tional language challenges the effectiveness of sentiment predictions']","['Only publicly available posts were accessed and users were de-identified.', ""In order to ensure full protection of users' privacy and compliance with General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR 2016/679), raw data used in the study cannot be made publicly available.""]",[],['General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR 2016/679)'],[],[],[],[],[],r packages and codes used for natural language processing (https://www.tidyt extmi ning.com/index.html) and sentiment analysis (https://cran.r-proje ct.org/web/packa ges/syuzh et/vigne ttes/syuzh et- vigne tte.html) are openly available online.,Summaries of data are available into a Dryad online repository linked to this manuscript (https://doi. org/10.5061/dryad.cjsxk sn3h).,,https://doi. org/10.5061/dryad.cjsxk sn3h,,,,culturomics; digital conservation; ecotourism; opinion mining,,,
_Havinga et al.-2021-Social media and deep learning c.txt,Social media and deep learning,,,,,,,,,,,,,,landscape aesthetics,,"{'Aesthetical Values': 'Artistic – The study uses Flickr images to predict scenicness, implying an appreciation for the artistic or visually appealing aspects of landscapes. The deep learning models analyze image attributes related to landscape aesthetics.'}; {'Social Values': ""Emotional (collective) – The research aims to capture people's interactions with the landscape and how it contributes to their well-being, suggesting a focus on the communal value and sense of place attachment associated with aesthetic landscapes.""}; {'Ecological Values': ""Spiritual – The study explores the harmony between the landscape and people's aesthetic enjoyment, indicating a consideration of the spiritual connection between humans and their natural environment.""}",,Achmelvich Bay; Snowdonia National Park; Lake District; Scottish Highlands; London; Glasgow; Greater London; Richmond Park; Heathrow Airport; Lake District national park; Peak and Lake Districts; Pembrokeshire coast; Cairngorms,Great Britain; Wales; England; Scotland; Britain; European,,winter of 2009/2010,landscape aesthetics are an important source of cultural value but large-scale measurement for ES assessments is difficult due to a lack of survey data.,"In this study, we have demonstrated that models using Flickr images and deep learning enable a highly accurate measure of aesthetic landscape quality, with independence of the scale of measurement.",Flickr,images; image metadata,geo-located images,,time-series analysis,,,9.8 million outdoor images,,,,,,,between 2004 and 2020,Great Britain,downloaded using Flickr’s API and accessed using the Python programming language,Flickr’s API,"A script was developed which iterated over a 1 × 1 km grid, requesting the metadata of the 4000 most recent, geo-located images per grid cell.",resized to 250 ×,outdoor images using its binary indoor/outdoor scene predictions,,,[],,scene recognition; predict scenicness,deep learning models; convolutional neural network; random forest model,Places365-Res Net-50; So N Res Net,available pre-trained on the Image Net database,,,Flickr images; 365 scene classes; 102 SUN image attribute scores; image scenicness score,,"70% training, 10% validation and 20% test areas",mean squared error loss function,stochastic gradient descent,,16,,,randomly flipped horizontally,{},,,,,,,We constructed this model using a modified Res Net-50 convolutional neural network,"The network was trained and tested using So N images according to the 70% training, 10% validation and 20% test areas. For training, this consisted of 152,470 images resized to 500 × 500 pixel dimensions. Images were also randomly flipped horizontally to increase the size of the training dataset. Batch size was set to 16. Model weights were optimised using stochastic gradient descent and a mean squared error loss function.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,r2; root mean squared error (RMSE); Kendall’s τ,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],"70% was randomly allocated for training, 10% for validation and 20% for testing",,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['EU GDPR on data protection and privacy were followed in the carrying out of the research.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Code and data associated with this study can be obtained at https:// doi. org/ 10. 5281/ zenodo. 55340 28.,The data used in this study are open-source and publicly available.,,https:// doi. org/ 10. 5281/ zenodo. 55340 28,Python 3.8.3,Py Torch 1.6.0; R 3.6.3,,ecosystem services; aesthetic quality; scenicness; landscape quality; cultural value; natural areas; human well-being; environmental indicators; remote sensing data; national parks; MODIS satellite data; Digital Elevation Model (DEM); visual preference concepts; ecosystem type map; historical points of interest (POI); Open Street Map; spatial autocorrelation; stochastic gradient descent; mean squared error loss function,,,
_Havinga et al.-2023-Social media and deep learning r.txt,Social media and deep learning reveal specific cultural preferences for biodiversity,Ilan Havinga; Diego Marcos; Patrick Bogaart; Dario Massimino; Lars Hein; Devis Tuia,,2023,journal article,People and Nature,,https://doi.org/10.1002/pan3.10466,https://besjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/pan3.10466,,981–998,,,,cultural ecosystem services (CES) (13); cultural value (2); conservation (5),,"Social – Cultural identity, motivation and pride, sense of “place attachment,” and communal value. The study discusses how social media can contribute to a stronger sense of collective identity by sharing interactions with biodiversity, fostering a sense of community and shared appreciation for nature.; Ecological – Harmony between the building and its natural/artificial environment. The study explores the relationship between human interactions with species and ecological measures of biodiversity, suggesting an implicit recognition of the harmony between human activities and the natural environment.",,Great Britain (7); Peak District (3); Greater London area (3); Snowdonia National Park; North Pennines; Scottish Highlands; Yorkshire Dales; Lake Districts; Cairngorms national park,Europe (2); England (2); Wales; Scotland; Channel Islands; Isle of Man,,,"Social media has created new opportunities to map cultural ecosystem services (CES) related to biodiversity at large scales. However, using these novel data to understand people's preferences in relation to these CES remains a challenge.",The objective of our study is to assess the potential of deep learning and social media to measure people's appreciation of biodiversity as a cultural service.,Flickr (20); i Naturalist (15),images (20); photos (2); metadata records (2); image urls (2),geo-­located images,spatial distribution; spatial metrics,monthly interactions; migratory periods,,metadata records; taxonomic information,9.8 million geo-­located images,"44,523",,"941,812",,,9.8 million,,Great Britain,downloaded,Flickr Application Programming Interface (API) (2),"randomly generated sample grid, representing a 10% random sample of a 25 km resolution grid over the whole of Europe",filtered to outdoor images using the Places365 deep learning model,,manual,authors themselves,[],,classification (3); detection,deep learning (10); artificial neural networks,Res Net-­18; Inception V3; Places365,pretrained on Image Net (2),,,images,,70%/10%/20%,cross-­entropy learning loss (2),Adam,1e05,,10,,,{'β dilution coefficient': ''},,,,,,,Our model consisted of a Res Net-­18 with an additional five layers replacing the two final layers,The first step was to train the model to distinguish between images depicting human–­species interactions and generic interactions. All i Naturalist images were labelled as human–­species interactions and all Flickr images were labelled as generic interactions.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (10); entropy (9),"['97.8%', '97.6%', '95.6%', '90%']",[],[],,"Overall, we observed a good ability by the β = 0.01 model to identify species images, even at low confidence levels, with a very small amount of generic-­type images present among the models' predictions and the most and least confident predictions staying consistent.",[],[],the inherent noise in the Flickr data could be better accounted for with the implementation of the β dilution coefficient,[],[],[],block holdout approach,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['implementation of the β dilution coefficient'],[],[],"['Accurate information on the demographics of social media users is very difficult to obtain, especially at large scales']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],The code and data associated with this study can be obtained at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7675393,can be obtained at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7675393,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7675393,,Py Torch,,biodiversity (10); machine learning (2); AI,,,
_Havinga et al.-2024-Understanding the sentiment asso.txt,Understanding the sentiment associated with cultural ecosystem services using images and text from social media,Ilan Havinga; Diego Marcos; Patrick Bogaart; Devis Tuia; Lars Hein,"Environmental Systems Analysis Group, Wageningen University, PO Box 47, 6700 AA Wageningen, the Netherlands; Inria, University of Montpellier, Montpellier 34090, France; National Accounts Department, Statistics Netherlands, Henri Faasdreef 312, 2492 JP The Hague, the Netherlands; Environmental Computational Science and Earth Observation Laboratory, Ecole Polytechnique F´ed´erale de Lausanne, Industrie 17, Sion, Switzerland",2023,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier B V,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2023.101581,www.elsevier.com/locate/ecoser,,,,,,,,,land use planning (1); land use management (2); recreational land use management (2),,Great Britain (3); England (7); USA (1); Scotland (1); Wales (1),,Covid-19 pandemic,"To address this gap, we apply several Computer Vision (CV) and natural language processing (NLP) models to link CES estimates for Great Britain based on the content of images to sentiment measures using the accompanying text, and compare our results to a national, georeferenced survey of recreational well-being in England.","Therefore, the objective of our study is to examine the positive experiences associated with CES supply using images and text from social media, along with the required NLP and CV methods, and validate these measures through a comparison with comprehensive survey data.",Flickr (17); Twitter (6); Reddit (2); Instagram (1); Whats App (1),images (21); text (22); image descriptions (3); metadata (2); photos (2),,,time series (1),,titles (3); tags (3); descriptions (6),9.8 million outdoor images,,,,,,,January 2018 to December 2021,Great Britain,accessing the Flickr Application Programming Interface (API) using the ‘flickrapi’ library in Python,Flickr Application Programming Interface (API),,"removed all html, numeric characters, punctuation, English stopwords and words less than three characters in length using the ‘tm’ package in R. Second, we removed local toponyms by querying local place names using a 5 km grid overlay and the OS Place Names API",only used images with at least three words to limit the bias of single or small groups of words on overall sentiment,crowdsourced (3),Amazon Mechanical Turk platform (1),"['negative', 'neutral', 'positive']",,sentiment analysis (8); species identification (1); image classification (1),Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) (3); machine learning (5); deep learning (10),Ro BERTa (10); Sentiment140 (5); Places365 (5),"trained on 124 million tweets from January 2018 to December 2021, and finetuned for sentiment analysis using a crowdsourced, annotated training dataset of tweets",,bag-of-words (2); term frequency-inverse document frequency (tf-idf) (3),image content (2); text (1); image descriptions (2); adjectives (5),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,linear regression analysis (3); Pearson’s R (4); Welch Two Sample t-test (2); Wilcoxon rank sum test (2),[],[],[],p <.001 (11); p <.0001 (10); p =.001 (2),,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],"['Overall, there was a clear relationship between aesthetic quality and sentiment, with a clear uptrend towards higher aesthetic ratings.']",[],[],"['public health (3)', 'recreational land use management (2)']",[],[],[],[],['Not all positive experiences related to nature may be expressed on social media while the full range of positive experiences that are expressed in users’ text may not be fully captured through the application of NLP models.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Scripts used in this study are available on request.,The data used in this study are open-source and publicly available.,,http://sce nicornot.datasciencelab.co.uk,,Python (3); R (3),,,,,
_Hegde et al.-2022-DA-AE Disparity-Alleviation Aut.txt,DA-AE: Disparity-Alleviation Auto-Encoder Towards,,,2022,conference paper,2022 IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Workshops (CVPRW),IEEE,10.1109/CVPRW56347.2022.00557,,,,Cultural heritage (4),,heritage images (2); heritage sites (3); Indian Heritage sites,preservation (1); conservations (1); Digital conservation (1); Cultural heritage (4); Cultural heritage preservation (1); conservation (2); restoration (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The paper discusses the importance of preserving cultural heritage for future generations, which relates to cultural identity, motivation, and pride.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The paper mentions that conservation entails more than simply maintaining a facade or immobilising a monument in time, suggesting the potential of heritage assets to provide knowledge about the past in the future.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The paper mentions the ambiance and character of the past, which relates to the original product of creativity and imagination.'}",preservation (1); conservations (1); Digital conservation (1); conservation (2); restoration (1),,globe; Indian,,,Recent survey on preservation of her- itage shows demand for the digitization and conservations of heritage sites owing to their susceptibility to natural dis- asters and human acts.,"In this paper, we propose to learn representations of cul- tural heritage data towards categorization and 3D recon- struction towards presentation in digital space.",,photographs; video; audio; text,,,,,,70000,,,2944,,,,,U.S.,Crowdsourced data collection,,,,,,,[],,categorization; clustering,Auto-encoder,DA-AE; AE; VAE,,,,,,,MSE; SDSIM,adam,0.0001,512,,,,{'α': '0.75'},KMeans; GMM; LSNMF; AC; SSC- OMP; En SC; SC; AECL; Deep Clus- ter; DCN; DEC; IDEC; SR-KMeans; Va DE; JULE; DEPICT; Dyn AE,,,,,,The architecture of DA-AE consists of an DA encoder and decoder as shown in Figure 2.,,no,[],