_Huang et al.-2025-Exploring the recreational micro.txt,Exploring the recreational micromobility in relation to historic urban areas and social media: Insights from machine learning approaches,Hao Huang; Haochen Shi; Xinjian Li; Yankai Wang,"School of Architecture, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China; College of Architecture and Urban Planning, Guangzhou University, Guangzhou, Guangdong 510006, China; State Key Laboratory of Subtropical Building and Urban Science, School of Architecture, South China University of Technology, Guangzhou, Guangdong 510641, China",,journal article,Landscape and Urban Planning,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.landurbplan.2025.105447,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.landurbplan.2025.105447,,,Cultural Heritage (1); historic urban areas (9),tangible (3),historic urban areas (9); heritage sites (4); historical streets (3),tangible heritage (1); heritage sites (4); historical streets (3); cultural elements (1); heritage assets (1); cultural value (1),,"{'SOCIAL': {'Emotional (collective)': 'The study mentions that tangible heritage can increase community diversity and shape positive public opinion, which can be reflected in social media and encourage recreational micromobility in historic urban areas. This relates to cultural identity and communal value.'}}; {'HISTORIC': {'Historic-Artistic': 'The study highlights the unique architectural and cultural fabric of historic urban areas, suggesting that these areas are rare or unique examples of historical or artistic movements.'}}; {'AESTHETICAL': {'Artistic': 'The study mentions that heritage sites can provide aesthetic values, indicating that these sites are original products of creativity and imagination.'}}",heritage conservation (1); preservation (1),Guangzhou (China) (3); Historic Urban Area of Guangzhou (2),China (3); Guangzhou (6),,,How recreational micromobility is related to historic urban areas and social media remains less known.,"(1) How do historic urban areas and social media influence recreational micromobility? (2) Which machine learning approach is best suited to explore nonlinear relationships between recreational micromobility, urban historic area, and social media?",Social Media (13); Dianping App (1); Weibo App (1),GPS trajectories (2); texts (1); photographs (1); travel tips (1); reviews (2); recommendations (1),,spatial features (1); spatial heterogeneity (1),,likes (1); shares (1); favorites (1); comments (2),,,,,,,,,2022 (A whole year) (1),Historic Urban Area of Guangzhou (2),,2bulu App (1),,"Several key steps were taken in the pre-processing of this GPS data. First, micromobility trajectories were filtered by speed (below 45 km/h). The following phases involved the removal of abnormal trips and the application of noise-smoothing techniques (Shi et al., 2024).",micromobility trajectories were filtered by speed (below 45 km/h) (1),,,[],,nonlinear relationship modeling (2),machine learning (ML) (13); ensemble learning method (2); deep learning neural networks (1),Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGBM) (3); Random Forest (RF) (2); Gradient Boosting Decision Tree (GBDT) (2); Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGB) (2); Support Vector Machines (SVM) (2); Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) (2); Keras Regressor (KR) (1); SHAP (Shapley Additive ex Planations) (2),,,,,,8:2 (1),,,,,,,,{},linear regression model (3),,,,,,,"For these seven ML models, we divide the dataset into a training set and a test set in an 8:2 ratio. During training, we use Bayesian Optimization (BO) based on the Tree Parzen Estimator for hyper-parameter tuning to derive the optimal parameters for each ML model.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) (2); Mean Absolute Error (MAE) (2); Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) (2); Adjusted R-squared (2),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],8:2 (1),linear regression model (3),[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Developing an innovative HS-5D framework that can be used to guide exploration of the relationships between recreational micromobility, urban historic areas and social media (1)', 'Explaining and visualizing complex nonlinear relationships between historic urban areas, social media and recreational micromobility through advanced interpretation techniques including SHAP values and dependence plots (1)']",['Identifying that the Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGBM) model optimized with Bayesian optimization algorithm has the highest explanatory power and robustness (1)'],[],"['our HS-5D framework requires extended calibration and validation to reflect better the complex and detailed settings of historic urban landscapes (1)', 'we could not incorporate sociodemographic status (e.g., age, gender, and income), which limits our exploration of individual travel behavior differences due to the unavailability of private personal data (1)', 'our HS-5D framework focuses on objective features (e.g., street layouts) and is weak in collecting subjective perceptions of individuals regarding the detailed features of the historic urban landscape that may shape recreational micromobility (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Data will be made available on request (1),Data will be made available on request (1),,,,,,recreational micromobility (26); HS-5D framework (12); 5D framework (12); Bayesian optimization algorithm (2); GPS trajectories (2); Tree Parzen Estimator (TPE) (5),,,
_Huang et al.-2025-Unveiling the potential supply o.txt,Unveiling the potential supply of cultural ecosystem services on the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau: Insights from tourist hiking trajectories☆,Sheng Huang; Jiawei Yi; Yunyan Du; Fuyuan Liang; Rui Xu; Nan Wang; Jiale Qian; Wenna Tu; Peixian Luo; Andrew Z.F. Xing,"State Key Laboratory of Resources and Environmental Information System, Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100101, China; College of Resources and Environment, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China; Key Laboratory of Poyang Lake Wetland and Watershed Research Ministry of Education, School of Geography and Environment, Jiangxi Normal University, Nanchang 330022, China; School of Public Policy and Management, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China; Beijing Academy, Beijing 10018, China",2025,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2025.101711,www.elsevier.com/locate/ecoser,,,Cultural Heritage (2),,plateau landscapes (1); Tibetan cultural heritage (1),cultural ecosystem services (CES) (12); sustainable tourism (2); sustainable landscape (1); ecosystem management (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'The study mentions opportunities for recreation, spiritual enrichment, personal growth, and social connections, indicating a discussion of social values related to cultural ecosystem services.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'The study discusses tourism as a critical driver of economic development and poverty alleviation, accounting for significant portions of GDP in Qinghai and Tibet provinces, indicating a discussion of economic values.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'The study highlights the dramatic landscapes of the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, such as the Himalayas, Mount Kailash, and Qinghai Lake, suggesting a discussion of aesthetic values.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': 'The study mentions the fragile ecosystems of the QTP and the need to balance economic growth with conservation, indicating a discussion of ecological values.'}",sustainable tourism (1); resource management (2); conservation (1),Qinghai-Tibet Plateau (QTP) (11); Himalayas (1); Mount Kailash (2); Qinghai Lake (3); Mount Namcha Barwa (2); Mount Everest (2); Jiuzhaigou Scenic Area (1); Jade Dragon Snow Mountain Scenic Area (1); Lake Azar (1); Zari Mountain (1); Amne Machin Mountain (1),Qinghai (4); Tibet (5); Sichuan (3); Yunnan (1); Gansu (1); Xinjiang (2); China (4); Southeast Asia (1); Brazil (1); Chile (1); Germany (1); Europe (1),,,"Assessing the supply of cultural ecosystem services (CES) in this vast, data-scarce region holds paramount significance for local sustainable tourism development. However, limited research has addressed this issue.","This study aims to address these challenges by proposing a datadriven framework to evaluate CES supply on the QTP, combining geo tagged social media data with machine learning techniques. By inte grating outdoor hiking trajectories and multi-source GIS data, we assess both realized and potential CES supply across the plateau.",Flickr (2); Twitter (1); 2bulu (3); Weibo (1); Douyin (Tik Tok) (1); Instagram (1),GPS trajectories (3); geotagged photos (4); spatial maps (1); GIS data (1),GPS coordinates (2); geotagged (4),spatial distribution (2); spatial clusters (1); spatial autocorrelation (1),years 2017 to 2020 (2),,,,"7,259 (1)",,"800,000 (1)",,,,2017–2020 (1),Qinghai-Tibet Plateau (QTP) (1),Users of the 2bulu mobile app can record their GPS trajectories during outdoor trips and voluntarily share the data.,,random sampling (1),implemented several validation measures to assess the dataset’s repre sentativeness as some users may visit the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau for reasons unrelated to CES.,proportion of non-tourist trajectories and geotagged photos,manual validation (1),,[],,classification (2); regression (1),Random Forest (RF) (5); machine learning (1),,,SHapley Additive ex Planations (SHAP) (2),,biophysical factors (3); socioeconomic variables (2); infrastructure (2); tourism attractions (2),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"We developed a three-step data-driven framework to assess and predict the supply of CES on the QTP (Fig. 2). The framework involves: (1) quantifying the RSCES, (2) modeling the relationship between the realized CES supply and the selected explanatory variables using the Random Forest (RF) algorithm (Breiman, 2001); and (3) mapping po tential areas with high CES supply based on RF predictions.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,model fit accuracy (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],99 random spatially stratified sampling experiments (1),,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['tourism planning (4)', 'ecotourism strategies (2)']",['resource management (1)'],"['novel methodological approaches (1)', 'data-driven evaluation framework (1)']",['empirical evidence (1)'],[],"['selection bias in the social media data (1)', 'underestimation of CES supply (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['A major limitation is the selection bias in the social media data (Zheng et al., 2024), which pre\ndominantly represents a younger, more adventurous demographic, particularly outdoor enthusiasts.', 'Furthermore, our reliance on photographic behavior as a proxy for\nCES does not fully capture the diversity of cultural and recreational services.']",Data will be made available on request.,Data will be made available on request.,,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2025.101711,,,,outdoor recreation (1); hiking trajectories (3); plateau ecosystems (1); Volunteered Geographic Information (VGI) (2); Public Participatory Geographic Information Systems (PPGIS) (2); ecotourism (3),,,
_Huo et al.-2024-Measuring the relationship betwe.txt,Measuring the relationship between museum attributes and visitors: An application of topic model on museum online reviews,Huo H; Shen K; Han C; Yang M,,2024,journal article,PLoS ONE,PLoS,https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0304901,https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0304901,,,Cultural Heritage (1),tangible (2); intangible (2),museums (21); museum online reviews (4),cultural values (1); museum cultural heritage (2),,,,British Museum; National Gallery; Natural History Museum; Victoria and Albert Museum,UK (4); London (2); Italy (1); Mexico (1); Poland (1); Samoa (1); Spain (2); Turkey (1); Britain (1),,,"To address these research gaps, this study employs structural topic model to extract com- prehensive and scientific museum attributes from online reviews.",How does the structural topic model approach aid in identifying museum attributes? How do these attributes impact the experience and satisfaction of museum visitors?,Trip Advisor (7),text (1); online reviews (11); visitor ratings (2),,,review times (2),,review time (1); visitor ratings (1),"118,966",,"102,728",,,,,"January 1, 2010, and April 1, 2023",four museums in the UK,crawler program,,collected only English-language reviews,"word tokenization, text normalization (converting uppercase letters to lowercase), elimination of numbers, white- space, and punctuation, and removal of stop words using lexical property (POS) tagging and the Python Natural Language Processing Toolkit (NLTK)",English-language reviews,,,[],,topic modeling (4),structural topic model (STM) (8); generalized linear model (1),,,,lexical property (POS) tagging,reviews text (1); review time (2); review rating (2),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"First, each topic of the document is extracted from a generalized linear model based on covariates and a set of logical normals. Then, given a topic prevalence vector associated with a particular topic Zd,n, the process is as follows, which n is the index of each word in the document d. Next, assign the words of each document to the topic.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['this study is the first to use the structural topic model technique to analyse Trip Advisor online reviews in the museum context.', 'this study combines STM techniques with structural equation modeling (SEM) to address the gap in research methodology in the field of empirical museum research.', 'this paper not only enriches the research on museum attributes, but also introduces new service attribute indicators that can reflect the unique value of museums on the basis of existing research literature, which sets a new paradigm for future empirical research in related fields, and strongly promotes the innovation of museum management and marketing strategies in the digital era.']",[],[],"['the sample reviews collected in this study were limited to four museums in the UK and were exclusively in English.', 'due to the limited literature on museum attribute research, it can be challenging to identify all museum attribute dimensions.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,This paper used the Tripadvisor 4-core dataset which is publicly available on https://www.tripadvisor.cn/.,,https://www.tripadvisor.cn/,R programming language,Python Natural Language Processing Toolkit (NLTK); STM package in the R programming language; Amos 22.0,,museum tourism; museum attributes; museum service quality; museum online reviews; visitor experience; visitor satisfaction; structural topic model; online reviews; topic model; factor analysis; structural equation modeling; Trip Advisor; electronic word of mouth; core offerings; peripheral services; ambiance; museumscape,,,
