_Jingu et al.-2024-Social Media as a Lens for Citiz.txt,Social Media as a Lens for Citizen Science: Investigating Visitor Contributions in a Forest Recreational Area,"Jingu, S.; Ogawa, Y.; Yamaki, K.; Miyamoto, A.; Takayama, N.",,2024,journal article,Sustainability,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/su16135804,https://www.mdpi.com/article/10.3390/su16135804/s1,,,,,forest recreational area (3); forest recreational use (3); traditional agricultural landscapes (2); rural landscapes (2); rural forest landscape; urban environment,cultural ecosystem services (6); biodiversity (14); Nature Positive (7); ecosystem services (1); interpretation (3); conservation (6); management (3); environmental education (3),,"{'SOCIAL': 'Emotional (collective) – The study highlights the importance of social media in sharing experiences and perceptions of nature, fostering a sense of community and shared value for biodiversity.'}; {'ECONOMIC': 'Entertainment – The study acknowledges the role of social media in promoting recreational use of natural areas, which can contribute to tourism and economic benefits for local communities.'}; {'POLITICAL': 'Educational – The study emphasizes the potential of citizen science and social media to educate the public about biodiversity and promote conservation efforts, aligning with political strategies for environmental protection.'}; {'HISTORIC': 'Educational – The study mentions the use of historical data and management reports to understand the evolution of the Ushiku Nature Sanctuary, highlighting the educational value of historical context in heritage management.'}; {'AESTHETICAL': 'Artistic – The study analyzes photographs posted on social media to assess the aesthetic value of landscapes and natural elements, reflecting artistic appreciation and visual preferences.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL': 'Spiritual – The study suggests that repeat visitors to the Ushiku Nature Sanctuary may develop a deeper connection with nature, observing seasonal changes and appreciating the ecological harmony of the environment.'}",forest management; conservation (2); forest recreation; nature conservation (3); conservation policies,Ushiku Nature Sanctuary; Ushiku City; Ibaraki Prefecture; Japan,Japan (4); Tokyo (2); Kanto region (2),2010s (4),site was closed for an extended period due to COVID-19,"Despite the current availability of large amounts of spatially dense data, a critical limitation is the insufficient understanding of how users’ posts on natural recreational experiences on popular social media platforms and the information they can provide facilitate the aims of citizen science.","Therefore, this study explored the potential of widely used social media platforms for citizen science initiatives. We reveal that analyzing user contribution behavior on social media can help identify a distinct pool of citizen science contributors.",social media (25); Google Maps (13); Twitter (now X) (3); Flickr (3); Instagram; e Bird (2); i Naturalist (2); Google Places; Google Local Guide Help Center,photos (30); images (10); text (1); locational data (2); media (1); URL lists,locational data (2),,seasonal trends,reviews; photographs; ratings,account names,1116 photos,143 contributors,,,,,,October 2009 to 2022,Ushiku Nature Sanctuary,manual web scraping,Google Places Application Programming Interface (API),,"Posts were publicly available on the Internet at the discretion of the contributors. However, whether contributors fully understood the extent to which their data could be used and whether they would have granted permission for its use in further applications is unclear.","Invalid data were removed from the analysis, such as posts unrelated to the site, posts by location owners on advertisements, and posts with insufficient information.",multiple experts evaluated and categorized valid subjects,first author and a research collaborator,"['man-made objects', 'landscapes', 'biological elements (bio)']",,,,AI-based species identification algorithms,,,,,,,