"_Jones et al.-2019-The enthusiast, the interested,.txt","The Enthusiast, the Interested, the Sceptic, and the Cynic:",C. E. (Kate) Jones,,2019,journal article,"ACM Journal on Computing and Cultural Heritage, Vol. 12, No. 1, Article 6",ACM,,www.crosscult.eu,,19,cultural heritage (14),,cultural heritage landmarks (1); places of social and cultural interest (1); historical digital resources (1); city (10); history (6); historical topics (2); historical sites (1); historical novels (1),cultural heritage (14); historical (10); archives (1); multimedia (1); public history (1); linear route (1); 3D data (1); reconstruction of historical sites (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'The study discusses the importance of shared public consciousness and collective memory, indicating a focus on emotional (collective) values related to cultural identity and communal value. The game aims to contribute to a shared collective memory and serve as a source for public history on the topic.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'The study emphasizes the use of historical images and stories to trigger reinterpretation of the city and its history, suggesting a focus on historic-educational values. The game aims to stimulate reflection on historical topics and trigger reinterpretation of the city and its history.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': ""The study mentions the game's role as a mediator between the city and society, inviting citizens to interact with and reflect on public space, which implies a discussion of management values. The game invites citizens to interact with and reflect not only on the parts of the public space that are visible but also on those that are hidden or absent from public consciousness.""}",,"Luxembourg City (2); Maltese capital Valletta (1); Belval (4); Belval, in southern Luxembourg (1)",European (1); Luxembourg (5),,,The challenge then becomes how to evaluate the game app. Is it indeed useful? Will it offer perceived value to the citizens who engage with it?,"First, we aim to understand the users’ perceptions and initial impressions of their experience, which helps unravel the perceived value and motivations that players have towards the adoption of cultural heritage mobile apps. Second, we aim to understand the different emotional viewpoints of the participants and explore this using sentiment analysis.",Google Play Store (1); Apple App Store (1),location information (1); historical images (1); stories (11); multimedia objects (1); user-generated reflections (1); log files (1),GPS (5); geolocated (10); location-based (13); geofence (5); geocaching (4); geolocated services (1); geotagged (1),,,rating (3); tagging (3); comments (3),,,30 valid questionnaires,,,,,,2-month period between late September and mid-November 2017,Luxembourg,field trials with controlled experiments,,,cleaning the data,,qualitatively using a thematic approach and quantitatively using sentiment analysis scores,,[],,sentiment analysis (4),natural-language processing (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,mobile devices (2); smartphones (3); mobile technology (1); location-based services (1); map (10); digital (4); location-aware (1); location-based cultural heritage game (2); mapping interface (1); digital cultural resources (1); physical places (2); digital realm (1); virtual space (2); GPS (5); Wi Fi (2); Android (3); i Phones (1); Mapbox (1); Crowd Flower (1); Likert scale (3); UX (10); POI (10); API (1),,,
