_Kang et al.-2021-Transfer Learning of a Deep Lear.txt,Transfer Learning of a Deep Learning Model for Exploring Tourists’ Urban Image Using Geotagged Photos,Youngok Kang; Nahye Cho; Jiyoung Yoon; Soyeon Park; Jiyeon Kim,,2021,journal article,ISPRS Int. J. Geo-Inf,MDPI,https://doi. org/10.3390/ijgi10030137,https://doi. org/10.3390/ijgi10030137,,,,,Korean palaces (2); cultural elements,,,,,Seoul; Korea,Korea; Seoul; London; Peru; Beijing; Hong Kong,,,"previous studies have limitations in properly reﬂecting unique landscape, cultural characteristics, and traditional elements of the region that are prominent in tourism",(1) develop a tourism photo classiﬁcation by analyzing the characteristics of photos; (2) propose detailed procedures of training a deep learning model to enhance the model accuracy; and (3) analyze the urban images of tourists visiting Korea by applying the ﬁnal model to the entire dataset.,SNS; Flickr,photos; location; time information,,dense areas,,,,"168,216",5609,,,,,,six years from 1 January 2013 to 31 December 2018,Korea,crawled,public application programming interface (API) provided by Flickr,,"removing the posts deleted by the user or the data with latitude, longitude, and temporal errors",excluding residents,"manually labeling, semi-supervised labeling method",human eyes,"['75 scenes', '13 categories']",,image identiﬁcation; classiﬁcation,convolutional neural network (CNN); deep neural networks,Alex Net; Goog Le Net; Res Net; VGGNet; Dense Net; Mobile Net; Inception-v3,transfer learning of Inception-v3 model,,,photos,,"60% training data, 20% validation data, and 20% test data",,Adam,0.0001,128,,,panning; zooming; rotating; brightness adjustment; horizontal ﬂip; vertical ﬂip; shearing,{},,p3.16xlarge speciﬁcation provided by Amazon Web Service,NVIDIA Telesa V100,8,RAM 488GB,,the original network architecture of Inception-v3 was maintained,"All photos were divided into 60% training data, 20% validation data, and 20% test data to train and evaluate the model. First, we selected representative photos for each of the 75 scenes to build a training dataset. We built the ﬁrst training dataset by extracting 50 photos in each scene after manually labeling 20% of the training dataset. After training the model based on 50 photos per scene, it was applied to other training datasets to check the accuracy of the model. From this, the number of photos in the training dataset was gradually increased to improve the accuracy of the model.",,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; recall; precision; F1-score,['The ﬁnal model shows high accuracy of 85.77% for the Top 1 and 95.69% for the Top 5.'],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['This study has novelty in that it developed a tourist photo classiﬁcation suitable for local characteristics and showed the process of re-training a deep learning model to effectively classify tourism photos.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],source code of ﬁnal model constructed in this study are publicly available on the website: https://github.com/ewha-gis/Korea-Tourists-Urban-Image,training dataset for 75 scenes publicly available on the website: https://github.com/ewha-gis/Korea-Tourists-Urban-Image,,,"Python 3.6.5, anaconda 3.5.2 and Tensorﬂow 1.13.0",,,,,,
_Kang et al.-2023-Assessment of Society’s Percepti.txt,"Assessment of Society’s Perceptions on Cultural Ecosystem Services in a Cultural Landscape in Nanchang, China",N. Kang; G. Xie; C. Liu,,2023,journal article,Sustainability,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/su151310308,https://doi.org/10.3390/su151310308,,,Cultural Heritage (1),tangible (1); cultural (1),cultural landscape (5); ancient villages (10); ancient village landscapes (2); historic structures (2); traditional structures (1); ancient buildings (6); historical sites (1); cultural sites (1); heritage buildings (1); rural landscape (1),Cultural Significance (1); cultural landscape (5); cultural ecosystem services (CES) (10); heritage (3); traditional (4); historic (4); conservation (3); protection (5); planning (3); intangible cultural heritage (4); ancient (23),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study highlights the importance of cultural identity and the sense of place attachment that people feel towards the cultural landscape of ancient villages, contributing to communal value.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study emphasizes the potential of the ancient villages to provide knowledge about the past, particularly through their historical structures and cultural resources.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study focuses on the aesthetic qualities of the cultural landscape, including the visual harmony, unique shapes, and exquisite workmanship of the ancient villages.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': 'Spiritual – The study mentions the harmony between the buildings and the natural environment.'}",heritage (3); conservation (3); protection (5); planning (3),Anyi ancient villages (7); Nanchang (3); Suzhou Humble Administrator’s Garden (2),China (10); Tibetan Plateau (1); Finnish Forest Lapland (1); Mediterranean (1); Jiangxi Province (1); Sweden (1),Tang Dynasty (2),,"Assessment of society’s perceptions on cultural heritage landscapes can improve the integration of cultural heritage values into decision-making processes that affect landscapes, thereby contributing to maximizing the beneﬁts people receive from cultural ecosystem services.","(1) develop an assessment indictors framework for cultural landscapes from the perspectives of experts and the public through two different methods; (2) reveal the potential link between multisensory perception, behavior experience, and landscape preference in cultural landscapes; and (3) investigate the perception processes of different sensory experiences and their contributions to landscape assessment.",Qunar (1); Ctrip (1); Fliggy (1); Tuniu (1); Travel Go (1); Ma-feng-wo (1); Sina microblog (1); Twitter (1),text (2); photos (1),,,,,,"412 travel notes and microblogs, 14,833 comments, and 1627 photos",,"412 travel notes and microblogs, 14,833 comments",1627,,,,from 2017 to 2022,Anyi ancient villages,Data mining,Python v3.10,,The recorded UGC text information was then converted into an ANSI-coded TXT ﬁle,,grounded theory (three-stage coding),,[],"the average reliability is 0.9135, with all themes more than 0.8",semantic network analysis (2),,ROSTCM v6.0 (2),,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,"['social semantic network (1)', 'co-occurrence network (2)']",words,co-occurrence,,,,Word Segmentation and Net Draw technologies in ROSTCM v6.0 software were applied to visually construct the co-occurrence network structure of highfrequency terms,,,to further investigate the association between keywords and subjective experiences,Holsti’s reliability procedure (2),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],Sankey diagram (2),[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['A hierarchical framework of multisensory indicators is proposed for the evaluation of China’s cultural heritage within an ecosystem services context.'],[],[],"['the extraction of evaluation indexes did not consider the inﬂuence of climate, weather, and surrounding environment of ancient villages on a macro scale.', 'it is unable to collect users’ social information such as gender, age, occupation, and education due to privacy protection', 'insufﬁcient survey samples in the public statistics for elders, kids, and persons with impairments.', 'a failure to consider negative perceptions']",['privacy protection (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['the extraction of evaluation indexes did not consider the inﬂuence of climate, weather, and surrounding environment of ancient villages on a macro scale.', 'it is unable to collect users’ social information such as gender, age, occupation, and education due to privacy protection', 'insufﬁcient survey samples in the public statistics for elders, kids, and persons with impairments.', 'a failure to consider negative perceptions']",,Not applicable,,,,,,landscape experience (4); landscape assessment (5); cultural landscape (5); ecosystem services (7); multisensory perception (4); behavior experience (3); landscape preference (5); sensory experience (10); visual stimulation (2); touch perception (3); hearing (5); smell (10); taste (7); historicity (2); culture (10); localism (1); ancient villages (10); ancient village landscapes (2); historic structures (2); traditional structures (1); ancient buildings (6); historical sites (1); cultural sites (1); heritage buildings (1); rural landscape (1),,,
_Kang et al.-2023-Revitalizing Island Tourism in t.txt,Revitalizing Island Tourism in the Digital,Hyun-Chul Kang; Woo-Yeul Baek; Jae-Yong Choi; Jong-Sung Kim,"College of Creative Engineering, Kyonggi University, Republic of Korea; Department of Sport Science, Kyonggi University, Republic of Korea; Department of Research Planning, ␀e Hwaseong Institute, Republic of Korea; College of General Education for Truth, Sincerity, and Love, Kyonggi University, Republic of Korea",2023,journal article,Journal of Marine and Island Cultures,"Institution for Marine and Island Cultures, Mokpo National University",10.21463/jmic.2023.12.2.05,https://jmic.online/issues/v12n2/5/,,69-81,,,island regions (2); Jebudo Island (12); aging tourist destination (2),placeness (5); local economy (1); regional development (1); local characteristics (2); tourism resources (6); digital transformation (8); local resources (2); tourism hub (1); island tourism (4); tourism programs (2); cultural contents (1); tourist destination (4); tourism campaigns (1); Hwaseong City Tourism Comprehensive Plan (1); marine scenery (1); geological heritages (1); topographical heritages (1); ecological heritages (1); geological attractions (1); art park (2); tourism activities (3); regional discrimination (1); content development (3); scenic views (1); hardware-based experiences (1); experience-based tourism activities (1); regional extinction (2); sense of place (2); eco-friendly leisure activities (1); beech combing (1); full-time operating institutions (1); rural area decline (1); island tourism development projects (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study emphasizes the importance of cultural contents that appeal to young people and strengthening the existing programs to attract visitors, which can enhance cultural identity and pride.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment – The study focuses on revitalizing island tourism and enhancing the tourist appeal of Jebudo Island, which can boost the entertainment value and attract more tourists.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Management – The study discusses the need for comprehensive and innovative strategies to strengthen the tourist appeal of Jebudo Island and transform it into a modern and sophisticated tourist destination, indicating the incorporation of the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study mentions the potential of Jebudo Island as a geoscience education site due to its various geologic, topographical, and ecological heritages, highlighting its potential to provide knowledge about the past in the future.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': ""Artistic – The study mentions the 'art' theme promoted by Hwaseong City and the creation of an art park in Jebudo, indicating the artistic value of the island.""}; {'AGE VALUES': 'Existential – The study discusses the importance of developing strategies for placeness that correspond to the local characteristics of islands, which can represent memory and the lives of past generations.'}",tourism development (4); landscape improvement (2); eco-friendly branding (1); tourism offerings (1); digital technology-based publicity and marketing (1); revitalizing island tourism (3),Jebudo Island,island regions; Hwaseong-city; Gyeonggi province; Hwaseong City; West Coast; metropolitan area; Ansan City; island region; Seoul,,COVID-19 pandemic,"Previous studies have linked digital content and island regions, but no study has yet provided effective policy implications for revitalizing island tourism and the local economy in the current era of digital transformation.",This research aims to employ the focus group interview methodology to extract important insights and policy recommendations for the revitalization of tourism in island regions during the era of signi¦cant digital transformation.,social media (4); the internet (2); Instagram (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,focus group interview,,,,,,,[],,,,arti¦cial intelligence (3); Chat GPT (3),,,,,,,,,,,,,,