_Karayazi et al.-2022-AN EXPLORATION OF INTERACTIONS B.txt,AN EXPLORATION OF INTERACTIONS BETWEEN URBAN HERITAGES AND TOURIST’S DIGITAL FOOTPRINT: NETWORK AND TEXTUAL ANALYSIS VIA GEOTAGGED FLICKR DATA IN AMSTERDAM,S. S. Karayazi; G. Dane; T. Arentze,"Urban Systems and Real Estate, Department of Built Environment, Eindhoven University of Technology, The Netherlands",2021,other,,,,,,,Urban Heritage (2),cultural (1),heritage artifacts (2); heritage buildings (1); historic cities (1); heritage areas (1); historic urban landscape (1); National Monument (1); urban heritages (4),Urban heritage (2); heritage artifacts (2); heritage values (1); heritage areas (1); heritage types (1); heritages (6); National Monument (1); Cultural Heritage Agency (1); National monuments dataset (1); registered heritages (1); Cultural Heritage (1); protected (1),,"{'value': 'Social (Collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study explores the relationship between tourists and heritage sites, aiming to distribute tourists throughout the city and reduce pressure on popular sites. This implicitly discusses the collective emotional value of cultural identity and sense of place attachment.'}; {'value': 'Economic (Entertainment)', 'reasoning': 'The research focuses on tourism patterns and how tourists perceive different places, suggesting alternative locations to visit. This implicitly discusses the entertainment value of heritage assets in the contemporary tourism market.'}; {'value': 'Political (Management)', 'reasoning': 'The study aims to support policy makers in reducing tourist pressure in certain areas by understanding spatial interactions between urban heritages and tourist patterns. This implicitly discusses the incorporation of heritage assets into political strategies and policies.'}; {'value': 'Historic (Educational)', 'reasoning': 'The study uses heritage data to understand attractive heritage locations and the built environment characteristics that make them attractive. This implicitly discusses the potential of heritage assets to provide knowledge about the past in the future.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetic (Notable)', 'reasoning': 'The study mentions artistic values and architectural descriptions of heritage sites, such as churches and museums. This implicitly discusses the value of heritage assets created by recognized artists or designers.'}",sustainable tourism (2); sustainable tourism policies (1); heritage data (5); heritage (11); heritage artefacts (1); heritage types (1); heritage sites (1),"Amsterdam (16); Amsterdam’s historical core (3); European cities such as Amsterdam, Barcelona, Venice, Berlin, Copenhagen (1)",Amsterdam (16); European (4); Netherlands (5); Germany (1); Belgium (2); France (1); Noord Holland (2),Seventeenth-Century,Covid- 19 (1),"There is a need to understand tourists’ movements in the city, and how tourists perceive and experience different places (Trinh & Ryan, 2017). Based on this understanding, tourists can be provided with location suggestions not only of well-known places but also underrepresented areas with similar heritage types for reducing tourism pressure by distributing tourists throughout the city.","By building on previous research, our paper presents an approach to understand spatial interactions between urban heritages and tourist patterns in Amsterdam with Flickr data and heritage data from Amsterdam for supporting policy makers to reduce tourist pressure in certain areas.",Flickr (14); location-based social networks (LBSN) (4); Trip Advisor (4); Twitter (2); Foursquare (2),geotagged Flickr data (3); spatial information (2); non-spatial information (1); photograph’s location (1); unique owner ID (1); photograph’s taken time (1); photograph’s textual data (1); tags (6); descriptions (6); GPS data (2); image captions (2),geotagged (3); location (3); coordinates (2),spatial clustering (1); hotspot analysis (1); spatial patterns (3); spatial centrality (1); spatial interactions (3); spatial distribution (1); heatmaps (