_Kim et al.-2024-Where and Why Travelers Visit C.txt,Classifying Coastal Tourism Activities Using Geotagged Image Content from Social Media Data,Gang Sun Kim; Choong-Ki Kim; Woo-Kyun Lee,,2024,journal article,ISPRS Int. J. Geo-Inf.,MDPI,doi.org/10.3390/ijgi13100355,https://doi.org/10.3390/ijgi13100355,,,World Heritage Site (1),,coastal environment (4); coastal land (2); island areas (2); natural environment (3); coastal space (4); Seongsan Sunrise Peak (2); Hyangilam hermitage (1),coastal tourism (17); spatial planning (7); coastal tourism management (1); UNESCO (2); marine tourism (2); coastal conservation (1); ecosystem services (3),,"{'value': 'Social - Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study discusses the importance of coastal tourism for stress relief and relaxation, which can be considered ecosystem services. This relates to the emotional and communal value that people derive from coastal tourism.'}; {'value': 'Economic - Entertainment', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions the role of coastal tourism in recreation and leisure, indicating its entertainment value in the contemporary market, especially tourism.'}; {'value': 'Ecological - Essential', 'reasoning': 'The paper highlights that coastal tourism depends on a well-managed coastal environment, providing a rationale for conserving and managing such environments. This indicates the presence of ecological ideologies in the design and construction.'}",spatial planning (2),South Korea (7); Incheon (4); Mokpo (3); Yeosu (7); Busan (6); Gangneung (5); Jeju Island (6),South Korea (4); West Sea (1); Korea (4); Seoul (1); West Sea (1),,,"Over the past decade, research has proven that geotagged social media data can be a good proxy for visitor numbers; however, such data also have several limitations [14,15], such as data preprocessing problems that prevented the extraction of complete tourism-related records and the inability to check detailed tourism activities, which were left for future research [18].","To this end, we first created tourism activity classification criteria and training data to apply the supervised classification method for coastal tourism activity classification and then examined the classification accuracy and validity of the created model. Second, the classification model was applied to the study area (South Korean coastal area) to create a map of the distribution of coastal tourism activities and to understand the patterns revealed by the spatial environment. Finally, the combination of quantitative and qualitative information to understand spatial information and context is explored through examples.",Flickr (10); X (formerly Twitter) (6); SNS (Flickr platform) (1),images (10); photos (6); text (3); geographic information (2); spatial data (5),Geographic coordinates (3); geographic information (1),spatial distribution (4); spatial insights (2); spatial environment (3); spatial patterns (3); spatial context (3); spatial autocorrelation (4); spatial hotspots (6),time taken (2); date (2),,User ID (de-identified) (2); time taken (2); Geographic coordinates (2); Picture content (2); grid-feature ID (2),,,,"12,229 images (1)",,,,2013–2020 (2),coastal land of South Korea (1),"Flickr data were collected using the Flickr API and the R open library “Photosearcher”, which simplifies the utilization of the Flickr API in R programming [37].",Flickr API (2),"A total of 10,000 images were randomly selected from the Flickr dataset collected to make training data.",duplicates were removed through date and user to calculate the PUD for each grid.,,"Three assistants, including two master’s students specializing in big data and one undergraduate student focused on statistics, visually categorized the images based on the established criteria.","Three assistants, including two master’s students specializing in big data and one undergraduate student focused on statistics, visually categorized the images based on the established criteria.","['recreation activity', 'appreciation activity', 'education activity', 'other activities']","In cases of disagreement among the assistants, the category value was assigned based on a majority vote. If all three assistants assigned different category values, the authors made the final judgment.",classification (13),convolutional neural network (1),VGG16 (2),"The transfer-learning method, which leverages a ready-made algorithm architecture, was performed using the VGG16 (Visual Geometry Group 16) algorithm developed by the VGG team at the University of Oxford [51,52].",,,images (1),,"the data were divided into training, validation, and test datasets.",,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,"The VGG16 model is a convolutional neural network algorithm that first appeared in the Imagenet Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge 2014 (ILSVRC, 2014).","The algorithm was trained and implemented using Python and the Keras library [53]. Cross-validation is essential in machine learning to mitigate overfitting and obtain a generalized model. To perform cross-validation, the data were divided into training, validation, and test datasets. The model training process was iterated multiple times, where the training data were used to train the model, and the validation data were used to assess the model’s performance and guide adjustments to achieve a better fit in subsequent training iterations. Finally, the test data were used to select the best-performing model among several models created. A total of 999 images, which accounted for 1/10 of the total data, were allocated as test data, whereas the remaining data were split into training and validation data in a 7:3 ratio. Ten models were built during training iterations, and the model demonstrating the best performance was selected for analysis",,[],,,,,,,,,,Accuracy (4); sensitivity (4); precision (4),"['the test accuracy was found to be 0.7244 (1)', 'The test accuracy of the classification model was 0.7422 (1)']","['The sensitivity and precision of the appreciation item were 0.8422 and 0.8143, respectively, while those of the other activities item were 0.7959 and 0.7390, respectively.', 'The sensitivity and precision of the appreciation item were 0.8422 and 0.8143, respectively, while those of the other activities item were 0.7959 and 0.7390, respectively.']",[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],"To perform cross-validation, the data were divided into training, validation, and test datasets.",,[],,"['approximately 43% of the SNS (Flickr platform) image data in the study area are other activities, 39% are appreciation activities, and 18% are recreation and education activities', 'natural environments such as beaches have more appreciation activities and fewer other activities, and the opposite trend is observed in urban areas with a high population density', 'Cities with high population densities have a higher proportion of other activities and a smaller proportion of appreciation activities than other cities.']",[],[],[],[],['spatial hotspots are mainly formed in urban centers or around famous tourist attractions'],[],[],['This research contributes to the literature by introducing a novel methodological approach that leverages deep-learning and transfer-learning techniques for the classification of coastal tourism activities through geotagged social media data.'],[],[],"['First, although we used two SNS platforms and other data to estimate the number of tourists to control for data bias, we could only use data from Flickr for the tourism activity classification process because of data acquisition problems. Future classification studies using different platforms may yield less biased results.', 'Second, the data and analyses were limited to coasts and islands. Therefore, the impact of the land adjacent to the coast was not considered in this study. Future studies that include all contiguous spaces will provide a more accurate picture of the factors driving these patterns.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,,,,Keras,,coastal tourism activities; geotagged social media; spatial data analysis; image classification; tourist behavior insights; data integration; user-generated content (UGC); deep-learning algorithms; qualitative content; transfer-learning method; marine-dependent; marine-related,,,
_Koga and Yamamoto-2024-TOURISM SUPPORT SYSTEM TO UTILIZ.txt,Article ABSTRACT:,Yuro Koga; Kayoko Yamamoto,,2023,journal article,ISPRS Int. J. Geo-Inf.,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/ ijgi12080301,https://doi.org/10.3390/ ijgi12080301,,,,,urban tourist destinations (3); urban tourist destination (1),,,,,Osaka City (10),Japan (5); Osaka Prefecture (2),,COVID-19 pandemic (3),Tourists who are not familiar with the tourist destinations may ﬁnd it hard to obtain necessary information and use this to create a satisfactory sightseeing plan that tours various sightseeing spots.,The present study aims to take the above academic and social background into consideration in order to integrate an original tourism planning support system with the web-geographic information systems (Web-GIS) to develop a system that provides tourism supports speciﬁcally for tourists who are not familiar with the urban tourist destinations.,social media (10); Twitter (4); Flickr (3); You Tube (2); Facebook (1),images (5); text (1); videos (2),,,real time (5),reviews (2); comments (2); likes (3),user name (1); date of registration (1); title (3); website (2); address (1),,160 (2),,,,,,one-month period from 21 December 2022 to 24 January 2023 (2),Osaka City (2),API (4),"Twitter Search API (1), Flickr API (1), You Tube Data API (1), Arc GIS API (1)",,,excluding sensitive submissions and retweets (1),,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],Pearson’s chi-square test (3),,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Data sharing is not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analyzed during the present study.,,,,,,tourism planning (3); support system (7); web-geographic information systems (Web-GIS) (5); virtual reality (VR) space (3); dynamic information (7); social media (7); sightseeing spots (13); 3D city model (3); mobile information terminals (4); 2D digital map (6); 3D digital map (6),,,
_Koh et al.-2022-Towards a “City in Nature” Eval.txt,Towards a “City in Nature”: Evaluating the Cultural Ecosystem Services Approach Using Online Public Participation GIS to Support Urban Green Space Management,Y.F. Koh; H.H. Loc; E. Park,,2022,journal article,Sustainability,MDPI,doi.org/10.3390/su14031499,https://doi.org/10.3390/su14031499,,,,urban,urban green space (UGS) (3); Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park (BAMKP) (14),cultural ecosystem services (CES) (30); cultural ecosystem beneﬁts (CEB) (14); cultural values (10); urban green space management (4); biodiversity (6); naturalisation (2); rewilding (2); natural designs; plantings; conservation; naturalistic landscapes; naturalised water features,,"{'Social': 'The study discusses the importance of social interaction and community gathering within urban green spaces, indicating a focus on collective emotional values and cultural identity.'}; {'Aesthetical': 'The paper highlights the aesthetic qualities of the park, particularly the renaturalized river and green spaces, suggesting an appreciation for artistic and notable design elements.'}; {'Ecological': 'The study emphasizes the integration of nature and greenery within the urban environment, reflecting essential ecological values and the harmony between built and natural elements.'}; {'Economic': 'The paper mentions recreational activities and tourism, indicating the entertainment value of the park. It also touches on the potential for future use and the value of its continued existence.'}; {'Political': 'The paper discusses the educational role of the park and its incorporation into Singapore\'s ""City in Nature"" vision, suggesting a management value and the use of the park for political strategies and policies.'}",UGS management (4); planning (6); re-naturalisation,Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park,Singapore (11); China; Asia (2); Vietnam; Mekong Delta,,,"However, such a deﬁnition has proven to be problematic for urban planners to directly incorporate CES into decision making due to the conﬂation of the distinct terms of “services” and “beneﬁts” [6,8].",This paper thus proposes the following four research questions: 1. What are the public’s cultural values of Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park? 2. What are the cultural ecosystem services offered in Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park? 3. What are the cultural ecosystem beneﬁts enjoyed in Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park? 4. What is the relationship between services and beneﬁts in Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park?,Google (3),text (3); spatial data,,spatial distribution; heat maps; spatial correlation,,,,5762 reviews,100 valid participants,5762 reviews,,,,,until 17 November 2020,Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park,"online survey, text mining analysis","Map-Me, Google Forms, Scrape Hero",Convenience snowball sampling,"Reviews with non-English or no textual reviews were ﬁrst removed before the remaining review sentences were split into individual words. Misspelled, non-English and less meaningful stop words were then removed, resulting in 2356 unique words for further analysis.",Reviews with non-English or no textual reviews,,,[],,sentiment analysis (3),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],one-way ANOVA tests,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],bar charts; lollipop charts; heat maps,[],[],[],"['Generally, ﬁndings from the sentiment analysis of Google reviews have yielded that the public’s cultural values towards the park were highly positive (see Figure 4).']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['Declaration of Helsinki'],[],[],[],[],[],,Not applicable.,,,R software [34],,,public participation geographic information system; PPGIS; Park Connector Network; PCN; Coast-to-Coast; C2C; River Plains; Pond Gardens; Likert scale; Jaccard’s Index,,,
_Kong and Sarmiento-2022-Utilizing a crowdsourced phrasal.txt,"Utilizing a crowdsourced phrasal lexicon to identify cultural ecosystem services in El Cajas National Park, Ecuador",Inhye Kong; Fausto O. Sarmiento,"Department of Geography, University of Georgia, Athens, GA 30602, United States; Neotropical Montology Collaboratory, Department of Geography, University of Georgia, Athens, GA 30602, United States",2022,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier B V,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2022.101441,www.elsevier.com/locate/ecoser,,,,,El Cajas National Park (CNP) (8),cultural diversity (1); cultural ecosystem services (CES) (20); biocultural heritage (1); indigenous culture and heritage (1),,,conservation (3); conservation practices (1); conservation policy (1); conservation priorities (1); conservation strategy (1); protected area management (1),El Cajas National Park in Ecuador,Ecuador (4); North America (2); Western Europe (2); South America (1); Asia (1); Africa (1),,,One of the most critical challenges to consider CES in current con servation policy has been the lack of data and tools to distinguish intangible CES embedded in the landscape and community,"we propose a novel approach to develop a crowdsourced phrasal lexicon for eight CES. After applying the lexicon to the texts, we filtered CES-positive reviews to find that Tri p Advisor showed higher CES frequencies based on greater verbosity than Google Maps, while Google Maps contained more non-English reviews to represent Spanish-speaking users. The frequency of CES showed little difference for aesthetic value, yet English reviews outperformed in describing biological and recreational values whereas Spanish reviews excelled in describing spiritual values.",Trip Advisor (11); Google Maps (11); Instagram (2); Twitter (1),texts (2); online tourism reviews (3); unstructured texts (1); text content (1); geolocations (1); photo tags (2),geolocations (1),spatial distribution (2),,,,"3,201",,,,,,,February 2021,El Cajas National Park in Ecuador,manual data scraping (1),R scripts (1),,"We cleaned the unstructured text of the reviews using a natural language process (NLP), which included tokenization, lem matization, part-of-speech (POS) tagging, and the removal of punctua tion, emojis, and selective determinators (i.e., a, an, the).",We filtered CES-positive reviews,crowdsourced (4),Amazon Mechanical Turk (MTurk) (3),"['aesthetic', 'biological', 'cultural', 'educational', 'identity', 'recreational', 'social', 'spiritual']",satisfactory coherency for the annotations (1),named entity recognition (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"Using the natural language process and text-mining techniques for the unstructured texts of online reviews, we filter phrasal expressions that contain higher contextual meanings. Then, we apply a crowdsourced method to annotate the phrases with relevant CES as a novel strategy to minimize expert bias.",yes,['network (6)'],lemmas (4),consecutive pairs of lemmas in the phrases (1),,,in-degree centrality (1); outdegree centrality (1); degree centrality (4),,,,assess the relationship of a system of connected elements (1),,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['data-driven analysis for protected area management (1)'],['implement CES-inclusive conservation strategies (1)'],"['originality of the crowdsourced phrasal lexicon to provide high-context information with public annotations, which can improve the accuracy and accountability of CES analysis (1)']",['linguistic distinctions in describing CES between English and Spanish reviews are highlighted in a non-English-speaking country (1)'],[],"['social media contents are known to be inclined to non-local tourists who prefer to share enjoyable moments or tourism-related advice and recommendations (1)', 'the findings may have overlooked the experiences of non-tourists such as local communities (1)']","['The content does not include any personal identifiable informa\ntion nor user IDs, and has been stored in a personal hard drive of the first author (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],https://github.com/ih Kong/phrasal_lexic on_CES (1),,,,,spacyr (1); udpipe (3); rvest (1); Rselenium (1); igraph (1),,online reviews (7); phrasal lexicon (7); noun phrases (13); verb phrases (13); natural language process (NLP) (2); part-of-speech (POS) (4); Amazon Mechanical Turk (MTurk) (3); POS-based parsing (1); phrase parsing (2); tourist destination (1); Ramsar Wetland (1); UNESCO Biosphere Reserve (1); p´aramo environment (1); payment for ecosystem services (1),,,
_Konstantakis et al.-2017-Formalising and evaluating Cultu.txt,Formalising and evaluating Cultural User Experience,and universal concept,"Intelligent Systems, Content and Interaction l b; N i l T h i l U i i f A h pp , y",2009,other,SIGCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems,ACM,,,,,Cultural Heritage (1),,cultural objects (3); cultural artifacts (2); cultural spaces (3); museum collections (1); ancient ruins (1); artefact exhibitions (1); cultural institutes (1),cultural experience (10); cultural user experience (CUX) (8); cultural interactive experiences (1); cultural applications (2); cultural industry (1); cultural background (2); cultural identities (1); cultural heritage users (1); cultural product (1); cultural environments (2); cultural exhibitions (3); cultural spaces (3); cultural services (2); museum (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The paper discusses how cultural heritage plays an important role in the cultural fabric of society and how museums, ancient ruins, and artefact exhibitions represent sources and instruments of education that should be available to a wide range of people.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Educational – The paper mentions that museum collections, ancient ruins, and artefact exhibitions represent at the same time sources and instruments of education that should be available to a wide range of people.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The paper discusses the potential of cultural heritage to provide knowledge about the past, particularly through museum collections, ancient ruins, and artefact exhibitions.'}",,,,,,"in both Information Systems and Cultural Technology research, little attention has been paid to the evaluation of UX with technologies in cultural heritage environments.",This paper attempts to analyze and evaluate the aspects of CUX methodologies that are currently available and to specify future designing improvements for UX evaluation methods.,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,