I and II[cite: 842]; WW II battleﬁeld scenery, Leros Island[cite: 847]; Battle of Leros (Operation Leopard) [cite: 989]","The goal of this article is to ‘unbury’ land and maritime, tangible and intangible, cultural remains and use them to unveil heritage-led development paths of small Mediterranean islands. [cite: 846]","The goal of this article is to ‘unbury’ land and maritime, tangible and intangible, cultural remains and use them to unveil heritage-led development paths of small Mediterranean islands. [cite: 846] and is accomplished by a participatory spatial planning framework, embedding scenario planning, spatial data management, and e-participation technologies (Web-GIS, social networks) for community engagement. [cite: 848]",social networks; Facebook; Instagram; Web-GIS; Google Docs’ form,e-participation technologies; Web-based questionnaires; interactions/likes; views; open question replies,Web-GIS visualization of scenarios; GIS mapping; geospatial information; map zoom in and out; GPS,"spatially delineated, potential cultural development trails; spatial distribution of these resources in the land and maritime part; spatial pattern of cultural tourism development (concentrated or de-concentrated)",The online questionnaire was available on the Web for three months (June to August 2018); targeted campaign through Facebook and Instagram was carried out for the time span 17 June–17 August 2018,interactions/likes; views,"audience prerequisites; respondents’ profile (gender, age groups, educational profile, unemployment); post IDs",,,"204 questionnaires were reaped [cite: 1143], nine replies (open question suggestions) [cite: 1156]",,,,,June to August 2018 [cite: 1141],Leros Island [cite: 1139],"Web-based participatory assessment of scenarios and priority axes [cite: 905], digitally engaging local community as consultants [cite: 905], online questionnaire (Google Docs’ form) [cite: 1131], targeted campaign was also carried out through Facebook and Instagram [cite: 1138]","Web-GIS platform [cite: 906], ESRI “Story Maps” [cite: 1126], Google Docs’ form [cite: 1131], Facebook and Instagram [cite: 1138]","targeted communication... addressing an audience that fulﬁlled the following prerequisites: residents of Leros Island or persons originated from there; fall into the 18-65+ age groups; and be interested in themes associated with WW II, diving, Leros island, cultural tourism, wrecks, tourism, Aegean Sea islands, and/or alternative tourism. [cite: 1139, 1140]",,,"Respondents were asked to evaluate the current state of the island’s cultural development [cite: 1132], rate their preference with respect to proposed scenarios [cite: 1133], and select three out of the eight priority axes [cite: 1134]",local community [cite: 906],"['Current state evaluation (scale 1-5)', ""Scenario preference (Scenario A' or B')"", 'Priority axes ranking (select 3 out of 8)']",,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,"['social networks', 'road network']",,,,,,A network analysis was also carried out by use of the road network of the study region [cite: 1128],,,"spreading information with regard to planning choices and engaging local community in their assessment [cite: 907]; structuring of additional information to those interested in taking a certain tour, such as distance by car, traveling time, etc. [cite: 1128]",Rating Scale (1 to 5); Percentage (%),"Out of this Web interaction, 204 questionnaires were reaped[cite: 1143]; 54% of respondents were women[cite: 1144]; 52% were 18–35 age group, 30% were 36–50 age group[cite: 1145].","22%, 38%, and 32% of respondents’ replies fall into rates 1, 2, and 3, respectively[cite: 1148]. Scenario B' (64%) preferred[cite: 1153, 1165]; Priority axes: 'designation of local cultural identity...' (45%), 'development of alternative...' (35%), and 'balanced cultural tourism development...' (30%)[cite: 1151, 1165].",,,Respondents realize the uniqueness of Leros Island in terms of its WW II CH and especially UCH and the role these can play for serving sustainable development objectives of the area[cite: 1149]; The majority of them seem to converge on the necessity to adopt a more systematic and integrated cultural planning and (U)CH management approach for serving long-term prosperity of the area in a sustainable and resilient way[cite: 1150].,"nine replies [cite: 1156] in the open question, including: adding value by means of additional cultural content [cite: 1157], provision of sources of historical information [cite: 1158], narration of personal stories of respondents [cite: 1160], provision of suggestions as to how to promote the island’s cultural heritage[cite: 1160].","Scenario B’, according to the local community’s view, seems to be a more promising option towards a sustainable, resilient, spatially balanced, qualitative, and longer-term future[cite: 1154].",,[],[],[],,,[],Web-GIS platform; GIS mapping,"[""The strong association of Leros's cultural tourism with WW II events and remains (mono-thematic approach in Scenario B')[cite: 1091].""]","['Local population exhibited a high level of familiarization and skills for handling internet applications and social media[cite: 1145].', 'High engagement rate (799 interactions/likes and 8038 views)[cite: 1142].']","['(U)CH is perceived as largely underexploited[cite: 1148].', 'The local community attaches high value to WW II events and their remnants, sealing the ‘body’, history, and people of Leros[cite: 1153].']","[""Respondents showed 'passion of people for their history, identity, and values' and 'their concern for keeping them alive for the future generations'[cite: 1161, 1162].""]","['High educational profile of respondents (57% PhD, MSc, or University Degree) [cite: 1146] compared to general small-island population profile (usually digitally illiterate and of low educational profile)[cite: 882].']","[""Scenario A' predicts a polycentric spatial deployment with four distinct nodes[cite: 1071]."", ""Scenario B' is mono-thematic and spatially concentrated[cite: 1070].""]","['Heritage-led local development [cite: 846]', 'Future cultural tourism trails (diving tourism, battleﬁeld/dark tourism) [cite: 845, 1077]', 'Strategic planning [cite: 848]', 'Community engagement [cite: 848]', ""Local development planning (shift of island's image) [cite: 1017]""]","['Need for integrated planning approach combining land and maritime CH[cite: 1168].', 'Need to raise awareness and cultivate responsibility among local population regarding the value of CH[cite: 850, 1183].', 'Integrated approach of natural and cultural resources[cite: 1020].', 'Adopt a more systematic and integrated cultural planning and (U)CH management approach[cite: 1150].']","['Proposes an integrated methodological framework for heritage-led local development of insular communities[cite: 889].', 'Framework embeds scenario planning, spatial data management, and e-participation technologies[cite: 848].', 'Use of Web-GIS application/mobile phone environment for participatory assessment[cite: 1125, 1129].']","[""GIS mapping of Leros Island's WW II (U)CH and natural resources[cite: 1026]."", ""Structuring of alternative future heritage-led development scenarios (Scenario A' and B')[cite: 1062]."", 'Quantitative and qualitative assessment of scenarios based on local community preferences[cite: 1143].']","[""Preservation and sustainable management of CH is seen as a 'two-sided coin' (European identity/memory vs. cultural tourism trails)[cite: 844]."", 'The study attempts to address the gap in integrated management of land and underwater cultural heritage[cite: 1168].']","['Coastal/maritime stakeholders and community actors do not share the same values regarding (U)CH exploitation[cite: 1211].', ""Use of Web-GIS was not the wisest choice in hindsight due to local population's skill proﬁle and lack of previous engagement experience[cite: 1219]."", 'Scarcity, scattering, and accessibility issues of reliable spatial data of maritime origin[cite: 1224, 1225].', 'Prevailing model of hiding (U)CH from public view[cite: 1206].', 'Major challenge relies on establishing substantial communication and interaction among multidisciplinary expertise[cite: 1202].']",[],"[""Respondents' profile (high educational profile, 18-50 age majority) deviates from the general small-island population profile (low educational profile, digitally illiterate)[cite: 882, 1145, 1146].""]","['UNESCO Convention Concerning the Protection of the World Cultural and Natural Heritage (1972) [cite: 924]', 'UNESCO Convention on the Protection of the Underwater Cultural Heritage (2001) [cite: 927]', 'European Convention on the Protection of the Archaeological Heritage (Valetta or Malta Treaty, 1992) [cite: 938]', 'European Landscape Convention (Florence Convention) [cite: 939]', 'Convention on the Value of Cultural Heritage for Society (Faro Convention, 2005) [cite: 942]']","['Community engagement for imbuing a UCH narrative with meaning, stories, and identity[cite: 863].', ""Community engagement acts as a 'learning platform for increasing awareness and cultivating responsibility'[cite: 1183]."", 'Importance of indigenous knowledge and visions[cite: 1184].', 'Need for balancing different stakes and expectations of various stakeholders’ groups[cite: 1034].']","['EU blue growth strategy [cite: 867]', 'Maritime Spatial Planning (MSP) [cite: 869]', 'Integrated Coastal Zone Management (ICZM) [cite: 869]', 'Law 3028/2002 “For the Protection of Antiquities and Cultural Heritage in general” (Greece) [cite: 961]', 'Ministerial Decision of 2003 (Greece) (shipwrecks as monuments after 50 years) [cite: 966]', 'Law 3409/2005 (diving parks) [cite: 967]', 'Law 4179/2013 (access to Maritime Archaeological Sites) [cite: 969]']","['UCH is threatened by both natural (e.g., climate change) and anthropogenic (e.g., ﬁshing, looting) dangers[cite: 861].', 'Risks inherent in UCH protection (anthropogenic and natural origin)[cite: 929].']",['This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). [cite: 1375]'],"['Use of Web-GIS application... seems, in hindsight, not to be the wisest choice in the speciﬁc case study context[cite: 1219].', 'scarcity, scattering, and accessibility issues of reliable spatial data of mainly maritime origin[cite: 1224].', 'The lack of exploration of UCH due to identification/protection difficulties results in a knowledge deficit[cite: 858].']",,,,https://goo.gl/t M1Nny [cite: 1134],,,,insularity; e-participation technologies; Web-GIS; spatial data management; scenario planning; World War (WW) I and II; diving community; blue growth strategy; Maritime Spatial Planning (MSP); Integrated Coastal Zone Management (ICZM); Valetta or Malta Treaty; Faro Convention; Lakki settlement (Porto Lago); Saint Marina settlement; Platanos; Partheni settlement; Xirokampos settlement; ‘Soul House’; Operation Leopard; Network analysis; ESRI “Story Maps”; Google Docs’ form; cultural tourism trails; dark or battleﬁeld tourism; European identity; knowledge deﬁcit; wicked problem,"TITLE: The full title is not explicitly in the text, forcing the rest to be inferred from the filename. [cite: 841]; AUTHORS: Inferred from the author contribution section and the filename. [cite: 1234]; PLATFORM: 'social networks' [cite: 848] is general; only Facebook and Instagram are explicitly confirmed as used for campaigns[cite: 1138].","The core of the methodology is a non-AI/ML planning framework, so Blocks 4 and 5 are largely empty of AI/ML specifics. The output file name was not provided in the instructions. Figures 1-9 are referenced but not visible. The Web-GIS link is provided and noted as being in Greek.","The study region, Leros Island, has a history marked by foreign occupation (Turkish, Italian) [cite: 980, 981], and traumatic events: concentration camp (1957–today) [cite: 994], exile camp (1967–1974) [cite: 995], and a 'hot spot' for refugees (2016–today)[cite: 996]. The primary goal is to shift the island's image from a 'Soul House' to a place of multiple opportunities[cite: 1069]."
_Kovárník et al.-2025-A comprehensive framework for au.txt,A comprehensive framework for automated identification of cultural ecosystem services,Richard Kov´arník; Jitka Janov´a; David Hampel,"Department of Statistics and Operation Analysis, Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Zemˇedˇelsk´a 1, 61300 Brno, Czech Republic",2025,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2025.101770,www.elsevier.com/locate/ecoser,,,,,national parks (NPs) (1); protected landscape areas (PLAs) (1),,,"Spiritual – Beliefs, myths, religions (organized or not), legends, stories, and testimonials of past generations. The text mentions spiritual enrichment as a non-material benefit people derive from nature.; Emotional (collective) – Cultural identity, motivation and pride, sense of “place attachment,” and communal value. The text refers to shaping people’s emotional and cultural connections to the environment.; Aesthetical – Original product of creativity and imagination. The text mentions aesthetic appreciation as a non-material benefit people derive from nature.; Entertainment – The role the asset plays in the contemporary market, especially tourism. The text mentions recreational experiences as a non-material benefit people derive from nature.",,ˇSumava National Park; Podyjí National Park; Moravský kras (Moravian Karst); P´alava; ˇZď´arsk´e vrchy,Czech Republic; Austria; Germany; Europe; Central Europe; Moravia; South England,,COVID-19 pandemic,"Given that a large part of the information regarding the use of CES is captured in text form on social media and other platforms (Comalada et al., 2025), it is possible to automate the classification of content and analyse the semantics and sentiment.",The goal of this paper is to demonstrate the potential of obtaining and utilizing textual user reviews from Google Maps platform to identify CES in selected national parks (NPs) and protected landscape areas (PLAs). Subject these data to topic modelling based on natural language processing methods and subject the information thus obtained to statistical testing to identify statistically significant CES and their differences within selected locations.,Google Maps; Flickr; Instagram; Twitter; Reddit; Trip Advisor,text; image data,,,,star rating,date of posting,"4,760",,,,,,,2018–2020,selected national parks and protected areas across the Czech Republic,dynamic web scraping techniques,Python programming language (version 3.12) and Selenium library (version 4.20.1),,"removal of irrelevant content such as special characters, and excessive whitespace. Additionally, all reviews were converted to lowercase to maintain uniformity. Stop words—commonly used words that do not contribute meaningful information to the analysis—were removed from the reviews. To further clean the text, any non-alphanumeric characters (such as punctuation marks and numbers) were eliminated.",publicly published reviews containing textual commentary in a given location. Reviews that did not contain a textual rating were not included in the analysis.,,,[],,topic modelling; text clustering; text classification; text summarization,transformer-based models,BERT; BERTopic,,,,text data,,,,,,,,,,{},Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA),,,,,,,"The complete BERTopic process thus involves text embedding through a pre-trained transformer model, followed by dimensionality reduction, clustering, and extraction of topic representations using c-TF-IDF (Grootendorst, 2022).",,[],,,,,,,,,,p-value,[],[],[],chi-square test of homogeneity; Haberman residuals,,[],[],,[],[],[],,Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA); Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF),[],sign plot; correspondence analysis,"['recreational, aesthetic or spiritual values that ecosystems offer them', 'cycling', 'tourism', 'aesthetic assessment of the landscape', 'expression of spiritual feelings and feelings of belonging to the landscape']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['the possible unrepresentativeness of the authors of the text reviews', 'Google Maps reviews may be biased toward visitors who are either highly satisfied or highly dissatisfied, which could skew the results', 'the number of newly created reviews has a decreasing tendency, the number of individual reviews can change significantly over time', 'the study period also covers the time of the COVID-19 pandemic, which may have influenced people’s behavior']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],The data and code are available in the following Git Hub repository: https://github.com/xkovarn1/ces_crowdsourced.,,,https://github.com/xkovarn1/ces_crowdsourced,,BERTopic library (version 0.17.0); Prince library (version 0.16.0),,cultural ecosystem services (CES); cultural ecosystem services; ecosystem services (ES); non-material benefits; spiritual enrichment; recreational experiences; aesthetic appreciation; opportunities for learning and inspiration; crowdsourced data; remote sensing; machine learning techniques; natural language processing (NLP); topic modelling; web scraping; statistical methods; questionnaire survey; text data; image data; Google Maps; social media; national parks (NPs); protected landscape areas (PLAs); user reviews; Czech Republic; BERTopic model; c-TF-IDF (class-based Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency); UMAP (Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection); HDBSCAN (Hierarchical Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise); Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA); Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF); homogeneity tests; mobile network data; high-resolution satellite imagery,,,
_Krataithong et al.-2025-A Tourist Behavior Analysis Usin.txt,A Tourist Behavior Analysis Using Probabilistic-Based Tourist Trip Inference from Taxi GPS and Social Media Data,Pattama Krataithong; Chutiporn Anutariya; Marut Buranarach,"National Electronics and Computer Technology Center, Pathum Thani, Thailand; ICT Department, School of Engineering and Technology, Asian Institute of Technology, Pathum Thani, Thailand",2025,journal article,,Springer Nature,,,,,,,,,,,,Bangkok (2),Thailand (2); Ontario; Canada; Shanghai; Shenzhen; Tokyo; Japan; Beijing,,Songkran Festival (4),"However, a key challenge in utilizing taxi GPS data in the tourism do­ main is the lack of semantic information regarding trip purpose and user profile that could facilitate an in-depth analysis of tourist behavior.",RQ1: How can we extract tourist trajectories and infer their activities from taxi GPS data? ● RQ2: How can we integrate taxi data and social media data to develop a dynamic trip inference to classify different types of tourist trajectories? ● RQ3: How can we use tourist trajectories to discover more about tourist behav­ ior?,Twitter (10); Foursquare (2),GPS data (12); taxi trajectory data (4); social media data (5); place data (6); geo-tagged Twitter data (2); tweet messages (4); tweet locations (2); timestamps (3); user locations (2); CSV files (2),GPS data (12); GPS coordinate (2); geo-tagged (2); location information (3),spatial distributions (2); spatial interaction; spatial rule,timestamps (3); temporal distribution (2); time distribution,,user profiles; user location (3); trip purposes (2); taxi OD points (2); place category/subcategory (3); opening hours (4); POI rating (2); common place activities; travel time (2); total distance; travel time; tourist spending type,"approximately 6,000 unique tweets","2,486 users",,,,,,"April 4–24, 2019 (4)",Bangkok (5),collected,Twitter APIs (2),"queries related to tourism, geolocation (Bangkok), and the English language","remove taxi GPS data