_Lee et al.-2015-Predicting geo-informative attri.txt,Predicting Geo-informative Attributes in Large-scale Image Collections,Lee; Seewald; Mooney; Crandall,,2015,other,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,U.S.; China,,,Most images cannot be geo-located by either approach — there are simply not enough training images to densely cover the surface of the world.,"In this paper, we introduce the problem of classifying geo-informative attributes of where a photo was taken, in- cluding geographic properties like elevation and land type as well as demographics like wealth and population.",Facebook; Flickr; Yahoo’s Flickr,images,geo-tags; GPS coordinates,,timestamps,,geo-tags; timestamps,about 40 million,,,,,,,,worldwide,downloaded using the public API,public API,discretize the world into 0.01◦×0.01◦latitude-longitude bins (roughly 1 km × 1 km at the middle latitudes),"From this set, we ﬁltered out photos having imprecise geo-tags (with Flickr precision value less than 13, implying less accurate than about a city block).","photos having imprecise geo-tags (with Flickr precision value less than 13, implying less accurate than about a city block)",automatically,,[],,classification,deep convolutional neural networks (CNNs),,pretrained on Image Net,,Histogram of Oriented Gradients (HOG); GIST,image pixels,,"60%, 10%, 30%",,stochastic gradient descent,0.001,128 images,"25,000 batches",,,{},linear SVM,high-performance computing resources of Indiana University,,,,,"neural network architecture proposed by Krizhevsky et al. [19], with ﬁve convolutional layers (C1 to C5) followed by three fully connected layers (FC6 to FC8), and the same mechanisms for contrast normalization and max pooling","The network for each classiﬁer was trained independently using stochastic gradient descent with a batch size of 128 images. The learning rate was set at 0.001 and decreased by an order of magnitude every 2500 batches. The training process was allowed to continue for a maximum of 25,000 batches, but generally converged much earlier for our problems. To avoid overﬁtting, the validation set was evaluated every 500 batches and the weights with the lowest validation error were used for testing.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],"We partitioned the data into training, validation, and test sets (about 60%, 10%, 30% respectively) on a per-user basis to prevent leakage between the sets.",GIST; spatially-pooled color histograms; HOG,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['We believe that this is the ﬁrst paper to propose gen- eral geo-spatial attribute recognition as an important task, to apply deep learning techniques to problems related to geo- localization, and to evaluate the feasibility of geo-spatial attribute recognition on over a dozen attributes and on a worldwide scale.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],http://caffe.berkeleyvision.org/.,,,,,Caffe,,GIS maps,,,
_Lee et al.-2019-Mapping cultural ecosystem servi.txt,Mapping cultural ecosystem services 2.0 - potential and,Heera Lee; Bumsuk Seo; Tobias Kändler; Sven Lautenbach,,2019,journal article,,,,,,,,,,Cultural ecosystem services (CES) (7); ecosystem services (ES) (2),,"{'value': 'Aesthetical', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses landscape aesthetics as a cultural ecosystem service, analyzing photos to understand socio-cultural usages of landscapes. This aligns with the definition of aesthetic values as original products of creativity and imagination.'}; {'value': 'Social', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the socio-cultural usage of landscapes and recreational activities, which relates to the social values of cultural identity, motivation, and pride associated with a sense of place attachment and communal value.'}; {'value': 'Economic', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions the use of geotagged photos to understand socio-cultural usages of landscapes, which can be linked to tourism and recreational activities, thus relating to the entertainment value within economic values.'}",,,Saxony; Germany (3); Mulde river basin; Czech,,,"Both approaches are challenged with respect to consistency and cost-eﬃciency, especially for large-scale studies with an enormous volume of photos.",This study aims at developing a new method to analyze the content of large volumes of photos and to derive indicators of socio-cultural usage of landscapes.,Flickr (8); Panoramio (2); Instagram; Youtube,photos (15); geotagged photos (6); spatial information; temporal information; thematic information; videos,geotagged photos (6); location information,spatial pattern,,,user id; pictured date; coordinates,"12,635",725,,,,,,2005–2016,"Mulde basin in Saxony, Germany",The geotagged photos were identiﬁed and acquired through the Flickr Application Programming Interface (API) based on the location information of the photos.,Flickr Application Programming Interface (API),We only considered publicly available photos.,"For each polygon, we created the smallest bounding box covering the polygon. The photos found in the bounding box were once again ﬁltered by the polygon.",,automatic tagging,,[],,cluster detection; image recognition,deep convolutional neural networks (DCNNs) (2),,We used a pre-trained model (general-v1.3) provided by Clarifai to assign 20 tags per photo together with the associated probability of each tag,,,visual patterns of images and videos,,,,,,,,,,{},,cloud computing platform,,,,"Assigning 20 tags per photo for the 12,635 photos took approximately 3 hours",,,yes,"['tag-network (3)', 'social network']",machine-learned tags,co-occurrence of tags in the photos,"1,316",,eigenvector centrality (3); degree centrality; modularity (6); mean degrees; median degrees,We converted a 2-mode matrix (a photo-by-tags matrix) to a 1-mode tag co-occurrence matrix.,Fast-greedy algorithm (3); Walktrap algorithm (5); Force Atlas2 multi-gravity algorithm,Gephi (2),"To identify themes of photos, we investigated networks of assigned tags based on tag cooccurrence.",Hamming loss (3); accuracy (2),"['the average Hamming loss of the assigned tags, i.e., the ratio of the mislabeled tags, was 0.196, which can be interpreted as 80.4% accuracy']","['The lowest Hamming loss was found for photos associated with the cluster “landscape aesthetics” (0.13), whereas the highest Hamming loss was associated with the photos related to the cluster “cultural landmark” (0.39).']",[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This approach could be used to improve the commonly used ES evaluation tool, In VEST recreational model (Sharp et al., 2016), which calculates the photo-user-days based on the total number of geotagged photos with no consideration of the photo contents.']","['Through content analysis, land managers can obtain not only information on hotspots where users frequently visit, but also on the activities performed by landscape users.', 'This information allows land managers to respond accordingly, e.g. by restricting access to a speciﬁc vulnerable area or by providing facilities to steer the use of the landscape.']",['The combination of automatic tagging and tag-network analysis proposed in this study opens up a feasible way to analyze the contents of a large volume of photos.'],[],[],"['concerns remain regarding the representativeness of populations who uploaded photos in social media databases (Ruths and Pfeﬀer, 2014).']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,"GNU R (R Core Team, 2016) package clarifai (Sood, 2016).",igraph; User Net R; raster; rgdal; rgeos; sp; spdep,,social media photo services; cloud computing; tagging; keywords network analysis; mapping ecosystem services; big data analysis,,,
_Lee et al.-2020-Analysis of cultural ecosystem s.txt,Analysis of cultural ecosystem services using text mining of residents’ opinions,Jae-hyuck Lee; Hong-jun Park; Ilkwon Kim; Hyuk-soo Kwon,"Division of Social Environment, Sustainability Strategy Research Group, Korea Environment Institute, Sejong 30147, Republic of Korea; Team of Ecosystem Service, Division of Ecological Assessment Research, National Institute of Ecology, Seocheon 33657, Republic of Korea",2020,journal article,Ecological Indicators,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2020.106368,,,,,,"Namhansan Fortress (1); Gyeongan River Wetland Eco Park (1); Paldang Mist Park (1); Paldang Lake (2); Hwadam Botanical Garden (2); Tianjin Rock Valley (1); Gwangdong Cheongjeong Wetland (2); Yulbom Botanical Garden (1); Gyeongan River (1); Gonjiam Ceramics Park (2); Mugap Peak (1); Jungdae Mulbit Park (1); Paldang Observation Tower (1); Bunwon Royal Porcelain Museum (1); Natural Ecology Center (1); Paleolithic Heritage Site in Samni, Gwangju (1); Gonjiam River (1); Baekma Mountain (1); Cheonjinam Sacred Site (1); Munhyeong Peak (1); Sueojangdae Tower (1); Cherry Peak (1); Joseon White Porcelain Kiln Site Exhibition Hall (1); Namhan Mountain (1); Docheok Reservoir (2); Toechon Botanical Garden (2); Taehwa Mountain (1); Jeongam Mountain (1); Cheongseok Park (1); Baegindae Pavilion (1); Osaekhyang Farm (1); Chilsa Mountain (1); Jiwol Farm (1); Gonjiam Berry Farm (1); Public Playground (1); Yujeongtteul Farm (1); Haehyeop Peak (1); Jungdae Mulbit Park (1); Gonjiam (1); Daerimbae Farm (1); Bali Peak (1); Reservoir (1); Yongma Mountain (1); Suriul Farm (1); Cheondeok Peak (1); Geumbongsan (1); Hailong Mountain (1); Cheonglyang Mountain (1); Hope Artiﬁcial Marsh (1); Ssangnyeong Park (2); Mareum Mountain (2); Gugsu Peak (1); Art gallery (1)",cultural heritage (5); ecological resource (13); ecological resources (14); ecological knowledge (3); ecosystem services (13); cultural ecosystem services (24); cultural services (5); ecological place (1); sacred Catholic site (1); spiritual/religious value (3); Tangible Cultural Asset (1); heritage (1),"health values (2); Functions that had been simpliﬁed merely as “recreation” in existing cultural ecosystem services were related with “health values” such as “mountain climbing,” “exercise,” and “strolling” in Gwangju because many studies have shown that these kinds of activities are helpful for mental and physical health; educational value (3); “Cultural heritage” functions were speciﬁcally associated with “history” and “ceramics” because of their historical aspects related to ceramics and association with “educational value.”; aesthetic value (2); “Aesthetic value” was regarded as a beneﬁt that accompanied the experience of “mountain climbing” and “exercise,” to be enjoyed when visiting places of “nature,” “ecosystems,” or “plants” with “family” and “social relations.”; social relations value (1); “Aesthetic value” was regarded as a beneﬁt that accompanied the experience of “mountain climbing” and “exercise,” to be enjoyed when visiting places of “nature,” “ecosystems,” or “plants” with “family” and “social relations.”; spiritual/religious value (3); Moreover, “spiritual/religious value” is related to “cultural heritage in Catholic sacred sites.”","{'SOCIAL VALUES': {'Spiritual': 'The paper mentions “spiritual/religious value” related to “cultural heritage in Catholic sacred sites,” indicating the importance of beliefs and religious sites as part of cultural ecosystem services.', 'Emotional (collective)': 'The paper discusses cultural ecosystem services contributing to local economies and promoting biodiversity, suggesting a sense of pride and communal value associated with these services.'}}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': {'Entertainment': 'The paper mentions recreational activities like mountain climbing and farm programs, indicating the role of cultural ecosystem services in tourism and entertainment.'}}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': {'Educational': 'The paper mentions “cultural heritage” functions specifically associated with “history” and “ceramics” because of their historical aspects related to ceramics and association with “educational value.”'}}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': {'Artistic': 'The paper mentions “aesthetic value” as a benefit that accompanied the experience of “mountain climbing” and “exercise,” to be enjoyed when visiting places of “nature,” “ecosystems,” or “plants” with “family” and “social relations.”'}}",,Gwangju (Gyeonggi-do) Province,South Korea; Gwangju (Gyeonggi-do) province,Joseon Dynasty,,"Existing quantitative methods based on questionnaire surveys and geotagged photographs are useful in comparative analysis; however, they only address the items presented by researchers. This makes it diﬃcult to explain unique user activities related to cultural ecosystem services in a speciﬁc area and the reasons behind their use.","Thus, the purpose of this study is to identify and evaluate the local and speciﬁc indicators of cultural ecosystem services by qualifying people’s opinions of local ecological resources.",,text,,,,,,53 ecological resources,37,opinions on ecological resources,,,,,"October 12, 2018",Gwangju (Gyeonggi-do) province in South Korea,citizen workshop,text mining,Participants were asked to name 25 ecological resources that they wished to recommend to others from among the ecological resources presented by the workshop coordinators and participants.,major noun keywords were selected via morphological analysis,"The most frequently used words were selected, and words with a frequency of 10% or more regarded as a high level of information strength, were extracted.",,,[],,factor analysis,,,,,morphological analysis,noun keywords,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['mountain climbing', 'rural experiences', 'cultural heritage']",[],[],[],[],[],"['management of local ecological resources', 'themes for local ecotourism planning']",[],['used text mining to connect the quantitative approach with the qualitative data (resident opinions)'],"['This study gathered various stories about ecological resources from residents and performed a factor analysis by keywords to identify the speciﬁc activities and local user activities related to the cultural ecosystem services of each major ecological resource, thereby unveiling how such activities may be interconnected.']",[],"['the inability to determine local user activities in a given area', 'the inability to identify how such activities can be standardized and used for comparisons with other regions']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,SPSS 22.0,,,,,
_Li and Dong-2025-Exploring the Mental Health Bene.txt,Exploring the Mental Health Benefits of Urban Green Spaces Through Social Media Big Data: A Case Study of the Changsha–Zhuzhou–Xiangtan Urban Agglomeration,"Li, Z.; Dong, T.",,2025,journal article,Sustainability,"MDPI, Basel, Switzerland",https://doi.org/10.3390/su17083465,https://doi.org/10.3390/su17083465,,,,,urban parks (6),,,,,Changsha–Zhuzhou–Xiangtan urban agglomeration,China; CZXUA; Changsha; Zhuzhou; Xiangtan; Belfast; UK; Denver; Colorado; Nanjing; Tabriz; Iran; Mountain Creek; Alabama; Philadelphia; Shijiazhuang,,COVID-19 pandemic,"However, these studies often lack a comprehensive exploration of the various elements within green spaces that influence residents’ mental health. Additionally, traditional data collection methods—such as questionnaires and telephone surveys—have limitations, including restricted sample populations, low efficiency during data collection, small sample sizes, limited real-time capabilities, and high subjectivity.",This study aims to use geographical information and social media data to analyze residents’ perceptions and emotional experiences in UGS. It establishes a methodology to examine the impact of various UGS elements on mental health.,Dianping.com; Baidu Maps; Google Maps; Weibo,user comments; text; geographical information; POI; AOI; reviews; NDVI; satellite images,geographic coordinates,spatial distribution,,rating,park name; username; review text; review date,"104,795",,"103,901 reviews",,,,,July 2023 to July 2024,62 UGSs in the CZXUA,API,"Baidu Maps’ API, Dianping.com’s API",Urban parks with fewer than 100 online reviews were excluded to ensure methodological rigor.,"After excluding empty, irrelevant, and duplicate reviews, a total of 103,901 reviews from July 2023 to July 2024 were selected for analysis.",small UGSs lacking data in digital maps and parks with fewer than 100 reviews on Dianping.com were excluded.,manual,,"['positive sentiment', 'negative sentiment']",,sentiment analysis; text analysis,,Naive Bayes model,,Word2Vec algorithm,,word frequency; word type,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"First, large amounts of textual data are labeled, followed by the extraction of text features, such as word frequency and word type. A Naive Bayes model is employed to train on these features, generating the probability of each feature with a specific sentiment category. Finally, these probabilities are aggregated to produce a sentiment score, classifying the text as either positive or negative",no,[],,,,,,,,,,Pearson correlation coefficients (PCCS); p-value,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],heatmap,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Since this study sources data from Dianping.com, its findings are limited by the user demographics of the website and may not fully represent all visitors to UGSs.', 'Due to data limitations, this study could not consider all potential factors influencing residents’ mental health, such as age [75], gender, income [76], education level [77], seasonal changes [78], and plant structure [79].', 'While big data from social media provide unprecedented scale for analyzing user perceptions, their use may carry risks of selection bias. Individuals posting online reviews may systematically differ from general park visitors in demographics or behavior, influenced by access to technology, motivation to engage publicly, or cultural norms. Consequently, emotional responses captured via this approach may inadequately represent all UGS users.']",,All data sources are described in Section 3.1. of the manuscript.,,,Python® 3.1.15,Snow NLP library; IBM SPSS® Statistics,,urban green space; social media data; mental health; urban agglomeration; points of interest (POIs); areas of interest (AOIs); user comments; sentiment analysis; geographic information data; structural equation model (SEM); landscape elements; environmental factors; residents’ emotions; natural elements; artificial elements; vegetation; water; roads; squares; buildings; urban parks; remote sensing technology; digital maps; landscape features; user experiences; service quality; cost-effectiveness; visitors’ emotions; lexicon construction; perceptions; emotional tendencies; social media reviews; primary data sources; Chinese lexicon; sentiment analysis library; Yangtze River; urbanization rate; green heart area; online reviews; Connswater Community Greenway (CCG); biopsychosocial pathways; emerging big data technologies; urban morphology; human behavior; environmental studies; Google Maps reviews; public perceptions; environmental justice; geolocation information; textual information; human-centered focus; deep learning technologies; machine learning; natural language processing (NLP); keyword analysis; cultural services; psychological well-being; data acquisition; data preprocessing; statistical analysis; Jieba; word segmentation tool; cultural ecosystem service elements; Python; Pearson correlation coefficients (PCCS); IBM SPSS® Statistics; confirmatory factor analysis; path analysis; measurement models; structural models; observed variables; latent variables; path diagrams; directional relationships; structural equations; probabilistic model; predictor; response variables; causal network; chi-square; degrees-of-freedom ratio; goodness-of-fit indices (GFI); incremental fit index (IFI); Tucker–Lewis index (TLI); comparative fit index (CFI); root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA); sentiment hotspots; sentiment cold spots; comprehensive parks; recreational parks; cultural relics parks; ecological parks; community parks; Origin 2021; transport conditions; availability; connectivity; size area; boundary shape; ecosystem cultural service elements; outdoor workouts; aesthetic appreciation; social interaction; history/culture; man-made structures; High Line Park; urban green corridor; landscaped pathway; recreational zones; hardscape elements; green infrastructure; artistic interventions; industrial heritage; adaptive reuse; urban fabric; community engagement; sustainable development; online platforms; offline survey data; selection bias; general park visitors; demographics; behavior; access to technology; motivation; cultural norms; emotional responses; UGS users; science-based planning; park managers; planners; landscape designers; policymakers; theoretical implications; native plants; local natural conditions; invasive alien species; biodiversity; habitats; birds; wildlife; multiple sensory experiences; sight; smell; hearing; touch; taste; interactive landscape designs; aesthetic preferences; emergency shelter; extreme weather; natural disasters; heatwaves; typhoons; heavy rains; earthquakes; barrier-free facilities; mobility disabilities; strollers; wheelchair users; walking aids; park inclusivity; green space equity,,,
_Li and Qiu-2024-Using Deep Learning Approaches t.txt,Article ABSTRACT:,Y.L.; B.Q.,,2024,journal article,Sustainability,MDPI,doi.org/10.3390/su16093602,https://doi.org/10.3390/su16093602,,,World Heritage Site (2); linear cultural heritage (4); cultural heritage (10),tangible; intangible,Chinese Grand Canal’s landscape; Yangzhou Ancient Canal (8); linear heritage site; heritage canal; historic districts; historic villages,heritage value (2); cultural interactions; authenticity; heritage site (11); cultural heritage protection (2); heritage channel (2); heritage sites (3); World Heritage; cultural relics; heritage corridor; heritage landscape (3); heritage of the Chinese Grand Canal (2); heritage composition; heritage protection plan; heritage of coastal settlements,,"{'Social': 'The study discusses the importance of public perception and cultural interactions related to the Chinese Grand Canal landscape, highlighting the emotional connection and cultural identity associated with the heritage site.'}; {'Historic': 'The paper emphasizes the historical significance of the Chinese Grand Canal and its role in connecting various geographical and cultural regions, indicating the educational and symbolic value of the heritage site.'}; {'Aesthetical': 'The research focuses on landscape preferences and visual elements, suggesting an appreciation for the artistic and notable aspects of the Chinese Grand Canal landscape.'}",conservation; protection (7); preservation (2); planning (7),Chinese Grand Canal; Yangzhou Ancient Canal (10); Slender West Lake; Tianning Temple; Rowan Palace; Chongning Temple; Ge Garden (5); Wanglumen Salt Merchant Residence; Lu Shaoxu Salt Merchant Residence; Yanzong Temple; Canal Sanwan Park; Dongguan Historical and Cultural Tourism Area (3); Nanmen Ruins Pier; Guanchao Road; Shashi River area; Sanwan Wetland Park (2); Dongguan Street (2),China (4); Yangzhou (7); Jiangsu Province (2); Beijing; Hangzhou; Inner Mongolia; Fujian Province; South China; Guang