_Li et al.-2025-Relationship Between Landscape C.txt,"Relationship Between Landscape Character and Public Preferences in Urban Landscapes: A Case Study from the East–West Mountain Region in Wuhan, China","Li, X.; Pang, W.; Han, L.; Yan, Y.; Pan, X.; Yang, D.",,2025,journal article,Land,"MDPI, Basel, Switzerland",doi.org/10.3390/land14061228,https://,,,Cultural Heritage (2),,urban landscapes (7); historic cities; historical landmarks; ancient structures,cultural heritage values (1); cultural resources (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study discusses public preferences and emotional responses to different landscape characters, indicating a focus on cultural identity and sense of place attachment. The EWMR integrates the dual attributes of ecological barrier and cultural heritage preservation.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment – The study mentions eco-tourism development and the surge in cultural service value, indicating the role of heritage assets in the contemporary market, especially tourism. The East Lake Scenic Area exemplifies this synergy: its cultural service value surged to CNY 2.09 billion by 2019, tripling since 2011 due to eco-tourism development'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Management – The study discusses the need for effective strategies for urban landscape planning and management that incorporate public subjective perceptions, thereby informing appropriate spatial planning measures to revitalize urban landscapes. Since 2019, Wuhan’s Cultural Greenway planning has been emphasizing holistic integration of natural and cultural resources, necessitating a bottom-up approach to align landscape management with public perspectives.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Historic-Artistic – The study mentions historical landmarks such as the Yangtze River Bridge and ancient structures on Guishan and Sheshan Mountains, indicating the presence of rare or unique examples of historical, stylistic, or artistic movements now considered historical. The East–West Mountain Region (EWMR) of Wuhan, as a crucial “urban–nature transition area”, integrates the dual attributes of ecological barrier and cultural heritage preservation.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study discusses the visual esthetic value and the subjective experiences elicited by landscape character within esthetic activities. Landscape preference results from the public’s assessment based on their perception of landscape characters'}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': 'Spiritual – The study mentions the harmony between the building and its natural/artificial environment. Urban natural mountains and lakes, as critical resources in urban environments, have been extensively studied for their ecological services, cultural heritage values, and recreational benefits across landscape planning, ecosystem service assessment, and historic conservation'}",historic conservation (1); landscape protection (1); landscape management (5); conservation (1); preservation (1),East–West Mountain Region (EWMR) of Wuhan; East Lake Scenic Area; Yangtze River Bridge; Guishan and Sheshan Mountains; Hubu Alley; Wuhan University; Guanggu Roundabout; East Lake Ecological Tourism Scenic Area; Yellow Crane Tower Park,China (4); Germany (1); United States (1); Wuhan (6); Phoenix; Berlin; Brandenburg; Wudang; Dahong Mountains; Yangtze River; Hongshan; Wuchang District,2015–2020,,This study aims to address the problems of landscape character degradation and weakened public preferences caused by rapid urbanization and proposes a research framework integrating landscape character assessment and public preferences.,"(1) to quantitatively assess the landscape character of urban landscapes and analyze their spatial distribution patterns; (2) to identify public perception hotspots and their spatial characteristics based on UGC net-work texts and NLP emotion analysis; (3) to investigate the associations between landscape character and public perception using quantitative analysis with the So IVES model and Arc GIS Pro 3.0, and provide a basis for urban landscape planning and management.",Weibo (4),text (3); geotagged posts (1),geographic locations (latitude and longitude) (1),spatial distribution (4); spatial clustering (2),timestamps (1),,user IDs (1); usernames (1),"20,002",,,,,,,,the EWMR in Wuhan,web crawler (1),Tencent Cloud API (1),,removing invalid information using Excel (1),"(1) news advertisements and explanatory texts disseminated by public accounts; (2) excessively brief texts consisting solely of locations, names, or pure emoticons; and (3) comments that mentioned the research region but were irrelevant to the study’s content.",,,[],,emotion analysis (5); clustering (5),Natural Language Processing (NLP) (5),,,,,Weibo text (1),,30% of emotional value points as test data,,,,,five times,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC) (2); Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) (4); Kaiser–Meyer–Olkin (KMO) (2),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,scenic nature landscapes; landscape character; public preferences; user-generated content; So IVES; urban landscape planning; urban landscape resources; EWMR; Natural Language Processing (NLP); GIS; Arc GIS Pro 3.0; K-means cluster analysis; Digital Elevation Model (DEM); Geospatial Data Cloud of the Chinese Academy of Sciences; Esri; Impact Observatory; Microsoft; Sentinel-2; Auto Navi Map; Mapping Essential Urban Land Use Categories in China (EULUC-China); Peng Gong; Bin Chen; Jieba Segmentation tool; Tencent Cloud API; Max Ent 3.4.1; Wudang; Dahong Mountains; Yangtze River; Guishan; Sheshan Mountains; Greenway network; East Lake Scenic Area; Cultural Greenway planning; urban–nature transition area; ecological barrier; cultural heritage preservation; ecological backbone; biodiversity; historical landmarks; urban landscape heterogeneity; sub-regional control planning; Optics Valley business area; Optics Valley Pedes