es on a historic international settlement, highlighting the importance of preserving and understanding its historical significance.; Economic: The study mentions the potential of social media analysis to inform decision-making in urban heritage conservation, which could have economic implications for tourism and development.; Scientific: The study employs computer vision and machine learning technologies to analyze social media data, indicating a scientific approach to understanding heritage preferences.",heritage conservation (6),"Kulangsu (6); Beijing’s bell and drum tower neighbourhood (1); Dashilar renewal project (1); urban-proximate parks of Boulder, Colorado, USA (1); Berlin (1); Camargue region in Southern France (1); Singapore (1)",Chinese (4); China (4); Europe (1); Xiamen (1); Colorado (1); USA (1); Southern France (1); Singapore (1),early 20th century (1),World War II (1),"However, engaging residents to cultural heritage sites and understanding their preferences through social media have not been well-evaluated and explored in the Chinese context.",The article offers a method to understand the residents’ preferences by identifying more than two thousand urban-heritage- linked images acquired from the dataset of Weibo.,Weibo (5); Flickr (2),images (10); texts (1); photographs (2),,,,,personal address (1),more than 1500 (1),,1500 (1),1931 (2),,,,the year 2020 (1),Kulangsu,Web Scraping tools (1),Python (1),"Local residents, as our targeted research group, were selected by an additional restriction on personal address. (1)","Repetitive images with highly similar contents were removed by resizing and matching. Also, images with different sizes and minor differences, such as images with watermarks, were considered the same. (1)",,manual (5),"two volunteers, one architectural professional and the other non-professional (1)",[],,image recognition (2); content-based image retrieval (CBIR) (1); labelling (1),,Google Cloud Vision (GCV) (10),trained by Google compan