_Lingua et al.-2022-Valuing cultural ecosystem servi.txt,Valuing cultural ecosystem services combining deep learning and benefit transfer approach,Federico Lingua; Nicholas C. Coops; Verena C. Griess,"Department of Forest Resources Management, Faculty of Forestry, University of British Columbia, Forest Sciences Centre, 2424 Main Mall, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z4, Canada; Institute of Terrestrial Ecosystems, Department of Environmental System Sciences, ETH Zurich CHN K72.2, Universit¨atstrasse 16, 8092 Zurich, Switzerland",2022,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier B V,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2022.101487,https://www.elsevier.com/locate/ecoser,,,,,,,,,,British Columbia; Canada; Cypress park; Lac du Bois park; Garibaldi; Blackcomb glacier parks; Shannon falls; Wells Grey; Little Qualicum falls; Brandywine falls; E.C. Manning; Mount Seymour,British Columbia; Canada; Alberta; Ontario; United States; United Kingdom; Australia; Germany; Iberian Peninsula; Northern Portugal; Southern Spain,,,"In this context, the overall objective of this study will be to bridge this knowledge gap, exploring the potential of social media image content analysis in characterizing and valuing CES provision.","To do so, images taken in British Columbia (Canada) provincial parks system will be retrieved from social media and analyzed by combining freely available pre-trained CNNs and purposely trained CNNs.",Flickr,images; metadata,geotagged images; coordinates,,timestamps,,image identification code; author identification code; date on which the image was taken; coordinates of where the image was taken,"1,398,737 images","4,923 authors",,"91,819",,,,January 2005 and December 2020,BC,Flickr crowdsourced images,Flickr API,,,,manual,authors,[],,classification; scene recognition,convolutional neural networks (CNN); deep learning neural network,Res Net-152,pre-trained on the Places365 database,,,digital images,,80/20,,ADAM algorithm,2 e-4,10,20,,,{},,Colab (Google Research),,,,,"residual neural network architecture, 152 layers deep, that employs residual learning units easing the training of deeper neural networks","In the training process a transfer learning approach was used, starting from the Res Net-152 architecture pre-trained on the Image Net Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge (ILSVRC) image classification and localization dataset.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; precision; recall; F1-score,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Data will be made available on request.,Data will be made available on request.,,,,Pytorch framework; fast.ai library,,,,,
_Lingua et al.-2023-Assessing forest recreational po.txt,Assessing forest recreational potential from social media data and remote sensing technologies data,Federico Lingua; Nicholas C. Coops; Verena C. Griess,"Department of Forest Resources Management, Faculty of Forestry, University of British Columbia, Forest Sciences Centre, 2424 Main Mall, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z4, Canada; Institute of Terrestrial Ecosystems, Department of Environmental System Sciences, ETH Zurich CHN K72.2, Universit¨atstrasse 16, 8092 Zurich, Switzerland",2023,journal article,Ecological Indicators,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2023.110165,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2023.110165,,,,,BC forested parks,Cultural Ecosystem Services (CES) (7); recreation (14),,,,BC’s provincial parks system; Tuscany (Italy); Zhejiang Province (China); Iberian Peninsula (Spain and Portugal); British Columbia (Canada); Cypress provincial park,Canada; British Columbia (BC); Tuscany (Italy); Zhejiang Province (China); Iberian Peninsula (Spain and Portugal); North America,,,"In particular little is known about how to transform the information that can be extracted from crowdsourced social media data into useful outputs for the inclusion of CES in ecosystem management plans. Furthermore, remote sensing data and the output of automated image content analysis have not often been combined before, and the potential of their integration is not been fully explored.","The overarching objective of this study, therefore, is to address these knowledge gaps, developing a methodology to effectively integrate CES in forest management plans, relying on remote sensing and crowdsourced social media data.",Flickr (10),text; images; video; metadata,geotagged images (4); geographic coordinates,,temporal trends,,image identification code; author identification code; date on which the image was taken; coordinates of where the image was taken,"1,398,737 images",,,"1,398,737",,,"91,819",2005/01/01 and 2020/12/31,BC,downloaded,Flickr’s API (6),,"classified and filtered based on the recreational activities depicted in them, using convolutional neural networks (CNNs)",,automated,,"['relevant images', 'not-relevant images', 'aesthetic ecosystem service', 'recreational ecosystem service', 'skiing', 'hiking', 'camping', 'wildlife viewing', 'biking']",,classification,convolutional neural networks (CNN) (7); deep-learning neural network,Res Net-152 (6),"pre-trained on the Image Net (Deng et al., 2009) and Places365 databases (Zhou et al., 2017), to new classification tasks",,,digital images,,30%/70%,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,"a residual neural network architecture, 152 layers deep, that employs residual learning units","The general idea of transfer learning is to fine-tune existing CNN to adapt it to a new classification task (Torrey and Shavlik, 2010). To do so, the last layers of the original CNN are discarded and replaced by new layers designed for the intended classification task. Lastly, the modified CNN was re-trained with a training set of images opportunely selected.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (2); precision; recall; F1-score (4),[],[],[],ANOVA test,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['plan and locate the implementation of forest recreational infrastructures such as hiking or biking trails', 'providing detailed insights into the potential costs of partial park closure or the impacts of forest disturbances']",[],[],[],[],"['social media users’ demographic characteristics could substantially differ from the ones of forest recreationists, causing the selection of a nonrepresentative sample', 'the various forest recreational activities could be characterized by the different frequencies of image acquisition', 'the crowdsourced benefit transfer approach that was used in this study does not allow the assessment of the value of recreation in areas in which no images were acquired', 'the possibility of freely accessing social media data is not granted, since changes in social media platform policies can happen abruptly', 'the share of relevant images among the ones gathered is only 4.6%']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Data will be made available on request.,Data will be made available on request.,,,,scikit-learn library,,forest management plans; cultural ecosystem services; remote sensing technologies; social media data; forest recreational potential; benefit transfer approach; random forest model; consumer surplus; sustainable forest management plans; public participatory GIS (PPGIS); natural capital; application programming interfaces (API); deep-learning; computer vision; transfer learning; Image Net; Places365 databases; Python application; bbox arguments; residual learning units; image database; Advanced Spaceborne Thermal Emission and Reflection Radiometer (ASTER); digital elevation model; Landsat satellite imagery; airborne laser scanning (ALS); k-Nearest Neighbor approach; surface reflectance composites; Landsat Thematic Mapper; Enhanced Thematic Mapper Plus imagery; Gini impurity,,,
_Littles et al.-2023-Recreational beneficiaries and t.txt,Recreational beneficiaries and the estuarine landscape: A final ecosystem goods and services assessment,Chanda Jones Littles; Nathaniel S. Lewis; Theodore H. De Witt; Matthew C. Harwell,,2023,journal article,,,https://doi.org/10.1080/26395916.2023.2276756,https://doi.org/10.1080/26395916.2023.2276756,,,,,coastal landscapes (1); estuarine landscapes (2),cultural ecosystem services (1); ecosystem-based management (2); nature-based tourism (1); recreational ecosystem services (2); recreational services (1),,,restoration (2),"Tillamook Bay, OR (10); Tampa Bay, FL (12)",Oregon (3); Florida (3); United States (2); Gulf of Mexico (1),,,"Typical indicators for recreational ecosystem services include income from tourism, the number of visitors to a location, travel costs and willingness to pay (Liquete et al. 2013). These can be helpful in regional planning but may not provide sufficient information about underlying habitat conditions that sustain human-use or are most valued.",This study aims to characterize the value associated with nature-based recreational opportu­ nities and identify estuarine attributes most valued by users.,Flickr (10),images (10); photos (10); metadata (3),geographic coordinates (2); geo-tagged (1),spatial distribution (1); spatial grid (1),monthly (1); yearly (1),,photograph identifier (2); date taken (2); hyperlink (2); location identifier (1),"1,384 (1)",,,,,,,2014–2017 (3),Tillamook and Tampa Bay estuaries (1),downloaded images directly from the Flickr platform (1),Flickr application programming interface (API) (2),sub-selected the ten grid cells with the highest average PUD for further Flickr image analysis (1),"The R packages ‘dplyr’, ‘lubridate’ and ‘stringr’ were used to clean and prep data out­ puts to facilitate subsequent evaluation (1)",limited results to photographs with confirmed geographic coordinates (1),evaluated and characterized based on image content (1),,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,