_Liu et al.-2022-Semantic segmentation and photog.txt,Semantic segmentation and photogrammetry for remote monitoring of built heritage sites using crowdsourced data,Z. Liu; R. Brigham; E. R. Ledesma; L. Wang; A. Fathi; S. A. Oreyzi; J. Gonzalez,"University College London, Central House, 14 Upper Woburn Place, London WC1H 0NN, UK; Historic Environment Scotland, Salisbury Place, Long‑ more House, Edinburgh EH9 1SH, UK",2022,journal article,,,,,,,heritage sites (2); built heritage sites (2); cultural heritage (4),built,built heritage sites (2); heritage sites (2); historic structures; historic stones; historic buildings; Bothwell Castle (4); medieval castle,authenticity; physical integrity; preservation (3); reconstruction; conservation (2); monitoring (7); damage detection (3); remote monitoring (2),,"{'Aesthetical': 'The paper mentions that deterioration in cultural heritage, such as plant growth, reduces the aesthetic value of the heritage sites. This implies a discussion of aesthetic values.'}; {'Historic-Artistic': 'The paper discusses the use of CNNs for documentation and damage detection in masonry walls, identifying spalling, cracks, and efflorescence in bricks at the Palace Museum in Beijing. This relates to the historic and artistic aspects of the site.'}",preservation (3); reconstruction; conservation (2); monitoring (7),Bothwell Castle (4),"Scotland (2); South Lanarkshire, Scotland",thirteenth century,sieges during the Wars of Independence,"extraction and analysis of crowdsourced imagery data becomes an issue for cultural heritage practitioners as a consequence of two features brought by crowdsourcing. First, due to the diversity of camera angles of crowdsourced images, properties (e.g. areas of objects) cannot be directly compared between distinctive images. Second, as crowdsourced images were taken by multifarious photographic equipment under different lighting conditions, the sizes and qualities of these images are various. As a result, objects within the images are difficult to be recognized automatically.",this article tests a combination of computer vision and photogrammetry to measure potentially damaging plant growth from crowdsourced images of built heritage sites.,Facebook; Twitter,photographs; videos; imagery data,,,,,,113 photographs,,,113,,,,January 2019 and March 2020,"Bothwell Castle in South Lanarkshire, Scotland",asked to submit their photographs via email or social media platforms,,,,,manually labelled,,"['window', 'sky', 'plant', 'masonry', 'hole', 'gate', 'signboard']",,classification; object detection; semantic segmentation,convolutional neural networks (CNNs); Deep Lab family models; dilated convolutional models,Alex Net; Deep Lab; Deeplab V3; Deeplab V3+,"backend models pre-trained on Pascal VOC 2012, SBD and Cityscapes datasets",,Scale-Invariant Feature Transform (SIFT),image pixels,,93 images and a test set (20 images),weighted cross-entropy loss function,Adam optimizer,0.01,six images,100,,"the color parameters (brightness, saturation, hue and contrast) of the training images are randomly changed within a small range when training the model to avoid overfitting",{},Model1,personal computer,NVIDIA RTX2070,,32G RAM,approximately 1 h,"Model architecture in terms of convolutional layers and pooling layers for each model used in this research is shown in Table 4, where square brackets represent processing blocks.","crowdsourced photos are collected and manually segmented to train one-stage and two-stage models. In the inference phase, the newly collected crowdsourced photos will be processed by the (one-stage or two-stage) image segmentation model and a photogrammetry model to obtain a segmented 3D model, which can be used for further estimation of area of interest",no,[],,,,,,,,,,Precision; Recall; Intersection over Union (Io U or Jaccard Index); F1-score; Mean Pixel Accuracy (MPA),"['Model3 has the best performance in all metrics over other models reflected by its overlall Io U of 66.9% (one-stage plant 54.6%, two-stage plant 56.2%), overall F1-score of 74.3% (one-stage plant\n69.6%, two-stage plant 70.5%) as well as overall MPA of\n84.4% (one-stage plant 94.3%, two-stage plant 93.7%).']",[],[],,"Model3’s prediction is smoother and able to detect small objects such as the plants at the left-upper corner in Fig. 8. Therefore, Model3 is chosen as the default model for this task.",[],[],"the precision rate is consistently lower than the recall rate. This indicates that the models tend to aggressively predict pixels to the plant class, resulting in a large number of FPs to lower the precision rate.",[],[],[],randomly split into training set (93 images) and test set (20 images),Model1,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['monitor other damages in historical sites, such as cracks, erosion and insects based on 2D image data', 'semantic masking tool in photogrammetry workflows and applications']",['significantly save human and capital resources spent on on-site inspection and management of remote built heritage sites.'],"['proposes to use Deeplab V3+, a deep-learning based semantic image segmentation\nalgorithm that is capable of handling segmentation tasks in complex scenarios. It is further combined with photogrammetry to automatically process the crowdsourced photographs.']",[],[],"['difficulty in small objects detection', 'large number of FPs in the predictions, which may lead to an overestimation of the plant area', 'the requirement of photographs with comprehensive angles taken within a specific period of time']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],code can be found at [63],Please contact the corresponding author for data requests.,,,"Py Torch as the framework of the image segmentation models, combined with its associate computer vision library Torchvision and an implementation of Deeplab V3+ [58]",Open CV,,crowdsourced data; remote equipment; citizen scientists; visitors; erosion; cracks; plant growth; physical damage; aesthetic value; computer vision; photogrammetry; convolutional neural networks (CNNs); image classification; object detection; semantic segmentation; spalling; efflorescence; bricks; Palace Museum in Beijing; multi scale image segmentation; polygonal stones; castellated walls; stone management database; mould; deterioration; ventilation systems; historic libraries; missing decorative tiles; ancient roofs; yellowglazed roof tiles; Image J; Easy Leaf Area; Leaf-IT; terrestrial laser scanning (TLS); 3D models; point clouds; architectural elements; laser scans; Monument Monitor; Institute of Sustainable Heritage; Labelbox; Alex Net; Image Net; Deep Lab family models; FCN; U-Net; Mask R-CNN; Deep Lab family; Deeplabv; Atrous Spatial Pyramid Pooling (ASPP); Res Net101; Deeplab V3+; Xception network; density-based spatial clustering of applications with noise (DBSCAN); Structure from Motion (Sf M); Scale-Invariant Feature Transform (SIFT); bundle adjustment; Patch-Match algorithm; Open CV; Visual SFM; OpenMVS; Torchvision; Adam optimizer; Pascal VOC 2012; SBD; Cityscapes datasets,,,
_Liu et al.-2023-Measuring the impact of COVID-19.txt,Measuring the impact of COVID-19,Ziwen Liu; Sean A. Orr; Pushpi Kumar; Javier Grau-Bove,,2023,journal article,,Springer Nature,,http://creativecommons.org/ licenses/by/4.0/.,,,,,,heritage sites (4); heritage tourism (3); heritage site management,,"Social: Emotional (collective) – The study discusses visitor sentiment towards COVID-19 preventive measures, indicating a sense of communal value related to safety and well-being at heritage sites.; Political: Management – The paper explores how COVID-19 measures impact heritage site management, reflecting the incorporation of the asset into political strategies and policies.; Economic: Entertainment – The research examines the impact of COVID-19 on visitor numbers and the tourism industry, highlighting the role of heritage sites in the contemporary market, especially tourism.",heritage management (2); sustainable heritage management strategies,,UK (6); England; Northern Ireland; Scotland; Wales; Europe,,COVID-19 pandemic (20),This paper reveals several empirical ﬁndings that can inform the management of heritage prepared at the national level to understand the efﬁcient allocation of resources.,"In this study, we collected user review data for 775 sites on Google Maps and analyzed it using state-of-the-art machine learning methods to detect and quantify the impact of COVID-19 on heritage tourism in the UK, aiming to (1) help the recovery of heritage tourism in the UK during the “post-COVID” era and summarizing lessons for the heritage tourism in preparation for the next potential severe public health emergency, and (2) showcase the efﬁcacy of advanced machine learning techniques in interpreting unstructured data for potential relevant future research.",Google Maps (4); Twitter,text (2); comments (13); reviews (4),,,monthly counts; trends over time,,,~1.4 million reviews,,"15,300 comments",,,,,January 2016 to April 2022,689 sites,collected user review data,,,,We only used English data in our analysis.,,,[],,sentiment analysis (4); text classiﬁcation,neural network models,"BERT (2); PEGASUS (Zhang et al., 2020)",fine-tuned on the reddit_tifu dataset,,bag of words; lexicon-based methods,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,"Summary generated from the model for negative comments of wearing face coverings: “had to wear a mask to escape the crowds of people not wearing masks."" Summary generated from the model for negative comments of social distancing: “didn’t follow COVID-19 social distancing guidelines and had to walk around with no social distancing.”",[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],['this paper reveals visitor sentiment towards different measures taken in response to the COVID-19 pandemic.'],"['urban/rural perspective', 'indoor/outdoor']",[],[],"['enhanced support policies and ﬁnancial planning for urban, indoor sites that heavily rely on international tourism', 'effective and consistent enforcement of COVID-19 preventive measures by both staff and visitors is crucial in maintaining a positive visitor experience during a pandemic.', 'Areas should be kept as accessible as possible while ensuring safety to reduce visitor disappointment.']",['advanced machine learning techniques used in this study to extract information from online reviews have demonstrated their effectiveness in determining visitor perceptions towards speciﬁc facets of heritage sites.'],[],[],"['Ambiguity in language is a concern.', 'The diversity of topics is also an important consideration.']",[],"['the data may contain more opinions from young visitors or people who are proﬁcient in using social media platforms.', 'we only used\nEnglish data in our analysis. This may cause a bias toward opinions from native\nEnglish speakers and ignore some international non-native English speakers who account for a signiﬁcant part of the consumers of heritage tourism in the UK.']",[],[],[],[],[],"['Despite the limitation of ambiguity in language, it is important to note that this challenge is not unique to the method proposed in this paper.', 'However, with unstructured data like user reviews, some topics may be more prevalent than others.', 'researchers cannot ask speciﬁc questions that may be important for their analysis.']",,"The research data generated and analyzed during the study are available from https://zenodo.org/record/8130804 (Liu et al., 2023).",,https://zenodo.org/record/8130804,,,,visitor involvement; visitor experience; visitor sentiment,,,
_Liu et al.-2025-Evaluation of Cultural Ecosystem.txt,Evaluation of Cultural Ecosystem Services in Macau Urban Parks Based on Social Media Data,K.L.; P.W.; J.L.,,2025,journal article,,,,https://www.openstreetmap.org,,,Cultural Heritage (7),cultural (3),Macau urban parks (6); urban parks (21); urban green spaces (4); Historic Centre of Macau (2),cultural heritage (10); cultural ecosystem services (CES) (29); cultural diversity (2); cultural heritage value (2); cultural heritage conservation (4); cultural services (16); cultural heritage elements (3); UNESCO World Heritage site (1); cultural interpretation services (1); cultural transmission (1); cultural expression (1); cultural heritage resources (1); cultural landscape designs (1); historical narrative exhibitions (1); local cultural symbols (1); heritage transmission functions (1); cultural value (1); user identification (1); historical and cultural resources (1); ecological conservation (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': ""Emotional (collective) – The study highlights the importance of landscape experience and social interaction in enhancing visitors' overall experiences and satisfaction in Macau's urban parks, indicating a sense of place attachment and communal value.""}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': ""Educational – The study emphasizes the role of urban parks in Macau as carriers of local cultural heritage, providing opportunities for public education and knowledge dissemination about the city's history and culture.""}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': ""Artistic – The study identifies esthetic appreciation as a core dimension of cultural services in Macau's urban parks, with parks that effectively integrate natural landscapes with cultural heritage elements providing strong visual appeal and emotional resonance.""}",cultural heritage conservation (4); heritage-related functions (1),Macau Peninsula (5); Macau (21); Shanghai (1); New York City (1); Guia Hill Park (3); Lou Lim Ieoc Garden (2); Mount Fortress Park (1); Vasco da Gama Garden (2); Song Yusheng Park (2); Areia Preta Park (1); Montanha Russa Municipal Park (3); Monte Fort Park (4); Hac Sa Van Park (2); Dr. Sun Yat Sen Municipal Park (2),China (4); Guangdong Province (1); Hong Kong (1); Europe (1); North America (1),,,"However, current research on Cultural Ecosystem Services (CES) in Macau’s urban parks remains fragmented. Existing studies mainly employ questionnaires, interviews, and behavioral observations [10]. These methods are insufficient to comprehensively capture the dynamic characteristics and spatial distribution patterns of cultural services. In the current era of rapid development in information technology and big data, traditional research methods are limited by inadequate data coverage and timeliness. These limitations restrict the systematic quantification and spatial analysis of cultural ecosystem services provided by urban parks.","Therefore, this study aims to establish a quantitative evaluation system of CES based on social media data from a cultural heritage perspective. This system is utilized to systematically assess the characteristics and spatial distribution of cultural ecosystem services in 12 major urban parks on the Macau Peninsula. Natural language processing techniques and spatial analysis methods are combined to extract emotional expressions and spatial characteristics from user-generated content. This approach highlights differences in CES dimensions among Macau’s urban parks and identifies the factors influencing these differences. This study provides scientific support for urban park planning and cultural heritage conservation in Macau. Additionally, it offers a novel theoretical and practical framework for future quantitative CES research.",social media platforms (5); Dianping platform (1); Twitter (1); Flickr (1); Google Maps (2); Trip Advisor (1); Apple Store (1),social media data (11); text (2); images (3); geographical tags (1); user comments (3); multimedia interaction (3); sensor data (1),,spatial distribution (7); spatial analysis (6); spatial autocorrelation analysis (3); spatial clustering (3); spatial configurations (1); spatial heterogeneity (1),temporal resolution (1); time-series data (1),ratings (2); stars (1),usernames (1); review dates (1),"over 26,000 valid text entries",,,,,,,between 2020 and 2024,12 urban parks in Macau,web-crawling techniques (2),Octoparse software 8 (1),,"manual proofreading (1), removing special symbols, formatting tags, and meaningless characters (1)","comments unrelated to cultural ecosystem services (CES), such as those about transportation or dining, were removed (1). Second, duplicate entries, irrelevant content, and promotional comments were excluded (1)",classified systematically (1),research team consisting of five experienced researchers (1),[],Multiple rounds of evaluation and feedback were conducted. Ambiguous or unclear terms were carefully reviewed and reclassified by the research team until a consensus was reached (1),sentiment analysis (6); cluster analysis (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],Radar chart,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['This research provides a novel CES quantification method based on social media data. It also offers theoretical and practical frameworks for urban planning and cultural heritage conservation in Macau.'],[],[],[],[],"['Experiences of elderly users and infrequent visitors may therefore be underestimated, leading to biases in the coverage of different population groups.']",[],[],[],[],[],"['First, the representativeness of social media data is limited.', 'Second, the specific roles of urban facilities within parks (such as benches, walkways, and lighting) were not deeply examined.', 'Finally, the unstructured characteristics of textual data and existing limitations in sentiment analysis techniques restrict the in-depth analysis of intangible cultural services (such as inspiration and sense of place).']",,The original contributions presented in this study are included in the article.,,,Python 3.12 (1),Arc GIS pro software (1); SPSS 27 software (2),,User-generated content (UGC) (7); natural language processing (NLP) (7); web-crawling techniques (2); sentiment scores (4); emotional expressions (2); spatial analysis methods (1); multimedia interaction techniques (1); ecological services (1); social interaction functions (1); social media data mining (1); multimedia interactive evaluation techniques (1); ecological exhibition paths (1); interactive educational activities (1); digital science content (1); virtual reality (VR) (1); augmented reality (AR) (1); digital guided tours (1); dynamic crowd control measures (1); ecological conservation (1); time-series data (1),,,
_Lo Presti and Carli-2021-Italian Catacombs and Their Digi.txt,Italian Catacombs and Their Digital Presence: A Proposal for Long-Term Sustainability,Ornella Lo Presti; Maria Rita Carli,,2021,journal article,Sustainability,,,,,,Cultural Heritage (7),tangible (2); intangible (2),catacombs of Italy (3); underground cultural heritage (2); underground built heritage (2); historic centers (1),heritage conservation (2); long-term sustainability (3); preventive conservation (1); cultural sustainability (3); world heritage sites (1); material heritage (1); sustainable development (5); conservation (3); preservation (3); enhancement (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study emphasizes the importance of cultural heritage for local communities, highlighting its role in fostering identity and relationships within the territory. This aligns with the emotional (collective) value, as it underscores the communal value and sense of place attachment associated with heritage sites.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment – The paper discusses the potential of virtual and augmented reality to enhance the visitor experience, suggesting a focus on tourism and engagement. This relates to the entertainment value, as it highlights the role of heritage assets in attracting tourists and generating economic activity.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Educational – The study proposes the use of virtual technologies to promote awareness of the past and local history, particularly for schools. This aligns with the educational value, as it emphasizes the potential of heritage assets to provide knowledge and promote cultural understanding.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Archaeological – The study focuses on catacombs, which are ancient underground cemeteries, indicating a connection to ancient civilizations. This aligns with the archaeological value, as it highlights the historical significance and connection to past societies.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study mentions the potential of virtual reality to reconstruct 3D environments, suggesting an appreciation for the artistic and visual aspects of heritage sites. This aligns with the artistic value, as it highlights the creative and imaginative elements associated with cultural heritage.'}; {'SCIENTIFIC VALUES': 'Technological – The study emphasizes the use of digital technologies, virtual reconstructions, and augmented reality for the preservation and dissemination of heritage. This aligns with the technological value, as it highlights the application of advanced techniques and materials in heritage conservation.'}; {'AGE VALUES': 'Existential – The study aims to preserve culture for future generations, emphasizing the importance of transferring knowledge and awareness of the past. This aligns with the existential value, as it represents memory and the lives of past generations.'}",heritage conservation (2),catacombs of Italy (2); Rome (7); Naples (1),Italy (7); Vatican City (2); Lazio region (4); Sicily (4); Campania (4); Tuscany (5); Abruzzo (4); Apulia (3); Sardinia (3); Umbria (4); central Italy (2); southern islands in Italy (1); Sicily (2); Sardinia (2),second and the beginning of the third century AD (1),COVID-19 pandemic (2),"there are still very few studies on cultural sustainability below ground. Moreover, research on the use of multimedia and interactive technologies as a strategic variable for a sustainable use of cultural heritage is still in its infancy.","The work presented here focuses on the analysis of the digital presence in the catacombs of Italy, an example of underground cultural heritage among the most exposed to the risk of deterioration over time and in urgent need of interventions for its sustainability.",Facebook (4); Instagram (4); Twitter (4); You Tube (7); Google Arts and Culture (5); Trip Advisor (3); Google Reviews (4); Google Maps (2),multimedia (5); interactive technologies (2); virtual reality (7); augmented reality (7); 3D technologies (3); virtual reconstructions (2); photographic overview (1),,,,reviews (7); comments (3),,,,,,,,,between the 21 May 2021 and the 21 July 2021 (1),63 Christian catacombs in Italy (1),analysis,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['virtual access to local communities and tourists (1)', 'transferring their value to future generations (1)', 'visit support for closed and deteriorating sites (1)', 'educational courses for schools in the area aimed at training them to trigger a process of awareness of the past, their identity and local history (1)']","['making its conservation sustainable in the long term (1)', 'better management of information (1)', 'radical change in the management of assets that could be made ‘visitable’ without the use of staff (1)']",[],[],[],"['almost total absence of 3D technologies, virtual reconstructions or augmented reality (1)', 'sites closed to the public are also those that offer fewer opportunities for multimedia visits (1)', 'states of disappointment and the consequent mechanism of detachment in the visitor are obvious and could certainly be remedied by the use of virtual technologies that would allow for better management of information and a radical change in the management of assets that could be made ‘visitable’ without the use of staff (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Not applicable (1),,,,,,digital presence (4); multimedia tours (2); virtual heritage (4); immersive storytelling (1); digital technologies (3); virtual visit (1); virtual technologies (2); multimedia and interactive technologies (1); virtual and immersive digital environment (1),,,
_Loke et al.-2022-Heritage site-seeing through the.txt,Heritage site-seeing through the visitor’s lens on Instagram,Tania Loke; Yayoi Teramoto; Chico Q. Camargo; Kathryn Eccles,"Oxford Internet Institute, University of Oxford; Oxford Brookes University; Computer Science, University of Exeter",2022,journal article,Journal of Cultural Analytics,,https://doi.org/10.22148/001c.38966,,English,,cultural heritage (2),,historic sites (2); historic buildings; monuments; places; prehistoric sites; medieval castles; Roman forts; stately houses; ruins of numerous abbeys; priories; villages; heritage sites (13); unstaffed heritage sites (5); Rollright Stones (5); Castlerigg Stone Circle (2); Rufford Abbey (5); Reculver Towers and Roman Fort (5); Bury St Edmunds Abbey (5),cultural heritage (2); historic sites (2); historic buildings; monuments; places; prehistoric sites; medieval castles; Roman forts; stately houses; ruins of numerous abbeys; priories; villages; heritage sites (13); unstaffed heritage sites (5); English Heritage (4); conservation; protection; site characteristics; site-specific features (3),,,,English Heritage sites; Rollright Stones (5); Castlerigg Stone Circle (2); Rufford Abbey (5); Reculver Towers and Roman Fort (5); Bury St Edmunds Abbey (5),UK (2); United Kingdom (2); China (2); United States; Finland; South Africa; Zaragoza; Shiraz (2); Melbourne (2); Barcelona; Australia; Venice; Hong Kong; England,Roman,,"As such, there is little information about visitor attendance and behaviour in those sites—a challenge common to other non-ticketed heritage sites.",This study aims to improve our understanding of cultural behaviour in unstaffed heritage sites using publicly available Instagram data.,Instagram (24); Flickr (7); Twitter (2); Pinterest (3); 500px,images (11); photographs (33); posts (16); reviews; comments (3); metadata (8); text-based UGC; image-based UGC (3); CSV file,,spatial clustering analysis,temporal metadata; temporal patterns (3); Unix timestamp;