,[],[],['audience segmentation tools'],[],"['the present methodology can be readily applied in other contexts (be they different destinations, collections of images, or social media platforms) to understand how visitors engage with tourist destinations through their photographs.']",[],[],"['Firstly, our analysis neglected image captions.', 'Secondly, results from object detection included false-positive and falsenegative predictions.', 'Thirdly, our analysis of visitors’ performativity in their photographs drew upon illustrative examples that may not be representative of all images in which people and salient site features co-occur.', 'Finally, this study’s analysis of Instagram data is removed from the physical and social contexts creating these posts.']","['When displaying these data, we de-identify images to protect users.']","['Gender and age biases exist amongst Instagram users', 'this study’s findings concern a specific subset of visitors and cannot be generalised to all who visit the heritage sites under study.']","['Following the University of Oxford’s Central University Research Ethics Committee best practice guidance for Internet-based research, users’ specific consent is not required when collecting their publicly available Instagram posts.']",[],[],[],[],[],Available from https://github.com/tensorflow/models/tree/master/research/object_detection (accessed 22 February 2022).,Data Repository https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/OGAMMY,,https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/OGAMMY,,Tensorflow,,visitor behaviour (3); tourist behaviour (2); visitor engagement (3); tourist engagement; posting activity (4); photograph user-days (PUD) (4); object categories (10); site features (4); site characteristics (3); visual content; visual attributes; visual characteristics; visual representation; tourist gaze (4); self-presentation (5); strategic impression management; extended selves (3); performativity (6); vernacular tourist photography; online tourist photography; tourist photography (