_Lu et al.-2025-Cultural ecosystem services in C.txt,Cultural ecosystem services in China’s national parks and their impact on,Juan Lu; Huiying Zhang; Xingnian Zhang,"College of Management and Economics, Tianjin University, Tianjin, China; College of Politics and Public Administration, Qinghai Nationalities University, China",2025,journal article,Journal for Nature Conservation,Elsevier Gmb H,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnc.2025.126969,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnc.2025.126969,,,,,national parks (29),Cultural ecosystem services (CES) (20); cultural heritage (2); natural heritage (1); ecosystem protection (1); biodiversity conservation (2); protected areas (3); conservation agenda (1); science popularisation (2); publicity (1); management (6),ecological value (10); educational value (10); social value (13); cultural value (13),"{'SOCIAL': 'The study discusses social values by highlighting the role of national parks in deepening the connection between society and families, enhancing human well-being, and shaping cultural identity.'}; {'ECONOMIC': 'The study implicitly discusses economic values by mentioning the synergistic development of economy and ecology through the establishment of national parks and the assessment of their economic value and tourists’ willingness to pay.'}; {'POLITICAL': 'The study discusses political values by mentioning the role of national parks in policy management and the integration of cultural heritage with natural landscapes in Chinese parks.'}; {'HISTORIC': 'The study implicitly discusses historic values by referencing the integration of cultural heritage with natural landscapes in Chinese parks, reflecting the diversity and complexity of interactions between landscapes, cultural backgrounds, and society.'}; {'AESTHETICAL': 'The study discusses aesthetic values by mentioning the aesthetic experiences derived from human-ecosystem interactions and the emphasis on aesthetic and recreational value in U.S. national parks.'}; {'SCIENTIFIC': 'The study implicitly discusses scientific values by mentioning the science popularisation and publicity efforts in national parks and the use of natural language processing and machine learning algorithms for data analysis.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL': 'The study discusses ecological values by highlighting the role of national parks in biodiversity conservation, provision of ecosystem services, and the synergistic development of economy and ecology.'}",,China’s national parks (10); Three-River-Source National Park (3); Northeast China Tiger and Leopard National Park (7); Giant Panda National Park (7); Wuyishan National Park (7); National Park of Hainan Tropical Rainforest (8); U.S. national parks (3),China (10); United States (2); South African (1); Jilin Province (2); Heilongjiang Province (1); Sichuan Province (2); Shanxi Province (1); Gansu Province (1); Fujian Province (2); Jiangxi (1); Hainan Island (1); Hainan Province (1),,,"However, the use of social media data in CES research remains underexplored, with challenges such as data representativeness, quality, and methodological complexity limiting its potential. Therefore, how to convert social media data into specific policy recommendations or management measures still requires further exploration.",(1) What are the main types of CES provided to the public through Douyin short videos? (2) Whether and how CES types affect public online engagement?,Douyin (16); Tik Tok (2); You Tube (1); Twitter (3); Trip Advisor (2); Facebook (2); Weibo (2); We Chat (2),text (4); visuals (1); audio (1); short videos (14),,,time stamps (1); posting time (1); tourism seasonality (5); seasonal differences (1); time trend effects model (1),likes (8); collections (6); comments (6); shares (7); views (1),account names (1); video length (2); tags (5); post date (1),630 Douyin short videos,"broad, diverse audience",,,630,,,between 3 September 2020 and 29 March 2024,"four national parks: the Northeast China Tiger and Leopard, the Chengdu Giant Panda, the Wuyishan, and the National Park of Hainan Tropical Rainforest",Python crawling techniques,Python (3),"The dataset includes all available videos, ensuring completeness and minimizing bias, with themes covering natural landscapes, cultural heritage, recreational activities, and wildlife.","Data checks revealed no missing values, duplicates, or outliers. The post date was converted to datetime, and numerical data (likes, collections) to integers. Video titles and account names were kept as strings, and video length was standardized to seconds.",,manual (1),three authors,"['ecological value', 'educational value', 'social value', 'cultural value']",,topic modeling (4); text analysis (1); natural language processing (NLP) (3),Transformer models (1),BERT (3); BERTopic (11); Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) (1),pre-trained bert-base-Chinese language model (2),embedding vectors (1); embedded representations (1); document vectors (1),classbased term frequency-inverse document frequency (c-TF-IDF) algorithm (2),text (1),,,,,,,,,,"{'UMAP model': 'n_neighbors = 15; n_components = 5; min_dist = 0.0; metric=’cosine’; random_state = 42', 'HDBSCAN model': 'min_cluster_size = 10; min_samples = 1; prediction_data = True', 'BERTopic model': 'diversity = 0.8'}",,,,,,,,"The BERTopic model uses powerful context embedding and the classbased term frequency-inverse document frequency (c-TF-IDF) algorithm to extract different topics and corresponding probabilities for each article (Raman et al., 2024). In this study, the text for topic modeling consists of the titles of the short video (with tags), and the main steps are as follows.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],"The first-order autocorrelation (AR (1)) test yields p-values below 0.05, signifying significant first-order autocorrelation, inherent in dynamic panel models.; The second-order autocorrelation (AR (2)) test shows p-values above 0.05, suggesting no over-autocorrelation.; Sargan test p-values also exceed 0.05, confirming the validity of instrumental variables and model appropriateness.",,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['environmental education (1)', 'sustainable tourism (1)', 'ecological conservation (1)']","['policy (1)', 'management strategies (2)']","['This study innovatively combines BERTopic modelling and panel data fixed effects model analysis methods to analyse the problem, providing a technical way that can be used for CES identification and impact analysis, and demonstrating the potential of social media data in CES research.']","['The results showed that ecological and educational value types played a significant moderating role in promoting public online engagement, and the impact of cultural value showed a lagged effect.', 'The peak tourist season (MayOctober) significantly enhanced the positive effects of environmental and cultural values on public online engagement.']","['Theoretical contributions of this research include providing unique insights into the relationship between CES types and public online engagement, enhancing the understanding of public participation and CES theory in the digital age.']","['First, the limitation of the data sources. This study mainly focuses on the short video data from the Douyin platform.', 'Second, the limitations of the influencing factors. This study mainly focuses on the types of national park CES and controls the characteristic factors of short video format.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Data will be made available on request.,Data will be made available on request.,,,,Jieba library in Python,,Common International Classification of Ecosystem Services (CICES) V5.1 (5); public online engagement (13); fixed effects model (2); panel data (3); information-behaviour’ framework (2); tourism seasonality (5); Consumer Price Index (CPI) (4); pro-environmental behaviour (1); Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) (2); dynamic panel data model (1); Least Squares Dummy Variable (LSDV) method (1); Difference Generalised Moment Estimator (Difference GMM) (1); Bootstrap resampling (1); Qingbo Index-Douyin Communication Power Index (1),,,
_Lu et al.-2025-Exploring the impact of landscap.txt,OPEN ACCESS ABSTRACT:,Zekun Lu; Shunhe Chen; Chao Qiu; Yuchen Lin; Yichen Lu; Rongxiang Chen; Ying Xu,,2025,journal article,"Computers, Environment and Urban Systems",,,https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0329118.g001,,,Cultural Landscape (1),,Coastal National Parks (24); marine protected areas (MPAs) (1); marine parks (2); marine special protected area (1); marine ecological and cultural resources (1); marine natural landscapes (3); marine theme parks (1); coastal landscapes (1); traditional villages (1); marine ecosystems (2),ecological protection (3); conservation (4); ecological conservation (2); biodiversity restoration (1); sustainable tourism (3); sustainable development (5); marine environment (3); ecological degradation (1); tourism resources (1); Environmental Psychology (2); Emotional Geography (2); marine biodiversity (1); marine resources (1); marine tourism (2); marine natural landscape systems (1),,"{'SOCIAL': ""Emotional (collective) – The study highlights the importance of landscape environments in Coastal National Parks for tourists' emotional responses, indicating a connection to cultural identity and a sense of place attachment.""}; {'ECONOMIC': ""Entertainment – The study focuses on the impact of landscape environments on tourists' emotions, suggesting that the aesthetic and recreational aspects of these parks contribute to their economic value through tourism.""}; {'POLITICAL': ""Management – The study aims to provide practical insights for optimizing landscape planning to enhance tourists' emotional experiences, indicating a focus on incorporating the asset into political strategies and policies related to tourism and environmental management.""}; {'HISTORIC': 'Educational – The study mentions the role of Coastal National Parks in integrating public functions such as scientific research and environmental education, suggesting a potential for these parks to provide knowledge about the past and natural history.'}; {'AESTHETICAL': 'Artistic – The study focuses on the visual and linguistic cues that influence tourist emotions, suggesting that the artistic and aesthetic qualities of the landscape environment play a significant role in shaping emotional responses.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL': 'Spiritual – The study emphasizes the importance of natural marine landscapes in alleviating stress and enhancing health, suggesting a harmony between the built environment and its natural environment.'}",ecological protection (3); resource management (1); landscape planning (3); ecological conservation (2); tourism planning (2); visitor management (1); ecological capacity assessments (1); environmental education (2); sustainable marine tourism practices (1); conservation (4),Fujian Province (5); Port-Cros National Park in France (1); Cape Lookout National Seashore in the United States (1); Machalilla National Park in Ecuador (1); Taean Coast National Park (1); Fuyao Islands (2); Changle (5); Xiamen (5); Chengzhou Island (2); Chongwu (5); Meizhou Island (4); Pingtan Island (3),France (1); United States (1); Ecuador (1); Korea (1); China (7); Fujian Province (5); East China Sea (1); Mediterranean region (1); North African (1); Thailand (1),,,"In summary, the relationship between tourists’ emotional responses and spatial environments in Coastal National Parks remains underexplored, particularly regarding the mechanisms linking built environment features and emotional feedback from a multimodal data perspective.","Therefore, this study focuses on representative coastal national marine parks in Fujian Prov­ ince and proposes the following research objectives: First, to construct a multimodal data analysis framework by integrating user-generated images and textual content, extracting tourists’ emotional responses to landscape environments, and devel­ oping a perceptual system for analyzing emotion–space relationships. Second, to identify key elements of the built environ­ ment by using the Light GBM model to quantify spatial variables affecting tourist emotions, and applying the SHAP method to interpret each variable’s relative contribution. Finally, to examine the mechanisms between spatial environments and emo­ tional responses by analyzing how different types of built environments in coastal parks shape tourists’ emotional fluctuations, thereby offering theoretical and practical insights for emotion-oriented spatial planning and management.",Weibo (1); Mafengwo (4); Ctrip (5); Dianping (4),text (10); images (16); videos (1); reviews (4); ratings (1); metadata (2); UGC (10); spatial data (4); remote sensing data (1),,spatial relationships (1); spatial distribution (6); spatial heterogeneity (1); spatial correlation (1),,ratings (1),,"3,914 data records (1)","over 100 million users (1), 18.9 million daily active users (1), over 400 million users (1)","3,758 valid records (1)","3,758 valid entries (2)",,,,August 2019 and August 2024 (1),seven national-level Coastal National Parks in Fujian Province (4),web scraping techniques (2),Python-based web scraping techniques (1),,"deduplication, formatting, outlier handling, and noise removal (1)","156 invalid or low-quality entries—such as duplicates, empty comments, or irrelevant content—were removed (1)",,,[],,sentiment analysis (5); semantic segmentation (3); regression (2); prediction (7),machine learning (12); natural language processing (NLP) (4); semantic segmentation models (1); Gradient Boosting Decision Tree (GBDT) (4); Light Gradient Boosting Machine (Light GBM) (10); Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) (3); Robustly Optimized BERT Approach (Ro BERTa) (3); Pyramid Scene Parsing Network (PSPNet) (6); Res Net-101 (1),BERT (3); Ro BERTa (3); Light GBM (10); PSPNet (6); ERNIE (2); SKEP (2); Res Net-101 (1); XGBoost (1),"pretrained on Image Net (1), fine-tuned on the ADE20K dataset (1)",,,text data (3); image data (10); environmental indicators (1); visual elements (1); spatial variables (1); natural landscape features (1),,,,SGD (1),initial learning rate = 0.001 (1),,60 epochs (1),weight decay = 0.0001 (1),random scaling (1); flipping (1); cropping (1),{'momentum': '0.9 (1)'},,,,,,,"PSPNet incorporates a Pyramid Pooling module that extracts both global and local contextual information at multiple scales, enabling better understanding of semantic content in images and improving segmentation performance (1)","This study employs NLP models trained using Baidu’s ERNIE multimodal pretraining technology, including the BERT and the Ro BERTa architectures (1)",no,[],,,,,,,,,,mean Intersection over Union (m Io U) (1); pixel accuracy (1); coefficient of determination (R²) (1); root mean square error (RMSE) (1); mean absolute error (MAE) (1); Spearman correlation coef (1),"['m Io U) of 43.3% (1)', 'pixel accuracy of 80.8% (1)', 'R²\u2009=\u20090.9897 (1)', 'RMSE\u2009=\u20090.0300 (1)', 'MAE\u2009=\u20090.0143 (1)', 'Spearman correlation coef (ρ\u2009=\u20090.9011, p\u2009<\u20090.0001) (1)', 'Spearman R² of 0.8120 (1)', '87.06% of overall emo\xad tions were positive, while 12.93% were negative (1)']",[],[],p < 0.0001 (1),,[],[],,[],[],[],,FCN (1); Deep Lab (1); PSPNet (1),[],kernel density analysis (3); SHAP radar charts (1); SHAP swarm plot (2); SHAP feature importance analysis (1); SHAP partial dependency plots (1); SHAP heatmap analysis (1),[],"['Tourists tend to prefer images with green landscapes, suggesting that the landscape design and planning in these areas are better compared to others (1)']",[],[],[],[],"['eco-engineering techniques to restore marine ecosystems through seagrass planting or artificial reef construction (1)', 'smart visitor management systems (1)']","['incorporating elements of plant diversity in Coastal National Parks (1)', 'strengthening landscape infrastructure, such as minimally invasive floating walkways, eco-floating viewing platforms, and coastal eco-friendly trails (1)', 'optimizing visitor route planning can prevent overcrowding and reduce environmental pressure (1)', 'implementing ecological capacity assessments (1)', 'introducing environmental education and training signage (1)', 'establishing ecological buffer zones (1)']",['This study proposes innovative solutions and analytical strategies aimed at promoting sustainable development in the environments of Coastal National Parks (1)'],"['This study emphasizes the importance of precise built environment indicators on emotional responses in Coastal National Parks, addressing gaps in prior research on landscape visual environment indicators (1)']","['This study, using Fujian’s Coastal National Parks as a case study, proposed a reliable sentiment evaluation framework for guiding landscape planning in Coastal National Parks (1)']","['policy restrictions and data security concerns may hinder data collection, affecting the analysis of emotional differences among tourists of different genders, age groups, or geo\xad graphic regions (1)', 'datasets constructed from user feedback or image content may be insufficient to accurately reflect and represent human emotional states, potentially introducing biases when analyzing tourist emotions (1)', 'the collection and processing of social media image data overlooked dynamic factors affecting tourists’ emotions, such as daily variations, different weather conditions, seasonal factors, and subjective or objective influences during photo-taking (1)', 'The experiment employed a binary classification method to categorize emotions into posi\xad tive and negative, which simplified the complexity of emotional experiences and failed to capture more nuanced emotional states such as joy, awe, satisfaction, and excitement (1)']","['Only publicly available information was extracted during data collection, with no access to private user data, account information, or sensitive content (1)', 'All data collection and analysis procedures strictly adhered to the data usage policies and terms of service of the respective platforms (1)', 'All data were anonymized and analyzed statistically within an academic framework (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,marine-themed landscapes (1); eco-floating viewing platforms (1); coastal eco-friendly trails (1); thermal imaging (1); cameras (1); Radio Frequency Identification (RFID) (1); big data analysis (3); eco-engineering techniques (1); seagrass planting (1); artificial reef construction (1); ecological benefits (1); long-term conservation plans (1); floating walkways (1); smart visitor management systems (1); online educational resources (1); on-site interpretation services (1); ecological capacity assessments (1); ecological buffer zones (1); GIS (4); kernel density estimation (1); sentiment evaluation framework (2); interactive mechanisms (1); environmental indicators (4); visual design (1); rural landscape resources (1); adaptive conservation strategies (1); original built environments (1); precise built environment indicators (1); international Coastal National Park landscapes (1); global marine ecosystems (1); marine natural landscape systems (1),,,
_Luo et al.-2025-Using large language models to i.txt,Using large language models to investigate cultural ecosystem services perceptions: A few-shot and prompt method,Hanyue Luo; Zhiduo Zhang; Qing Zhu; Nour El Houda Ben Ameur; Xiao Liu; Fan Ding; Yongli Cai,"School of Design, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai 200240 China; Digital Technology Innovation Center for Territorial Optimization & Ecological Governance, Shanghai 200240 China; Shanghai Tongji Urban Planning & Design Institute, Tongji University, Shanghai 200092 China; National Observation and Research Station of Erhai Lake Ecosystem in Yunnan, Dali 671000 China; Yunnan Dali Research Institute of Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Dali 671000 China",2025,journal article,Landscape and Urban Planning,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.landurbplan.2025.105323,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.landurbplan.2025.105323,,,World Cultural Landscape Heritage site (1),cultural; natural,cultural landscape (3); traditional Chinese landscape culture (1); educational culture (2); religious culture (3); modern Chinese political culture (1),cultural heritage (3); UNESCO (2),,"{'SOCIAL': 'Cultural identity, motivation and pride, sense of “place attachment,” and communal value. The study mentions the spirit and cultural life of the Chinese nation closely associated with the landscape.'}; {'AESTHETICAL': 'Artistic – Original product of creativity and imagination. The study mentions the landscape with exceptionally high aesthetic value.'}; {'HISTORIC': 'Educational – Potential of the asset to provide knowledge about the past in the future. The study mentions the region is further enriched by a diverse cultural history, exemplified by more than 3,000 poets who composed over 16,300 poems over the past 2,000 years'}",heritage conservation (1),Lushan Scenic Area (10); Guling Town (1),China (6); Jiangxi Province (1); Yangtze River Delta (1); Dutch (1),,COVID-19 pandemic (2),"However, a gap remains in understanding sentiment toward specific