_Manley and Egoh-2022-Mapping and modeling the impact.txt,Mapping and modeling the impact,Kyle Manley; Benis N Egoh,,2022,journal article,Environmental Research Letters,,,stacks.iop.org/ERL/17/054025/mmedia,,,,,,ecosystem services (14); recreation (20); cultural ecosystem services (6); ecosystems (10); ecosystem service (4); protected areas (2),,"{'value': 'Economic – Use', 'reasoning': 'The study mentions that recreation contributes direct benefits to national, statewide, and surrounding local economies. This implies a functional and utility value of recreational assets.'}; {'value': 'Social – Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study mentions that recreation contributes to individual psychological and emotional well-being through good social relationships and cultural identity, indicating a sense of place attachment and communal value.'}; {'value': 'Social – Emotional (individual)', 'reasoning': 'The study mentions that recreation contributes to individual physical health, and psychological and emotional well-being, indicating memory and personal life experiences.'}",land management (6); conservation (2); restoration (1); planning (2); management plans (2),California; Yosemite; Sequoia; Lassen Volcanic; Point Reyes; Death Valley; Mojave; Joshua Tree; Muir Woods; Sierra Nevada; Central Valley,California; North America; Southern Hemisphere; Mediterranean; Ionian Islands (Greece); Sierra Nevada; Central Valley,,,This study looks to fill knowledge gaps relat- ing to fully integrating socio-ecological aspects of ecosystem services in order to better assess climate change impacts.,"(a) mapping the current patterns in recreational ecosystem service use in California, (b) modeling the relationship between the demand for these ecosystem services and biophysical influences, and (c) model- ing and mapping how future climatic changes will impact those relationships and thus, recreational eco- system services in California in the future.",Flickr (8),photos (4); geolocated global photographs,geolocated; geolocated global photographs,,monthly; seasonal; annual,,,millions of geolocated global photographs,,,∼34 750 Flickr photos,,,,2005–2017,California,queried,In VEST recreation model,,"We use the 2010 adjusted urban area bound- aries shapefile provided by the California Depart- ment of Transportation derived from the 2010 Census to mask out the urban regions, in order to best assess recreational ecosystem services provided by nature.",focus solely on services outside of these urban areas,,,[],,regression,random forest,,,,,maximum temperature; precipitation; wind speed; Koppen-Geiger climate classifications; land cover; distance from highways; trail density; distance from water bodies,,80%/20%,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,The random forest algorithm then creates an ensemble of hundreds of ‘decision trees’ for each pixel that each test a large and randomly selected subset of various combinations of all the input variables and a subset of the data between those variables.,"Using the PUD within different areas in California and the social and biophysical data within these regions, we created a random forest regression model to con- services to social, environmental, and climate vari ables.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,R2; p-value; standard error; Pearson correlation coefficient,"['The training runs resulted in an R2 of 0.924, a p-value of <0.001, and a standard error of 0.002.', 'validation resulted in a lower R2 (0.305)', 'A calculated Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.94 confirms this spatial correlation between the empirical and modeled PUD.']",[],[],p-value of <0.001,the model still is able to effectively predict overall spatial and magnitudinal patterns in recreational ecosystem services,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['This inform- ation can be used to include more comprehensive cli- mate change impacts into land management plans and inform investments in areas that are predicted to see increased recreation.'],['decision- makers can use these insights along with others from similar climate change impact studies to assess trade- offs and identify where land management might be most effective and efficient'],['Our study demonstrates that the concurrent use of crowdsourced data and machine learning has great potential to close socio-ecological gaps in ecosystem service research and brings the possibility to more holistically assess climate change impacts to human well-being.'],[],[],"['The social media data inherently has uncertainties as it is just a proxy for visitation.', 'social media data popular- ity can change from year to year', 'the demographics of users can be biased towards certain age groups, genders, and locations', 'Our ana- lysis did not assess all influential factors like air quality and wildfire.']",[],"['the demographics of users can be biased towards certain age groups, genders, and locations']",[],[],[],[],[],"['The social media data inherently has uncertainties as it is just a proxy for visitation.', 'social media data popular- ity can change from year to year', 'the demographics of users can be biased towards certain age groups, genders, and locations', 'Our ana- lysis did not assess all influential factors like air quality and wildfire.']",,All data that support the findings of this study are included within the article (and any supplementary files).,,,,,,socio-ecological; biophysical; climate change; human well-being; recreational ecosystem services; crowdsourced data; machine learning; random forest; land management; mitigation; adaptation; urban areas; non urban recreational ecosystem services; peak season; summer recreation season; environmental; climatic; trail density; average maximum temperature; average wind speed; average precipitation; distance from major highways; Koppen-Geiger climate classification; distance from water bodies; land cover; forest; shrublands; agricultural lands; desert regions; coastal regions; mountain regions; northern CA; southern CA; Central Valley; Sierra Nevada; Yosemite; Sequoia; Lassen Volcanic; Point Reyes; Death Valley; Mojave; Joshua Tree; Muir Woods,,,
_Manley and Egoh-2024-Climate and biodiversity change.txt,Climate and biodiversity change constrain the flow of cultural ecosystem services to people: A case study modeling birding across Africa under future climate scenarios,Kyle Manley; Benis N. Egoh,"Department of Earth System Science, University of California Irvine, Irvine, CA 92697, USA",2024,journal article,"The Science of The Total Environment, 919",Elsevier B V,10.1016/j.scitotenv.2024.170872,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2024.170872,,,,,,cultural ecosystem services (13); ecosystem services (11); biodiversity conservation (2); protected areas (7),,,conservation (6); sustainable management (3); management (5); sustainable development (2); policy (2); planning (1); restoring ecosystems (1); protecting ecosystems (1),Africa (20); Kruger National Park (1); Lake Victoria (1); Tanzania (3); Kenya (3); Uganda (5); Kavango Zambezi Transfrontier Conservation Area (2); Okavango Delta (1); South Africa (7); Zambia (2); Namibia (2); Madagascar (1); Mozambique (1); Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) (2); Algeria (1); Cote d'Ivoire (1); Nigeria (1); Angola (2); Botswana (2); Zimbabwe (1); Ghana (1); Rwanda (1); Equatorial Guinea (1); Cameroon (1); Senegal (1),Africa (31); US (1); Northern Africa (1); Western Africa (1); East African Rift (1); Southern Africa (2); Central Africa (2); Eastern Africa (1); Maghreb (1),,global COVID pandemic (1),"Yet, our understanding of impacts to social-ecological systems remains limited, particularly in the Global South, due to a lack of data, tools, and approaches accounting for social and ecological aspects of ecosystem services.","The objective of this study is multifaceted and uses birding cultural ecosystem services and Africa as a case study to: 1) develop an approach that can more holistically map and model cultural ecosystem services, particularly at large spatiotemporal scales and in data-poor regions, 2) analyze the underlying biophysical and social drivers of cultural ecosystem services provided by birds in Africa, and 3) assess large-scale spatial and temporal shifts of cultural ecosystem service suitability caused by climate, land use, and biodiversity change.",e Bird (18); Flickr (1),data (22); crowdsourced data (5); datapoints (1); checklists (2); satellite (1); demographic data (1); observations (1); geolocated BUD points (1),,spatial shifts (2); spatial scales (2); spatial extents (1); spatial distribution (1),timeframe (2); temporal scales (1); temporal shifts (1); annual average (1); historical period (1); future scenarios (6); future suitability (3); recreation season (1),,geolocated BUD points,"10,472,514 records","13,689 unique users",,,,,,2010–2019 (3),Africa (1),queried billions of crowdsourced datapoints,platform e Bird (1),Data from e Bird was retrieved from the Global Biodiversity Information Facility e Bird Observation Dataset,"We use a database of urban areas across Africa that cross-references satellite and demographic data (Heinrigs, 2020) to delete entries within urban areas, keeping only data relevant to non-urban nature-based birding.",keeping only data relevant to non-urban nature-based birding,"individually verified in a two-stage system, with automated filters and subsequently local experts",automated filters and subsequently local experts,[],,regression (1); classification (3),Random Forest (6),Maxent (12),,,,covariate data (6); climatic covariates (1); Social covariates (1); Biodiversity covariates (1); land use (3); BUD points (2),,three-fold cross validation (2),,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"To create the Maxent model, we use the Maxent tool within Arc GIS Pro. Individual BUD points were used to represent the presence of birding and covariate data was extracted for each presence point. Along with the presence points Maxent uses unknown presence points, or background points, to contrast the conditions between the areas of presence and the rest of the study region. We generate 50,000 random points throughout Africa and extract the covariate data for each point to use as background points. The Maxent model is then trained on the presence and background data with a cutoff value of 0.5 and used to predict the suitability of birding for a 25km2 resolution grid across the entirety of Africa.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,R2 (1); root mean squared error (1); ROC (2); AUC (1); MAE (1); omission rate (1); Youden's Index (2); sensitivity (1); specificity (1),"['r2 = 0.299', 'MAE = 0.465', 'RMSE = 3.126', 'correct classification of 92.5\n%, 92.9 %, and 92.6 % for each testing fold', 'omission rate of 0.07', 'AUC of 0.92', 'correct classification of ~92 % of the ~151,000 presence points', 'Approximately 93 % of birding occurs in pixels with a Maxent suitability score of 0.5 or higher and ~79 % at 0.6 or higher']",[],[],,"Overlay of Maxent response curves onto the Random Forest partial dependence plots, reveals notable agreement between the models of the marginal effect of the covariates on birding (Fig. 2). The observed consistency instills greater confidence in the validity of the identified relationships between predictor and response variables, reassuring us that these associations are not merely internal artifacts specific to a single model but likely reflect genuine patterns in the underlying data.",[],[],,[],[],[],three-fold cross validation,,[],ROC curve (1); Scatter plot (1); Maps (1); Heatmap (1),[],[],[],[],[],[],"['monitoring (1)', 'assessing future trade-offs (1)', 'designing national development plans (1)', 'development assistance programs (1)', 'international adaptation financing negotiations (1)']","['strategic adaptive and mitigative decisions (1)', 'protecting and/or restoring ecosystems important for birds (1)', 'implementing recreational infrastructure in cooler more resilient regions (1)']","['developing innovative tools and approaches that enable the integration of social-ecological feedbacks (1)', 'integrating social science data and coupling ecosystem service supply with use to better understand service co-production, flows, values, and consequences to human well-being (1)']",[],[],"['Cultural ecosystem services are much more complex than can be analyzed through human behavior across a landscape, thus we behavior that can be derived from social data on birders, data on attri\nbutes hypothesized and tested to be relevant to birders, and ML that can identify relationships between the two.', 'This leaves out the less tangible aspects of these relationships, for example what are the perceptions and relational values of these birders or how does this non-monolithic group derive benefits from these services differently.', 'our models do not account for all factors that likely drive birding, like perception of safety of a destination, visitation costs, human and physical infrastruc\nture, availability of guides/experts, among a plethora of other social, economic, and political factors', 'Other limitations related to the data source include an underestimate of total birding as not all birders use e Bird (although overall patterns are likely captured) and an inability to capture relationships related to ca\nsual birders, as most e Bird users are serious birders']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Data will be made available on request.,,,,,,,birding (20); avitourism (4); ecotourism (4); nature-based tourism (3); social-ecological systems (10); human well-being (4); biodiversity (14); climate change (11); land use change (6); ecosystem resilience (1); relational values (3); sustainable development goals (2); CBD (1); Global South (3); crowdsourced big data (3); machine learning (ML) (10); biomes (6); species richness (9); protected area (6); accessibility (6); max temperature (4); vulnerability (5); resilience (2),,,
_Marchesani and Testa-2026-Understanding municipalities and.txt,Understanding municipalities and visitors tourism perspectives: An AI-driven analysis of Instagram posts in Italian and French villages,Filippo Marchesani; Ginevra Testa,"Department of Management and Business Administration, University “G. d Annunzio” Chieti-Pescara, Pescara, Italy; CIELI - Italian Centre of Excellence on Logistics, Transport and Infrastructure, University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy",2025,journal article,Tourism Management,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tourman.2025.105279,www.elsevier.com/locate/tourman,,,Cultural Heritage (2),cultural (1); rural (1),villages (27); rural villages (1); heritage destinations (1),cultural heritage (2); authenticity (4); heritage preservation (2); local identity (2); cultural experiences (1); heritage conservation (1); national cultural heritage (1),,"Social: Emotional (collective) – Cultural identity, motivation and pride, sense of “place attachment,” and communal value. The paper discusses how villages need to preserve local identities and how destination authenticity fosters tourist loyalty through place attachment.; Economic: Entertainment – The role the asset plays in the contemporary market, especially tourism. The paper discusses how villages can leverage digital innovations to attract and engage visitors.; Political: Management – The incorporation of the asset into past or present political strategies and policies. The paper mentions the need for governments to focus on protecting and valorise villages and communities through targeted tourism funding and strategic policies.; Aesthetical: Artistic – Original product of creativity and imagination. The paper mentions landscapes of unparalleled beauty and significant tourist appeal.; Age: Existential – Represents memory and the lives of past generations. The paper mentions the importance of preserving and enhancing the authenticity of villages.",heritage preservation (2); rural heritage conservation (1),Italian villages (3); French villages (3); Basilicata (1); Andalusian (1); Sant’Antonino (France) (1); Asolo (Italy) (1),Italian (2); French (2); Europe (3); Italy (6); France (7); Germany (1); Greece (1); Austria (1); Spain (1); European (1); U.S (1); Japan (1),,,Research on the interaction between villages and tourists through social media tools is still in its early stages and requires both (i) novel tools and (ii) insights to align visitors’ preferences and behaviours with villages’ communication strategies.,"To advance this discourse, this research note contributes to the advancement of visual and digital methodologies in tourism research by proposing a replicable framework based on artificial intelligence for visual content analysis, focusing on 33 rural villages in France and Italy to explore the dual communicative perspectives of visitors and municipalities.",Instagram (6); Facebook (1); Tik Tok (1),images (6); photographs (10); visual content (3); text (1); video content (1),geographical tag (1),,,likes (2),,"660 photos (330 photos shared by municipalities authorities and 330 photos shared by the public, specifically 400 photos related to Italian villages and 260 related to French villages)",,,,,,,up to the time of the analysis conducted in May 2024,33 villages (one per region) in Italian (20) and French (13) regions,social media analysis,AI BOT named AI_Detect Keywords,"stratified sample reflecting the heterogeneity of geographic distribution, tourist proximity, and eco­ nomic development across the Italian (20) and French (13) regions","Photographs con­ taining text and writing (up to 35 percent), photo albums, and photo­ graphs used for commercial or advertising purposes (e.g., product sales and business marketing) were excluded from the sample","Photographs con­ taining text and writing (up to 35 percent), photo albums, and photo­ graphs used for commercial or advertising purposes (e.g., product sales and business marketing) were excluded from the sample",automated,,"['structured taxonomy of visual elements', 'rule-based classification framework']",,classification (4); feature detection (1),Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) (1),DALL⋅E Image Generation (2),trained (2),,,images (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,The system integrates a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) for feature detection and a Natural Language Processing (NLP) module that maps each visual element to a stable and interpretable keyword.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (2),['accuracy of 98.27 %'],[],[],cross-location variance in keyword classification remained below 1.3 %,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['aligning communication strategies with user perceptions, local authorities can enhance the authenticity and impact of their tourism promotion and communication efforts by achieving a higher level of visibility']","['introduce a novel methodological approach based on AI to analyse and code social media content, offering a new lens for examining tourist behaviour and preferences in villages']",[],"['conceptual framework that highlights the discrepancies between visitor perceptions and municipal communication strategies, advancing our understanding of how villages can more effectively leverage social media to enhance tourist engage\xad\nment']","['the sample is limited to only two countries, Italy and France, which may restrict the generaliz\xad\nability of the findings', 'the innovative methodology employed in this study could be extended to other social media platforms such as Facebook or Tik Tok, with different communication formats in the first case visual content and the second case video content', 'some photos may be influenced by the user base and investments of the villages (visitors and villages promotion of the posts), which can affect the selection of images by refocusing attention on vil\xad\nlages sites where investments have been particularly concentrated', 'the AI BOT has demonstrated excellent performance metrics, its accuracy may still be affected by the complexity of certain visual inputs, compromising the consistency of recognition and classification in more ambiguous cases']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],The BOT (AI Detect Keywords) is available at the following link: https://c,All data and photos are available in anonymous folders for readers’ access.,,https://mega.nz/folder/v Rs ERAqa#a6Hnq A1b0Of A7EMzouvv Lg,,MAXQDA24 (1),,slow tourism (12); rural tourism (17); digital gaps (1); digital innovation (2); digital methodologies (1); visual methodologies (2); BOTshit (2); BOTwise (1); user-generated content (UGCs) (1); e Wo M (1),,,
_Marine et al.-2022-Can We Foresee Landscape Interes.txt,Can We Foresee Landscape Interest?,N. Marine; C. Arnaiz-Schmitz; L. Santos-Cid; M.F. Schmitz,,2022,journal article,Land,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/land11050715,https://doi.org/10.3390/land11050715,,,,,landscape (6); heritage landscape,Cultural Significance; UNESCO’s World Heritage Site; Cultural Interest Asset; Heritage Cultural Landscape,,"Aesthetical Values: Artistic – The Lozoya Valley is characterized by a hydraulic system that combines rivers and artiﬁcial elements such as reservoirs, the presence of water is taken as a characteristic aesthetic value of the valley.; Ecological Values: Essential – The study area is within the boundaries of the Sierra de Guadarrama National Park and the Sierra del Rincón Biosphere Reserve, and it also belongs to the European network of protected sites Natura 2000.",,Lozoya Valley; Madrid; Montejo de la Sierra beech forest; Monastery of El Paular; Neanderthals Valley; High Valley of the Lozoya River,Madrid; Spain; European continent,,,"Based on these arguments, this paper takes as its starting point a criticism that The Natural Capital Project [14] had already made of predictive models: that they require assuming that people’s responses to environmental variables will not change over time.","The objectives of the study are the following: i. To determine the validity of social media geotagged photos as a basis, and of Max Ent as a tool, for predictive studies of future landscape users’ behavior. ii. To determine the differences between the quantiﬁcation of the future spatial distribution of geotagged photographs and their real qualitative changes. iii. To propose a comprehensive approach to the actual complexity of the photographs uploaded by users to social networks to assess future CES interest.",social media (7); Panoramio (12); Google Earth (2); Google Maps (2); Flickr (6); Mapio,photographs (27); geotagged photographs (10); georeferenced photographs (10); audiovisual material; videos (2),geotagged (10); georeferenced (10),spatial distribution (3); hotspot areas,,,username; upload date; title; hashtags; location of the photo,"54,956 photographs",,,3192 photographs,,,,between 2007 and 2015,Region of Madrid,professional services of a company were hired to bulk download all the photographs uploaded to Panoramio in the Region of Madrid between 2007 and 2015.,APIs,PUD (Per User Day) method,a sieve is applied to the collection of images to remove unwanted tendencies,,manually,"two different researchers, and the differences are then compared by a third one","['Natural system', 'Urban system', 'Rural system', 'Water bodies', 'Recreational activities', 'Cultural activities']",,distribution modeling,maximum entropy models (4); machine learning technique,Max Ent (23),,,,point data,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,area under the receiver operating characteristic curve; AUC,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Not applicable,,,,,,cultural ecosystem services (CES) (10); social media geotagged photos; maximum entropy approach; jackknife resampling; affective computing; environmental variables (7); socio-ecological value; agropastoral landscape; rurality loss; urban sprawl; naturalistic; Kernel tool in Arc Map 10 5 1; ACISS ﬁle; Band Collection Statistics tool; Arc Map 10.5.1 Spatial Analyst; covariance and correlation matrices,,,
_Marius et al.-2022-ANALYSIS OF VISITORS' OPINIONS O.txt,ANALYSIS OF VISITORS’ OPINIONS ON ROMANIAN MUSEUMS’ ACTIVITIES,Marius Surugiu; Raluca Mazilescu; Camelia Surugiu,"Institute of National Economy, Bucharest, Romania; Faculty of Administration and Business, University of Bucharest, and National Institute for Research and Development in Tourism, Bucharest, Romania.",2022,journal article,Journal of Tourism Management Research,Conscientia Beam,10.18488/31.v9i1.3044,https://www.open-heritage.eu/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/riches-book.pdf.,,53-63,cultural heritage (4),tangible; intangible,Romanian museums (5); museums (30); museum exhibits,tangible heritage; intangible heritage; cultural heritage product; Digitization of cultural heritage; cultural goods,,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective): The study analyzes visitor comments on Facebook to understand their emotional responses to Romanian museums, indicating a focus on cultural identity and a sense of place attachment related to these institutions.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment: The study explores how museums can use social media to attract visitors and improve their experiences, suggesting a focus on the role of museums in the tourism market.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Educational: The study aims to provide insights for museum managers to develop cultural heritage products and target consumers effectively, indicating a focus on the educational role of museums.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Historic-Artistic: The study focuses on museums, which are institutions that preserve and present historical and artistic artifacts, indicating a focus on the value of these artifacts as rare or unique examples of historical and artistic movements.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic: The study analyzes visitor comments on Facebook to understand their perceptions of museums, suggesting a focus on the artistic value of the exhibits and the overall aesthetic experience.'}; {'SCIENTIFIC VALUES': 'Conceptual: The study explores how technology can be used to improve the presentation of museum exhibits, indicating a focus on the conceptual intentions behind the design of these exhibits.'}; {'AGE VALUES': 'Existential: The study focuses on museums, which are institutions that preserve and present artifacts from the past, indicating a focus on the value of these artifacts as representations of memory and the lives of past generations.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': 'Spiritual: The study explores how museums can use technology to create engaging presentations for visitors, suggesting a focus on the harmony between the museum and its environment.'}",conservation (2); restoration,"Romanian museums (5); Dimitrie Gusti National Village Museum; National Museum of the Romanian Peasant; National Museum of Romanian History; Grigore Antipa National Museum of Natural History; National Museum of Art of Romania; King Ferdinand I National Military Museum; Cotroceni National Museum; National Museum of Geology; National Museum of Romanian Aviation; National Museum of History of Transylvania; George Enescu National Museum; Peles National Museum; National Museum of Romanian Literature; Bran National Museum; Astra National Museum Complex / Astra Museum in Sibiu; Neamț National Museum Complex, Piatra Neamț; National Philatelic Museum, Bucharest; National Museum of Agriculture, Slobozia; Museum of Viticulture and Pomiculture, Goleşti; Palace of Culture in Iași / Moldova National Museum Complex; National Museum of the Romanian Navy, Constanța; Banat National Museum, Timișoara; National Museum of Bukovina; Suceava History Museum; Suceava Princely Fortress; Museum of Natural Sciences, Suceava; Princely Inn Ethnographic Museum, Suceava; Bukovina Village Museum; National Museum of the Union, Alba Iulia; National Museum of Romanian Literature, Iasi; Brătianu National Museum, Ștefănești; National Museum of Maps and Old Books, Bucharest; Brukenthal National Museum, Sibiu",Romania (6); Thessaloniki; Italy; European Union (EU); Europe,,COVID-19 pandemic,"Romanian museums have a rich cultural heritage that is intrinsically valuable to its citizens, however, the number of visitors has been decreasing in recent years as individuals prefer to visit more dynamic and more effectively promoted tourism and cultural establishments.","This article investigates 1,790 comments posted on Facebook from January to June 2021 by visitors of the Romanian museums to find the emotional responses regarding their experiences.",Facebook (12); Twitter (4); Instagram (3); You Tube,text (2); images (3); videos (2); photos,,,,,,"1,790",,,,,,,January to June 2021,Romania,The data is collected from museums’ profiles on Facebook.,,,,,,,[],,sentiment analysis (2); text mining,,,,,,text,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"This study developed the sentiment analysis using R software and packages: • Tm (for text mining operations, such as removing numbers, special characters, punctuations, and stop words, such as “the”, “is”, and “are”). • Snowballc (for reducing words to their base/root form, for example: reducing the words “hunting”, and “hunter” to “hunt”). • Wordcloud (for word cloud plot generation). • RColor Brewer (for obtaining color palettes for plots). • Syuzhet (for sentiment scores and emotion classification), and ggplot2 (for plotting graphs).",no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['the study has some limitations related to choosing the Romanian museums for analysis.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,PyTorch; TensorFlow; scikit-learn,,cultural establishments; cultural knowledge; virtual exhibitions; digital exhibits; digital content; virtual collections; cultural goods; digital services; cultural content; heritage goods; multimedia techniques,,,
_Marulli et al.-2016-What's the Matter with Cultural.txt,What’s the matter with Cultural Heritage tweets?,F. Marulli; A. Chianese; F. M. Piccialli,,2016,other,,,,,,,Cultural Heritage (CH) (6),,,#culturalheritage (2); CH-related (4); Cultural Heritage Sensitivity Performance Indicators (CH-SPI); CH Global Density (CH-GD) (4); no CH Global Density (NOCH-GD) (2); CH Category Density (CH-CD) (5),,,,Italy (2); Napoli; Bari; Venezia; Roma,Italy (2),,TIM Big Data Challenge 2015 (2),"In the last years, while an increasing number of studies and initiatives about tweets, in both the academic and business worlds, raised, a lack of quantitative studies devoted to assess their potential and effectiveness for organizations promoting Cultural Heritage was recorded.","The aim of this research is to describe how cultural organizations could exploit Twitter to reach potential visitors of exhibitions, as well as users of a wide spectrum of CH-related services. This exploratory study also seeks to ascertain a more in-depth look at how users in Italy use Twitter in relation to cultural or arts topics and a detailed snapshot into the types of interactions that are occurring among Cultural Heritage related (CH-related) tweets and space and temporal analysis is given.",Twitter (17),text (3); geo-space coordinates,geo-space coordinates,Geo-space distribution,temporal analysis (2); timestamp (2); time distribution,,anonymous user identifier; reference language,399999 tweets,,,,,,,December 2014 to May 2015,seven Italian city,collected,API provided by Twitter,,data source normalization and filtering of noise (no useful data and metadata removal),,automatically,CH domain experts,"['ART', 'ARTWORK', 'ARTIST', 'SCULPTOR', 'MUSEUM', 'MONUMENT', 'HUMANIST']",,semantic matching,,,,,,entities; terms; concepts,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,"algorithm was designed to be powerful and efficient in managing large amount of data, Big Data as a matter of fact. So, it was implemented as a set of scripts conceived to exploit the associative technology of the BI engine Qlik View.","extract for each tweet in the dataset, the list of belonging terms and concepts; automatically discovers the CH-related instance types in the set of analyzed tweets; detect how CH-related terms are distributed in the data we annotated and compare them to the distributions of terms in other categories.",,['Social Influence Network'],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['ontology-based approach supporting a set of analysis and\nBI services aimed to provide a quantitative estimation of cultural heritage sensitivity levels, computed by the mean of investigations about relationships occurring among cultural heritage related terms frequencies and geographical and temporal dimensions.']","['assessment of a quantitative baseline for CH\nsensitivity, useful as reference value for future studies concerning the contribution of social networks application in the improvement and increase of users’ engagement level with cultural heritage topics.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,http://www.telecomitalia.com/tit/en/innovazione/big-datachallenge-2015.html,,,,social media mining; DBPedia ontology (3); Art & Architecture Thesaurus (AAT) (2); Getty Vocabularies; Qlik View (3); J-SON file format; RDF triples,,,
_Matasov et al.-2023-COVID-19 pandemic changes the re.txt,COVID-19 pandemic changes the recreational use of Moscow parks in space and time: Outcomes from crowd-sourcing and machine learning,Victor Matasov; Viacheslav Vasenev; Dmitrii Matasov; Yury Dvornikov; Anna Filyushkina; Martina Bubalo; Magomed Nakhaev; Anastasia Konstantinova,"Smart Urban Nature Research Center, Agrarian-technological Institute, Peoples’ Friendship University of Russia (RUDN University), 6 Miklukho-Maklaya Street, Moscow 117198, Russian Federation; Faculty of Geography and Geoinformation Technology, Higher School of Economics (HSE University), 11 Pokrovsky boulevard, 109028 Moscow, Russian Federation; Soil Geography and Landscape Group, Wageningen University, 6708PB, Wageningen, the Netherlands; Best Place LLC, Leninskaya Sloboda street, 26/28, Moscow 115280, Russian Federation; Laboratory of carbon monitoring in terrestrial ecosystems, Institute of Physicochemical and Biological Problems of Soil Science of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Institutskaya str., 2 142290, Pushchino,; Independent Researcher, Russian Federation; Kadyrov Chechen State University, 32 Sheripova st., Grozny, Chechen Republic 364093, Russian Federation",2023,journal article,Urban Forestry & Urban Greening,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ufug.2023.127911,www.elsevier.com/locate/ufug,,,,,urban parks (3); urban green spaces (UGS) (1); urban green infrastructure (2); natural sites (1); historical Neskuchny garden (1),Cultural ecosystem services (1); urban green spaces (UGS) (1); urban green infrastructure (2),,"{'Social': 'The study highlights the importance of urban green spaces for stress reduction, relaxation, physical activity, inspiration for culture and arts, educational and aesthetic values, indicating a discussion of social values related to well-being and community.'}; {'Aesthetical': 'The study mentions aesthetic values related to outdoor recreation and the importance of green spaces for inspiration in culture 