_Medlin et al.-2025-Examining subpar park experience.txt,Examining subpar park experience: A mixed-methods analysis of online reviews,Chris A. B. Zajchowski; Jessica P. Fefer,"Natural Resources and Society, University of Idaho, 875 Perimeter Drive, Moscow, ID 83844-9803, USA",2025,journal article,Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/rmle21,,https://doi.org/10.1080/23750472.2023.2223606,https://doi.org/10.1080/23750472.2023.2223606,,617 631,,,Urban parks (4); performance venues (2); Wolf Trap National Park for the Performing Arts (2); national park (2); urban park (2); tourism destinations,,,,,"Wolf Trap National Park for the Performing Arts; Virginia, USA; Washington-Arlington-Alexandra DC-Virginia-Maryland-West Virginia metropolitan statistical area",United States (U.S.); Finland; South Africa,,COVID-19,"As park managers seek to continually improve the visitor experience, social media is a useful data source to capture visitor sentiment, particularly surrounding subpar experiences.",The objectives of this social media analysis were two-fold: (1) to fully understand park users’ perceptions based on online reviews; (2) to examine park users’ sentiment towards various aspects of the park.,social media (14); Twitter (3); Google Reviews (8); Google Maps (3); Flickr (2); Instagram (2); Yelp,text (10); images (2); online reviews (15),geotagging,,,likes; star ratings (5),,3289,,1601,,,,,between 15 June 2011 and 30 October 2021,Wolf Trap,web scraping,,,"all the unnecessary words from the text reviews were removed, including punctuation, emoji, and stop words that do not contain useful contextual meaning, such as “wolf,” “trap,” “and,” “so,” “just,” “that,” “this,” “yes,” “no,” “don’t,” “didn’t,” etc.",,deductive coding (3),lead author,[],,topic modeling (6); sentiment analysis (7); classification,,Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) (2),,word2vec,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,['Reviews were generally positive (81.33%)'],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Results inform managers of themes within online reviews and parameterize the overall experience and online image of the park.', 'Findings are transferable to other park and performance venues seeking to meet diverse visitors’ needs.']","['This study evidences integration of different analytical techniques to understand visitor experiences in sport and leisure contexts, with a particular focus on subpar experiences.']",[],[],[],[],"['individuals commenting on social media may not be representative of the population they are assumed to represent, leading to a similar representation bias as present in survey research']",[],[],[],[],[],"['researchers may potentially face a number of methodological challenges such as incomplete information, data noise, consistency, ethics, and lack of demographic data']",,,,,,,,Natural Language Processing (NLP) (9); bi-grams (4); uni-gram; Senti Words (2),,,
_Melelli et al.-2023-HUSH (Hiking in Urban Scientific.txt,"Article ABSTRACT: industry and enhance its educational experience. One such endeavor is urban trekking and geo- tourism, which have emerged as important means of promoting geological knowledge. The recent advancements in augmented reality technologies as well as the increasing availability of ‘born digital’ data such as those gathered from social media create a basis for the development of immersive and customized touristic experiences. Urban scientiﬁc heritage, augmented reality, and data mining are the key elements of the HUSH project. Its ﬁrst focus is the identiﬁcation of the naturalistic components in a given urban area (ﬂora, fauna, and geological features) through literature surveys and scientiﬁc research. These factors become points of interest (Po Is) along touristic paths, where they are connected to the historical and artistic components of the area. Augmented reality serves as the medium through which the user can access this content. The contents are delivered as videos, text, images, or interactive 3D models. The mobile application from this project is a useful tool for promoting geoheritage and naturalistic values in urban areas and for improving the awareness and the sustainability of our cities. Citation: Melelli, L.; Bianchini, G.; Fanò, L. HUSH (Hiking in Urban Scientiﬁc Heritage): The Augmented","Melelli, L.; Bianchini, G.; Fanò, L.",,2023,journal article,Appl. Sci.,"MDPI, Basel, Switzerland",https://doi.org/10.3390/ app13158857,https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/ 4.0/,,,Urban Scientiﬁc Heritage; geoheritage; natural heritage,,urban area of Perugia; historical buildings; historical streets; historic center; Umbria region; historical cities; Etruscan Arch; St. Ercolano Church; St. Pietro Church,geoheritage; natural heritage; geological heritage; naturalistic heritage; historical–artistic monuments; historical and architectural heritage; scientiﬁc content; potential heritage site,,economic; educational; artistic; historic; scientific,,Perugia; Umbria region; Central Italy,Umbria region; Central Italy; Perugia; Italy; Molise region; Poland; Czech Republic; China,X century B.C.,,"Naturalistic content therefore represents unexpressed potential, which if appropriately valued in AR could represent a strong precursor of development.","To identify in an urban context naturalistic values, starting from the historical center and ending in green areas. To realize innovative fruition via AR tools of virtually moving in space and time, triggering awareness-raising practices. To expand the potential for economic development by linking AR content to local businesses (e-advertising). To integrate society into the processes of scientiﬁc research.",social media,data mining; videos; text; images; 3D models,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,[],,machine learning,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,virtual machine,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['urban trekking', 'geo-tourism']",[],[],[],[],[],['privacy restrictions'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Data is unavailable due to privacy restrictions.,,,,Word Press; Unity3D,,urban trekking; geo-tourism; augmented reality; data mining; urban scientiﬁc heritage; geological features; scientiﬁc research; points of interest; touristic paths; historical and artistic components; mobile application; geoheritage; naturalistic values; urban areas; urban geology; abiotic components; biotic parameters; green solutions; geological characteristics; geomorphological conditions; natural hazards; natural green areas; biodiversity preservation; fauna; ﬂora; anthropic component; digital infrastructure; multimedia materials; immersive experience; visual recognition tools; naturalistic heritage; digital multimedia content; machine learning techniques; historical–artistic monuments; local economy; naturalistic values; geological heritage; building stones; geomorphological features; hydrogeological and hydrographic resources; abiotic component; endogenous parameters; exogenous modeling agents; faunistic and ﬂoristic heritage; mineralogical and petrographic characteristics; regional geology; paleontological subjects; topographic layout; Historical manuscripts; ancient paintings; maps; anthropic underground cavities; geognostic surveys; anthropic deposits; digital evolution model; palaeogeographical reconstruction; drainage network; monumental trees; urban parks; urban vegetable gardens; wildlife; spontaneous vegetation; wildlife cameras; infrared sensors; scientiﬁc information; multimedia tools; points of interest; digital content creation; local economy; database; open-source content management system; machine learning algorithm; PHP; Python scripts; No SQL Mongo DB database; virtual machine; augmented reality format; architectonical or artiﬁcial elements; treasure hunt; social network; geoheritage,,,
