_Mizuuchi and Nakamura-2024-Does nationality affect landscap.txt,Does nationality affect landscape perceptions of urban sacred forests? A Japan–China cross-cultural comparison,Y. Mizuuchi; K. W. Nakamura,,2024,journal article,Jpn Archit Rev,,https://doi.org/10.1002/2475-8876.12460,https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/2475-8876.12460,,,Cultural Heritage (1); sacred forests (13),,sacred forests (13); shrine forests (7); Meiji-Jingu forest (8),cultural ecosystem services (3); sacred (4); shrines (6); conservation (3); planning (4); management (6),,"Spiritual: The study explores the cultural ecosystem services of sacred forests, focusing on the Meiji-Jingu Shrine’s forest, which implies a spiritual connection to the site.; Aesthetical: The study assesses the forest’s sublimity and landscape perceptions among visitors, indicating a focus on the aesthetic qualities of the forest.; Social: The study examines landscape perceptions between Japanese and Chinese visitors, suggesting an interest in the social value of the forest as a shared cultural space.",forest management (2); forest planning (2),Meiji-Jingu Shrine; Tokyo,Japan (8); China (7); Africa (1); India (1); Tokyo (2); East Asia (1),,climate change and ongoing oak wilt,One research question that remains to be explored is whether individuals who do not have a Japanese cultural background can perceive the sublimity of its forests.,Our main objective was to ascertain whether the sense of sublimity and landscape perceptions of the Meiji-Jingu forest differed by nationality. We examined whether the following aspects differed between Japanese and Chinese visitors: (1) spatial trends in the distribution of places perceived as sublime; (2) landscape elements perceived as sublime; and (3) the evaluation structure of the forest.,,geotagged photographs (7),geotagged (7); global navigation satellite system (GNSS),spatial trends; spatial analysis,,,,359,30,,359,,,359,"February 2 and 3, 2019","Meiji-Jingu shrine, Shibuya wa