_Mouttaki et al.-2022-Classifying and Mapping Cultural.txt,ECOSYSTEM SERVICES OF WETLANDS,Mehdi Maanan; Ieva Misiūnaitė; Hatim Radi; Mohamed Elhassani; Marinus L. Otte; Isabelle Boulangeat,"Faculty of Sciences Ain Chock, Department of Earth Sciences, University Hassan II, Casablanca, Morocco; Nature Research Centre, Vilnius, Lithuania; Marine Research Institute, Klaipėda University, Klaipėda, Lithuania; LETG UMR 6554, University of Nantes, Nantes, France; Faculty of Sciences Ain Chock, Department of Mathematics & Computer Science, University Hassan II, Casablanca, Morocco; Polydisciplinary Faculty of Khouribga, Sultan Moulay Slimane University of Beni Mellal, Khouribga, Morocco",2018,journal article,Wetlands,Springer Nature,,https://www.flickr. com/,,,,,Lithuanian coast (11); coastal cities (2); historical monuments (4); natural reserves (2); protected areas (3); coastal zone (4); coastal areas (2); natural areas (2); wetland environments (1),"Cultural ecosystem services (CES) (24); biodiversity conservation (2); UNESCO World Heritage sites (1); archaeological, architectural, and artistic heritage (1); environmental heritage (1); nature conservation (1); blue flag beaches (1)",,"{'value': 'Social: Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study analyzes social media photos to understand cultural ecosystem services, which inherently relate to cultural identity and a sense of place attachment, indicating a collective emotional value.'}; {'value': 'Economic: Entertainment', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the use of social media to monitor tourism and recreational activities, suggesting that the cultural ecosystem services contribute to the entertainment value of the area through tourism.'}; {'value': 'Political: Management', 'reasoning': 'The study aims to inform urban planning and improve nature reserve management, indicating the incorporation of heritage assets into political strategies and policies related to environmental management.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical: 