_Nilashi et al.-2022-Customer satisfaction analysis a.txt,Customer satisfaction analysis a,Mehrbakhsh Nilashi; Alireza Fallahpour; Kuan Yew Wong; Fahad Ghabban,"Centre for Global Sustainability Studies (CGSS), Universiti Sains Malaysia, 11800 Gelugor, Penang, Malaysia; UCSI Graduate Business School, UCSI University, No. 1 Jalan Menara Gading, UCSI Heights, 56000 Cheras, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia 3; School of Mechanical Engineering, Universiti Teknologi Malaysia, 81310 Skudai, Malaysia 4; College of Computer Science and Engineering, Information Systems Department, Taibah University, Medina, Saudi Arabia",,journal article,,,,,,,Cultural Heritage,,historic sites (7); historic district,heritage tourism (6); heritage sites (3); heritage preservation (2),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) - The study analyzes customer satisfaction in historic sites, which relates to cultural identity and a sense of place attachment.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment - The study focuses on customer satisfaction in historic sites, which are often visited for entertainment and tourism purposes.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational - The study analyzes customer satisfaction in historic sites, which have the potential to provide knowledge about the past.'}",,George Town; Malaysia; Malaysian historic sites,"Malaysia; US; America; Sweden; Canada; Brazil; USA; Spain; Italy; Iran; Thailand; Phuket; Tehran; Marrakech; Ubud, Bali; Floriano´polis-SC, Brazil; Canary Islands",,,This paper aims to analyze customer satisfaction in historic sites through electronic word-of-mouth.,The main goal of this research is to evaluate tourists’ online reviews to determine the service quality that causes traveler satisfaction and dissatisfaction in the historic sites.,Trip Advisor (14); social networking sites; social media applications; bulletins sponsored by companies; tripadvisor.com,"text (posts, captions, comments, reviews, hashtags); online reviews (27); text-based reviews; user-generated data",,,,ratings; reviews,customers’ information; historic sites information,2141 customers’ reviews,,,,,,,,8 Malaysian historic sites,crawler,web programming technologies,,"text pre-processing was used in the ﬁrst step of text analysis which has been used in prior studies [95, 103]. In this step, tokenization (i.e., splitting joint terms into multiple terms) and stemming were applied, resulting in dictionary terms. In addition, we performed the tokenization (i.e., and stemming on the textual reviews) and edited the text by converting the upper-case letters to lower case. In stemming, Porter’s stemming algorithm was adopted.",,,,"['[very low]', '[low]', '[moderate]', '[high]', '[very high]']",,clustering; topic modeling; sentiment analysis; preference prediction,supervised learning techniques; unsupervised learning technique; machine learning,LDA (7); LVQ (10); ANFIS (13); SVM (2); RBF; SOM (2); CART; HOSVD; DT (2); NB (2); KNN,,,,customers’ online reviews,,tenfold cross-validation approach,,,,,200 epochs,,,{},neural network (NN); ANFIS; multiple linear regression (MLR),,,,,,,"Latent dirichlet allocation, learning vector quantization and Adaptive Neuro-Fuzzy Inference System were used to analyze the customers’ online reviews to reveal their satisfaction level in historic sites.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,root mean square error (RMSE) (3),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],tenfold cross-validation approach,neural network (NN); ANFIS; multiple linear regression (MLR),[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['This study developed a new method for the ﬁrst time in the literature on tourism for analyzing the e WOM for customer satisfaction in historic sites.'],[],[],"['First, this study used LDA for text analysis to discover the satisfaction dimensions. Other topic modeling methods based on LDA can be incorporated into the proposed method for more accurate text analysis.', 'Second, ANFIS was applied as a non-incremental approach with the aid of Gaussian membership functions for preference prediction. Future studies can further improve our method with incremental learning approaches (e.g., incremental ANFIS) and implement the prediction models with the other membership functions.']",[],"['Neutrality is an important issue, and neutral evaluations should be taken into account in sentiment analysis [104].']",[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,e WOM (14); electronic word-of-mouth (10); Consumer-Generated Content,,,
