_Papaioannou and Schiza-2017-MINING AND AGGREGATION OF CULTUR.txt,MINING AND AGGREGATION OF CULTURAL DATA IN SOCIAL,,Department of Cultural Heritage Management and New Technologies of the University of Patras,,other,,,,http://culturalminer.culture.upatras.gr,,,Cultural Heritage (2),,archaeological sites (2); museums (2); local cultural character (1); cultural history (1); tradition (1),cultural (34); cultural events (6); cultural activities (6); cultural background (2); cultural information (5); cultural character (2); cultural history (1); tradition (1); cultural groups (1); cultural management (2); cultural cooperation (2); cultural environment (4); cultural canvas (1); cultural data (3); cultural miner (5); cultural interest (2); cultural view (2); archaeological sites (2); museums (2); local cultural character (1); cultural history (1); tradition (1),,"{'value': 'Social (Emotional (collective))', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses how social networks allow users to socialize, communicate, and reproduce information promoting local, national, and international activities closely related to their cultural background, fostering a sense of cultural identity and communal value.'}; {'value': 'Political (Educational)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions that social networks can promote freedom, respect, and solidarity in a multicultural environment by providing knowledge through cultural information exchange, which aligns with the educational role of heritage assets for political purposes.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical (Artistic)', 'reasoning': 'The paper refers to cultural events related to music, dance, theater, cinema, gastronomy, performances, and exhibitions, which are all forms of artistic expression and contribute to the aesthetic value of cultural heritage.'}",cultural management (2),Santorini Island; Greece; Santorini,Greece,,,"However, it remains an important challenge to efficiently mine useful data from such populated, diverse and vaguely structured spaces.","Motivated by the case of Santorini Island, Greece and a strong recent observation that local traditional activities or special (multi-)cultural events and activities tend to be absent from touristic guides and plans, we present a Word Press-based website which automatically collects cultural data from Facebook and presents it in a comprehensive way for promoting cultural activity in Santorini.",Facebook (13); Twitter (3),text (1),Google maps (2),,,,,,"in the third quarter of 2012, the number of active Facebook users had surpassed 1 billion",,,,,,,Santorini,automatically collects,Custom Facebook Feed plugin,,,,,,[],,data mining (3),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,Apache webserver,,,,,,,no,"['social networks (7)', 'communication network (1)', 'transportation network (1)']",,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,PHP 5.2.6 and a My SQL client version 5.0.51a,,,social networks (7); social network perspective (1); social actors (1); social interaction (1); communication network (1); transportation network (1); Word Press (6); php (2); sql (2); html (1); CMS (2); My Sql (2); Java Script (1); Google Translate (1); Google Language Translation plugin (1); Jot Form (3); captcha (1),,,
_Paradise-Vit et al.-2024-Automated photo filtering for to.txt,ORIGINAL RESEARCH ABSTRACT:,Abigail Paradise‑Vit; Aviad Elyashar; Yarden Aronson,"Information System, The Max Stern Yezreel Valley College, Emek Yezreel 1930600, Israel; Department of Computer Science, Shamoon College of Engineering (SCE), 56 Bialik, Be’er Sheva 8410802, Israel",2024,journal article,,Springer Nature,,https:// doi. org/ 10. 5281/ zenodo. 11650 464,,,,,,,,,,Israeli cities; Eilat; Nazareth; Netanya; Haifa; Tel Aviv; Jerusalem; London; New York; Sydney; Los Angeles; Chicago; Melbourne; Miami; Toronto; Singapore; San Francisco,Israel; Israeli; China,,COVID-19 pandemic,"However, photos shared with location-specific hashtags, even those related to tourist attractions, do not always reflect the actual destination, creating challenges for potential visitors seeking accurate information.","To assist tourists in finding pertinent tourism information for specific destinations, we propose VISTA: Visual Identification of Significant Travel Attractions.",Instagram; Twitter; Facebook,photos; videos; text,,,publication date,likes,publication date; number of likes; location; text,"791,352",,,,"49,037",,,"November 1, 2011 to February 16, 2023",10 most popular Israeli cities,downloaded Instagram posts,Rapid API website to connect with the Instagram API,randomly downloaded 5% of the posts for each city,,,manual,two human annotators,"['tourism-related', 'non-tourism-related']",a third annotator determined the label after discussion among the annotators,classification; object detection,convolutional neural network (CNN),Res Net-101; VGG-19; Inception V3,trained on the Image Net dataset,,,photos,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,"consists of several convolutional layers with small 3 × 3 filters, followed by maximum pooling layers and fully convolutional layers. There are 19 layers of weight in the VGG-19 network, which determine the network’s depth.","The iterative process of training the classifier, which is illustrated in Fig. 5, consists of four sub-phases: (1) selecting the tourist destination and using the previous machine learning classifier to classify the tourist destination photos, (2) photo inspection by annotators, (3) training a new machine learning classifier, and (4) evaluating the results.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; weighted F1 score,"['The classifier obtained an accuracy score of 0.965 and a weighted F1 score of 0.964.', 'Evaluating our classifier’s global generalization on the Insta Cities100K dataset, derived from Insta Cities1M, yielded an accuracy score of 0.958 and a weighted F1 score of 0.959.']",[],[],Mann–Whitney U test with p < 0.001,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],histograms,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['We present VISTA, a novel method that utilizes machine learning, and more specifically, deep learning, as well as active learning, to streamline tourists’\nsearch for information about their chosen destination.', 'To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to combine deep and active learning to differentiate between relevant and irrelevant photos associated with tourism.']","['We present three novel datasets: (1) the Instagram Top-10 Israeli Cities dataset, which was created for this study; (2) a dataset of tourism-related photos filtered from the Instagram Top-10 Israeli Cities dataset; and (3) a dataset of tourism-related photos filtered from the Insta Cities1M dataset.']",[],"['Specifically, our VISTA\nmethod may overlook relevant tourism-related photos when users fail to include location-specific hashtags in their posts.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"The supplementary materials for this paper, including the trained classification model, are available on Zenodo with the https:// doi. org/ 10. 5281/ zenodo. 11650 464. Additional code and resources can be found on Git Hub at https:// github. com/ aviad e5/ vista.","The datasets used in this study, such as the Instagram Top-10 Israeli Cities and the Insta Cities100K dataset, and the validation sets used in our performance evaluation are available upon request.",,,,,,machine learning; deep learning; active learning; social media; tourist; tourism; Instagram; photos; hashtags; classification; CNN; VGG-19; Inception V3; Rapid API; Image Net dataset,,,
_Payntar et al.-2021-Learning patterns of tourist mov.txt,Learning Patterns of Tourist Movement and,Nicole D. Payntar; Wei-Lin Hsiao; R. Alan Covey; Kristen Grauman,The University of Texas at Austin; Facebook AI Research,2020,preprint,Tourism Management,,,,,,heritage tourism (6); archaeological heritage (3),cultural; archaeological,archaeological heritage circuit (2); heritage landscape (3); historic site representations; Inca Empire; UNESCO World Heritage sites (4); archaeological properties; Sacsayhuaman; Tip´on; Pikillacta (5); Tambomachay (6); Puca Pucara (6); Chinchero (5); Ollantaytambo (7); Pisac (7); Moray (6); Qenqo (5); Machu Picchu (14),heritage scholars; heritage regulations (2); heritage landscape; heritage sites (7); UNESCO World Heritage sites (4); heritage tourism (6); heritage hierarchies; economic and cultural values; heritage landscapes; heritage circuit (2); heritage conservation (2); heritage narrative; heritage route; heritage attractions; heritage spaces; heritage attractions; heritage discourses; aesthetic legacies; sustainable heritage practices; heritage research; sustainable heritage practices,"cultural values (1); The perception of timelessness depicted in Cuzco’s imagery aids in the creation of heritage hierarchies, aestheticizes places, and assigns economic and cultural values to heritage landscapes by selecting what is meant to be “seen” and preserved (Watson and Waterton 2010).","{'value': 'Economic – Entertainment', 'reasoning': ""The paper discusses the economic impact of tourism in Cuzco, Peru, and how the region's heritage sites contribute to the tourist market. The popularity of Machu Picchu and other sites directly relates to their role in the contemporary tourism industry.""}; {'value': 'Political – Educational', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions how heritage sites are curated for mass consumption and how imagery produced by explorers influenced the curation of archaeological sites. This curation serves an educational role, shaping the narrative presented to tourists.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical – Artistic', 'reasoning': ""The paper analyzes aesthetic preferences and visuality in relation to tourist expectations. It explores how canonical images define the tourist experience and reinforce the region's aesthetic legacy, indicating a focus on artistic and visual appeal.""}; {'value': 'Historic – Symbolic', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the Inca past and its link to the present, highlighting the symbolic association of sites like Machu Picchu with the Inca civilization. The historical context and symbolic importance of these sites are central to their heritage value.'}",heritage regulations (2); heritage conservation (2); reconstruction projects; promotion; entry regulations,"Cuzco, Peru (13); Machu Picchu (14); Sacsayhuaman (6); Tip´on (5); Pikillacta (7); Tambomachay (6); Puca Pucara (6); Chinchero (7); Ollantaytambo (8); Pisac (8); Moray (7); Qenqo (6)",Peru (6); Andes mountains of Peru; Andean region; US,Inca Empire (1438-1532 CE),earthquake that destroyed most of Cuzco’s modern buildings in 1950,"Heritage studies focused on visuality and tourism currently remain theoretically driven, with few moving beyond the conceptual stage to incorporate methods that generate empirical evidence and quantify visual culture (i.e. photographs).","to understand how the intensiﬁcation of tourism intersects with heritage regulations and social media, aiding in the articulation of travel patterns across a known heritage landscape; and to assess how aesthetic preferences and visuality become entangled with the rapidly evolving expectations of tourists, whose travel narratives are curated on social media and grounded in historic site representations.",media sharing platforms; social media (4); Flickr (2); community photo websites; photo sharing platforms; internet photo community; social media platforms,geotagged internet photographs; geotagged photos (3); images (15); photos (30); timestamps (4); user IDs (2); meta-data; image’s url; owner’s ID; GPS coordinates (2); tourist photos (4); street scenes; self-portraits; aerial photography; satellite imagery; drones,geotags (3); GPS coordinates (2); latitude and longitude (2),spatial extent; spatial density map; landscape hotspots,timestamps (4); time series analysis; temporal context,,image’s url; owner’s ID; geotag; timestamp,"57, 804 images","2, 261 users",,"57, 804",,,,2004-2019 (6),"12 archaeological sites in Cuzco, Peru",collected data from community internet photos,Google Earth,"querying with the site’s GPS coordinates and taking the top 4, 000 retrieved images from each site",Images with GPS coordinates beyond the estimated buﬀer from the central point of the site were later eliminated.,,,,[],,clustering (5); visual recognition; object detection; image recognition,machine learning (10); computer vision (10); deep neural networks; convolutional neural networks (CNN) (4),Res Net50 (5),pre-trained on Image Net (2),t-sne (3); visual embedding,,image features; pixels,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,higher layers are known to capture semantic features such as object categories,,yes,"['Markov chain', 'network']",data points as nodes,exchanges messages between nodes,,,,calculate the transition frequencies from site to site to obtain these probabilities,aﬃnity propagation (AP) (2),,"discover popular transitions between sites, how transitions are aﬀected by policies regulating heritage landscapes (e.g., the release of the BTC ticket package (Sec. 5)), and how transitions change over time",separation score; compactness score,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['Common scenes occurring across all BTC and UNESCO sites were: ‘Mountain Path’ and ‘Valley’ (i.e. mountains), ‘Archaeological Excavation’ and ‘Ruins’ (i.e. stonework), and ‘Amphitheater’ (i.e. agricultural terracing).', 'modern tourists continue to be drawn to the same exotic viewsheds depicted in early expeditionary images: Andean landscapes (e.g. terracing), Inca ruins, indigenous people, and Colonial architecture']",[],[],[],[],[],"['heritage conservation (2)', 'management of archaeological sites']",[],['application of computer vision and machine learning algorithms to quantify the visuality of heritage landscapes and analyze the inﬂuence of heritage regulations on tourist circuits in the Cuzco region'],['analyze tourist movement amongst BTC and UNESCO sites'],[],"['not all tourists traveling to BTC and UNESCO sites take the same amount of photos, geotag photos, or even upload photos to community photo collections', 'biases may exist when calculating the average time a user spent at each site since our calculations are dependent on photo timestamps', 'This may also create biases when generating site popularity rankings across Cuzco’s heritage landscape', 'There is also the question of whether users choose to make their photos publicly available, further inﬂuencing sampling strategies and the overall results of our analysis.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,visual culture (4); visual experiences (2); aesthetic preferences; travel narratives; historic site representations; visual economy; visual dissemination; visual resource; iconic images; iconicity; canonical views (5); visual heritage narrative,,,
_Payntar-2022-Archaeological heritage 'on-the-.txt,Archaeological heritage 'on-the-rise': detecting emerging tourist attractions in Peru using machine learning and internet photo communities,Nicole D. Payntar,,2022,journal article,,,,www.tandfonline.com/journals/rjht20,,,archaeological heritage (11),,archaeological heritage sites (3); archaeological attractions (7); archaeological landscapes (1); historic colonial center (1); archaeological site (2),heritage attractions (2); heritage managers (2); sustainable heritage planning (2); archaeological conservation (2); national heritage (1); sustainable heritage goals (1); heritage management (4); UNESCO World Heritage (8); World Heritage in Danger (1); national heritage discourse (1); sustainable heritage growth (1); Intangible Cultural Heritage (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The paper discusses the importance of cultural identity and sense of place attachment by identifying emerging archaeological sites, which can foster a sense of pride and communal value within local communities.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment – The paper focuses on identifying emerging archaeological sites that can attract tourists, thereby contributing to the local economy through tourism-related activities.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Management – The paper discusses the need for sustainable heritage management strategies to protect archaeological sites from over-tourism and damage, highlighting the role of political strategies and policies in heritage conservation.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Archaeological – The paper focuses on archaeological sites and their significance in understanding ancient civilizations, emphasizing their connection to ancient history and cultural heritage.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Notable – The paper identifies archaeological sites that are becoming popular among tourists, suggesting that these sites possess unique aesthetic qualities that attract visitors and contribute to their cultural value.'}; {'SCIENTIFIC VALUES': 'Technological – The paper utilizes machine learning and computer vision techniques to identify emerging archaeological sites, showcasing the application of technology in heritage research and management.'}; {'AGE VALUES': 'Existential – The paper discusses the importance of preserving archaeological sites as representations of past generations and their cultural heritage, emphasizing their role in connecting people to history and memory.'}",heritage management (4); archaeological conservation (2); heritage planning (1),Peru; Machu Picchu (6); Cuzco (12); Chan Chan (3); Caral-Supe (3); Kuelap (1); Huaca de la Luna (2); Huaca del Sol (2); Sipán (1); Lima (8); Trujillo (10); Arequipa (5); Ica (10); Acueductos de Cantalloc (4); Cerro Sechín (5); Chauchilla (4); Pumamarka (4); Huchuy Cusco (5); Ninamarca (6); Intipata (2); Wiñay Wayna (2); Sayacmarca (2); Phuyupatamarca (2); Willka Raqay (2); Llactapata (2),Peru (20); Cuzco region (4); Peruvian (2); Americas (1); North American (1); Central American countries (1); European (1); North American (1); Asian tourist markets (1); Sacred Valley (2); Andes mountains (1); North Coast (2); South coasts (2),Inca (7),COVID-19 pandemic (3),"How can scholars detect unmonitored, ‘on-the-rise’ archaeological heritage attractions before they are exposed to over-tourism?","(1) to provide a novel method capable of being scaled globally to identify emerging archaeological tourist attractions that are unmonitored by ticket sales, (2) to identify ‘on-the-rise’ archaeological attractions in Peru, and (3) to identify ways to mitigate future risk (e.g. overtourism, pollution, destruction and damage) at identified sites as tourist itineraries continue to expand",Flickr (16); Instagram (7),images (11); photos (23); geotagged images (2); metadata (5),geographic coordinates (2); latitude (3); longitude (3); GPS coordinates (4); geotags (3),spatial patterns (1),timestamps (2); trends over time (2); time series analysis (1),,user ID (1); URL (1),"198,933 photos","7,086 unique Flickr users",,"198,933",,,,2004-2019 (4),"five Peruvian cities (Lima, Cuzco, Ica, Trujillo, Arequipa) (2)",automated photo collection,Flickr API (3),digital transect points,Duplicate data entries were removed to account for regional proximity and a 2 km overlap between the radius of each transect point.,Images were only collected if they included a geotag (either manually entered by the user or created by camera or GPS).,manual,author,"['Archaeological Excavation', 'Ruins']",,scene recognition (5); classification (2),deep convolutional neural networks (CNNs) (2),Res Net50 (1),pre-trained using MIT Places (1),,,images (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,virtual machine,,,,less than 24 hours,"A Res Net50 (He et al., 2016) architecture was adopted and pre-trained using MIT Places (Zhou et al., 2017), a scene-centric database consisting of over 10 million images and 400 unique scene categories (Zhou et al., 2014).",Pre-training the visual encoder allows transfer learning to occur across architectural layers of the CNN and generates ﬁxed feature detectors that increase accuracy and eﬃciency.,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (6); sensitivity (1); precision (2); speciﬁcity (2); false positive rate (2); error rate (3),"['Algorithm accuracy was determined to be 97.3% for Ruins and 84.5% for Archaeological Excavation while the average for the two classes is 90.9%.', 'The classiﬁer’s overall accuracy when determining if an image is Archaeology or Non-Archaeology is 85% with an error rate of 0.15.']",[],[],,,[],[],,"['The majority of false positives within the Ruins category were attributed to Spanish Colonial stone architecture and rock outcrops while images of Asociación de Scouts del Perú in the ﬁeld and rock formations account for false positives in the Archaeological Excavation class.', 'False positives were highest in Arequipa and were attributed to Spanish Colonial stonework, particularly churches and plazas.']",[],[],,,[],confusion matrix (2); heat maps (2),[],[],[],[],[],[],"['sustainable heritage planning (1)', 'archaeological conservation initiatives (1)']",['Knowing which archaeological sites tourists are incorporating into their travel itineraries is crucial for the conservation and preservation of sites that may otherwise be open-access and whose visitation rates are not currently recorded.'],['This study provides a novel application of computer vision and machine learning algorithms and geotagged community internet photos to detect emerging archaeological tourist destinations across ﬁve heritage regions in Peru.'],['The results of this study identiﬁed two emerging archaeological attractions as well as four points of interest for future monitoring.'],[],"['It should be noted that a 1:1 visitor to photograph ratio does not exist in the dataset (i.e. one photo does not equate to one visitor) and is not consistent with the reality of tourist photos or photo sharing.', 'Not all tourists traveling within the ﬁve study regions produce the same number of photos, include geotags, or even choose to upload photos to Flickr.', 'For these reasons, biases may exist in terms of the total number of photographs taken and the number of unique users captured at identiﬁed AOIs.']",[],[],[],[],[],"['Providing coordinates for site locations could lead to targeted looting, site destruction, or increased site damage because the majority of smallscale archaeological sites are unprotected, unmonitored, and open-access.']",[],[],,"Publicly available, geotagged images from Flickr provide a way to monitor emerging archaeological attractions without necessarily requiring additional labor or increased funding",,,,,,open-access (4); over-tourism (4); mass tourism (5); tourist itineraries (3); sustainable tourism (5); destination marketing (3); tourist circuits (3); tour operator (4); tour packages (2); tourist destinations (4); tourist attractions (7); visitor statistics (2); travel itineraries (3); travel decisions (1); travel restrictions (1); tourist economy (1); tourist arrivals (2); tourist destination (2); tourist photos (3); tourist movement (2); tourist hotspots (2); tourist trends (2); tourist hub (1); tourist markets (1); tourist region (1); tourist circuits (3); tourist ticket (1); tourist draw (1); tourist oﬀerings (2); tourist sector (1); tourist numbers (1); tourist sites (3); tourist groups (1); tourist itineraries (3); tourist economy (1); tourist arrivals (2); tourist destination (2); tourist photos (3); tourist movement (2); tourist hotspots (2); tourist trends (2); tourist hub (1); tourist markets (1); tourist region (1); tourist circuits (3); tourist ticket (1); tourist draw (1); tourist oﬀerings (2); tourist sector (1); tourist numbers (1); tourist sites (3); Gringo Trail (2); Pre-Columbian archaeological complexes (1); Incanismo (1); archaeological features (4); archaeological research (2); archaeological sites (11); archaeological heritage (11); archaeological tourism (3); archaeological excavation (7); archaeological landscapes (1); archaeological attractions (7); archaeological heritage sites (3); archaeological areas (1); archaeological class (1); archaeological features (4); archaeological research (2); archaeological sites (11); archaeological heritage (11); archaeological tourism (3); archaeological excavation (7); archaeological landscapes (1); archaeological attractions (7); archaeological heritage sites (3); archaeological areas (1); archaeological class (1); MIT Places (4); Flickr API (3); Google Earth (2); Google Cloud Platform (2); QGIS (4); Python (1); UNESCO (8); MINCETUR (8); DMOs (2); UGC (1); AOI (12); E-AOI (10); M-AOI (9); BTC (5); CBT (1); CNN (5); GPS (2),,,
_Peng and Huang-2017-A Novel Popular Tourist Attracti.txt,Article ABSTRACT:,Peng X.; Huang Z.,,2017,other,,"MDPI, Basel, Switzerland",,http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/,,,,,tourist attractions (21); scenic hotspots (2),,,,,Beijing; Forbidden City; Summer Palace; Tiantan Park; Tiananmen Square; People’s Heroes Monument; Shichahai; Wangfujing; Jingshan Park; Sanlitun; Mutianyu Great Wall; Beihai Park; Nanluoguxiang Lane; Qianmen; Lama Temple; Beijing 798 Art District; Drum Tower; Bird's Nest; National Theatre; Badaling Great Wall; Tiananmen; Eiffel Tower,Beijing; China; Hong Kong,,,"There are many shortcomings as to the widely-used density-based clustering algorithm like DBSCAN. On the one hand, there is a need to determine ﬁxed clustering thresholds (e.g., density and cluster number) in advance, which results in difﬁculty to adapt to the clustering scenes when the regional density has a large difference. On the other hand, it is difﬁcult to distinguish adjacent high-density areas, resulting in multiple different categories being assigned to the same cluster class.","This paper proposes a new popular tourist attraction discovery approach based on Flickr geo-tagged photos, taking into account the irregular shape of the clustering area and the uneven distribution of the original coordinate points. Popular tourist attractions are extracted effectively through spatial clustering of the geo-location dataset and text mining methods. Then, popularity analysis of the extracted tourist attractions is performed in different contexts.",Flickr (7); Sina micro-blog; Dianping.com; Baidu Near You; Jiepang.com; Instagram; Baidu tourism; Bread Trip; Yahoo Travel,geo-tagged photos (10); check-in data (2); text (4); picture (2); GPS trajectory data (2); semantic text tags (2),geographic location information; geo-tagged; latitude and longitude (3); GPS trajectory data (2); geo-coordinates (2),spatial clustering (10); heat distribution (7); heat map (6); geographical distribution,time information (2); temporal sequences; time; temporal perspective; divided by quarters; divided by months; slack/busy seasons; weekend/working days,,title; tag,"213,938 geo-tagged photos","22,354 users",,,,,,from 1 January 2005 to 1 January 2016,"Beijing, China",Flickr public API,Flickr public API,,"remove the redundancy of the photos; a person may photograph a few times in a short time at the same place, and these photos are upload onto the Flickr website in all.",The photos taken by the same user within one hour in the same place should be treated as one.,manual,,[],,spatial clustering (4); class mergence (3); semantic annotation (2); clustering (20); classification (2),,DBSCAN (14); CFSFDP,,TF-IDF (3),,geo-tagged points; point coordinates; semantic text tags,,,,,,,,,,{},DBSCAN (4),,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,Overall Accuracy (OA) (2); Average Accuracy (AA) (2),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,DBSCAN,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['tourism planning (3)', 'tourism resource development (2)', 'intelligent travel recommendations (2)']",[],"['We ﬁrst apply the new clustering method CFSFDP in tourist attraction discovering, and in the process of spatial clustering, the zoning and standardization step is added to make our approach more adaptive to the clustering scenes when the regional density has large difference.', 'In addition to the new clustering method, we also use the TF-IDF method to generate the tag vector for each initial cluster and then perform vector similarity calculation to merge adjacent semantically similar clusters, which enables more effective discovering of the tourist attraction that has a large geographical area and an irregular shape.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,Sckitlearn,,geo-tagged social media big data (2); Location-Based Service (LBS); anti-geocoding API interface,,,
_Peng et al.-2020-Perceiving Beijing's City Ima.txt,Perceiving Beijing's City Image Across Different Groups Based on Geotagged Social Media Data,Xia Peng; Yi Bao; Zhou Huang,"Tourism College, Beijing Union University, Beijing; Institute of Remote Sensing and Geographical Information Systems, Peking University",2020,journal article,IEEE Access,IEEE,10.1109/ACCESS.2020.2993183,,,,,,city image (15); urban locations (2); Beijing’s hotspots (4); royal gardens (5); landmark architectures (1),,,,,Beijing; Palace Museum; Tiananmen; Great Hall of the People; CCTV Headquarters; Beijing National Aquatics Center; Wangfujing; Peking University; Yonghe Temple; Temple of Heaven; Jingshan Park; Old Summer Palace; Summer Palace; Beihai Park; Olympic Forest Park; Workers’ Stadium; 798 Art District; Yuyuantan Park,Beijing; China; Guangzhou,1960s,burning of the palace by the Anglo-French expedition forces,"Existing research on city image can be categorized into three categories: research based on Lynch’s ﬁve-element model for element recognition in city image, research focusing on the unique elements in city image to reveal a city’s characteristics, and research that evaluates image elements to improve a city’s spatial quality [4].","The research objective of this work is to propose a new framework of perceiving city image by leveraging geotagged social media data, which is more efﬁcient in data collection and more powerful in approximating subjective perception.",Weibo; Sina Weibo; Twitter,texts; check-ins; photos; videos; geotagged data,latitude and longitude coordinates,spatial clustering; spatial heat map,post time; time interval,,user information; post location; text content; post time,"4,654,753 check-in entries","1,048,575 users","4,654,753",,,,"4,654,753",2016,downtown Beijing,"Using Sina Weibo’s ofﬁcial open APIs, a program has been developed to capture geotagged Weibo data",Sina Weibo’s ofﬁcial open APIs,,"Through regular expression based text replacement, all documents are cleaned by removing unwanted characters such as tags, punctuation marks, emoticons, special symbols and URL links. Then, a Chinese text segmentation tool named ‘Jieba’ is utilized to segment the cleaned texts and remove the stop words.",,manual annotation,,"['locals', 'non-locals']",,classification; topic clustering,random forest model,LDA,,t-SNE,TF-IDF,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"Based on the Beijing’s Weibo (microblog) data in the year of 2016, we use a random forest model to categorize user backgrounds into locals and non-locals. Meanwhile, the spa- tial clustering method CFSFDP was applied to discover hotspots. We retain 73 hotspots (shown in Fig.3), which are mainly landmarks of Beijing, as the spatial units for image perception. Then two text analysis methods, i.e. TF-IDF and LDA, are adopted to abstract topics regarding the different geographical hotspots in the city across two different groups of individuals.",,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; perplexity,['the accuracy of our RF-based classiﬁer is 97.7%'],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],radar chart,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['urban management', 'tourism resource development']",[],"['Adaptive spatial clustering is applied to process Weibo’s geotagged data to discover ‘natural’ urban hotspots.', 'a random for- est model is developed to categorize user backgrounds into locals and non-locals.', 'two text analysis methods including TF-IDF and LDA are adopted to abstract topics regarding the different hotspots in the city.']",[],[],"['Weibo users cannot represent the total population at Beijing.', 'The data bias needs to be overcome in the future.', 'using geotagged Weibo data might ignore some social groups, such as children, the elderly, the poor and foreign tourists.']","['For privacy purposes, the microblog data is anonymized, but with text and check-in information.']","['It should also be noted that Weibo users cannot repre- sent the total population at Beijing.', 'The data bias needs to be overcome in the future.', 'According to the statistics from Sina Weibo, its dominant user group is young people under 30 years old (nearly 80%), and more than half of the users have a bachelor’s degree or above.', 'Therefore, using geotagged Weibo data might ignore some social groups, such as children, the elderly, the poor and foreign tourists.']",[],[],[],[],[],"['Weibo users cannot represent the total population at Beijing.', 'The data bias needs to be overcome in the future.', 'using geotagged Weibo data might ignore some social groups, such as children, the elderly, the poor and foreign tourists.']",,,,,,sckitlearn,,hotspots; locals; non-locals; geotagged data; city image; urban management; tourism resource development; text mining; random forest model; spatial clustering; TF-IDF; LDA; API,,,
_Pili et al.-2021-A Geographical Abacus of the Urb.txt,A Geographical Abacus of the Urban Building,"Pili S; Poggi, F.; Frau C.; Desogus G.; Dessi A.",,2021,other,,Springer Nature Switzerland AG,,https://www.odyssee-mure.eu/publications/br/energy-efficiency-trends-policies-buildings.pdf,,,Urban Heritage (1),building,urban building heritage (2); building heritage (10); public buildings heritage (1); residential heritage (3); built heritage (4); historical typologies (1),building heritage (10); building values (1); historical information (2); historical studies (1); conservation (1); Public buildings heritage (1); Residential heritage (3),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study focuses on the building heritage and aims to promote the participation of local stakeholders, suggesting a sense of place attachment and communal value.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study uses historical information to classify the building stock, providing knowledge about the past.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study considers the architectural characteristics of the building heritage.'}; {'AGE VALUES': 'Existential – The study considers the age of the buildings as a characterizing factor.'}",building renovation (3); energy retroﬁt (3); building retrofitting (1); building conservation (1),Carbonia (4); Sardinia (3); Italy (1),Mediterranean (1); European (2); EU (1); Sardinian (2); Italy (1),1930s (1),fascist regime (2),The lack of shared knowledge between construction industry operators and non-technical actors about the building heritage is considered one of the main barriers in the recovery process.,"The aim of the AUREE Project (Urban Energy Abacus of Buildings) project is to develop and test a tool to support energy efﬁciency and renovation process of the building heritage based on a portal (Web - GIS, Geo Blog) which contains some speciﬁc interfaces aimed at sharing the knowledge of the building stock and promoting the participation of the local stakeholders.",,geographic OPEN data (1); ISTAT data (1); urban survey activities (1),,,,,,,Carbonia (about 27000 inh. ISTAT 2020) (1),,,,,,1700–now (1),Carbonia (1),Volunteered Geographic information (VGI) (2),,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['support energy management (1)', 'transparency of Public energy uses (1)', 'supporting the phases of project, construction, monitoring and management through BIM approaches (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],['privacy regulatory framework (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,Urban Building Energy Model (UBEM) (5); Web – GIS Geo Blog portal (1); Geo DB (1); ISTAT data (1); BIM approaches (1); UNI TS 11300 series (1); VGI based data (1),,,
_Pitakaso et al.-2025-AI-driven cultural urbanism A d.txt,"Regular Article SECTION: Methods Enhances ownership, satisfaction, and placemaking",Pitakaso; Surajet Khonjan; Thanatkij Srichok; Natthapong Nanthasamroeng; Paweena Khampukka; Arunrat Sawettham; Sairoong Dinkoksung; Chawapot Supasarn; Kanya Jungvimutipan; Yong Boonarree; Ganokgarn Jirasirilerd; Pornpimol Mongkhonngam,,2025,journal article,Social Sciences & Humanities Open,,,,,,Cultural Heritage (2),tangible; intangible,symbolic heritage (1); heritage buildings (1); old markets (1); heritage landmarks (1); heritage assets (1),"symbolic heritage (2); cultural theme (1); heritage buildings (1); intangible cultural knowledge (1); cultural theme (1); heritage (3); cultural identity (3); cultural relevance (2); heritage-aligned (1); cultural capital (1); heritage into vocational pathways (1); cultural tourism (2); cultural inference (1); cultural theme (1); transport heritage (1); vernacular architecture (1); myth, ritual landscape (1); cultural assets (2); cultural activity (1); cultural programming (1); cultural urbanism (1); cultural heritage (1)",,"{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study emphasizes the importance of community memory, place-based identity, and intergenerational perceptions of space and change, indicating a strong focus on collective emotional values.'}; {'value': 'Historic-Artistic', 'reasoning': 'The study involves the analysis of historical photographs, architectural drawings, and oral histories to reconstruct the built environment’s transformation across decades, highlighting the artistic and historical significance of the urban landscape.'}; {'value': 'Educational', 'reasoning': 'The study focuses on transforming public spaces into cultural and educational hubs, indicating a strong emphasis on the educational role of heritage assets in providing knowledge about the past.'}; {'value': 'Use', 'reasoning': 'The study explores the adaptive reuse of heritage and communal sites for learning purposes, indicating a focus on the function and utility of these assets in contemporary society.'}; {'value': 'Economic', 'reasoning': 'The study incorporates a targeted assessment of workforce aspirations and skill development to align spatial planning with emerging creative economies, digital transformation, and intergenerational knowledge transmission, indicating a focus on economic values.'}",Heritage reconstruction (1); adaptive reuse (3); heritage-rich (1),Warinchamrap; Ubon Ratchathani,Warinchamrap; Ubon Ratchathani; Thailand,mid-twentieth century to the present,,"In the absence of significant pre-existing tourism infrastructure, this study employed digital ethnography to uncover latent cultural interests and spatial behaviors through social media analysis.","To ensure the proposed Learning City reflects local values and lived experiences, a multi-method participatory research design was implemented. The aim was to integrate community memory and aspirations into urban learning infrastructure through inclusive data collection.",Facebook; Tik Tok; Instagram; You Tube,content; text; images,,,Temporal analysis,,,,,,,,,,,Warinchamrap and greater Ubon Ratchathani,digital ethnography,,,,,,,[],,topic modeling; sentiment analysis; image classification; clustering; predictive modeling,BERT; CNN; XGBoost; large language and diffusion models,BERT; GPT-4; Stable Diffusion,"pre-trained BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) model, fine-tuned on Thai-language corpora",BERT embeddings,,,,80/20 split,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,AUC-ROC; F1-score metrics; silhouette scores; Design Suitability Score (DSS),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],"['Insights revealed clusters of digital attention around the former railway station, riverside temples, street markets, and cultural festivals—indicating their relevance for future learning node development.']","['Local cultural explorers: Regional residents interested in nostalgic, heritage-rich spaces.', 'Youth content creators: Digitally savvy individuals producing creative content around murals, rituals, and crafts.', 'Faith-driven pilgrims: Intergenerational users engaged in temple visits and spiritual storytelling.']",['Temporal analysis suggested weekends and holidays as optimal periods for activation.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Oral histories and social media data were anonymized and used strictly for spatial pattern recognition, ensuring no personally identifiable infor\nformation was disclosed or stored.']",[],[],"['No vulnerable populations were targeted or involved without explicit consent, and community representatives were actively engaged in the iterative validation of design outputs to ensure cultural accuracy and inclusivity.']",[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,learning city; digital storytelling; predictive persona modeling; DSS evaluation; Multi-source data; NLP; GIS; XGBoost; generative AI; participatory validation; digital ethnography; Thai-language BERT model; NVivo software; QGIS; Arc GIS Pro; AR/VR technologies; AI-powered media tools; AI tutors; Design Intelligence Matrix; Design Suitability Score (DSS),,,
