_Potsiou et al.-2023-Geospatial Tool Development for.txt,Geospatial Tool Development for the Management of Historical Hiking Trails—The Case of the Holy Site of Meteora,"Potsiou, C.; Ioannidis, C.; Soile, S.; Boutsi, A.-M.; Chliverou, R.; Apostolopoulos, K.; Gkeli, M.; Bourexis, F.",,2023,journal article,Land,MDPI,10.3390/land12081530,https://doi.org/ 10.3390/land12081530,,,Cultural Heritage (CH) (6); cultural and natural heritage (1),cultural (6); natural (3); historical (7),historic hiking trails (2); historic trails (10); heritage trails (4); historical paths (1); archaeological site (2); Holy Site (5); linear CH sites (1); historic environments (1); historical and cultural trails (1),cultural heritage (10); conservation (4); preservation (4); historic (10); archaeological (4); UNESCO (5); World Heritage site (4); linear CH (2); sustainable development (4); biocultural (1); authenticity (2); monasteries (7); monastic (3); churches (2); hermitages (2); ascetics (2); cultural routes (3); cultural environment (2); natural environment (3); cultural criteria (1); natural criteria (1); Historic Pre-served Monument (1),,"Social (Emotional (collective)) – The study emphasizes the importance of heritage trails in reinforcing local identity and traditions, contributing to a sense of place attachment and communal value for local communities.; Economic (Use) – The study highlights the economic benefits of well-managed tourism related to heritage trails, indicating the functional and utility value of these assets for generating revenue and supporting local economies.; Political (Management) – The study discusses the integration of heritage trails into regional strategies and local government management models, suggesting the incorporation of these assets into political strategies and policies for sustainable redevelopment and management.; Aesthetical (Notable) – The study mentions the unique natural and cultural elements incorporated in the trails, such as landscapes and geological formations, suggesting the artistic and notable value of these trails as unique examples of natural and cultural heritage.; Scientific (Technological) – The study describes the use of geospatial infrastructure and GIS tools for the sustainable planning and smart management of trail networks, indicating the technological value of these tools in providing updated and reliable information for decision-makers and visitors.; Age (Existential) – The study refers to the historical paths of the area and the traces of historical trails, suggesting the existential value of these trails in representing the memory and lives of past generations.; Ecological (Spiritual) – The study mentions the harmony between the trails and their natural environment, including forests and unique natural habitats, indicating the spiritual value of these trails in connecting people with nature and promoting ecological ideologies.",conservation (3); protection (1); sustainable management (4); heritage preservation (1); rehabilitation (2); enhancement (2); maintenance (6); planning (11); monitoring (2); promotion (3); management (16); redevelopment (2),"Holy Site of Meteora (6); Meteora, Greece (2); Chios Island, Greece (1); Tambunan site (1); Kynouria’s archaeological routes in Peloponnese, Greece (1)",Greece (3); Central Greece (1); Trikala region (1); Danube Region (2); Latin America (1); Caribbean (1); Chios Island (1); Milan (1); Lisbon (1); Faro (1); Italy (1); Santa Elena Province (1); South America (1); Seville city (1); Texas Triangle (1); Austin (1); Dallas–Fort Worth (1); Houston (1); San Antonio (1); Thessaly region (1),12th century (3),,"To deal with the challenges and to ensure the sustainability of a historic site, geospatial documentation merging authoritative and crowdsourced data and a Web GIS- based spatial analysis is necessary.","This paper presents the creation of an innovative geospatial tool dedicated to the redevelopment, rehabilitation and sustainable management of historic trails of various levels of difﬁculty and thematic categories.",You Tube (3),photographic documentation (3); video recording (3); metadata (2); text (2); images (7); videos (6); links (1); charts (1); audio ﬁles (1); structured data (1); mosaics (2); raster (1); tabulated data (3); vector spatial entities (4),GPS (2); geospatial data (4); GIS (23); geocoding (1),spatial analysis (7); spatial reference (4); spatial entities (7); spatial data (3); geometric information (3); spatial operations (1); spatial interpolation (1); hotspot analysis (1); slope analysis (1); elevation profiling (1); contour analysis (1); viewshed analysis (1); buffer zones (1); buffer analysis (1); route analysis (1); connectivity analysis (2); land use analysis (1),real-time (1),,,,,,,,,,,network of 10 trails covers an area of 808 ha,"Data from various sources, including geospatial data, crowdsourced information, archival records, expert knowledge, and agency-provided data, are organized and structured within the geodatabase of the tool.",Arc GIS Pro (6),,"After quality, accuracy and relevance evaluation, the data are converted into compliant-with-GIS ﬁle formats, such as shapeﬁle, geojson, Keyhole Markup Language (.kml), CSV (Comma-Separated Values) and DXF (Drawing Exchange Format).",,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['sustainable mobility (2)', 'alternative touristic activities (2)', 'trail management (1)', 'operational planning (1)']",[],[],[],[],"['fragile ecosystems (1)', 'culturally signiﬁcant sites (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,,,,Leaﬂet.js (3); Esri Leaﬂet (1),,geodatabase (14); walking trails (4); hiking trails (3); trail network (10); web GIS (3); GIS-based (7); eco-tourism (3); crowdsourcing (3); map algebra operations (3); 2D maps (3); 3D (2); orthophoto (3); UAV (1); NATURA 2000 (1); Openstreet Map (1); ESRI (2); Arc 