_Qi et al.-2018-Profiling Macau cultural tourist.txt,Profiling Macau cultural tourist,Shanshan Qi; Cora Un In Wong; Ning Chen; Jia Rong; Jiahua Du,"Tourism College, Institute for Tourism Studies, Colina de Mong‑Ha, Macau, China; Department of Management, Marketing and Entrepreneurship, College of Business and Law, University of Canterbury, Christchurch, New Zealand; College of Engineering and Science, Victoria University, Footscray, Australia",2018,other,,,,,,,Cultural Heritage (2),,Historic Center of Macau (3); cultural attractions of Macau (3); Macau heritage sites (2); heritage attractions (6); cultural sites (10); heritage sites (11); antique buildings (1); historical center (1); cultural tourism attractions (1),UNESCO World Heritage List (2); cultural heritage management (1); heritage merits (1); heritage sites (5); cultural tourism (17); heritage attractions (3); heritage site (1); cultural contents (1); cultural activities (1); heritage experience (1); cultural attractions (6); heritage world site (1),,"{'value': 'Social (Collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study discusses the importance of cultural tourism in projecting a positive image for countries and affirming national pride, indicating a focus on cultural identity and communal value.'}; {'value': 'Economic (Entertainment)', 'reasoning': ""The paper mentions Macau's efforts to shift its image from 'Las Vegas of the East' to highlight its cultural and heritage merits, suggesting an understanding of the economic value of cultural tourism.""}; {'value': 'Political (Educational)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the educational role of heritage assets in promoting cultural tourism, which can be used for political purposes such as nation-building myths or glorification of leaders.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical (Notable)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions the unique combination of antique buildings in the historical center, the legacy of Portuguese and Chinese cohabitation, suggesting the artistic and notable value of the heritage assets.'}; {'value': 'Age (Existential)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the importance of understanding tourists’ motivations to visit a cultural destination, suggesting an understanding of the existential value of heritage assets in representing memory and the lives of past generations.'}",cultural heritage management (1); heritage nomination (1); heritage planning (1),Macau (24); Historic Center of Macau (3); Ruins of St. Paul’s Cathedral (4); Senado Square (4); A-Ma Temple (Ma Kok Miu) (3); Taipa Village (1); Carmel Garden (1); Taipa Praia (1); Museum of Taipa Houses (1); Mount Fortress (2); St. Dominic’s Church (2); Guia Fortress (2); St. Augustine’s Church (3); St. Lawrence’s Church (1); Hac-Sá Beach (1); Sai Van (1); Na Tcha Temple (2); Old City Walls (2); St. Augustine Square (1); Guia Lighthouse (1); Lilau Square (1); Sir Robert Ho Tung Library (1); Lou Kau Mansion (2); Protestant Cemetery (1),Macau (21); China (4); Hong Kong (5); Mainland China (1); Asia (3); Oceania (1); Europe (1); North America (1); South America (1); Italy (1),,inscription of the Historic Center of Macau into the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2005 (2),"Some recent studies discussed various empirical methodologies, but yet to achieve an agreement on either theoretical understanding or typology methodology, indicating a research gap on this topic.",The aim of this work is to discover visitation patterns to the nucleus of Macau’s culture by analyzing user-generated content data using text analysis approaches.,Trip Advisor (5),text (1); comments (4); ratings (2),,,,ratings (3),User ID (2); Age (2); Gender (3); Country or Region (2),2271,,,,,,,between 2005 and 2013 (2),Macau (2),collecting online content,Hypertext Preprocessor 5.3 (PHP 5.3) (2),,"converted all comments to lowercases and further tokenizes each of them into a sequence of words. Meanwhile, function words, such as ‘the’, ‘is’, ‘with’, ‘at’, etc., and stopwords that frequently occur but carry little information (e.g., ‘I’, ‘myself’) were filtered out.",Reviewers not having demographic information are removed from this work.,,,[],,text mining (3); segmentation (1),,,,,,text (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"The proposed model works by establishing a mapping between user-generated comments and tourist types. First, each unique term in the first four categories was initiated with a weight relating to its occurrence.",yes,[],,,,,,,,,"The results illustrate the extent of linkage between the heritage sites, showing the frequencies of any two attractions visited by the same reviewer.",,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['The Macau government and tourism authorities may consider vertical development to emphasize the value and essence of their heritage attractions and leave authentic experiences to tourists.'],['This study proposes an approach of creating an international cultural tourist typology via social media.'],[],[],['A possible limitation of this study is that the collected reviews on Trip Advisor restrict the samples of informants to those who care to write such reviews.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This limitation remains in almost all big data analysis, but the potential of its applications and implications of analysing big data from internet is still widely acknowledged.']",,,,,,NLTK (1); gensim (1),,cultural tourist typology (4); user-generated content (4); social media (3); text mining (2); cultural tourism (17); cultural sites (10); heritage sites (11); cultural attractions (6); heritage attractions (6); cultural experience (6); cultural tourist (22); cultural tourism attractions (1),,,
_Qiao et al.-2024-Interpretation of associative cu.txt,"Revealing the association between natural elements and “religion, culture, or art” through text mining of poetry provides a new perspective for in-depth interpretation of Associative Cultural Landscape.",JQ; SL; GZ; XX,"1 College of Water Resources and Civil Engineering, China Agricultural University, Beijing 100083, China; 2 College of Architecture and Engineering, Zhejiang Industry Polytechnic College, Shaoxing 312000, China; 3 College of Horticulture, China Agricultural University, Beijing 100193, China",2024,journal article,Heritage Science,Springer Nature,,https:// whc. unesco. org/ archi ve/ 1995/ whc- 95- conf2 03- inf9e. pdf,,,Cultural Heritage (2); World Heritage Protection System (1),,Associative Cultural Landscape (21); Cultural Landscape (16); Natural Landscape (20); Humanistic Landscape (13); Folk Landscape (13); Tianmu Mountain (40); Road of Tang Poetry in Eastern Zhejiang (4),Outstanding Universal Value (1); conservation (2); sustainable utilization (2); World Heritage Committee (2); cultural traditions (1); spiritual beliefs (1); intrinsic practices (1); cultural significance (2); natural geographical features (1); cultural fabric (1); landscapes of memory (1),,"{'Social': 'The study discusses the spiritual and cultural traditions associated with Tianmu Mountain, reflecting collective emotional values related to cultural identity and a sense of place attachment. The association between natural elements and religion, culture, and art highlights these social values.'}; {'Historic': ""The study explores the historical significance of Tianmu Mountain through the analysis of poetry from different dynasties, emphasizing its role in Chinese history and culture. The mountain's association with famous poets and historical events contributes to its historic value.""}; {'Aesthetical': ""The study focuses on the picturesque charm and poetic grace of Tianmu Mountain, highlighting its artistic and aesthetic qualities. The blending of natural landscapes with cultural elements creates a unique aesthetic experience, contributing to the mountain's artistic value.""}",World Heritage Protection System (1); overall conservation (3),Tianmu Mountain; Mount Lu in China; Royal Hill of Ambohimanga in Madagascar; Bamiyan Valley in Afghanistan; Mount Wutai in China,Eastern Zhejiang; China (3); Australia; Madagascar; Afghanistan,Eastern Han Dynasty to the Qing Dynasty,,"Therefore, how to interpret them has become an important research topic in understanding Associative Cultural Landscape.",The aim is to reveal the characteristics and association between natural and cultural elements in the Cultural Landscape of Tianmu Mountain from the perspective of poetry. This research seeks to provide methodological support for the interpretation and value clarification of global Associative Cultural Landscape. It offers crucial scientific foundations for the overall conservation and sustainable utilization of these landscapes.,,text,,,,,,444 poems,269 poets,"56,511 characters and comprising 7432 verses",,,,,"approximately 1800 years, from the Eastern Han Dynasty (25 AD–220 AD) to the Qing Dynasty (1636 AD–1912 AD)",Tianmu Mountain,"Take photos of the poems that describe Tianmu Mountain from the compiled “Tianmu Mountain Local Chronicle” of various dynasties [15]. Then import the images into an OCR tool (CS Cam Scanner software) for text recognition, and finally vectorize all 444 poems. Conduct a comprehensive manual inspection and correction to ensure the accuracy of each poem’s information.","OCR tool (CS Cam Scanner software), “jieba” word segmentation tool in Python",,comprehensive manual inspection and correction,Words with a frequency of two or more were further categorized through manual interpretation to determine if they were Landscape Terms.,manual interpretation,,"['“Natural Landscape”, “Humanistic Landscape”, and “Folk Landscape.”']",,word classification; clustering; topic modeling,,,,,,poetry texts,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,['co-occurrence networks'],Landscape Terms,co-occurrence level between Landscape Terms within the same poem,60 Landscape Terms nodes,929 undirected edges,Node Degree; Node Connection Edge Weight; modularity,"two nested for loops, which is then imported into Gephi tool to create an undirected edge network visualization of Landscape Terms co-occurrences",“Modularity” function in Gephi,Gephi tool,Analyze the association between various categories of Landscape Terms.,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['The natural phenomenon that repeats itself—The changing animal and plant landscapes in the four seasons—The beautiful and timeless natural mountain and water landscapes', 'The convenient and accessible water and land transportation—The flourishing market towns and villages where people live and work in peace and contentment—The thriving Buddhist temples and Taoist monasteries', 'The popular stories and legends—The unique scenery and special products—The colorful arts and crafts and folk songs and dances']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['theoretical model for interpreting the multirelationship within Associative Cultural Landscape'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Firstly, when counting Landscape Terms, only words with a frequency of 2 or more after word segmentation were included for analysis, which may result in some key\nLandscape Terms not being included in the analysis due to insufficient frequency, which could affect a comprehensive understanding of Associative Cultural\nLandscape.', 'Secondly, the statistical analysis in this paper was based solely on all the poetry included in the “Tianmu Mountain Local Chronicle” [16], which is a relatively complete collection of poems about\nTianmu Mountain from various dynasties. Further expansion of the sources of poetry, covering works from different historical periods and different poets, can further improve the accuracy of the research.', 'Thirdly, Classical Chinese Poetry has the complex characteristic of “multiple meanings for a single word.” The extracted and statistically analyzed Landscape Terms in this research only focus on the meanings of the vocabulary itself, without involving the complex multiple meanings such as “double entendre, emotional charm, symbolism, depth, implicit meaning” behind the Landscape Terms.', 'Fourth, this study exclusively focuses on the “Objects” level of poetry, analyzing and tallying the three categories of\nLandscape Terms—“Natural Landscape, Humanistic Landscape, and Folk Landscape.” The study does not delve into the “Imagery” level, which involves the poet’s Background (History, Society, etc.), and Emotions (Homesickness, Joy, etc.), as well as the poetry’s\nExpressive Techniques (Metaphor, Parallelism, etc.), and Vocabulary (Adjective, Pronouns, etc.).']",,Data are available via request xixuesong@cau.edu.cn.,,,,Python,,Genetic Code; Cultural Gene Bank; jieba; Openpyxl; Force Atlas2; Dominant-Subordinate; Objects—Imagery; five senses; five facial features; learning from nature; man-nature harmony; blending picturesque charm and poetic grace; poetic landscapes,,,"“Sea*” in poetry refers to the exaggerated technique employed by poets when describing large lakes. In the paper, it is used to depict the lakes. “Natural Breaks” refer to the points of significant change in the attribute values among a set of landscape frequency values. “ACL” refers to Associative Cultural Landscape."
_Qin et al.-2019-Exploring the spatial characteri.txt,Article ABSTRACT:,J.Q.; C.S.; M.T.; Y.Z.; J.W.,,2019,journal article,,MDPI,,www.ﬂickr.com/services/api,,,,,,,,,,Beijing; Shanghai; Xi’an; Guiling; Chengdu; Jiuzhaigou; Lijiang; Lhasa; Kunming; Diqing; Dali; Aba; Tianjin; Chengde; Shijiazhuang; Yichang; Shigatse; Shannan,China; Taiwan; Hong Kong; Macao; Beijing-Tianjin-Hebei Region; Yangtze River Delta Zone; Pearl River Delta Zone; Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region; Yunnan Province; Tibet Autonomous Region; Liaoning Province; Central and Western China; Asia-Paciﬁc countries; Eastern China; Western China; Hainan Island,Qing Dynasty,,"However, because it is diﬃcult to obtain city-level data on the spatial and temporal behavior of tourists, especially in developing countries [4], our ability to study the behavior of tourists at the city level is signiﬁcantly limited.","To this end, photos with geographical and corresponding metadata covering the entire country from 2011 to 2017 are used to explore the spatial characteristics of China’s inbound tourist ﬂow, the spatial patterns of tourist movement, and the tourist destination cities group based on data mining techniques, including the Markov chain, a frequent-pattern-mining algorithm, and a community detection algorithm.",Flickr; Panoramio,photos; metadata; GPS location,geotagged photos; GPS location; geographic coordinates,spatial distribution; spatial concentrations,shooting time; upload time,comments,user ID; photo ID; user’s country of origin; shoot time; upload time; shoot location (latitude and longitude); photo caption; photo description; comments of other users,"282,787 records",3680 tourists,,,,,,between January 2011 and December 2017,"China (latitudes 3.86◦N–53.55◦N and longitudes 73.66◦E–135.05◦E), not including Taiwan, Hong Kong, and Macao",network crawling technology to retrieve geotagged photos and their corresponding metadata from Flickr’s public Application Programing Interface (API),Flickr API,,data-cleaning techniques to control the initial data quality and establish the basic database,Users whose country of origin is China and null are excluded,,,[],,frequent-pattern-mining; community detection,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,['tourist transition network'],tourist destination cities,tourist ﬂow,n vertices,m edges,modularity,aggregating the number of ﬂows between prefectural cities in China,Combo algorithm,,grouping characteristics of destination cities can be detected based on the compactness of connectivity (number of travel ﬂows) among cities,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['design international tourism routes', 'formulate international marketing strategies', 'develop regional tourism cooperation']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,Arc GIS10.3,,geotagged photos; inbound tourist ﬂow; spatial characteristics; tourist transfer pattern; tourist destination cities group; Markov chain; frequent-pattern-mining algorithm; community detection algorithm,,,
