_Ren and Chen-2025-Emotional and semantic analysis.txt,"RESEARCH ARTICLE ABSTRACT: Heritage parks preserve cultural heritage while contributing to education, recreation, and sustainable urban develop- ment. However, the relationships between landscape elements and visitors’ emotional responses remain underex- plored.",Si Yi Ren,,2025,journal article,Built Heritage,,,,,Page 14 of 20,Cultural Heritage (11),,heritage parks (26); cultural landscapes (5); Tang Dynasty (7); Big Wild Goose Pagoda (3); Tang Dynasty Heritage Park (2); Qujiang Pond Heritage Park (1); Tang Dynasty Ruins (1); Tang City Ruins Park (1); historical relics (2); archaeological site parks (1),cultural heritage (6); cultural significance (2); cultural relics (1); Ancient Capital (1); Ancient City (1); Cultural Heritage (1); Royal (1); Dynasty (2); Civilisation (1); Emperor (3); Ancient People (1); Folk Customs (1); Prosperous Tang Dynasty (1); historical (10); preservation (5); conservation (4); cultural (30); historical atmosphere (1); cultural identity (1); archaeological (1); relics (1); heritage features (1); heritage sites (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study highlights the importance of cultural landscapes and historical themes in shaping emotional responses, indicating a connection to cultural identity and a sense of place attachment.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Historic-Artistic – The paper emphasizes the historical significance of heritage parks, particularly those featuring a Tang Dynasty theme, reinforcing the role of cultural heritage in shaping emotional responses.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study mentions the aesthetic appeal of cultural landscapes and natural environments, suggesting an appreciation for the artistic qualities of these elements in heritage parks.'}",cultural heritage preservation (4); park management (6); cultural heritage conservation (3),Xi’an; five heritage parks in Xi’an; Tang Dynasty Heritage Park; Qujiang Pond Heritage Park; Tang Dynasty Ruins; Tang City Ruins Park; Big Wild Goose Pagoda,"Shaanxi Province; China; Hong Kong; Siem Reap/Angkor Cambodia; Bavaria (Germany); Malaysia; Spain; Yancheng, Jiangsu Province, China; Shanghai; Champaner-Pavagadh Archaeological Park, UNESCO World Heritage Site, Gujarat, Western India; Rhine River region, Germany; Ha Long Bay, Vietnam; Belgium; Mount Miaofeng, China; Huaihua City, China; Yuan River Basin in South-Western China; Beijing; Yayou Gou Village in Shandong, China; Ji-Town, China",Tang Dynasty (7),,"However, the relationships between landscape elements and visitors’ emotional responses remain underex- plored.","This study aims to bridge this gap by analysing social media reviews to understand how various landscape ele- ments in heritage parks influence emotional experiences, offering insights for enhancing park services and promoting practical cultural heritage preservation.",social media platforms (2); social media (10),social media reviews (6); text (1); online textual data (1); text data (1); image (3); geographic coordinate information (1),,,,likes (1),,"63,288 visitor reviews",,,,,,,2018–2023,five heritage parks in Xi’an,,,,,,,,[],,topic modelling (2); sentiment analysis (7); social network analysis (SNA) (5); NLP studies (2),machine learning (3),LDA (3),,,,text,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,"['social network graphs (1)', 'semantic social network (3)', 'co-visitation network (1)']","Nodes represent corresponding thematic terms, with larger nodes indicating greater attention to the respective terms.","The links signify associations between thematic terms, and the thickness of the connecting lines reflects the strength of these relationships.",90,,,"Text data were transformed into a network graph, which visually displays links among thematic words through nodes and lines",,,"Social network analysis provides an approach for identifying the relationships between high-frequency terms in landscape-related texts, revealing how visitors perceive and experience different elements of heritage parks.",,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,['heritage parks emphasize the coexistence of culture and nature'],[],"['cultural landscapes evoke the most potent positive emotions (64.9%) with the lowest negative sentiment\n(16.3%), highlighting their historical, aesthetic, and cultural significance, which fosters emotional connections.']","['Sentiment analysis revealed that cultural landscapes elicited the highest positive emotional response score (64.9%), reflecting their historical and aesthetic significance.', 'Framework and service facilities had the highest emotional intensity score (8.09), emphasising their functional role in enhancing visitor satisfaction.', 'In contrast, the biological and natural environments presented weaker emotional appeal (6.86).']",[],[],[],"['This study provides practical guidance for managing heritage parks, particularly in terms of enhancing visitor experiences and promoting cultural heritage conservation.']","['This study innovatively integrates LDA modelling with sentiment analysis and proposes a novel framework for quantifying the emotional associations of landscape elements, thereby addressing the research gap in emotional studies of heritage park landscapes.']",[],"['This study offers significant insights into cultural heritage conservation and promotion, contributing to the international discourse on emotional engagement and the integration of social media data in heritage research.']","['First, the digital divide related to the socioeconomic status, income, age, gender, and social power dynamics of social media users’demographic information is often unclear, leading to potential sample bias.', 'Second, social media content tends to be biased towards nonlocal tourists, who are more inclined to share pleasurable moments or post content that garners likes and acceptance.', 'Third, only the textual part of the comments was analysed, neglecting the shared image portion.', 'Finally, one limitation of this study is the exclusive focus on Mandarin Chinese in sentiment analysis, as the emotion dictionaries used primarily target this dialect.']",[],"['the digital divide related to the socioeconomic status, income, age, gender, and social power dynamics of social media users’demographic information is often unclear, leading to potential sample bias.', 'social media content tends to be biased towards nonlocal tourists, who are more inclined to share pleasurable moments or post content that garners likes and acceptance.']",[],[],[],[],[],"['the digital divide related to the socioeconomic status, income, age, gender, and social power dynamics of social media users’demographic information is often unclear, leading to potential sample bias.', 'social media content tends to be biased towards nonlocal tourists, who are more inclined to share pleasurable moments or post content that garners likes and acceptance.', 'only the textual part of the comments was analysed, neglecting the shared image portion.', 'one limitation of this study is the exclusive focus on Mandarin Chinese in sentiment analysis, as the emotion dictionaries used primarily target this dialect.']",,All data generated or analysed during this study are included in this published article [and its supplementary information files.,,,,,,"landscape elements (12); emotional responses (6); visitor experiences (5); park services (1); sustainable urban development (1); landscape research (1); public sentiment (2); societal values (1); machine learning techniques (1); Chinese landscape element dictionary (1); emotional engagement (3); sustainable tourism (2); cultural dissemination (1); digital divide (1); socioeconomic status (1); sample bias (1); Mandarin Chinese (2); emotion dictionaries (2); regional dialects (1); cultural contexts (1); non-Mandarin Chinese speakers (1); landscape perception (2); user-generated data (3); perceptual psychology (1); cultural ecosystem services (2); remote sensing image data (1); face-t