_Ren et al.-2025-Emotional landscape analysis of.txt,Emotional landscape analysis of heritage parks: integrating deep learning and social media data for cultural ecosystem services assessment,Siyi Ren; Xiaolong Chen; Hongfeng Zhang,,2025,other,,Springer Nature,,,,,Cultural Heritage (3),,heritage parks (11); heritage sites (1); cultural landscape elements,cultural significance (2); UNESCO World Heritage sites (1); heritage management (3); heritage preservation (3); cultural heritage preservation (1); heritage conservation (2); heritage value (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'The study discusses the importance of heritage parks for social relationships, recreation, and cultural identity, indicating an understanding of collective emotional values. The parks are seen as spaces for family activities and community bonds.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'The study mentions the historical significance of cultural landscape elements in heritage parks, which evoke stronger emotional responses, indicating an understanding of historic values.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'The study discusses the aesthetic value of landscapes, creatures, and architecture in parks, indicating an understanding of artistic and notable values.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': ""The study highlights the parks' educational role and their significance in heritage preservation, aligning with studies on cultural landscapes fostering identity and continuity, indicating an understanding of educational values.""}",heritage preservation (3); heritage conservation (2),Xi’an Heritage Parks (2); Big Wild Goose Pagoda; Daming Palace National Heritage Park (2); the ruins of Han Dynasty Chang’an City Weiyang Palace; Daming Palace National Heritage Park; Xingqing Palace; Daci’en Temple; Datang Hibiscus Garden; Qujiang Pond Heritage Park,"Xi’an, China (3); Shaanxi Province (2); Yanta; Beilin; Xincheng; China (3); Hong Kong (1); Shanghai (1); Ecuador (1); Beijing, China (1); United States (1); Europe (1); Northeast China (1)",Tang Dynasty (5),COVID-19 (1),"Heritage parks provide diverse Cultural Ecosystem Services (CES), yet the emotional connections between landscape elements and CES categories, particularly in culturally significant sites, remain understudied.",This research pioneers the integration of deep learning and large-scale social media text analysis to explore these connections.,Dianping (6); Meituan (6); Ctrip (6); Tongcheng (6); Weibo (6); Yelp (1); Trip Advisor (2); Sina Weibo (1); Facebook (1); Instagram (1); Twitter (1),text (1); comments (12); reviews (6); ratings (3); user details (1); images (3),,spatial distribution,time (1); real-time trends (1); time series analysis (1); long-term trends (1),reviews (10); ratings (2); comments (5),content (1); time (1); ratings (1); user details (1),"86,647 comments",,"63,288 valid comments",,,,,2018 to 2023,"five heritage parks in the Yanta, Beilin, and Xincheng districts",A Python web crawler was developed using the Requests and Beautiful­Soup libraries to extract comments,"Python web crawler, Requests and BeautifulSoup libraries","Based on parks with over 100 comments and diverse user demographics, Dianping, Meituan, Ctrip, Tongcheng, and Weibo were selected for data collection","During preprocessing, regular expressions from the re-library were employed to remove redundant or poorly formatted posts, resulting in 63,288 valid comments.",removing irrelevant terms and selecting high-frequency words appearing more than 50 times,manual,Four researchers independently reviewed the comments using a crowdsourced dictionary and categorization criteria derived from prior studies,"['Recreational', 'Aesthetic', 'Social Relationships', 'Biological', 'Cultural', 'Sense of Place', 'Architectural', 'Spiritual']",Cohen’s Kappa scores ranging from 0.38 to 0.68,text classification (2); topic modeling (2); sentiment analysis (4),Transformer models (2); Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) (3); Topic Models,Text-CNN (6); BERT (5); LDA (4); HIT Chinese BERT model (chinese_bert_wwm_L-12_H768_A-12) (1),Model construction begins by loading the pre-trained HIT Chinese BERT model (chinese_bert_wwm_L-12_H768_A-12),word2vec (2); Glove (2); [CLS] token vectors; sentence embeddings,TF-IDF method,text data (2); comments (2); [CLS] vectors,,80% allocated for training and 20% for testing,,,,,,,,{},RNN (1); LSTM (1),,,,,,"This network includes convolutional layers with kernels of sizes 3, 4, and 5 to extract local features, followed by max-over-time pooling.","The [CLS] vectors, along with other embeddings, are processed by removing special tokens and then passed through the Text-CNN network. This network includes convolutional layers with kernels of sizes 3, 4, and 5 to extract local features, followed by max-over-time pooling. The pooled vectors are concatenated with the BERT [CLS] vector and processed through a fully connected layer, with classification results generated by the softmax function.",yes,"['social network graphs (1)', 'semantic social network analysis (1)', 'co-visitation network']","Nodes represent corresponding thematic terms, whereas larger nodes indicate greater attention to these terms.","The links signify associations between thematic terms, with the thickness of the connecting lines reflecting the strength of these associations.",,,,"Text data are transformed into a network graph, visually displaying links among thematic words through nodes and lines",,,"To comprehend the connections between CES and landscape elements, as well as the interrelations among these elements",accuracy (2); recall (2); F1 score (2); loss (1),[],[],[],adjusted R² value of 0.832,"Social network analysis revealed that heritage parks cater to diverse communities and themes, from cultural and historical elements to children’s activities and floral landscapes, highlighting their multifunctionality.",[],[],,,,[],,,[],Sankey diagram,"['cultural and historical themes', 'children’s activities', 'floral landscapes']",[],"['Emotional sentiment emerged as a key factor, positively influencing CES perception across all clusters.', 'Cultural landscape elements in heritage parks, which possess historical signifi\xadcance, evoke stronger emotional responses']",['the emotional experience triggered by landscape elements is a key driver in shaping visitors’ overall perception of the park’s CES'],[],[],"['landscape planning (2)', 'park management (3)', 'cultural landscape analysis (1)', 'urban planning (1)', 'heritage conservation (1)', 'cultural ecosystem service management (1)']","['Optimizing these elements can help design parks that not only enhance emotional well-being but also increase the perceived value of CES, offering more effective strategies for landscape planning and park management.']","['This paper presents a new CES assessment framework that employs deep learning on social media data.', 'we propose a novel methodology that utilizes Text-CNN and LDA models to transform unstructured text data into CES clusters and landscape element categories.']",[],[],"['the digital divide related to socioeconomic status, income, age, gender, and social power dynamics often leads to unclear demographic information and potential sample bias', 'social media content tends to favor non-local tourists', 'the analysis focused solely on textual data, neglecting shared images', 'the study selectively used online reviews published after 2018 to control for biases, which may limit generalizability']",[],"['digital divide related to socioeconomic status, income, age, gender, and social power dynamics often leads to unclear demographic information and potential sample bias', 'social media content tends to favor non-local tourists']",[],[],[],[],[],"['the digital divide related to socioeconomic status, income, age, gender, and social power dynamics often leads to unclear demographic information and potential sample bias', 'social media content tends to favor non-local tourists', 'the analysis focused solely on textual data, neglecting shared images', 'the study selectively used online reviews published after 2018 to control for biases, which may limit generalizability']",,No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.,,,Python (5),sklearn library,,Cultural Ecosystem Services (CES) (15); landscape elements (12); emotional connections (1); culturally significant sites (1); deep learning (4); large-scale social media text analysis (1); sentiment (12); social network analyses (1); semantic associations (1); online travel review platform (1); tourism websites (1); online tourism users (1); jieba segmentation module (1); social network analysis (SNA) (3); online textual discourse (1); perceptual preference (1); emotional attitudes (1); sentiment dictionary algorithm (1); emotional tendencies (1); grounded theory analysis method (1); emotional factors (1); Public Participation Geographic Information Systems (PPGIS) (1); machine learning (3); NLP (3),,,This data-driven approach provides valuable tools for planners and policymakers in participatory landscape planning and management.
_Richards and Friess-2015-A rapid indicator of cultural ec.txt,A rapid indicator of cultural ecosystem service provision at coastal mangrove forests using content analysis of social media photographs,Daniel R. Richards; Daniela Friess,,2015,journal article,Ecological Indicators,Elsevier Ltd,http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2015.01.034,http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2015.01.034,,,,,coastal mangrove forest habitat patches (2),,,,conservation (2); management (3),Berlayer Creek; Chek Jawa; Sungei Buloh Wetland Reserve (SBWR); Mandai,Singapore (10); Southern Chile,,,"Existing social media indices of visitor pressure as quantiﬁed by photograph density can be used for broad-scale analyses of CES provision, as they indicate popular areas for visitors. However, photograph density does not help us to assess the cultural role of a site, or the reasons why it may be appreciated by society.","(1) compare CES provision between four mangrove sites in Singapore, by downloading and classifying images hosted on Flickr; (2) analyse spatial variation in cultural usage within one mangrove site, by modelling the distribution of different types of photographs in relation to habitat characteristics and; (3) assess the time required to implement this method, by analysing the sensitivity of the photograph classiﬁcation to the number of photographs classiﬁed.",Flickr (7); Instagram,photographs (18); geo-tagged photographs (3),geo-tagged pictures,spatial distribution,,,,760,,,683,,,,2012 and 2014,ﬁve mangrove sites in Singapore,downloading and classifying images,Flickr API,,,,,eight people,"['nature appreciation', 'landscape', 'social recreation', 'research', 'infrastructure appreciation', 'fishing', 'history']",0.75,classification (2),,Max Ent (4),,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],"Chi-squared = 436.8, d.f. = 32, p < 0.001",,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['managers could seek to increase the diversity of cultural uses present at some of the sites, for example by replicating the diverse habitat types and infrastructure (both within and outside the mangrove) that are present at Chek Jawa.']",['content analysis of the photographs allows different cultural uses to be identiﬁed.'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,cultural ecosystem services (CES) (20); ecosystem services (6),,,
_Richards and Lavorel-2022-Integrating social media data an.txt,Integrating social media data and spatial modelling to inform landscape management decisions,Dylan Richards; Sandra Lavorel,,2022,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2022.101422,https://doi. org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2022.101422,,,,,landscape (11); coastal regions (1); mountainous scenery (1); upland landscapes (1); agricultural land (7); native forest (11); alpine ecosystems (2); wilderness land cover (1); human-dominated landscape (1); ornamental lavender fields (1),cultural ecosystem service (4); landscape appreciation (22); aesthetic benefits (1); cultural significance (1); aesthetic value (3),,"{'Aesthetical': 'Artistic – The paper discusses the aesthetic value of landscapes, particularly in relation to native forest restoration and agricultural land, implying an artistic appreciation of natural and cultivated environments.'}; {'Social': ""Emotional (collective) – The paper explores how landscape appreciation, as captured through social media photographs, reflects a cultural identity and sense of place attachment, particularly in New Zealand's recreational industries and culture.""}; {'Economic': 'Entertainment – The study examines how landscape appreciation, indicated by social media photographs, is linked to recreational industries and tourism in New Zealand.'}; {'Ecological': 'Spiritual – The paper mentions the harmony between the building and its natural/artificial environment.'}",landscape management (3); forest restoration (1); reforestation (1),New Zealand (20); South Island (2); North Island (2); Canterbury Plains (3); Southern Alps (1); Banks Peninsula (1); Manawat¯u-Whanganui coast (2); Wellington (1); Southland (1); southern Canterbury (1); Northland (2); Otago (1); Auckland (1); Jeju Island (1); Singapore (1); Germany (1),New Zealand (20); South Island (2); North Island (2); Canterbury (2); Europe (1); Germany (1); Singapore (1); Portugal (1); China (1); Turkey (1); Chile (1),,,"and analysed spatial mismatches between the most suitable areas to increase carbon stocks, and to increase landscape appreciation values.","The scenarios presented here thus provide insights into the potential impacts of native forest restoration in different areas, and may be used to inform planning and prioritisation of restoration efforts for either carbon stocks, landscape appreciation, or both simultaneously.",Flickr (3),images / photos (10); text (1),geo-tagged photographs (2),spatial variation (5); spatial variability (2); spatial mismatches (3),,,user (1),"153,794","2,980",,,,,,study period,New Zealand,extracted,Flickr Application Programming Interface,,,,manual,manual coding,['indicators of landscape appreciation photographs'],,classification (1),machine learning (2),,,,,environmental variables; human explanatory variables; land cover,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,ecosystem services (11); carbon stocks (4); carbon storage (3); cultural ecosystem services (5); landscape appreciation (22); native forest restoration (10); social media data (7); spatial modelling (2); spatial analysis (1); spatial variation (5); spatial variability (2); spatial mismatches (3); sensitivity analysis (4); explanatory variables (6); land cover (11); human factors (4); environmental conditions (2); recreational activities (2); aesthetic value (3); cultural heritage (2); cultural significance (1); biophysical variables (2); human variables (2); carbon stock (6); carbon gains (2); cultural values (1); aesthetic benefits (1); ecosystem service objectives (2); landscape management (3); forest restoration (1); reforestation (1); social media photographs (10); machine learning (2),,,
_Richards and Tunçer-2018-Using image recognition to autom_1.txt,Using image recognition to automate assessment of cultural ecosystem,Daniel R. Richards; Bige Tunçer,"ETH Zurich, Future Cities Laboratory, Singapore-ETH Centre, Singapore; Architecture and Sustainable Design, Singapore University of Technology and Design, Singapore",2017,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier B.V.,,www.elsevier.com/locate/ecoser,,318–325,Cultural Heritage (1),,,heritage values (1),,"{'Social': 'The study mentions recreational, spiritual, and heritage values as cultural ecosystem services. This suggests a discussion of social values related to cultural identity and communal value.'}; {'Economic': 'The study discusses the use of social media data to map cultural use or appreciation of ecosystems, which can inform urban planning. This implies a discussion of economic values related to the function and utility of the asset.'}; {'Political': 'The study mentions that automated assessment and mapping of cultural ecosystem services could be used to inform urban planning, suggesting a discussion of management values related to the incorporation of the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'Aesthetical': 'The study uses social media photographs as indicators of recreational and aesthetic cultural ecosystem service value, implying a discussion of artistic values related to the original product of creativity and imagination.'}",,Singapore (6); Sentosa Island (1); Ubin Island (1); Sungei Buloh (2); Central Catchment (2); Bukit Timah Nature Reserves (1); Sungei Buloh Wetland Nature Reserve (1); Gardens by the Bay (1),Singapore (10); Southeast Asia (2); North America (1),,,Quantifying and mapping cultural ecosystem services is complex because of their intangibility.,"The objectives of the study were (1) to quantify the occurrence of photographs relating to nature, (2) to analyse the drivers of spatial variation in nature photographs, and (3) to assess the accuracy of the automated classiﬁcation by comparison with a manual classiﬁcation conducted by a human.",social media (11); Flickr (6); Tripadvisor (1),photographs (17); geo-tagged photographs (4); geo-tagged images (1); text (1),geo-tagged (2),,,,tags (2); title (1); notes (1); comments (1),"130,115 unique photographs (1)",4174 photographers (1),,25000 photographs (2),,,200 million geo-tagged photographs (1),between 8/12/2015 and 5/6/2016 (1),"mainland Singapore, Sentosa Island, and Ubin Island (1)",laid out a regular grid of 165 locations and extracted all geo-tagged photographs from the social media website Flickr (1),Google Cloud Vision API (4),A random subset of 25000 photographs from Flickr (approximately 20% of the total) was then analysed further using image recognition. (1),,,manual (5),human (3),"['transport (n = 2768)', 'plant (n = 2485)', 'animal (n = 2951)', 'food (n = 1540)', 'people (n = 1951)', 'sports (n = 727)', 'landscape and miscellaneous photographs (n = 10,626)']",weighted Cohen’s kappa of the agreement between the manual and automated classiﬁcation was 0.83. (1),classification (10); clustering (5); image recognition (10),machine learning algorithm (2),Google Cloud Vision (7); Max Ent (6),,,,keywords (5); distance from the nearest major outdoor attraction (2); the presence of parks including nature reserves (2); the proportional coverage of forest within 50 m (2); the proportional coverage of managed vegetation within 0.01 km2 grid squares (2),,"80% of the dataset was used to train the model, and 20% was used to test the accuracy (1)",,,,,,,,{},,high-power cloud computing (1),,,,,,"Each image was sent to the Google Cloud Vision API, which used a machine learning algorithm to assign keywords to the images (Google Cloud Vision, 2017). The Google Cloud Vision API was accessed through the Roogle Vision package for the R statistical programming language (Teschner, 2016). A maximum of ﬁve keywords were returned for each image (Teschner, 2016).",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (7); percentage error (1); confusion matrix (2); weighted Cohen’s kappa (2); area under the curve (AUC) (2); receiver operating characteristic (ROC) plot (1),"['The overall accuracy of the photograph classiﬁcation was 85%. (1)', 'The weighted Cohen’s kappa of the agreement between the manual and automated classiﬁcation was 0.83. (1)', 'The AUC was high for both models; for the plant photographs the training AUC was 0.85 and the test AUC was 0.89, while for the social recreation photographs the training AUC was 0.87 and the test AUC was 0.89. (1)']","['The photograph group with the lowest accuracy was the landscape and miscellaneous category with an accuracy of 65% (Table 2). (1)', 'The accuracy of the plants category was 85% and the animals category was 95% (Table 2). (1)']",[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['Nature photographs tended to focus on particular organismal groups, such as ﬂowers, trees, and birds (Table 1), indicating that these taxa were either more common, more noticeable, or of greater interest to the photographer community (Richards et al., 2015). (1)']",[],[],[],[],['Nature photographs were not taken uniformly across Singapore but were clustered around key locations (Fig. 4). (1)'],"['Automated assessment and mapping of cultural ecosystem services could help to capture some of the complex value of these services, and could be used to inform urban and regional planning of land uses. (1)']",[],"['This study presents a method for using a publicly-available image recognition algorithm to interpret the content of a large number of images taken from social media, in order to quantify and map interest in nature. (1)']",[],[],"['The large landscape and miscellaneous category reﬂects a challenge in deﬁning photographs based on a limited amount of information (only ﬁve keywords), when there is a relatively large range of possible keywords. (1)', 'Any analysis of social media data is limited by the biases inherent in the source dataset, particularly as social media are most commonly used by younger people, and different demographic groups use different platforms (Pew Research Center’s Internet & American Life, 2013; van Zanten et al., 2016). (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,"Analysis of social media photographs should therefore not be the only approach applied to quantify cultural ecosystem services, but can be a useful tool to provide quantitative data over large spatial scales, that can supplement more in-depth qualitative analyses (Richards and Friess, 2015; Thiagarajah et al., 2015). (1)",,,"The Google Cloud Vision API was accessed through the Roogle Vision package for the R statistical programming language (Teschner, 2016). (1)",R (3),,cultural ecosystem services (10); ecosystem services (4); recreational ecosystem services (1); cultural use (1); cultural appreciation (1); nature tourism (1); urban greening programme (1),,,
_Riva and Agostino-2022-Latent dimensions of museum expe.txt,Latent dimensions of museum experience: A statistical analysis of online reviews,Paola Riva; Deborah Agostino,Politecnico di Milano; Politecnico di Milano,2022,journal article,Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/rmmc20,Taylor & Francis Group,,https://polimi-it.zoom.us/j/3163051571,,,Cultural Heritage (3),,Italian museums (4); Italian state museums (2),cultural heritage (3); museum management (2); museum artefacts (2); museum’s history (1); tradition (1); cultural landscape (1); museum building (1); exhibited collection (1); artworks (2); linguistic inclusion (2),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study identifies different dimensions of museum visit experience based on the language of the reviewers, suggesting that cultural background shapes perceptions of the museum experience, which relates to cultural identity and sense of place attachment.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study explores the latent dimensions of museum visitor experience, which can provide knowledge about the past and cultural heritage to visitors.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study analyzes online reviews to understand how visitors perceive the artistic and aesthetic aspects of museums, such as exhibited collections and artworks.'}",,Italian museums (2); Italian state museums (2); Terracotta Army Museum in China (1),"Italian (11); Italy (2); Phuket, Thailand (1); Barcelona (1); Europe (1); Parisian (1); Southern Poland (1)",,,Museums must assess visitors’ experience to understand them and their needs.,(1) RQ1: What are the latent dimensions of the museum visitor experience perceived by visitors who write reviews in the local language? (2) RQ2: What are the latent dimensions of museum visitor experience perceived by visitors who write reviews in non-local languages? (3) RQ3: What are (if any) the diﬀerences in latent dimensions of museum visitor experience perceived by visitors who write r