_Rossi et al.-2018-Venice through the Lens of Insta.txt,Venice through the Lens of Instagram,,,2018,conference paper,"WWW ’18 Companion, April 23–27, 2018, Lyon, France",ACM,https://doi.org/10.1145/3184558.3191557,https://doi.org/10.1145/3184558.3191557,,,,architectural,city of Venice (Italy) (2); architectural elements (2); bridges; churches; squares; Venetian masks (2),,,,,"Venice; Venice “Santa Lucia"" railway station; “San Marco"" square; “Rialto"" bridge; Biennale venues; “Venetian Arsenal""; “Guggenheim Museum""; “Palazzo Grassi""; “San Giorgio Maggiore""","Italy (2); Venice; Zaragoza, Spain",,Venice Carnival,In this paper we investigate the use of Instagram photos to analyse tourism consumption.,"In this paper, we propose to use images collected from Instagram to investigate tourism consumption in the city of Venice (Italy).",Instagram (14); Flickr (4); Foursquare,images (12); photos (14); metadata (2); textual information (2); geotagged data (3); tags (3); comments (2),geotagged (3); latitude/longitude coordinates (2),spatial distribution (3); heatmap (5); geographical distribution (4),temporal distribution (2); time (3); time series,,univocal ID of the image; url of the image; latitude and longitude of picture; tags; comments,90k photos (4),,,90k,,,90k,January 2014 and December 2015 (3),Venice,We retrieved a set of approximately 90k geotagged photos taken in Venice between January 2014 and December 2015 using the Instagram API,Instagram API (2),"We made use of the Media Search endpoint, which allowed us to search a circular area on a certain date by specifying a pair of latitude/longitude coordinates, a radius, and a date.",,,manual,,"['Lagoon', 'Townscape', 'Art', 'Folklore', 'Food', 'Other']",,classification (6),Support Vector Machine (SVM) (2); Convolutional Neural Network,,,Bag-of-Words (BOW) (3),Scale-Invariant Feature Transform (SIFT) (3),image,,5-fold cross validation (3),,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"The image classi￿cation framework is as follows. We use a version of the dense SIFT algorithm [10] implemented in the VLFeat library [22] to extract a set of keypoints for each image in the dataset. A SIFT keypoint is a circular region of the image with an associated feature vector which describes the local patch appearance. While being fast to compute, SIFT features are designed to provide invariance to image translation, scaling, and rotation, as well as partial invariance to illumination changes and robustness to local geometric distortions. With these feature vectors to hand, we use k-means to cluster them and extract a set of codewords that form the dictionary of our BOW representation [18]. Given an image with an associated set of SIFT features and a dictionary of codewords, we assign each feature to the closest codeword, allowing us to represent the original image as an histogram over the codewords. For a set of labelled images, the corresponding histograms are used to train a set of one-vs-one Support Vector Machine (SVM) classi￿ers using a Radial Basis Function (RBF) kernel [23].",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (2); f-measure (2),"['The average classi\uffffcation accuracy is 68% and the f-measure is 68%.', 'The corresponding average accuracy is 69% and the f-measure is 70%.']",[],[],,"These￿ndings provide a strong qualitative validation of the correctness of the image classi￿er, which further supports the quantitative evaluation of the previous sections.",[],[],"For example, we have seen that in some cases the classi￿er is unable to reach a clear consensus on the class membership of some pictures (see Fig. 3(b)).",[],[],[],5-fold cross validation (3),,[],heatmap (5); bar chart,"['presence of a number of touristic hotspots, associated with popular cultural as well as gastronomical venues.', 'spatial distribution of the photos can reveal the presence of preferred touristic routes during some events, as in the case of the Folklore Line during the Carnival period.']","['clear rise in Instagram activity over Venice.', 'presence of a peak in Instagram activity in the periods of Carnival in 2014 and 2015, re\uffffecting the increased number of tourists during Venice Carnival.']",[],[],[],"['emergence of the Folklore Line during the Carnival period, which instead disappears during the two weeks of March we considered.']","['urban planning', 'marketing', 'advertisement campaigns']",[],[],[],[],"['in some cases the classi\uffffer is unable to reach a clear consensus on the class membership of some pictures', 'some images are wrongly assigned to the Other class, thus reducing the amount of data that we can analyse.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,VLFeat library,,tourism consumption (4); touristic hotspots (2); touristic flows; Folklore Line (5); Venice Carnival (5); Biennale (2); Venice Film Festival; Art Biennale (2); Venetian folklore (2); Venetian masks (3); urban planning (2); marketing (2); gastronomical venues,,,
_Ruiz-Frau et al.-2020-Using graph theory and social me.txt,Using graph theory and social media data to assess cultural ecosystem services in coastal areas: Method development and application,A. Ruiz-Frau; A. Ospina-Alvarez; S. Villasanteb; P. Pita; I. Maya-Jariegod; S. de Juane,"Department of Marine Ecosystem Dynamics IMEDEA (CSIC-UIB), Miquel Marqués 21, 07190 Esporles, Spain; Faculty of Economics and Business Administration, University of Santiago de Compostela, Av Burgo das Nacions s/n, 15782 Santiago de Compostela, A Coruña, Spain; Campus Do Mar, International Campus of Excellence, Spain; Department of Social Psychology, Universidad de Sevilla, Calle Camilo José Cela s/n, 41018 Seville, Spain; Institute of Marine Science (ICM-CSIC), Passeig Marítim de la Barceloneta, n 37-49, 08003 Barcelona, Spain",2020,journal article,Ecosystem Services,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2020.101176,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2020.101176,,,,,coastal areas (1),,,,conservation (1); management (3),Great Barrier Reef (GBR) Marine Park; Galapagos Islands National Park; Easter Island National Park; Great Barrier Reef; Galapagos Islands; Easter Island,Spain; Australia; Ecuador; Chile,,,"Defining human-nature interactions in coastal areas and the type of CES offered, e.g. the activities people undertake, what they value, what habitats or species attract most attention, at scales relevant for marine and coastal management is time consuming and often requires resources which are not generally available (Waldron et al., 2013).","(i) to illustrate the application of a novel methodology to assess the demand of CES through GTNA, (ii) to compare the proposed methodology to existing image content analysis techniques, and (iii) to explore the complementarity of information extracted from different SM platforms.",social media (SM) (13); Facebook (3); You Tube (1); Instagram (15); Twitter (10); Flickr (5),data created and shared by users on SM platforms (1); tags (2); text (5); images (10); videos (2),,spatial distribution (2),,,user’s names (1); ids (1),ten thousand posts (3),"90 million monthly active users (Flickr, 2018), 1 billion monthly active users (Instagram, 2018), 2.26 billion users (Facebook)",,,,,,June 2019,"Great Barrier Reef (GBR) Marine Park in Australia, the Galapagos Islands National Park in Ecuador and Easter Island National Park in Chile",posts were downloaded through the corresponding application programming interface (API).,"Instagram public API, Twitter developer premium account","A sensitivity analysis using accumulation curves, representing the number f l t d h ht l t d ith th li ff t i di t d most used hashtags in each of the study areas.","datasets were manually filtered and cleaned in order to retain only relevant information for further analysis (Di Minin et al., 2018; Varol et al., 2017).","At user level, those users identified as bots were removed from the database. At post level, irrelevant posts related to advertisement (e.g., posts related to trading marks) were discarded from the analysis. At hashtag level, non-informative hashtags such as #instapic, #instaphoto, #instamood, #like4like, were removed from the database in order to obtain cleaner networks at a later stage.","manual procedure, undertaken by the authors of this paper, and an automatic procedure through machine learning technology.",authors,[],"Cohen’s Kappa coefficient (Cohen, 1960) was used to assess the level of agreement between the reviewers.",classification (4),,Captionbot (5),,,,photographs (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,"['graph theory network analyses (GTNA) (4)', 'social networks (2)']",vertices are represented by hashtags (2),edges illustrate the connections between hashtags (e.g. the hashtags included in the same posts and the frequency of those connections) (1),,,centrality measures (4); community structure (3),,"fast greedy modularity optimization algorithm (Clauset et al., 2004) (1)",,assess relationships between hashtags and identify emerging properties within the networks (1),"Cohen’s Kappa coefficient (Cohen, 1960) (3)",[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Here, we introduce GTNA as a novel way to analyse different sources of\nSM data to assess CES.']",[],[],"['SM studies suffer from several biases and limitations.', 'individual platforms suffer from population and content bias due to an unbalanced user composition and specific use of the platform (Ruths and Pfeffer, 2014).', 'the views and perceptions of people from areas of the world where no access or censorship might preclude the use of SM platforms, cannot be captured through this media.', 'the limitation associated with the translation to and choice of English as the main vehicle for this study.']","['For privacy and ethical reasons, no personal information, such as user’s names or ids, were used in this study.', 'Results are presented in an aggregate manner so that no information can be traced back to the individual user.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],A specific development in R was made by the authors for each API and similar data was extracted from the two SM platforms.,,,,"Data mining, analyses and graphical outputs were generated using R, a free and open source software (R Core Team, 2019).","R (2); igraph (1); Csardi and Nepusz, 2006 (1); ggraph v2.0 (1); Pedersen, 2020 (1)",,marine protected areas (1); marine ecosystem services (ES) (1); coastal ecosystem services (CES) (2); marine and coastal areas (4); relational values (7); eudaimonic (4); nature-inclusive eudaimonia (1); nature lovers (1); save our oceans (1); save the planet (1); cultural heritage preservation (1),,,
