_Shao et al.-2019-Personalized Travel Recommendati.txt,Personalized Travel Recommendation Based on Sentiment-Aware Multi-Modal Topic Model,X. Shao; J. Wang; B.-K. Bao; C. Xu,,2019,other,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"due to the rapid development of these travel websites, a large amount of unorganized information hinders users from quickly and efﬁciently ﬁnding the desired tourist attractions.","-We propose a SMTM model which takes into account of three preliminary factors in traveling recommendation problem and the advantages of SMTM model include: (1) the multi-modal data both for tourist domain and attraction domain is fully exploited for better semantics disclosing; (2) the topics in tourists domain and the themes in attractions domain are separately modeled for better disclosing rela- tionships in corresponding semantic space; (3) the tourist sentiments on topics are studied to obtain traveler’s opinion. -We propose a SMTM based traveling recommendation framework which employs mutual document projection for tourist domain and for attraction domain into a latent seman- tic space, and this latent space projection scheme can be further applied to two personalized recommendations, that is, the single platform traveling recommendation and the inter- platform traveling recommendation.",Trip Advisor; Trip,textual comments; images; sentiments,,,,,,"459,180 textual comments or descrip- tions, and 43,964 images from 14,648 tourist documents, while 392,680 textual comments and 26,182 images from 8,724 attraction documents",,,,,,,,,collected multi-modality data from tourist and attraction domains respectively,,,,,,,[],,topic modeling; sentiment analysis,Topic Models; Probabilistic Topic Models,LDA; SMTM; MMLDA; TS,,,SIFT-Bow features,text; images; sentiments,,80% are randomly selected for training and the remaining 20% are used for testing,,,,,200 Gibbs sampling iterations,,,"{'Dirichlet hyper parameters': 'βu 0 = βu 1 = βa 0 = βa 0 = 0.02', 'symmetric priors': 'αu = αa = 50/K, ηu = ηa = 0.01'}",LDA; Multimodal LDA (MMLDA); Topic-Sentiment (TS),,,,,,,"To simulate generation process of the observed tourists’ posts and the attraction documents, which are generated by different users from different perspec- tive, we ﬁrstly split the corpus on the platform into tourist domain and attraction domain. Then we perform semantic analysis for documents in two domains respectively, and asso- ciate them in some semantic spaces. Finally we try to capture the correlation between tourist topic space and attraction theme spaces for recommendation.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,perplexity; Precision; Mean Average Precision (MAP),[],[],[],,,[],,,[],[],[],,LDA; MMLDA; TS,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['traveling recommendation applications, such as attraction recommen-']",[],"['a SMTM is proposed to solve the travel recommendation problem by jointly modeling attraction theme, tourist preference and sentiment of the attractions']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,tourist; attraction; multi-modal data; sentiment analysis; topic model; Gibbs sampling; Senti Word Net; SIFT-Bow features; Part-of-Speech,,,
_Sharma and Jana-2025-Semantic Part Segmentation of He.txt,Semantic Part Segmentation of Heritage,Sharma; Jana,,2025,conference paper,2025 National Conference on Communications (NCC),IEEE,,,,,Cultural Heritage (1),,heritage monuments (3); Mughal monument (1); Mughal architecture (3); Taj Mahal (4); Humayun’s Tomb (2); Bibi Ka Maqbara (2); architectural heritage sites (1),preservation (1); 3D modeling (2); heritage sites (2); cultural assets (1); heritage architecture (2); heritage imagery (1); digital documentation (1); cultural heritage (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study focuses on heritage monuments like the Taj Mahal, Humayun’s Tomb, and Bibi Ka Maqbara, which are significant for cultural identity, pride, and a sense of place attachment for many people.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The paper mentions that heritage monuments offer insights into artistic accomplishments, architectural evolution, and civilizational history, indicating their potential to provide knowledge about the past.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The paper discusses the artistic accomplishments and architectural evolution reflected in heritage monuments, suggesting an appreciation for their original creativity and imagination.'}",,Taj Mahal; Humayun’s Tomb; Bibi Ka Maqbara,Rome (1); India (2); Agra (1); New Delhi (1); Aurangabad (1),Mughal-era (1),,"Given a set of crowdsourced images of a heritage monu- ment captured from varying viewpoints and under different lighting conditions, we aim to consistently segment iden- tical architectural components across all images, ensuring that the same parts are labeled uniformly throughout the dataset.","we aim to consistently segment iden- tical architectural components across all images, ensuring that the same parts are labeled uniformly throughout the dataset.",,crowdsourced images (4); 2D images (2); unstructured imagery (1),,,,,,1312 images (1),,,,,,,,,automated web crawler (1),,retaining the top 30 images per site based on quality and relevance for Sf M reconstruction (1),,,,,[],,semantic segmentation (6); object detection (1),transformer architectures (2); Fully Convolutional Networks (FCNs) (1),Segment Anything Model (SAM) (8); Mask R-CNN (1); Deep Lab (1); Segmenter (1); Mask2Former (1); DINO (1); CLIP (1); Language Segment Anything (LSA) (2); Personalized SAM (2),,image embeddings (1),,camera poses (3); 3D spatial context (3); bounding boxes (2); 2D bounding boxes (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,Point Containment Accuracy (PCA) (2); Intersection over Union (Io U) (3),[],[],[],,"The resulting segmenta- tions showed a high degree of coherence: domes were consistently identified and separated from minarets, the main body was clearly delineated, and chhatris and bases were reliably distinguished across diverse viewpoints and lighting conditions.",[],"This consistency is a significant im- provement over applying SAM alone, where semantically similar parts lack stable labels across images.","However, certain cases exhibited segmentation failures, primarily due to extreme pose variations (Figure 7).",[],"These include highly tilted or close-up views where only a fraction of the monument is visible, leading to incorrect or missing architectural labels. Additionally, strong occlusions, per- spective distortions, and lighting inconsistencies disrupted the alignment between 3D-projected bounding boxes and actual features in the image, particularly affecting smaller elements such as chhatris and minarets.",[],,Language Segment Anything (LSA); Personalized SAM,"As shown in Table I, our method surpasses both LSA and Per SAM across both metrics, demonstrating superior segmentation accuracy.",,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['we introduced an Sf M-guided framework inte- grating sparse 3D reconstruction with the Segment Any- thing Model (SAM) to achieve consistent semantic part segmentation of heritage imagery.'],[],[],"['However, this study represents an early attempt to fully automate fine-grained semantic analysis of heritage monuments.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Available: https://colmap.github.io/.,,,"Available: https://image-matching-workshop.github.io/IMW2020/, Accessed: Dec. 2024.",,,,sparse 3D reconstruction (3); Structure-from-Motion (Sf M) (11); crowdsourced images (2); architectural elements (3); camera parameters (3); segmentation masks (3); 3D point cloud (3); semantic labels (3); architectural components (2); fine-grained segmentation (2); semantic consistency (2); part-level segmentation (3); foundational models (2); Mughal architecture (2); neural rendering methods (1); Neural Radiance Fields (Ne RF) (1); photogrammetry (2); laser scanning (1); drone-based surveys (1); pixel-level prediction (1); region proposal network (RPN) (1); dilated convolutions (1); atrous spatial pyramid pooling (1); masked attention (1); contrastive losses (1); cross-view consistency (1); category-agnostic masks (1); vision-language model (1); text and image embeddings (1); expert-defined vocabularies (1); ambiguous features (1); sparse 3D geometry (2); inter-image labeling (1); geometric context (1); frontal view (1); feature point density (1); connected components labeling (1); outlier removal (1); morphological operations (1); axis-aligned 3D bounding boxes (1); pose variations (1); perspective distortions (1); lighting inconsistencies (1); ground truth annotations (1); proxy mea- sures (1); spatial overlap (1); semantic maps (1); construction techniques (1); decorative traditions (1); cultural contexts (1); digital documentation (1); diverse architectures (1); architectural motifs (1); unstructured internet imagery (1),,,
