_Song et al.-2024-TravelRAG A Tourist Attraction.txt,Travel RAG: A Tourist Attraction Retrieval Framework Based on Multi-Layer Knowledge Graph,Sihan Song; Chuncheng Yang; Li Xu; Haibin Shang; Zhuo Li; Yinghui Chang,Opening Fund of Key Laboratory of Geological Survey and Evaluation of Ministry of Education; Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities,2024,other,,,,,,,,,tourist attractions (1),cultural heritage (1),,,,Xitang (4),China (1); Italy (1),,,This framework seeks to address the challenges LLMs face in providing precise and contextually appropriate responses to domain-specific queries in the tourism field.,Travel RAG extracts information related to tourist attractions from User-Generated Content (UGC) on social media platforms and organizes it into a multi-layer knowledge graph.,,text (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,gathered from social platforms (1),,,,,,,[],,retrieval (6); knowledge graph construction (2),Transformer models (1); large language model (LLM) (4),Qwen2-Travel RAG (1); GPT-4-Travel Rag (1); GPT-4o-RAG (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,"['knowledge graph (11)', 'multi-layer knowledge graph (4)', 'tourism knowledge graph (2)']",entities (3),relational linking (1),,,,,,,"This knowledge graph also acts as the retrieval source for the LLM, guiding the model to generate answers based on this structured information.",accuracy (3); retrieval efficiency (1),[],[],[],,"Travel RAG provides users with more accurate, detailed and contextually relevant responses to their queries.",[],[],,[],[],[],,traditional RAG pipelines (2),[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['although LLMs have improved in understanding long contexts, they still struggle to accurately parse content in travelogues, which are rich in complex semantic information.', 'the construction of the knowledge graph depends on manually crafted prompts, which increases the labor cost of creating prompt templates.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,All data that support the findings of the study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.,,,,,,User-Generated Content (UGC) (1); travelogues (1); hallucinations (2),,,
_Song et al.-2025-Decoding the cultural heritage t.txt,Decoding the cultural heritage tourism landscape and visitor crowding,Huimin Song; Jinliu Chen; Pengcheng Li,"College of Tourism, Huaqiao University, Quanzhou, 362021, China; City University of Macau-Huaqiao University Joint Tourism Research Center, Macau 999078, China; Suzhou Institute of Future City Design, Suzhou City University, Suzhou, 215000, China; Purple Academy of Culture & Creativity, Nanjing University of the Arts, Nanjing, 210000, China; China-Portugal Joint Laboratory of Cultural Heritage Conservation Science, Suzhou, 215000, China; School of Architecture and Urban Planning, Suzhou University of Science and Technology, Suzhou, 215000, China",2025,journal article,Tourism Management,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tourman.2025.105180,https://www.elsevier.com/locate/tourman,,,Cultural Heritage (11),tangible (1); intangible (1),cultural heritage tourism landscapes (6); Chinese classical gardens (2); classical gardens of Suzhou (2); classical heritage gardens; heritage cultural landscapes (4); ancient Chinese garden morphology,cultural heritage elements (1); Heritage tourism (1); cultural vitality (1); Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) (1); cultural heritage spatial needs (1); embodied dimension (1); UNESCO (2); cultural heritage tourism space (1); heritage cultural spaces (1); heritage cultural landscapes (2); cultural heritage (1); heritage tourism sites (1); heritage tourism (1); cultural narratives (1); heritage sites (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'The study emphasizes community cohesion and social well-being by focusing on the preservation of historical and cultural diversity through cultural heritage landscapes. It also explores how tourism impacts social interactions and perceptions within these landscapes.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'The study discusses the economic development and tourism experience related to cultural heritage landscapes, highlighting the role of tourism in promoting economic benefits and the need for effective management and rational utilization of these landscapes.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'The study implicitly discusses the management and planning of cultural heritage landscapes, emphasizing the need for effective management and rational utilization to enhance tourist experience and satisfaction. It also touches on the role of cultural heritage in strengthening community cohesion.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'The study focuses on classical Chinese heritage gardens in Suzhou, which represent typical cultural heritage tourism landscapes. It explores the historical and cultural significance of these gardens and their role in preserving historical and cultural diversity.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'The study explores the aesthetic qualities of classical Chinese gardens, focusing on elements such as water, stone, plants, and architecture. It also discusses how spatial elements and visual combinations influence visitors’ crowding behaviors.'}; {'SCIENTIFIC VALUES': 'The study utilizes machine learning and mobile sensing technologies to evaluate the spatial quality of cultural heritage tourism in classical Suzhou gardens. It also explores the impact of physical microclimatic environmental factors on visitors’ crowding behavior.'}; {'AGE VALUES': 'The study focuses on classical gardens of Suzhou, an ancient city with a history spanning 2500 years. These gardens are considered the heart of Suzhou’s physical heritage and embody a microcosm of the world.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': 'The study explores the harmony between the built environment and the natural environment in classical Chinese gardens. It also discusses the importance of microclimatic conditions and environmental factors in shaping visitors’ experiences.'}",management (3); conservation (1); planning (2),classical gardens of Suzhou (2); Suzhou’s classical garden heritages; Yiyuan; Keyuan,China (4); Macau (1); Yangtze River Delta (1); Europe (1); Mexico (1); Los Angeles county (1); Hong Kong (1); Italy (1),ancient (1),,"Thus, this raises the critical question of how a framework for evaluating the quality of cultural heritage tourism landscapes can be constructed from the perspective of the multidimensional embodied in order to enhance tourist experience and satisfaction","This study aims to propose a multidimensional embodied cognition framework, which explores how the brain processes and interprets sensory information from the environment (Chen, Zhao, et al., 2024), forming an integrated and collective understanding of the surroundings based on a ""space-perception-behavior"" framework.",social media (10); Weibo (7); Facebook (2); Twitter (2); X (1),social media data (3); panoramic visual imagery (1); social media text data (1); spatial onsite crowding behavior (1); online check-in data (1); panoramic images (2); text (1); images (2); geotagged posts (2),geotagged posts (1),spatial density analysis (2); spatial distribution (4); spatial relationships (2); spatial openness (1),temporal conditions (1); temporal patterns (2); timestamps (2),,usernames (2); review timestamps (1),91379,,,,,,,2023–2024,ten gardens in Suzhou,"indirect data collection methods such as mobile sensing technology, panoramic imagery, and social media data",,,"data format conversion, outlier removal, and privacy protection measures",,,,[],,semantic segmentation (3); scene parsing (1); regression (3),machine learning (ML) (8); multilevel regression models (1),PSPNet (2); OLS (3); RF algorithm (2),,,,panoramic image features (2); TEMP (6); HUMI (7); PM10 (5); PM2.5 (7); Wi-Fi signal strength (4); noise levels (4); smell (2); architectural characteristics (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,R-squared value (4); Pearson correlation coefficient (r) (3),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['Virtual and augmented reality can be used to craft immersive experi\xadences that deepen visitors’ interactions with the gardens’ historical and cultural narratives.'],"['This finding provides destination managers with strategic guidance on enhancing heritage tourism spaces, improving spatial quality, and creating ""high-perception"" environments.']",['This study introduced a multidimensional embodied cognition framework using the “space-perception-behavior” triad to explore how cultural heritage landscapes influence visitors’ crowding behaviors.'],"['Empirical analysis of ten classical Suzhou gardens revealed that spatial elements, such as SVI and the window-to-wall ratio, significantly affect visitors’ behavior.', 'Additionally, each 1% increase in HUMI is correlated with a 13-percentage point increase in crowd density.']","['Meanwhile, this research lies in the development of a synergistic system of embodied cognition and tourism spatial quality evaluation to elucidate the in\xadteractions among spatial quality, individual behavior, and perception.']","['The comprehen\xadsiveness of the evaluation system is constrained by the need for longterm monitoring and the exploration of additional embodied cogni\xadtions, such as soundscapes and tactile experiences.', 'While advanced technologies such as ML were utilized, potential biases in data collection and processing, especially in quantifying subjective perceptions, remain.']","['To ensure data privacy and security, the collected data un\xadderwent preprocessing steps, including data format conversion, outlier removal, and privacy protection measures.']","['Traditional survey methods are often prone to recall bias, where participants’ memories may distort their perceptions of the environment, and to sample limitations arising from individual differ\xadences in environmental perception.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],,Not applicable,,,,Arc GIS,,tourism spatial quality evaluation (1); Visitors’ crowding behavior (4); microcosm of the world (1); embodied spatial perception (1); spatial quality index (1); spatial distribution characteristics (1); associative mechanisms (1); spatial distribution patterns (2); crowd aggregation (3); bioclimatic indicators (2); crowd dispersion (1); crowd density (3); spatial theories (1); governance mechanisms (1); thematic events (1); immersive experiences (1); word-of-mouth promotions (2); leisure (1); smart misting systems (1); air purifiers (1); sound-absorbing vegetation (1); acoustic barriers (1); Io T technologies (1); visual obstructions (1); social functionality (1); community cohesion (2); urban space management (1); digital landscape design (1); VR/AR simulation (1); restorative environmental studies (1); AIGC (1); GIS (2),,,"This study, framed through multidimensional embodied cogni­tion, identifies key factors influencing both crowding behavior and the cultural value of heritage sites, offering governance strategies for short-, medium-, and long-term management."
_Sontayasara et al.-2021-Twitter Sentiment Analysis of Ba.txt,Twitter Sentiment Analysis of Bangkok Tourism During COVID-19 Pandemic Using Support Vector Machine Algorithm,Thanapat Sontayasara; Sirawit Jariyapongpaiboon; Arnon Promjun; Napat Seelpipat; Kumpol Saengtabtim; Jing Tang; Natt Leelawat,"Department of Industrial Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Chulalongkorn University 254 Phayathai Road, Pathumwan, Bangkok 10330, Thailand; International School of Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand; Disaster and Risk Management Information Systems Research Group, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand",2020,other,,,,,,,,,Bangkok (5),,,,,Bangkok,"Thailand (10); Wuhan City, China; Middle East; Africa; South East Asia",,COVID-19 pandemic (14),"In this research, the views of people who were planning to travel to Bangkok, the capital city and most popular destination in Thailand, were retrieved from Twitter for the dates between April 3 and 30, 2020, the period during which the country underwent nationwide lockdown.","This research aims to identify the tourist sentiment (i.e., how foreign tourists feel) toward the Bangkok metropolitan area during the beginning of the lockdown in Thailand, by analyzing tourists’ reviews on online platforms.",Twitter (6),text (4),,,,,,"1,101 tweets",,,,,,,"April 3 to 30, 2020",Bangkok,collected using the Twitter API,Twitter API,,data preprocessing was performed in this research. The actual process started after the data that were not related to tourism in Bangkok were removed from the dataset,data that were not related to tourism in Bangkok were removed from the dataset,manual labeling,researchers,"['positive', 'negative', 'neutral']",,sentiment analysis (3); classification (6),Support Vector Machines (SVM) (10),SVM (14),,,TF-IDF,text,,75%/25%,,,,,,C,,"{'C': '10', 'Gamma': '0.1'}",,,,,,,,"The training data set was used to train the SVM. After that, the SVM was predicted by the testing data set to show its accuracy. If the accuracy was not satisﬁed, the SVM improved its accuracy using the shufﬂe–split cross-validation to ﬁnd the best kernel types and parameter values.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (7); precision (2); recall (2); F-1 score,"['71.03% classiﬁcation accuracy', 'precision was 81.65%', 'recall was 55.14%', 'F-1 score was 57.95%']","['The prediction accuracies of the negative and positive sentiments were 21.21% and 96.67%, respectively.']",[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],shuffle–split cross-validation,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['the result of this study can support the decision to plan potential tourism policies for promotion and campaigning'],"['provide suggestions to organizations related to the tourism industry, such as the Tourism Authorities of Thailand, for pointing out the strengths and weaknesses of Bangkok metropolitant tourism during the\nCOVID-19 pandemic.']",[],[],[],"['it is necessary to consider the small size of the data set as a limitation of this study.', 'each sentiment class’s unequal amount of data can create some subjective bias in the analysis.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,NLTK,,tourism (12); tourist (2); travelers (3),,,
_Sopyryaeva et al.-2024-A Lexicon for Social Media-Based.txt,A Lexicon for Social Media-Based Cultural Heritage Information in Crisis Situations,Elisabetta Ronchieri,,2024,other,Po S(ISGC2024)009,,,,,18,cultural heritage (15),tangible (3); intangible (3); natural (2),,cultural preservation (2); UNESCO (7); Cultural Statistics (3); tangible heritage (2); intangible cultural heritage (2); natural heritage (1); world heritage (1); heritage list (1); world list (1); heritage site (1); rich heritage (1); heritage significance (1); national heritage (1),"symbolic (1) including symbolic, historic, artistic, aesthetic, ethnological or anthropological, scientific, and social significance.; historic (1) including symbolic, historic, artistic, aesthetic, ethnological or anthropological, scientific, and social significance.; artistic (1) including symbolic, historic, artistic, aesthetic, ethnological or anthropological, scientific, and social significance.; aesthetic (1) including symbolic, historic, artistic, aesthetic, ethnological or anthropological, scientific, and social significance.; ethnological (1) including symbolic, historic, artistic, aesthetic, ethnological or anthropological, scientific, and social significance.; anthropological (1) including symbolic, historic, artistic, aesthetic, ethnological or anthropological, scientific, and social significance.; scientific (1) including symbolic, historic, artistic, aesthetic, ethnological or anthropological, scientific, and social significance.; social (1) including symbolic, historic, artistic, aesthetic, ethnological or anthropological, scientific, and social significance.","Social: Cultural identity is discussed through the mention of national and cultural identity in the context of cultural heritage objects.; Historic: The paper discusses the historical significance of objects within the cultural heritage domain, emphasizing their importance in understanding the past.; Aesthetical: The paper mentions artistic significance as a value of cultural heritage objects, indicating a discussion of their aesthetic qualities and artistic merit.",cultural preservation (2),,USA (1); San Diego (1); San Marino (1); San Antonio (1); Myanmar (2); gaza (1); Africa (1); Nepal (1); Rocky Mountain (1),,2015 nepal earthquake (1),"Instead, this research shifts the focus toward crises’ impact on cultural heritage, which has not been extensively explored in the academic literature.","To facilitate the textual data exploration, we have created lexicons able to detect tweets that hold relevance for the cultural heritage domain during crisis events, extract essential information embedded within these tweets, comprehend the domain, and facilitate further research in the field.",social media platforms (2); Twitter (7); Twitter API (4),textual data (1); tweets (39); messages (4); text (10); hashtags (10); emojis (5),,geographic distribution (1),,,,13 datasets (1),,,,,,,"January 1 to April 26, 2023 (1)",,collected from Twitter API (1),Twitter API (4),by considering 13 different categories (1),"Remove hashtags, emojis, and URLs from tweets (1)",lang:en -is:retweet -is:reply (1),manual labeling (3),,"['general', 'organisation', 'tangible', 'intangible', 'significance', 'about past']",,classification (4); topic detection (2); sentiment analysis (2),machine-learning classification algorithms (2); ML classification algorithms (1),,,,,textual features (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,NLTK (1),,uni-gram (8); bi-gram (12); n-gram (4); stopword (1); lemmatization (3); stemming (2); corpus (4); tweets detection (4); domain lexicons (2); key terms (10); key term set (2); tweet collection (2); tweet detection (2); disaster-related tweets (3); cultural heritage-related tweets (1); vandalism-related tweets (1); disaster lexicon (2); cultural heritage lexicon (2); vandalism lexicon (2); Crisis Lex Rec (8); EMTerms (5); Crisis Lex T6 (1); Emergency Management Terms (1); UNESCO Framework for Cultural Statistics (3),,,
_Soto-Rumiche and Caro-2024-Analysis of the Opinions of User.txt,Analysis of the Opinions of Users,,,,other,,,,,,,Cultural Heritage (6),cultural (3); monumental (2),cultural tourism resources (7); heritage monuments (2); museum exhibitions (1); architectural spaces (1); urban landscape (1); historic monuments (3); historic centre (2); historical monuments (1); industrial monument (1),cultural heritage (2); heritage monuments (2); historical value (1); monumental heritage assets (1); state of conservation (1); architectural value (2); industrial monument (1); environmental component (1); green areas (2); nature tourism (1); symbolic connection (1),"historical value (1) , highlighting the historical value and the synergy with green areas as an ideal complement that make up the urban landscape of the city of Malaga.","{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study highlights the admiration and appreciation for the symbolic connection (historical) of the monumental heritage assets, demonstrating the visitor’s empathy towards the cultural heritage, suggesting a sense of cultural identity and place attachment.'}; {'value': 'Economic Values: Entertainment', 'reasoning': 'The study focuses on the reviews of cultural tourism resources, indicating the role these assets play in the contemporary market, especially tourism.'}; {'value': 'Historic Values: Historic-Artistic', 'reasoning': 'The study mentions the architectural value of the museums and the conservation of an industrial monument, indicating the recognition of rare or unique examples of historical, stylistic, or artistic movements.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical Values: Artistic', 'reasoning': 'The study highlights the works of local artists in museums, indicating the recognition of original products of creativity and imagination.'}",conservation (2); protection (1),Málaga (8); Gibralfaro (2); Alcazaba (2); Cathedral (1); Picasso Museum (2); Automobile and Fashion Museum (2); Royal Tobacco Factory (1),Spain (1); Costa del Sol (2); Madrid (1); Barcelona (1),,,The aim of this research work is to shed light on the online reputation of the cultural heritage of the city of Málaga through reviews on social networks or Trip Advisor opinion web platforms.,"This research analyses the state of mind of Trip Advisor reviews, referring to the cultural tourist resources that conﬁgure the tourist offer of Malaga, which are ideal for the current study, through data mining, as they are unstructured texts. For this purpose, a qualitative analysis is carried out, examining the content, the themes of the reviews, and the feelings expressed by the users. By delving into the qualitative aspects of the reviews, this research aims to provide a deeper understanding of the impact of the reviews on the image of Málaga as a destination.",Trip Advisor (15); social networks (2),text (2); online reviews (7); user-generated content (UGC) (1); unstructured texts (1),,,,ratings (4),user ratings (1),18.227 reviews (1),,,,,,,from 2017 to 2022 (1),municipality of Málaga (2),online reviews have been analysed in Spanish using the phrase “cultural tourism in Málaga” on Trip Advisor.es (1),,,"data structuring has been employed, as proposed by the authors Farhadloo et al. (2016), intending to obtain semi-structured data, following the interest of the analysis of the sentiments of the reviews on Trip Advisor. (1)",,,,[],,sentiment analysis (7); text mining (3),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,['co-occurrence network graph (1)'],words (1),co-occurrence (2),57 (1),,density (1); degrees (1); correlation coefﬁcient (1),co-occurrences (2),,,emphasising the semantic aspect and the high degrees between the terms. (1),,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],"['admiration and appreciation for the symbolic connection (historical) (1)', 'visitor’s empathy towards the cultural heritage (1)', 'appreciation for the state of conservation (1)']",['high approval and high level of satisfaction of visitors (1)'],[],[],[],"['preserving cultural heritage (1)', 'encouraging sustainable practices (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,e WOM (1); UGC (1); online reputation (2); museum spaces (2); cultural tourism (6); cultural sphere (1); tourist offer (2); sentiment status (1); macro data (1); museum purposes (1); Trip Advisor reviews (5),,,
_Souza et al.-2024-Using social media and machine l.txt,Using social media and machine learning to understand sentiments towards Brazilian National Parks,Carolina Neves Souza; Javier Martínez-Arribas; Ricardo A. Correia; Jo˜ao A.G.R. Almeida; Richard Ladle; Ana Sofia Vaz; Ana Cl´audia Malhado,"Institute of Biological and Health Sciences, Federal University of Alagoas, Av. Lourival Melo Mota, s/n, Tabuleiro do Martins, 57072-90 Macei´o, AL, Brazil; CIBIO-In BIO, Research Centre in Biodiversity and Genetic Resources, University of Porto, Campus de Vair˜ao, 4485-661 Vair˜ao, Portugal; Biodiversity Unit, University of Turku, 20014 Turku, Finland; Helsinki Lab of interdisciplinary Conservation Science (HELICS), Department of Geosciences and Geography, University of Helsinki, 00014 Helsinki, Finland; Helsinki Institute of Sustainability Science (HELUS), University of Helsinki, 00014 Helsinki, Finland; Institute of Computing, Federal University of Alagoas, Av. Lourival Melo Mota, s/n, Tabuleiro do Martins, 57072-90 Macei´o, AL, Brazil; BIOPOLIS Program in Genomics, Biodiversity and Land Planning, CIBIO, Vairão, Portugal; NBI, Natural Business Intelligence, R´egia Douro Park, Andr˜aes, Vila Real, Portugal",2024,journal article,Biological Conservation,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2024.110557,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2024.110557,,,cultural heritage (1),natural (3); cultural (2),Brazilian national parks (10),conservation (10); Protected areas (PAs) (20); sustainable use (1); environmental education (1); management (12); governance (2); conservation initiatives (1); visitor experiences (1); conservation strategies (1); ecosystems (1); biodiversity (4); strictly protected areas (1); natural areas (1); ecological significance (1); threatened species (1); scientific research (1); environmental (4); natural heritage (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': {'Spiritual': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Emotional (individual)': 'The study explores sentiments towards national parks, indicating the emotional connection people have with these areas, influencing their behavior and perceptions.', 'Emotional (collective)': 'The study identifies topics that contribute to public perception of national parks, reflecting a collective emotional response to issues like wildfires, security, and regulations.', 'Allegorical': 'Not applicable in this context.'}}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': {'Use': 'The study mentions the recreational activities and assets of national parks, indicating their use value for visitors and society.', 'Non-use': 'The study implicitly discusses the non-use value of national parks by highlighting their importance for biodiversity conservation and ecosystem services.', 'Entertainment': 'The study mentions the potential of national parks to draw society’s attention through natural outdoor scenic attractions.', 'Allegorical': 'Not applicable in this context.'}}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': {'Educational': 'The study highlights the educational role of national parks in promoting environmental awareness and conservation.', 'Management': 'The study focuses on how sentiment analysis can assist in identifying opportunities to improve the management of Brazilian PAs.', 'Entertainment': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Symbolic': 'Not applicable in this context.'}}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': {'Educational': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Historic-Artistic': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Historic-Conceptual': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Symbolic': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Archaeological': 'Not applicable in this context.'}}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': {'Artistic': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Notable': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Conceptual': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Evidential': 'Not applicable in this context.'}}; {'SCIENTIFIC VALUES': {'Workmanship': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Technological': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Conceptual': 'Not applicable in this context.'}}; {'AGE VALUES': {'Workmanship': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Existential': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Maturity': 'Not applicable in this context.'}}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': {'Spiritual': 'Not applicable in this context.', 'Essential': 'The study emphasizes the role of protected areas in the conservation of natural resources and biodiversity.', 'Existential': 'Not applicable in this context.'}}",preservation (2); management (7); conservation (10); protection (3),Brazilian national parks (10); Iguaçu national Park (6); Tijuca national Park (2); Itatiaia national Park (4); Chapada dos Veadeiros national park (2); Lenç´ois Maranhenses national park (2); Chapada dos Guimar˜aes national park (1),"Brazil (12); Brazilian (17); Queensland, Australia (1); Amazon (2); northeast Brazil (1); Cerrado region (1)",2011−2022 (2),oil disaster off the Brazilian coast (1),"in Brazil, where limited resources and poor effectiveness lead to negative sentiments and are leveraged as criticism towards PAs, it is necessary to better comprehend public perceptions of Brazilian PAs and identify the key factors contributing to negative sentiments.","to understand the people’s perception about the Brazilian national parks through so­ cial media and sentiment analysis in the Portuguese language. Specif­ ically, we aim to identify the key topics that contribute to the public perceptions (positive, neutral or negative) of Brazilian national parks.",Twitter (10); Facebook (1); Instagram (3); X (1),text (4); images (2); videos (2); textual content (2); geographic data (1),geographic data (1); geographical metadata (1),,time series analysis (2); daily (1),likes (1); retweets (1); replies (1),"users (author_id, name, username) (1); date of publi­cation (1); geographic data of tweets (1); publication data (number of likes, retweets, replies and whether it is a reply to another user) (1); text (1)","106,240 (2)","38,432 (1)",,,,,,2011–2022 (3),Brazil (1),"collected textual content from Twitter posts, using the Twitter v2 API associated with a Twitter’s Academic access which allowed it to collect up to 10 million tweets monthly, access older conversation histories, and apply more search filters than the basic API.",Twitter v2 API (1),"A query composed of 18 keywords in Portuguese language (See supplementary material) was selected to extract tweets related to 11 categories of protected areas in Brazil, including national parks","filter was applied to the geographical metadata provided by Twitter, specifically in the ‘country’ and ‘country_code’ columns, to identify tweets originating from coun­ tries other than Brazil. Next, a manual review of each originally foreign tweet was carried out to exclude those whose textual content was not related to Brazilian protected areas",tweets that fell into the category of national parks,manually and independently annotated a random sample of 2000 tweets from our dataset into three classes of sentiment polarity,manual,"['positive', 'neutral', 'negative']",,sentiment analysis (8); topic modelling (3); classification (6); clustering (2),transformer embeddings (1),BERTimbau Base model (2); BERTopic model (4); BERT (4); Naive Bayes (1); Maximum Entropy (1); Support Vector Machine (SVM) (2),adapted a pre-trained BERTimbau Base model (1),transformer embeddings (1); BERTbase-portuguese-cased model (1),,text (1),,,,,,,two-epoch (1),,,"{'n_neighbors': '15', 'n_components': '5', 'min_dist': '0', 'metric': 'Euclidean', 'prediction_data': 'True', 'min_cluster_size': 'depending on the number of observations we have', 'nr_topics': 'auto'}",,,,,,,trained bidirectionally (1),"We used data similar to another study by Avanço and Nunes (2014), properly preprocessed to better adapt these data to the target tweets, as it produced the most accurate predictions for negative tweets which are the main focus of this work.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (5); F1-score (3),"['accuracy score of 0.81 (1)', 'F1 score of 0.83 (1)']","['accuracy score of 0.23 for neutral tweets (1)', 'accuracy score of 0.44 for positive tweets (1)', 'accuracy score of 0.83\nfor negative tweets (1)']",[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],line graph (1); bar chart (1),[],[],[],[],[],[],"['heritage monitoring (1)', 'improve strategies to maximise positive results and minimise negative impacts, such as creating fire brigades and environ\xad\nmental education and interpretation strategies on fire in protected areas (1)', 'understand the public’s perception of and response to any park use rules, promoting more effective communication, addressing concerns, clarifying misunderstandings and fostering trust and collaboration be\xad\ntween society and protected area management (1)']","['Monitoring public sentiment through social media could be a way to: (i) monitor events that cause more public in\xad\nterest and negative engagement, enabling rapid action by protected area management to crises and adverse events (1)', 'furnishing a potent analytical instrument for pro\xad\ntected area management (1)']","['By extending the use of sentiment analysis in conservation to the Portuguese language, (1)']",[],[],"['challenges encompass issues concerning data quality (1), the presence of subjectivity and irony within textual content (1), along with concerns relating to representative\xad\nness (1) and ethical considerations tied to data collection (1)', 'the datasets employed to train the model, encompassing both the categorised Twitter dataset and the dataset of opinions about online products, inadequately distinguished between positive and neutral sentiments, resulting in their aggregated categorization as non-negative (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],https://github.com/CIBIO-Tropi BIO/Sentiment-Analysi s-Brazilian-National-Parks (1),https://github.com/CIBIO-Tropi BIO/Sentiment-Analysi s-Brazilian-National-Parks (1),,,Python language program v.3.9 (1),BERTopic (4),,,,,The authors would like to acknowledge the support of Fundo Brasi­ leiro para a Biodiversidade (FUNBIO) and Instituto Humanize (#026/ 2021). RJL and ACMM are funded by Conselho Nacional de Desenvol­ vimento Científico e Tecnol´ogico (CNPq) (#309879/2019-1; #309980/ 2018-6). RJL and ACMM are also supported via the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under grant agree­ ment No. 854248. CNS acknowledges personal funding from CNPq doctoral grant (#141872/2020-9). RAC acknowledges personal funding from the Research Council of Finland (Grant agreement #348352) and the KONE Foundation (Grant agreement #202101976).
_Spyrou et al.-2016-Discovering areas of interest us.txt,Discovering Areas of Interest,Spyrou; Mylonas,,2016,other,,Springer International Publishing Switzerland 2016,,,,,,,urban areas (2); touristic attractions (3); places of interest (3),,,,,"Athens, Greece (3); city of Athens",Athens; Greece; European cities,,,"The recent great emerge of social media and social activities, together with advances in digital multimedia user-generated content, shifted research interest to unprecedented domains, like the ones related to the acquisition of information and analysis of the online “footsteps” or presence of users.","The herein proposed approach aims to extract areas of interest in urban areas, utilizing the increasing socially-generated knowledge from social networks.",Flickr (7),images; geo-tags (5); tags (16); metadata (6),geo-tags (5),,,,location-based information; user-generated tags,"79, 465 photos",5038 users,,"79, 465",,,,January 2004–December 2015,"center of the city of Athens, Greece",collected from Flickr using its public API,Flickr public API,"queried Flickr for a region covering what is in general considered to be the center of the Athens, (i.e., where the main touristic attractions are located) and retrieved all geo-tagged photos.","translated non-English tags (leaving English ones unchanged) using the Google Translate API, also created a manual stoplist, whose goal was to remove non–relevant (to our goals) tags.",removal of non–relevant tags,,,[],,clustering,graph-based representation,,,,,tags,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,"divides a large region into square “tiles” (sub–regions), of small, ﬁxed size, then adopts a graph-based representation to model connectivity of neighboring tiles, each described by a set of tags.",,yes,[],tiles,connectivity of neighboring tiles,770,,,4–connectivity,,,model connectivity of neighboring tiles,Jaccard distance (2); Jaccard similarity (3),[],[],[],,the result of Fig. 3(a) is quite close to the ground truth.,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['such an evaluation has been shown to be a diﬃcult and expensive task, which may involve empirical issues in the process.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,dataset is not available,,,,,,humanistic data; semantic geo-clustering; semantic knowledge; locality; landmark(s),,,
_Stamatiadou et al.-2021-Semantic crowdsourcing of sounds.txt,"Article ABSTRACT: model for soundscape heritage crowdsourcing, data-driven storytelling, and management in the era of big data and the semantic web",M.E.S. Stamatiadou; I. Thoidis; N. Vryzas; L. Vrysis; C. Dimoulas,,2021,journal article,Sustainability,MDPI,doi.org/10.3390/su13052714,https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/,,,Cultural Heritage (CH) (7); soundscapes heritage (4),audiovisual heritage (2); intangible (5),historical buildings (1); monuments (1); cultural items (1); landscapes (2); heritage sites (2); ancient theaters (2); historic urban (1); rural areas (1),cultural awareness (1); heritage management (2); acoustic sustainability (1); acoustic heritages (1); UNESCO world heritage sites (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study aims to engage the audience for sound-related CH capturing and semantic description, thus forming a mediated way of experiencing soundscapes, which can enhance cultural identity, motivation and pride, sense of “place attachment,” and communal value.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study aims to collect and document multimedia semantics of soundscape heritage, to be later used for data-driven storytelling, which can provide knowledge about the past in the future.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study aims to capture, preserve, and recreate soundscapes and open ancient theater acoustics, representing important cultural artifacts, which can be considered as original product of creativity and imagination.'}",heritage preservation (3); heritage collection (1); heritage management (2); soundscape conservation (1); safeguarding (1),,,,,"not many ICT tools and/or services have been developed to support people in contributing audiovisual data, assisting toward the design of a CH framework.","The utmost target is to collect the necessary volumes of data in an easy and entertaining way, provide in-situ/real-time and batch semantic analysis mode