_Su et al.-2023-Exploring City Image Perception.txt,Exploring City Image Perception in Social Media Big Data through Deep Learning: A Case Study of Zhongshan City,"Su, L.; Chen, W.; Zhou, Y.; Fan, L.",,2023,journal article,Sustainability,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/su15043311,https://doi.org/10.3390/su15043311,,,,,city image (21); urban landscape (3); historic buildings (1),,,"Aesthetical: Artistic – The study mentions the aesthetic value of Zhongshan's natural landscapes and public spaces, particularly the Nanyang-style arcade buildings on Sunwen West Road, indicating an appreciation for original creativity and imagination.; Social: Recreational – The study highlights the recreational value of various urban spaces, such as Lihe Square and Holiday Square, where people engage in leisure and recreational activities, indicating a focus on the role of these spaces in the contemporary market and tourism.; Historical: Historic-Artistic – The study discusses the historical and cultural value of landmarks like the Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall and overseas Chinese houses, suggesting an appreciation for rare or unique examples of historical, stylistic, or artistic movements.",,Zhongshan City; Shiqi District; East District; Qijiang New Town; Torch Development Zone; Xiaolan; Sanxiang; Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall; Sunwen West Road Pedestrian Street; Shaxi mahogany furniture market; forest park; ecological wetland; Xiaolan Ju Exhibition; sea erosion site; Zhongshan Hot Spring; University of Electronic Science and Technology of China; Zhongshan Institute; Lihe Square; Holiday Square; Huafa Commercial Square; Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall; Workers’ Cultural Palace; Lianfeng Mountain Park; Zi Ma Ling Park; Zhongshan Arboreal Park; Yixian Lake Park; Qijiang Park; Longrui Porridge City in Shaxi; Dayong Hongbo City; Port Wetland Park; Xurai Fitness Plaza; Tongxin Cultural and Sports Ecological Park in Triangle Town; Guzhen Sports Ecological Park; Zhan Yuan; Yiyou Film and Television Base; Quanlin Happy World; Dayong Town Culture and Art Square; Cuiheng National Wetland of Zhongshan; Guangdong,Zhongshan City; Pearl River Delta; Guangzhou; Hong Kong; Macao; Shenzhen; Guangdong–Hong Kong–Macao Greater Bay Area; China; British Columbia (Canada),Republic of China period,,"In view of the fact that the existing research on city image perception mostly focuses on the analysis and evaluation of urban image elements [71,72], the emotional perception of hot space [73,74], the sense of place of image [15], few have explored urban imagery from the perspective of the perception of cultural services by city residents and tourists.","This topic proposes to study the perception of city image from the perspectives of city image structure, city image types, and cultural service evaluation.",Weibo,text; images,,spatial distribution,time,,time; geographical location,7745,,7745,"17,654",,,,January 2021 to October 2022,Zhongshan City,data collector,Houyi Collector,,removal of redundant and useless parts of the image data and language data,"duplicate text, too little content, and deleted Weibo comments",,,[],,image semantic segmentation; semantic word frequency analysis,convolutional neural networks; recurrent neural networks,CNN; RNN; FCN,,,,Weibo image data; text data,,,,,,,,,,{},,local computer,,,limited GPU memory,,,"The geospatial information carried by the comments is analyzed by GIS space to obtain the image structure of the city, the image data in the comments are divided into image types by image semantic segmentation method based on deep full convolution neural network, and the text data in the comments are evaluated by semantic word frequency analysis to obtain the cultural service of the city image.",,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['urban spatial planning', 'urban landscape design', 'tourism industry layout']",[],['method of deep fully convolutional neural network-based image semantic segmentation and word frequency analysis to perceive and analyze city image'],[],['integrated and comprehensive perception of city image'],"['perception research of urban image is not subdivided according to user groups', 'lack of in-depth application discussion on the image perception results']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,Not applicable,,,,,,city image structure; city image types; cultural service evaluation; geospatial information; GIS; image semantic segmentation; deep full convolution neural network; semantic word frequency analysis; cultural service perception index words; social perception; big data; deep learning; urban spatial planning; urban landscape design; tourism industry layout; cognitive map; social media; smart mobile terminals; neural networks; computer vision; natural language processing; convolutional neural networks; recurrent neural networks; urban green lung,,,
_Su et al.-2025-Identifying Cultural Perceptions.txt,Identifying Cultural Perceptions by Nature Language Processing,C. Su,,2025,journal article,ACM Journal on Computing and Cultural Heritage,ACM,,http://www.dianping.com,,24,Cultural Heritage (1); World Cultural Heritage (1),cultural (7); historical (10),historical heritage (3); cultural landscapes (3); historical gardens (10); urban parks (22); urban green space (7); historical urban parks (1); Chinese private gardens (1); classical gardens (2),cultural value (2); cultural heritage (10); World Cultural Heritage (1); UNESCO (1); cultural landscape (3); historical heritage (3); historical gardens (10); heritage sites (1); preservation (4); conservation (3),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study mentions community sentiment, collective memory, and place attachment in relation to under-maintained historic parks and gardens, indicating a discussion of emotional (collective) social values.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study aims to investigate the cultural landscape perception of urban residents towards the cultural heritage landscape elements and ambience of urban parks and gardens, indicating a discussion of the educational historic value.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study discusses the aesthetic evaluation of parks and gardens, including the presence of traditional garden-style architecture and decoration, indicating a discussion of artistic aesthetical values.'}",heritage conservation (2); conservation (1),Beijing; Suzhou; Humble Administrator’s Garden; Lingering Garden; Gusu District; Wuzhong District,China (7); Jiangsu Province; Shanghai; Hong Kong; Greater London,Ming dynasty,COVID-19 pandemic,"Nevertheless, it remains a research challenge to ascertain whether NLP methods can be employed to obtain perceptions and preferences for landscapes with cultural value, such as cultural landscapes, historical heritage, and historical gardens, among urban residents.",(1) Which elements do park visitors perceive more strongly in historical gardens and new parks? (2) Is there a correlation between the cultural perception preferences and the spatial attributes of the gardens and parks? (3) What are the sentimental preferences of visitors between historical gardens and new parks?,Dianping; Ctrip; Weibo; Xiaohongshu,text; images; videos,,,,,,"37,734",,"25,189 online reviews for historical gardens and 12,545 online reviews for newly built parks",,,,,from 1 August 2015 to 1 August 2020,Gusu District and Wuzhong District in Suzhou City,Python was used to extract data from Dianping and Ctrip.,Python,the research area had to have over 100 online reviews,"Duplicate reviews, irrelevant comments, and comments from other sources were removed.",,manual review,,"['“positive”', '“negative”']",over 99.7% accuracy,sentiment analysis,,word2vec,pre-trained word2vec model provided by Tencent AI Lab Embedding Corpora,word2vec,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['To protect privacy, no identifiable information will be disclosed, and the data will be deleted after the project.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,Jieba; gensim; Paddle-NLP,,,,,
_Su et al.-2025-Length of Stay and Destination I.txt,ISSN: 1938-8160 (Print) 1938-8179 (Online) Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/wctr20,Xing Su; Amit Birenboim; Kun Zhang,College of Tourism and Service Management at Nankai University; Department of Geography at The Hebrew University of Jerusalem; College of Tourism and Service Management at Nankai University,,journal article,Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/wctr20,,,www.tandfonline.com/journals/wctr20,,,,,urban destinations (2); urban cores (1),,,,,Hong Kong (16); Beijing (1),China (7); Asia (1); mainland China (4); Kowloon Island (1); Hong Kong Island (1); European (1); Northern Norway (1); Serbia (1),,,"However, little attention has been given to how the distinct experiences of day trippers may result in incongruity in UGC compared to tourists, and how this could impact the destination image and subsequently affect promotional efforts in marketing settings.",The study addresses two research questions: 1) how does the length of stay affect the projected online destination image of visitors? 2) how is the multilayered destination image reflected in the texts and photos of social media posts?,social media platforms (10); Weibo (8); Instagram (1); Xiaohongshu (2); Mafengwo (1); Dianping.com (1); Trip Advisor (1); Ctrip.Com (1),text (6); photos (10); media files (2); metadata (2); visual content (3); texts (6); photographic social media content (1),geotagged (4); coordinates (1); location (4),spatio-temporal behaviors (1); spatially dispersed (1),timestamps (3); temporal dimension (1); time periods (1),,user account (1); gender (4); place of registration (1),"208,056 check-ins",511 million active monthly users in September 2020,"6,219 textual records for day trippers and 14,851 for tourists","4,326 photos for day trippers and 11,553 photos for tourists",,,"10,185 check-ins for day trippers and 24,777 check-ins for tourists",2016 (3),Hong Kong (10),open application programming interfaces (APIs) (3),APIs (3),double-filtration process (2),removed check-ins with missing coordinates or user attributes (1),"Applying the threshold of 30 days adopted by most previous studies (e.g. Su et al., 2020), we applied the visitor label where the interval was no more than 30 days; otherwise, the Weibo user was assumed to be a resident.",,,['visitor label (1)'],,scene recognition (4); image classification (1); topic modeling (2),deep-learning model (1); convolutional neural network (1),Res Net (3); Res Net-101 (2); LDA (4),,,Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) (4),texts (2); photos (3); Weibo texts (1); visual content (1),,10% of the training data above to experimentally test the model,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,"The advanced residual connections of Res Net-101 with a training set of 5,000 labeled photos formed the algorithm for scene recognition.",,no,[],,,,,,,,,,recall rate (1); false recognition rate (1); perplexity (2); coherence (2),"['the recall rate (i.e. proportion of scenes correctly identified) was 90%, while the false recognition rate was only 0.1%']",[],[],"X2 = 4.983, p = 0.892 (1); X2 = 27.388, p = 0.002 (1); X2 = 10.563, p = 0.393 (1); X2 = 6.376, p = 0.702 (1); X2 = 39.199, p = 0.000 (1)",,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],radar charts (2),[],[],[],[],"['male day trippers were more likely to share scenes of leisure and entertainment and of culture and history, whereas female day trippers preferred sharing scenes of nightscapes, shopping, food and beverages, and interpretation.']",[],"['DMOs can leverage UGC to gain a deeper understanding of the destination image and develop marketing strategies that emphasize strengths and address weaknesses, with the aim of converting more day trippers into longer-stay tourists.']","['DMOs should develop integrated marketing campaigns that showcase a wide range of travel experiences beyond primary attractions.', 'customizing marketing messages to different demographic groups based on their pre\nferences can make marketing efforts more effective, such as targeting women with content related to entertainment, culture and history, and men with content about nightscapes, food and beverages.']","['this study employs an innovative method by combining geotagged check-ins, texts, and photos from social media users to analyze the multi-layered destination image across different visitor segments.']","['Overall, tourists generated more diverse destination images than day trippers, including more holistic images and attributes of attractions, landscape, and hotels.', 'Day trippers, by contrast, shared more functional and utilitarian content associated with travel arrange\nments.', 'the findings showed high consistency between the two groups in the visual scenes highlighting Hong Kong’s common attractions, though, some noticeable differences in unique characteristics of destination image were identified when consider\ning users’ gender.']",[],"['This study has several limitations and provide directions for future research.', 'it uses data from only one social media platform (Weibo), which limits our perspective on destination image creation.', 'the study focused on a limited set of factors affecting destination image (length of stay and gender).']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,many social media platforms do not allow researchers to access all their UGC.,,,,Python libraries (1),,UGC (10); day trippers (23); tourists (33); destination image (36); social media (20); check-in (7); nightscape (7); Victoria Harbour (4); airport (5); Hong Kong University (2); Causeway Bay (2); Mong Kok (1); Disneyland (3); Ocean Park (1); high tea (1); dim sum (1); computer vision (2); text mining (1),,,
_Su et al.-2025-Mediating Roles of Cultural Perc.txt,"Mediating Roles of Cultural Perception and Place Attachment in the Landscape–Wellbeing Relationship: Insights from Historical Urban Parks in Wuhan, China",C. Su; X. Wang; Y. Wang; Y. Chen; F. Dai; X. Chen,,2025,journal article,Land,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/,https://doi.org/10.3390/ land14061176,,,,,historical urban parks (13); historical parks (10); historical sites (2); heritage features (2); ancient architecture (2); cultural relics (1); heritage landscapes (1); ancient building (1),cultural heritage (2); heritage conservation (1); historical significance (2); historical elements (6); cultural attributes (3); historical architecture (2); cultural relics (1); heritage landscapes (1); historical sites (2); heritage features (2); ancient architecture (2); heritage landmarks (10); ancient building (2); cultural perception (20); place attachment (28); cultural capital theory (2); historical knowledge (1); historical environments (2); cultural identity (3); historical and cultural narratives (1); historical and cultural information (1); historical and cultural characteristics (1); historical and cultural activities (3); historical and cultural city (1); historical and cultural heritage (1); historical and cultural Axis (1); cultural traditions (1); cultural interpretation (1); cultural resonance (1); cultural ecosystem services (1); cultural contexts (1); cultural cognition patterns (1); cultural involvement (1); cultural understanding (2); cultural identification (3),,"Social: Emotional (collective) – The study highlights how historical urban parks foster cultural identity and strengthen place attachment, contributing to a sense of communal value.; Historic: Symbolic – The material elements within historical parks, such as historical architecture and cultural relics, embody collective cultural traditions and establish tangible connections with the past.; Aesthetical: Artistic – The study mentions the esthetic quality of urban environments and residents’ quality of life being enhanced by historical parks.; Age: Existential – The study discusses how historical parks provide tangible links to cultural heritage and foster cultural identity, connecting individuals to past generations.",,"Wuhan (4); Hoi An, Vietnam (1); Edinburgh (1); Los Angeles County (1); Shanghai, China (1); Hangzhou, China (1); Tehran, Iran (1)",China (6); Yangtze River basin (1); South China (1); Europe (1); Paris (1); Tokyo (1); Scotland (1),Ming–Qing period (1),,"Despite established correlations between place attachment and well-being [23,24], current research tends to focus separately on either park functions [25,26] or theoretical well-being frameworks. This disciplinary segregation has created a knowledge gap regarding the mechanisms through which historical parks’ physical characteristics influence resident well-being [7,27–29].","Using park physical characteristics, cultural perception, and place attachment as independent variables with subjective well-being as the dependent variable, we aim to make three key theoretical contributions: (1) establishing an integrated framework connecting physical environment, cultural meaning, and psychological outcomes in historic park contexts; (2) quantifying the mediating effects of cultural perception and place attachment; (3) identifying optimal design interventions for different demographic groups.",Baidu Maps (4),images (3); questionnaire surveys (2); geospatial data (1),local land use data (1),,,,,599 shared images of residents from 11 historical parks on Baidu Maps,368 valid questionnaires were collected,,599,,,,16 November to 3 December 2023,11 historical urban parks in Wuhan,online crowdsourcing for physical characteristic assessments and questionnaire surveys for psychological evaluations,,,,,manual label verification,Three survey teams,"['trees', 'shrubs', 'lawns', 'buildings', 'sky']",,semantic segmentation (3); feature extraction (1),machine learning (4),Seg Former-B5 (3),,,,image data (1),,"20,210 images for training and an additional 2000 images as the validation set",,,0.00006,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"For the training process, we utilized the standard ADE20K dataset configuration with 20,210 images for training and an additional 2000 images as the validation set to assess model accuracy. Training was terminated when the accuracy exceeded 80%. The initial learning rate was set to 0.00006 with a “poly” LR scheduler using a default factor of 1.0.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,average intersection to union ratio (1); segmentation accuracy (1); confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) (2); Cronbach’s α (3); RMSEA (1); SRMR (1); HTMT (1); variance inflation factor (VIF) (3); Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) (3); Multiple R-squared (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['All participants were informed of the research purpose, procedures, and data usage prior to completing the questionnaire, and their participation was voluntary.', 'They retained the right to withdraw at any time, and all data were anonymized.', 'The study strictly adhered to privacy protection protocols, with personally identifiable information removed from the questionnaire data to ensure participant confidentiality.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],"['First, while encompassing 11 historical urban parks, the sample size was constrained to 368 valid questionnaires due to limited research resources.', 'Second, the single-city (Wuhan) focus, though representative, precludes cross-regional comparisons and fails to account for seasonal usage patterns.', 'Third, the cross-sectional design limits causal inferences, and the selected park feature indicators may have omitted potentially significant variables.']",,Data are contained within the article.,,,R language,,,urban park (10); perceived well-being (5); physical characteristics (10); environmental psychology theory (2); urban renewal (2); human settlement environment (2); urban regeneration (1); urban green