_Sun et al.-2025-Popularity influence mechanism o.txt,Popularity influence mechanism of coastal spaces in urban areas: Insights,Peijin Sun; Hanxu Zhao; Jingqiu Zhong; Shiyin Cao; Mengfan Gao,"Key Research Base of Humanities and Social Sciences of Ministry of Education, Institute of Marine Sustainable Development, Liaoning Normal University, China; Research Section of Environment Design, School of Architecture and Fine Art, Dalian University of Technology, China; State Key Laboratory of Resources and Environmental Information System, Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research, CAS, China",2025,journal article,Cities,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cities.2025.105909,www.elsevier.com/locate/cities,,,,,urban coastal spaces (6); coastal spaces (14); coastal areas (3); coastal park attractions (1); coastal parks (1),Cultural Ecosystem Services (CESs) (6); cultural ecosystem services (5); cultural services (2); heritage (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study mentions that urban coastal spaces enhance public health, reduce stress, and improve public happiness, indicating a sense of well-being and communal value derived from these spaces.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment – The paper highlights that urban coastal spaces attract tourists and boost commercial vitality, suggesting an entertainment value related to tourism and recreation.'}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'Management – The study emphasizes the importance of understanding public usage patterns for coastal management and urban planning, indicating a management value related to incorporating the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The paper mentions aesthetic appreciation as a non-material benefit derived from natural environments, suggesting an artistic value related to the original product of creativity and imagination.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': 'Spiritual – The study refers to the spiritual fulfillment provided by coastal parks, indicating a harmony between the building and its natural environment.'}",coastal management (2); urban planning (3); tourism development (1); coastal development (1),Dalian; Dalian's urban core coastal spaces,China; Northeast China; Dalian; Shanghai,,“Beautiful Bay” initiative,"Conventional check-in data may be compromised by time lags in user behavior, while mobile location data, although capable of delivering high-precision spatiotemporal trajectories, cannot easily account for population diversity or capture subjective evaluations.","(1) What are the key elements in visitors' perceptions of coastal spaces, and which factors receive the highest satisfaction? (2) Which factors contribute to the popularity of coastal spaces? (3) How do the strengths and interactions of these factors influence visitor attraction?",Xiaohongshu (2); Instagram; Pinterest; Dianping; Yelp; Trip Advisor; Weibo,text (6); image (10); video (2); geolocation-based check-in information (1); mobile signaling data (2); multisource geographic data (1),geolocation-based check-in information (1); geographic coordinates (2),,timestamp (2),likes (2); saves (1); comments (3),park name (1); user ID (1); title (1); content (2); publication date (1),"16,360 text entries",,"14,704 valid text entries","53,604 raw images, 28,821 valid images",,,,"January 15, 2023, to December 15, 2023",Dalian's urban core coastal spaces,"filtered the raw checkin data by timestamp, geographic coordinates, and tags",Python (2),,"removed entries that were incomplete or lacked substantial content, and those without emotional expressions or with hyperlinks, to minimize interference during data processing and model training. We also excluded advertisements from public accounts, posts with names like Dalian's coastal spaces but describing spaces in other cities, and posts mentioning coastal spaces but unrelated in content",,,expert evaluations,[],,sentiment analysis (3); classification (2); semantic segmentation (2); instance segmentation (2),Large Language Models (LLMs) (3); Natural Language Processing (NLP) models (1); Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) (2); deep learning algorithms (1),LLa MA (Large Language Model Meta AI) (5); LLa VA (Large Language Model Visual and Audio) (6); LLa MA2-13B model (2); Deep Labv3 model (1); YOLOv3 model (1),two pretrained large-scale language models,,n-gram model (1); Count Vectorizer (1),images (5); text (7),,20 % test set,,,,,,,90-degree rotation (1); 10 % zoom (1); horizontal flipping (1),{},,,,,,,,"converted the annotated data into fixed-length token sequences to meet the model's input requirements. We monitored model performance on a validation set to prevent overfitting and ensure both accuracy and stability. For image processing, the LLa VA model was used to transform the original images into feature maps, followed by a series of transformations through the Projection W process. This more advanced procedure enables the model to convert each feature map into distinct “image tokens.” These tokens are subsequently integrated with the text tokens, allowing the model to process and interpret multimodal data in a unified manner.",,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (3); confusion matrix (2); true positives (TP) (2); true negatives (TN) (1); false positives (FP) (1); false negatives (FN) (1); variance inflation factors (1); correlation tests (1); correlation coefficient (1); Partial Least Squares (PLS) regression model (2); R2 value (3); q-value (7),"['TP (correctly predicted i accuracy, calculated as TP / (TP + FP), was 89.20 %']",[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"[""Urban planners can optimize the design and management of these areas, enhancing their attractiveness and functionality, which supports tourism's economic benefits and promotes sustainable cultural and ecological development.""]","['Effective management of coastal spaces requires acknowledging the uniqueness and diversity of different park types. Treating all spaces as homogeneous entities may overlook key factors, leading to inefficient strategies. Therefore, planning and management should develop tailored strategies for each type.']",['This study integrates large language models (LLMs) like LLa MA and LLa VA with multimodal spatiotemporal data to offer new insights into the attractiveness of coastal spaces.'],[],[],"['This study draws on open-access social media platforms and data sources in China.', 'Second, results are primarily based on high-density coastal areas.', 'Social media data may be biased and not fully representative of all visitor behaviors and preferences.']","['The dataset primarily contains anonymized user location records, ensuring privacy protection.']",['Social media data may be biased and not fully representative of all visitor behaviors and preferences.'],[],[],"['This data was provided by the Dalian Municipal Institute of Land and Spatial Planning within a legal framework, ensuring compliance.']",[],[],"['This study has several limitations.', 'This study draws on open-access social media platforms and data sources in China.', 'Second, results are primarily based on high-density coastal areas.', 'Social media data may be biased and not fully representative of all visitor behaviors and preferences.']",Data will be made available on request.,Data will be made available on request.,,,,Python (4); Jieba tool (1); Sklearn (1); numpy library (1); tensorflow (1); scipy (1),,mobile signaling (1); mobile location data (2); mobile phone data (1); real-time user data (RTUD) (1); spatiotemporal data (3); spatiotemporal trajectories (1); Points of Interest (POI) (1); Tencent Street View (TSV) (2); Open Street Map (1); Baidu Maps (1); Landsat 8 OLI (Operational Land Imager) (1); Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) (2); Importance-Performance Analysis (IPA) (5); Partial Least Squares (PLS) regression (3); Geographic Detectors (3); Shannon Diversity Index (1),,,
_Tabib et al.-2020-Deep Features for Categorization.txt,Science Direct ABSTRACT:,Ramesh Ashok Tabib; et al.,,2020,other,Procedia Computer Science,Elsevier B.V.,,https://doi.org/10.1177/0165551512437638,,490,heritage site (4); heritage (3),,,,,,,,,,,Modeling a generalized classiﬁcation model for crowdsourced data is challenging as the data is invariant and high dimensional.,we propose a framework to categorize heritage site images based on unsupervised clustering for 3D reconstruction.,,images,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,crowdsourced data,,,,,,,[],,clustering; classification,Autoencoder,Inception based Variational Autoencoder(IVAE); VAE,,deep features,,2D images,,,variant of Variational Autoencoder (VAE) loss function,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"We model Inception based Variational Autoencoder(IVAE) for extracting deep 8 features from crowdsourced data in an unsupervised manner. Towards reﬁning deep features we propose a new loss function, variant of Variational Autoencoder (VAE) loss function and model IVAE accordingly. The extracted features are represented in latent space and clustered using clustering algorithms like K-Means, Mini-batch-K-Means.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,clustering accuracy,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,deep features; crowdsourced data; 3D reconstruction,,,
_Taecharungroj and Mathayomchan-2019-Analysing TripAdvisor reviews of.txt,"Analysing Trip Advisor reviews of tourist attractions in Phuket, Thailand",Viriya Taecharungroj; Boonyanit Mathayomchan,"Mahidol University International College, 999 Phutthamonthon 4 Road, Salaya, Nakhonpathom, 73170, Thailand",2019,journal article,Tourism Management,Elsevier,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tourman.2019.06.020,,,,,,beaches (4); islands (4); temples (3); pedestrian street (3); markets (2),,,,,Phuket,Thailand; Phuket; America; Asia Paciﬁc; Europe; Middle East; Africa; US; New York City; New Orleans; Goa; India; Beijing; China; Japan; Naples; Italy; UK; France; Netherlands; Bangkok,,"tour boat Phoenix was hit by ﬁve-metre waves and capsized on 5 July 2018, resulting in 47 deaths","Therefore, the objective of the current research is to develop a methodology that can analyse online reviews using machine learning techniques in such a way that practitioners in the ﬁelds of tourism and destination management can easily understand and apply the technique to improve their attractions.",(1) What are the dimensions of attractions? (2) How many reviews are there in each dimension of attractions? (3) How positive is each dimension of attractions? (4) How frequent and positive are the underlying terms of each dimension? (5) What is the prediction accuracy of the naïve Bayes algorithm for Trip Advisor's reviews of attractions?,Trip Advisor; Lonely Planet; Google Maps; Expedia; Yelp; Booking.com; Air Bn B; Google Maps Local Guide,text,,,,ratings,,"65,079",,,,,,,February 2019,"Phuket, a province in southern Thailand",Reviews in English were collected,,"To select the attractions, the authors explored travel websites that published posts about Phuket, such as Phuket.com's “15 Best Things to Do in Phuket”, Trip Advisor's “Things to Do in Phuket”, Expedia's “Things to Do in Phuket 2019”, and Phuket101.net's “18 Best Things to Do in Phuket”.","Data pre-processing begins with a punctuation eraser to remove punctuation from reviews, followed by a case converter, which changes all text to lowercase. Then, several ﬁlters are applied: ﬁrst, the number ﬁlter removes all numbers from the reviews. Second, the N Chars ﬁlter removes reviews with less than a speciﬁc number of characters; it was set at three in this research. Finally, the stop word ﬁlter is applied to remove insigniﬁcant words.",Reviews in English,,,[],,topic modeling; sentiment analysis; classification,Topic Models; Naïve bayes,LDA,,,,text,,70%/30%,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"To conduct naïve Bayes modelling, the reviews were converted into “bag-of-words”, whereby the order of words in the reviews is neglected. The frequencies of all terms were counted; infrequently found terms were removed from the analysis. Then, all reviews were randomly partitioned into training (70%) and testing (30%) sets.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['this study introduces a new method using machine learning techniques to extract comprehensive and comprehensible dimensions of attractions in Phuket, Thailand.', 'this research introduces two additional levels of valence: dimensional and lexical.']",[],[],"['First, the identiﬁed dimensions are from a particular point in time. Incremental data will alter the identiﬁcation and interpretation of the attraction dimensions.', 'Second, although the elbow method is an acceptable way to identify the number of dimensions, there is no universally accepted and robust technique.', 'Third, this research collected data only from Trip Advisor, which might have a platform bias (Xiang et al., 2017).', 'Fourth, although the LSVA is detailed, an analysis of the individual terms ignores the contextual meanings that result from the co-presence of terms.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,The data were pre-processed and analysed using KNIME Analytics Platform 3.7.1.,,,,,,
_Taecharungroj et al.-2024-Snapshots of nature Harnessing.txt,Snapshots of nature: Harnessing Flickr data to frame sustainable brand positioning strategies for Thailand’s national parks,Viriya Taecharungroj; Đorđije Vasiljevi´c; Ake Pattaratanakun,"Mahidol University Internatinal College (MUIC), Mahidol University, 999 Phutthamonthon 4 Road, Salaya, Nakhonpathom, 73170, Thailand; Department of Geography, Tourism and Hotel Management, Faculty of Sciences, University of Novi Sad, Trg Dositeja Obradovica 3, 21000, Novi Sad, Vojvodina, Serbia; Marketing Department, Chulalongkorn Business School, Chulalongkorn University, 254 Pathumwan, Bangkok, 10330, Thailand",2024,journal article,Journal of Outdoor Recreation and Tourism,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jort.2024.100765,www.elsevier.com/locate/jort,,,,natural; cultural,national parks (29),conservation (10); sustainability (14); sustainable tourism (3); sustainable (10); heritage (2),,,conservation (10); heritage (2),Thailand’s national parks (4); Thai national parks (1); Khao Khitchakut National Park (2); Doi Suthep-Pui (1); Tarutao (1); Erawan national park (2); Mu Ko Samet (1); Phi Phi (1); Chang (1); Tarutao (1); Khao Khitchakut (1); Erawan (2); Huai Nam Dang (1); Doi Inthanon (1); Phu Hin Rong Kla (1); Phu Kradueng (1); Khao Yai (1); Kaengkrachan (1); Ob Luang (2); Khao Phra Thaeo (1); Khao Sok (1); Lam Nam Kok (2); Khao Chamao (2); Khuen Si Nakarin (1); Sai Yok (1); Na Yung Nam Som (1); Ob Khan (1); Mae Phang (1); Mae Wong (1); Islas Marietas National Park (Mexico) (1),Thailand (10); Mexico (1),,,"Destination brand positioning offers a solution, yet techniques leveraging social media data for strategic positioning remain scarce.","This research introduces a method, using Flickr photos, to: (a) identify the key features that define the brand identity of Thailand’s national parks, (b) develop a categorical framework to group these parks based on their features and (c) uncover the distinctive features that dif