 experiment were 76.3% and 78.2%, respectively, in Case 2.']",[],[],,"Six images containing various damages with normal conditions were tested as shown in Fig. 15, which indicates that the damage detection results were very effective.",[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['The novel method combining MCS with the state-of-the-art deep learning technique to protect the Great Wall is a major breakthrough in the field of cultural heritage.'],[],[],"['However, the experiments did not achieve a higher accuracy classifier because of the small sample database and the relatively uniform size of the samples.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,Caffe framework,,Mobile crowd sensing (MCS) (6); Great Watcher system (7); region proposal network (RPN) (4); Auto Navi Map (3); PASCAL Visual Object Classes (VOC) format; Inner Product1 (IP1),,,
_Wang et al.-2021-Fine-grained assessment of green.txt,Fine-grained assessment of greenspace satisfaction at regional scale using content analysis of social media and machine learning,Zhifang Wang; Zhongwei Zhu; Min Xu; Salman Qureshi,"College of Architecture and Landscape Architecture, Peking University, Beijing 100871, PR China; Institute of Geography (Landscape Ecology), Humboldt University of Berlin, Rudower Chaussee 16, 12489 Berlin, Germany",2021,journal article,Science of the Total Environment,Elsevier B.V.,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2021.145908,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2021.145908,,,,,urban parks (2),green spaces (10); green infrastructure (3),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'The study discusses the importance of green spaces for improving physical and mental well-being, which relates to the emotional (individual) value of memory and personal life experiences, and the emotional (collective) value of cultural identity and sense of place attachment.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': ""The study mentions that green spaces can bring environmental, social, and economic benefits, which relates to the use value of the asset's function and utility, and the entertainment value of the asset's role in the contemporary market, especially tourism.""}; {'POLITICAL VALUES': 'The study discusses the role of green spaces in sustainable urbanization policy, which relates to the management value of incorporating the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'The study mentions landscape features such as water, trees, and terrain, which relates to the artistic value of the original product of creativity and imagination.'}; {'ECOLOGICAL VALUES': 'The study defines green spaces as land with vegetation set aside for ecological preservation, which relates to the essential value of the presence of ecological ideologies in the design and construction.'}",landscape planning (2),Beijing parks (2); 50 parks in Beijing (2); 167 parks within the Sixth Ring Road in Beijing,Beijing; China,,,Survey-based studies of perception yielded detailed assessments of green spaces but lacked regional comparisons. The few big-data-based studies of greenspace perception lacked ﬁne-grained explorations.,"(1) How do we identify landscape features from unstructured texts on social media? To identify landscape features in different parks for rapid assessment on regional scale, this study established a landscape-feature lexicon based on the Chinese language. (2) How do we rapidly and automatically transform volunteered textual information (emotions, opinions on social media) to GSS assessment? (3) Which aspects of landscape features are more critical to overall GSS? Are perceived frequencies of landscape features important or perceived satisfaction of different landscape features important? Which landscape features are more important to GSS?",DIAN PING,text; images,,,,,,"170,000 comments",over 200 million active users,,,,,,October 2006 to September 2018,Beijing,web crawler,Python-based web crawler,Parks with over 50 comments were selected for statistical analyses in Module 4 to ensure sufﬁcient sample sizes.,"Data were cleaned to merge or remove duplicates and blank entries. Hyperlinks of web pages and emojis without emotional expression (e.g., objects) were deleted to avoid interference with data processing and model training. Emojis with emotional expression (e.g., smiley faces) were standardized for inclusion into the emotion lexicon.",training data included only text with a string length of 10 to 300 (covering 95% of the original data).,manual,experts,"['satisfaction Likert scale (Likert, 1932), with 1 being extremely unsatisﬁed and 7 being extremely satisﬁed']",,regression; classification,Gradient Boosting Decision Tree,XGBoost; XGBRegressor; XGBClassiﬁer; Baidu NLP; ROST CM6; Snow N.L.P.,,word2vec,,textual information,,3600/900,,Grid search,0:05,,225,reg alpha ¼ 3; reg lambda ¼ 0:1,,"{'max depth': '5', 'min child weight': '3', 'seed': '0', 'subsample': '0:6', 'colsample bytree': '1', 'gamma': '0:3'}",XGBClassiﬁer; Baidu NLP; ROST CM6; Snow N.L.P.,,,,,,,"Our modeling started with data annotation using a satisfaction Likert scale (Likert, 1932), with 1 being extremely unsatisﬁed and 7 being extremely satisﬁed. A random-selection Python algorithm was used to pull 4500 comments from our database for annotation and model training.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,"Spearman's rank correlation coefﬁcient (ρ, at 0.05 P level); coefﬁcient of determination (R2); Mean Absolute Error (MAE); Mean Square Error (MSE)","['R2 between model-predicted satisfaction scores and true satisfaction scores was 0.655 (Spearman correlation = 0.812, P < 0.05)']",[],[],"Kolmogorov-Smirnov test (P ≥0.05, variable is normally distributed); Pearson correlation analysis; Spearman correlation analysis; ANOVA; Bonferroni post-hoc test; Tamhane's post-hoc test",,[],[],,[],[],[],,XGBClassiﬁer; Baidu NLP; ROST CM6; Snow N.L.P.,[],,[],[],[],['general park satisfaction was concluded to be very positive across different parks'],[],[],[],"['future management or renovations to green spaces should pay more attention to the quality of natural features and service facilities, rather than merely increasing their quantity']","['This study contributes to the current understanding of green spaces on a regional scale through developing an efﬁcient procedure that transforms volunteered and unstructured SMD into quantiﬁable assessments.', 'this study creates a landscape-feature lexicon that allows for easy and ﬁnegrained identiﬁcation of landscape features through word-matching of online comments', 'a machine-learning model is developed to rapidly autoscore satisfaction from those comments']",[],[],"['our test data was only drawn from one social media platform and focused only on one city', '20% were not appropriately classiﬁed by the model', 'the extent to which unstructured text online are comparable with structured questionnaires should be systematically examined']",[],"['social media platforms are generally biased toward higher satisfaction because Internet users tend to post positive content', 'SMD have several common shortcomings, including users with limited age and spatial coverage', 'sample selection bias', 'non-representativeness']",[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,gensim; Python-based Jieba package,,sustainable urbanization (3); greenspace satisfaction (GSS) (10); landscape features (15); social media data (SMD) (8); online comments (5); park satisfaction (5); feature frequency (5); feature satisfaction (6); natural features (10); cultural features (5); supporting facilities (5),,,
_Wang et al.-2022-Exploring Tourists' Multilevel S.txt,Exploring Tourists’ Multilevel Spatial Cognition of Historical Town Based on Multi-Source Data—A Case Study of Feng Jing Ancient Town in Shanghai,"Wang, L.; Ding, J.; Chen, M.; Sun, Y.; Tang, X.; Ge, M.",,2022,journal article,Buildings,MDPI,10.3390/buildings12111833,https://doi.org/ 10.3390/buildings12111833,,,Cultural Heritage (1),cultural (2); historical (6),Fengjing Ancient Town (13); historical town (7); ancient town (14); historical and cultural town (2); Jiangnan ancient town (2); historical relics (1); historical sites (1),cultural heritage (1); historical legacy (1); cultural deposits (1); cultural continuity (1); historical and cultural protection (1); historical and cultural aspects (1); historical and cultural relics (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study discusses how tourists develop a sense of place attachment and memory of Fengjing Ancient Town through their experiences and activities, contributing to their cultural identity and communal value.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': ""Educational – The study explores how tourists' spatial cognition of Fengjing Ancient Town is formed and changed, which can further determine whether tourism can continue, providing knowledge about the past in the future.""}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study analyzes the landscape characteristics of Fengjing Jiangnan Ancient Town, such as rivers, bridges, waterfront buildings, alleys, walls, and cornices, which are original products of creativity and imagination.'}",cultural protection (2); tourism development (7); Protective development (1),Fengjing Ancient Town (13); Shanghai (4),Shanghai (7); China (4); Jiangsu (3); Zhejiang (3); Yangtze River Delta (2); Anhui (1),Tang and Song dynasties (1),,"However, the current tourist cognition research is not comprehensive enough in terms of data sources, time dimension, and spatial objects.","This research takes Fengjing Ancient Town in Shanghai as an example