_Wartmann et al.-2018-Describing and comparing landsca.txt,Describing and comparing landscapes: combining empirical and data-driven approaches,Flurina M. Wartmann; Elin K. Acheson; Ross S. Purves,,2018,journal article,Taylor & Francis Online,,https://doi.org/10.1080/13658816.2018.1445257,www.tandfonline.com/journals/tgis20,,1572–1592,Cultural Landscape (1),,landscapes (30),landscape character assessments (2); European Landscape Convention (3); cultural values (2); cultural (5); cultural perception of landscapes (1); distinctiveness (1); authenticity (1); fascination (1); perceived landscape beauty (1); Landscape Character Assessments (LCAs) (1); Landscape Character Assessment (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study explores how people perceive landscapes and the elements they identify as characteristic, suggesting a focus on cultural identity and sense of place.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': ""Artistic – The research aims to arrive at descriptions and representations that better reflect people's notions of landscapes, indicating an interest in the artistic or aesthetic qualities of landscapes.""}",landscape management (2); landscape policy (1); landscape planning (2),"Switzerland (4); Oeschinensee; Seealpsee; Thurauen, River Thur; Bremgarten, River Reuss; Robenhuserriet, Pfäﬃkon; Ägeriried, Rothenthurm; Ufschötti, Lake Lucerne; Zürichhorn, Lake Zurich; Hochwacht, Lägern; Hochwacht, Pfannenstiel",Switzerland (4); Europe (2); US (1); United Kingdom (1); England (2); Scotland (2); Flanders (1); Belgium (1); Great Britain (1); Ireland (1),,,"How do people perceive landscapes? What elements of the landscape do they identify and pick out as being characteristic? And how can we arrive at descriptions, and ultimately computational representations that better reﬂect people’s notions of landscapes?","Our aim is to analyze to what extent diﬀerent methodological approaches, and consequently, diﬀerent data sources, result in diﬀerent descriptions of landscapes, a