_Wei and Chen-2025-Tracing the Evolution of Tourist.txt,Academic Editor: Harry Coccossis ABSTRACT:,Y.W.; M.C.,,2025,other,,,,,,,World Heritage (2); Cultural Heritage (10),tangible (2); intangible (2); cultural (20); natural (3),Lijiang Old Town (10); UNESCO World Heritage Site (2); Old Town of Lijiang (4); ancient town (4); historic architecture (2); historic districts (1); historic streets (1),Outstanding Universal Value (2); authenticity (2); cultural preservation (1); World Cultural Heritage (1); Conservation Plan (1); Naxi Dongba culture (1); Intangible World Heritage List (1),,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study mentions that CHT enhances cultural distinctiveness maintenance, helping to preserve traditional crafts, festivals, and rituals, which fosters pride and a sense of belonging among residents, contributing to cultural conservation.'}; {'ECONOMIC VALUES': 'Entertainment – The study discusses the role of CHT in stimulating the local economy through expenditures on accommodations, food, activities, and souvenirs significantly by tourists, further boosting local job creation, city revitalization, and community development.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study highlights the potential of CHT to provide knowledge about the past, transforming destinations into spaces of emotional connection and leaving a profound impression on visitors’ understanding of the past.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study mentions the aesthetic aspects of perceptions play an important mediating role in the intrinsic mechanisms connecting CHT and cultural identity.'}",heritage conservation (2); heritage tourism (1); heritage site management (2); preservation efforts (1),Lijiang Old Town (10); Old Town of Lijiang (4); Dayan (2); Black Dragon Pond (1); Baisha (1); Shuhe (1); Shennong Heritage Street (1); Jeju Island (1); Harbin (1); Gulangyu Island (1),China (10); Taiwan (1); Michigan (1); Finland (1); Macau (2); Yunnan (5); Dali (4),,COVID‑19 (13),"To address the gap, this study analyzes a total of 50,022 valid online reviews and applies a triangulated framework that combines TF‑IDF (lexical salience), SNA (semantic network structure), and LDA (latent topics).","(1) Exploring how a data‑driven mixed‑method design can trace the temporal evolution of destination image by triangulating salient terms, shifting semantic structures, and latent cultural themes, with attention to both stability and transformation across cri‑ sis stages. (2) Revealing the temporal shifts in the components of destination image in cultural her‑ itage tourism, and further examining how tourists’ culturally rooted and commer‑ cially oriented perceptions are restructured in response to external disruptions, with the pandemic as a representative case.",Ctrip (1); Qunar (1); Mafengwo (1); Dianping (1); Twitter (1),text (5); online reviews (7); user‑generated content (UGC) (6),,,seasonal change (1),,,"50,022",,,,,,,January 2017 to October 2024 (2017–2024),Lijiang Old Town,crawled,,,"cleaning the UGC data by removing noise, duplicate reviews, overly short comments, and eliminating symbols and unnecessary punctuations",,manual (6),authors,[],,topic modeling (3); clustering (3),Topic Models (1),LDA (14),,TF‑IDF (14),,text (1),,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,"This framework was operationalized using Python 3.12.6 for TF‑IDF and LDA computations, and Gephi 0.10.1 for SNA‑based network visualization.",yes,"['semantic network (7)', 'social network (SNA) (10)', 'co‑occurrence networks (1)']",keywords (4),co‑occurrence frequencies (2),,,degree (1); modularity (5); clustering coefficients (2); Betweenness Centrality (6); Closeness Centrality (6),"construct a word co‑occurrence matrix using Scikit‑learn, where each cell denotes the frequency with which two keywords appear together in individual comments",modularity analysis (2); k‑core filter (1),Gephi (4); Force Atlas2 layout (1),explore micro‑level rela‑ tionships among keywords as a complement to the TF‑IDF analysis,perplexity (2); coherence (3); Jensen–Shannon (JS) divergence (3),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],Word Clouds (2); Sankey diagram (2),"['The Lijiang’s destination image exhibited notable cultural stability throughout the three periods, as demonstrated by the consistent appearance of 53% cross‑stage keywords in the TF‑IDF analysis', 'the semantic network revealed a substantial reinforcement of the associa‑\ntion between “Mu Family Mansion” and “guided interpretation”', 'Tourists’ perception of\nNaxi culture appears to be largely constructed through guided narratives within the Mu\nFamily Mansion, with their understanding increasingly anchored to this specific cultural node.']",[],[],"['Tourist perceptions of Lijiang Old Town noticeably piv‑\noted toward introspective and relaxing elements, cultivating an emotional narrative built around “slow pace” and “tranquility”.']",[],[],[],"['cultural heritage tourism management should strengthen the cogni‑\ntive accessibility of diverse heritage expressions, encouraging deeper interaction between tourists and authentic cultural values']","['This study not only revealed the trend of structural reconfiguration of destination image under major public crises (i.e., the pandemic) but also validated the strengths of a mixed‑method approach in capturing semantic nuances and dynamic cogni‑\ntion.']",[],[],"['Although the content type was standardized, differences in user demographics across plat‑\nforms were not deeply analyzed, which may influence the interpretation of destination image construction.', 'the current analytical framework fo‑\ncused primarily on textual content and did not incorporate visual or multimodal expres‑\nsions, which may limit the comprehensiveness of tourist perception capture']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,,,Python 3.12.6,Scikit-learn; Network X,,,,,
_Wibawa et al.-2024-Exploring Visitor Sentiments A.txt,Exploring Visitor Sentiments: A Study of Nusantara Temple Reviews on,Hariyono; A.P.Wibawa; E.F.Nugroho; G.C.Laksana; H.R.Cahyo; A.B.P.Utama; F.A.Dwiyanto,Universitas Negeri Malang,2024,journal article,,,https://doi.org/10.47738/jads.v5i2.208,https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/,,,cultural heritage (3),,Nusantara Temples (5); Borobudur (6); Prambanan (7); Ijo (6); Plaosan (6); Mendut (6); historical tourism sites (2); ancient sites,cultural and historical legacy (1); cultural assets (2); historical tourism sites (2); historical and cultural significance (2); preservation (6); restoration (3); cultural heritage environment (1); cultural variety (1); cultural representatives (1); historical legacy (2); cultural identity (2); historical origins (1); historical legacy (1); cultural identity (1); historical origins (1); ecosystem services methodology (1); environmental conservation (3); cultural heritage conservation (2); cultural treasures (1),,"{'value': 'Social (Collective)', 'reasoning': 'The study focuses on understanding the sentiments of tourists towards the Nusantara Temples, which are significant cultural and historical sites in Indonesia. These temples contribute to the cultural identity and pride of the local community and the nation as a whole.'}; {'value': 'Economic (Entertainment)', 'reasoning': 'The study mentions that Indonesia is a top destination for foreign travelers and that the Nusantara Temples are well-known temples in Indonesia. This suggests that these temples play a role in the tourism market and contribute to the entertainment value for tourists.'}; {'value': 'Political (Educational)', 'reasoning': ""The study suggests that temples serve as educational resources for history, technology, architectural art, and ancient belief systems. Visiting temples offers significant education, expanding the present generation's knowledge about ancient civilizations.""}; {'value': 'Historic (Artistic)', 'reasoning': ""The study describes the temples as enduring symbols of intricate history, representing the magnificence of past Hindu and Buddhist societies. The temples' architectural designs and sculptures showcase the exceptional skill of artisans from the past.""}; {'value': 'Aesthetic (Artistic)', 'reasoning': ""The study mentions the aesthetic appeal of temples and describes them as architectural wonders. The temples' intricate construction, reliefs, and majestic sculptures are examples of original products of creativity and imagination.""}; {'value': 'Age (Existential)', 'reasoning': 'The study highlights that temples represent more than just tangible buildings; they serve as vital links between the past and present. Temples serve as a testament to the grandeur of Hindu and Buddhist civilizations, providing insight into our historical origins.'}",conservation (7); promotion (4); restoration (3); upgrading amenities (1); increasing accessibility (1); repair (1); upkeep (1); sustainable management (2); disaster education (1); protection techniques (1),Nusantara Temples; Borobudur Temple (10); Prambanan Temple (9); Ijo Temple (8); Plaosan Temple (8); Mendut Temple (7),"Indonesia (11); Central Java, Indonesia (1)",19th century (1),Volcanic eruptions and floods in the 19th century damaged Borobudur Temple in Indonesia,"Research on travelers' feelings towards Indonesian tourist locations is sparse, particularly in the field of sentiment analysis in English, despite the growing global interest in Indonesia's cultural assets.",This research aims to analyze English reviews on the Trip Advisor website to comprehend worldwide opinions of the Nusantara Temples.,Trip Advisor (5),text (2),,,,,,5469,,4975,,,,,February 2015 and February 2020,five well-known temples in Indonesia,"manual scraping procedures, which involved copying and pasting data from a website into a spreadsheet",,,,,,,"['Positive', 'Negative']",,sentiment analysis (5); classification (10),logistic regression; support vector machine,SGD (6); LR (8); SVM (7); LSTM (1); RNNs (1); CNNs (1); Bi-LSTM (1),,,bag of words (Bo W) (2),,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,Accuracy (6); Precision (3); Recall (3); F1-score (3),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Reliance on English data from Trip Advisor might create bias in sentiment analysis, particularly from local guests who speak other languages.', 'while employing classification methods like LR, SGD, and SVM provides acceptable outcomes, the study might benefit from investigating advanced classification approaches, such as Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) or Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), to improve the accuracy of sentiment analysis.', 'We acknowledge the limitation of our study in categorizing temples based solely on textual sentiment analysis.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,,,,,,English- language evaluations (1); English reviews (1); English data (1); synthetic minority oversampling technique (SMOTE) (4); synthetic data points (1); minority class (6); majority class (3); hyperplane parameters (1); Support vectors (1); bias value (1); logit function (1); maximum likelihood (1); R-Square (R2) (1); harmonic mean (1); true positive (2); false negative predictions (1); tourist operators (1); local people (1); local inhabitants (1); tourist guides (1); local and worldwide populations (1); ecosystem services methodology (1); disaster education (1); environmental conservation (1); action plan (2); implementation strategy (2); stakeholder collaboration (1); community engagement (2); capacity building (1),,,
_Wilkins et al.-2022-Promises and pitfalls of using c.txt,Promises and pitfalls of using computer vision to make inferences about landscape preferences: Evidence from an urban-proximate park system,Emily J. Wilkins; Derek Van Berkel; Hongchao Zhang; Monica A. Dorning; Scott M. Beck; Jordan W. Smith,"Department of Environment and Society, Utah State University, Logan, UT 84322, USA; Institute of Outdoor Recreation and Tourism, Utah State University, Logan, UT 84322, USA; School for Environment and Sustainability, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109, USA; Department of Geosciences and Natural Resources, Western Carolina University, Cullowhee, NC 28723, USA; American Farmland Trust, Washington, DC 20036, USA",2021,journal article,Landscape and Urban Planning,Elsevier B V,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.landurbplan.2021.104315,www.elsevier.com/locate/landurbplan,,124–140,,,,,,,,Boulder Open Space and Mountain Parks,"Colorado, USA; Denver; Southern France; Arctic",,,"While the rapidly growing body of research using social media is impressive, little work has been done to compare the image content of social media to preferences elucidated via more traditional methods.",Our goal is to determine if a computer vision algorithm can generate valid inference about recreationists’ landscape preferences. We address this goal by answering two research questions: 1. How do park visitors’ social media photographs of landscape features compare to those that visitors say positively impacted their park experiences? 2. Do landscape preferences vary between visitors who post photographs of their park visit on social media and those who do not?,social media; internet; Twitter; Flickr; Instagram; Facebook,photographs; text; metadata,,,,,,9394,885,,9394,,,,2004 to 2018,Boulder OSMP lands,downloaded,Application Programming Interface (API),stratified sampling approach based on the six OSMP landscape character areas,,,manual,two authors,[],94%,classification,,Google Cloud Vision algorithm,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],publicly available,publicly available,,doi:10.3886/E139681V1,R version 4.0.3,,,,,,
_Winder et al.-2022-An open-source image classifier.txt,An open-source image classifier for characterizing recreational activities across landscapes,Samantha G. Winder; Heera Lee; Bumsuk Seo; Emilia H. Lia; Spencer A. Wood,,2022,journal article,People and Nature,,https://doi.org/10.1002/ pan3.10382,https://besjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/pan3.10382,,1249–1262,,,,,,,,Middle Fork Snoqualmie River Valley; Mountain Loop Highway corridor; Mount Baker-­Snoqualmie National Forest,Washington; USA; western Washington; Seattle,,,"Few studies, however, have evaluated what biases are inherent to this approach and whether it is truly reproducible.","This study explores the potential for social media and computer vision to map where ecosystems create opportunities for recreational activity, as one example of a CES. We introduce a convo- lutional neural network (CNN) that uses AI to recognize outdoor activities in the content of photographs posted to social media.",Flickr; Twitter; Instagram,photographs; images; text,geotagged,,,,,,,,"14,839 photographs from the Middle Fork region and 18,350 photos in the Mountain Loop region",,,,between 2005 and 2018,Middle Fork and Mountain Loop regions,querying the Flickr application programming interface (API),Flickr application programming interface (API),randomly sampled,,,manually,researchers,['recreational activity classes'],,classification,convolutional neural network; CNN,Inception (Inception Res Net V2),re-­using the trained weights provided with the original CNN model,,,photographs,,60% (7140) of the labelled images and withheld 40% (4772) of the images for validation,categorical cross-­entropy loss function,Adam optimization algorithm,1e-­5,512,300,dropout layer,"random geometric transformations in each epoch (such as flipping, resiz- ing, brightening and rotating",{},,workstation with an eight-­core CPU (AMD Ryzen 2700x) and 64 GB memory,NVIDIA Titan Xp,single,12 GB of video memory,12.5 h,"The final model contained 164 layers, the first 160 of which were taken directly from the original Inception model.","We used the 11,912 labelled images to train the final four cus- tom layers in our CNN model. In other words, only the four layers were trained against the labelled Flickr photos while the early lay- ers were unchanged or ‘frozen’ during training (Chollet et al., 2018). We sought to minimize the categorical cross-­entropy loss func- tion, a common function used to estimate how well predicted class probabilities match the target classes, using the Adam optimization algorithm (learning rate = 1e-­5) (Chollet, 2015).",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy; precision; recall; F1 score; Cohen's unweighted Kappa,"['overall accuracy of 0.71', 'macro F1 = 0.61', ""Cohen's unweighted Kappa = 0.59"", 'overall accuracy of 0.60', 'macro F1 = 0.59', 'Kappa = 0.53']",[],[],one-­sided test of the overall accuracy p < 0.0001,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['This is, to our knowledge, the first open-\xadsource solution to be evaluated for this particular classification problem.']",[],[],"['not all recreational activities are represented proportionally in images posted to Flickr', 'Flickr users are unlikely to be a representative sample of visitors to the study region']",['Studies that store and analyse images containing people should follow practices that protect the privacy of individuals who appear in the images'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],The customized CNN model (called rec CNNize) is publicly available at our Github repository https://github.com/Outdo or RD/rec CN Nize.,"The training weights, survey data and all scripts used in this manuscript are available on OSF at https://doi.org/10.17605/ OSF. IO/SNHWJ (Winder et al., 2022).",,https://doi.org/10.17605/ OSF. IO/SNHWJ,,Tensorflow; Keras APIs,,cultural ecosystem services; recreational activities; environmental management; machine learning; open source; image recognition; convolutional neural network,,,
_Wu et al.-2025-Compensating eco-recreation oppo.txt,Compensating eco-recreation opportunities based on the supply-demand relationship between large-scale urban eco-spaces and communities,Yuchun WU; Zhaocheng Bai; Jun QI,"College of Landscape Architecture and Horticulture, Southwest Forestry University, Kunming 650224, China; Department of Landscape Architecture, College of Architecture and Urban Planning, Tongji University, Shanghai 200092, China; Engineering Technology Research Center of National Forestry and Grassland Administration on Southwest Landscape Architecture, Kunming 650224, China",2025,journal article,Sustainable Cities and Society,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scs.2025.106423,www.elsevier.com/locate/scs,,,,,,,,,,Kunming City,China; London; England; Kunming City; Panlong District; Wuhua District; Xishan District; Guandu District; Chenggong District; Jinning District,,,"However, existing research has predominantly focused on recreation opportunities within urban parks and open spaces, overlooking the eco-recreation potential of large-scale urban eco-spaces and failing to address disparities in access through targeted compensatory measures.","This study examines how the spatial relationship between communities and large-scale urban eco-spaces shapes disparities in ecorecreation opportunities, using Kunming City as a case study.",RED; Baidu Map; OpenStreetMap,text; images; geotagged posts,geotags; GPS coordinates,,,,location tags,9546,,,,,,,recent 5 years,Kunming City,collected posts,,,"filtering posts only in recent 5 years; (2) merging posts with identical names, such as ""Xishan"" and ""Xishan Scenic Area""; (3) removing hyperlinks and emoticons lacking emotional expression; and (4) denoising the data using a stopword list composed of common stopwords and high-frequency words irrelevant to recreation.",posts only in recent 5 years,manually labeling,,[],,topic modeling; classification; sentiment analysis,,LDA,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,