_Xia et al.-2025-Multi-Modal Social Media Analyti.txt,Multi-Modal Social Media Analytics: A Sentiment Perception-Driven Framework,Meng Xia; Zhengyang Lu; Feng Wang,"School of Design, Jiangnan University; School of Design, Jiangnan University; School of Design, Jiangnan University",2025,other,,,,https://github.com/Mnster00/NJ_socialmedia,,,Cultural Heritage (3),tangible (3); intangible (4),historical landmarks (1); historical architecture (1); historical building (1),cultural heritage (7); tangible heritage (2); intangible heritage (2),,,cultural preservation (2),Nanjing (20); Nanjing’s eleven districts (7); Gulou (9); Xuanwu (3); Qinhuai (6); Jianye (5); Gaochun (7); Jiangning (3); Luhe (3); Lishui (1); Yuhuatai (1); Pukou (1); Qixia (1),China (8); Chinese (7); Jiangsu Province,,,"This study investigates the complex urban-rural dynamics of Nanjing through a novel multi-modal analysis of 76,288 social media posts, addressing the critical gap in understanding intra-city variations in perceived urban characteristics across rapidly developing Chinese cities.","By combining advanced computer vision techniques with natural language processing, we develop a comprehensive framework for analyzing public sentiment and attention patterns across eleven districts.",Weibo (7),text (10); images (14); visual (11); spatial data (2); geospatial data (1); multi-modal data (11),geolocation tags (2); geotagged (1),spatial patterns (6); GIS mapping (1); spatial variations (5); spatial distribution (2); spatial autocorrelation (1),temporal variations (2); longitudinal trends (1); temporal dynamics (1); daily crawls (1); seasonal events (1),,,"76,288 (2)",,"76,288","35,118 (Gulou), 30,560 (Xuanwu), 2,477 (Gaochun)",,,,January to December 2023 (2),Nanjing’s eleven districts (2),"We collected data through Weibo’s official API v2, implementing rate-limiting and error handling to ensure data integrity.",Weibo’s official API v2,,"duplicate posts, validated geolocation tags",retaining only those posts explicitly tagged within Nanjing’s eleven administrative districts,manually annotated,,"['positive', 'neutral', 'negative']",,topic classification (6); sentiment analysis (10),Large Language Model (LLM) (10); convolutional neural networks (CNNs) (2); transformer models (2); Random Forest models (2),Res Net-152 (2); Qwen-7B-chat (3); BERT (3); Ro BERTa (2); De BERTa-v3 (3),fine-tuned (4),,,image captions; post text; visual features,,,,,,,,,,{},Ro BERTa; De BERTa-v3; BERT baseline,,,,,,,The LLM takes the encoded visual features as input and generates a descriptive caption through an autoregressive,no,[],,,,,,,,,,BLEU-4 (2); METEOR (2); CIDEr (2); SPICE (3); accuracy (5),"['topic: 0.872', 'sentiment: 0.836', 'Ro BERTa (0.858, 0.812)', 'De BERTa-v3 (0.863, 0.819)']",[],[],p < 0.01,,[],[],,[],[],[],,Ro BERTa; De BERTa-v3,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['We introduce a novel multi-modal framework for analyzing urban perceptions using social media data, integrating advanced computer vision and natural lan- guage processing techniques.']","['We provide empirical evidence of complex urban-rural gradients in digital perceptions across Nanjing’s dis- tricts, challenging traditional urban-rural dichotomies and contributing to theories of post-reform Chinese urbanization.']",[],"['The reliance on social media data may introduce biases due to varying platform usage across demographic groups and districts.', 'Additionally, the analysis captures perceptions at a specific point in time, potentially missing longitudinal trends in urban imagery.']",['data protection'],['biases'],['ethical guidelines'],[],[],[],[],[],we provide codes and keyword tables online at https://github.com/Mnster00/NJ_socialmedia,we have made available aggregated statistical data from our analysis and a sample of 500 anonymized posts at same website.,,https://github.com/Mnster00/NJ_socialmedia,,,,urban-rural dynamics (5); urban perceptions (6); digital urban perceptions (3); urban imagery (4); digital engagement (2); socioeconomic (5); urban planning (4); place-making strategies (2); urban governance (2); digital divide (2); urban-rural continuum (3); polycentric urban development (4); digital territories (1); socio-spatial imaginaries (1); digital-spatial dialectic (1); spatial consciousness (1); territorial identity (1); urban-rural integration (4); digital inclusion zones (1); Virtual District Forum (1); green corridors (1); Cultural Exchange Program (1),,,
_Xu et al.-2024-Exploring the Built Environment.txt,Exploring the Built Environment Factors Influencing Town Image Using Social Media Data and Deep Learning Methods,"Xu, W.; Zeng, P.; Liu, B.; Cai, L.; Sun, Z.; Liu, S.; Tang, F.",,2024,journal article,Land,"MDPI, Basel, Switzerland",https://doi.org/10.3390/land13030291,https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/ 4.0/,,14 of 21,,,characteristic towns (2); town spaces (1); town scale (4),,,,,Tianjin,China; Tianjin; United Kingdom; Canada; Africa; Uganda; Asia,,COVID-19 epidemic (1),"cities have lost their urban characteristics while gaining new faces [1], and are facing the problem of the retreat of city image [2].",This study attempts to explore the association between town image and objectified BE to enhance image performance from a human-centered perspective.,Sina Weibo; Little Red Book,pictures; texts,,,,,,"11,196 pictures",,"57,719 pieces of text data",,,,,January to April 2023,26 towns of Tianjin’s municipal-level characteristic towns,Python 3.10 to request the official open APIs of Weibo and Little Red Book,official open APIs of Weibo and Little Red Book,Using the town’s name as the keyword and selecting the column of “location check-in” to the search will ensure that the user has an image recognition of the target town.,"pictures that have nothing to do with town impressions, such as personal events posted by users, will be removed.","pictures that have nothing to do with town impressions, such as personal events posted by users, will be removed.",deep learning models,,"['Building', 'Public space', 'Landmark', 'Cultural custom', 'Mountain', 'Water', 'Animal', 'Plant', 'Sky']",,classification,convolutional neural network,VGG19,,,,,,7:3,,,,,100,,,{},,server,,,,,,"We trained for 100 epochs to obtain the most stable model, which achieved 93.2% accuracy.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy,['93.2% accuracy'],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['The acquisition of multi-source big data and the method of deep learning, so that the image can be accurately quantified.', 'The exploration of the influence of the BE on the UI at the town scale.', 'The reveal of how positive emotion regulates the relationship between different BE variables and the UI.']",[],[],"['due to the unstructured and voluntary nature of social media data, UI data may be underrepresented for the elderly population and low-income people', 'due to manpower and time constraints, this study is based on an empirical investigation of a characteristic town in Tianjin, China, resulting in a limited number of research cases.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author. The data are not publicly available because research is ongoing.,,,,,,town uniqueness image; built environment; positive emotions; moderating effects; stimulus organism response (S-O-R) model; cognitive map surveys; spatial syntax; multi-source big data; artificial intelligence technology; Chinese Twitter; Sina Weibo; Little Red Book; application programming interface (API); Gaode map; Open Street Map; China Statistical Yearbook (Township); Nature Language Processing (NLP),,,
_Xu et al.-2024-Spatial Quality Optimization Ana.txt,Spatial Quality Optimization Analysis of Streets in Historical,1Faculty; 2Faculty; 3Faculty; 4Dept,"Faculty of Architecture and Arts, Hefei Univ. of Technology, Heifei 230000, China; Faculty of Architecture and Arts, Hefei Univ. of Technology, Heifei 230000, China; Faculty of Architecture and Arts, Hefei Univ. of Technology, Heifei 230000, China; Dept. of Landscape Architecture, Kyungpook National Univ., Daegu 41566, South Korea",2024,journal article,Journal of Urban Planning,American Society of Civil Engineers,10.1061/JUPDDM.UPENG-4770,,,,Cultural Heritage (1),cultural (7); historic (14),historic urban areas (12); historic urban area (4); historic cities (1); historic towns (1); historic urban (1); Suzhou Ancient Urban (12); Gusu Ancient Area (1),cultural values (1); cultural history (1); cultural memory (1); cultural heritage (4); cultural landscapes (1); cultural monuments (1); cultural industries (1); cultural functions (4); cultural attributes (1); cultural characteristics (2); cultural significance (1); monuments (2); preservation (2); renewal (8); conservation (1); historic (14); historic buildings (3); historic streets (3); historic areas (11); historic collective memory (1); historic district (1); historic building (1); historic alleys (1); historic area (1); historic urban areas (12); historic urban area (4); historic cities (1); historic towns (1); historic urban (1); organic urban renewal (3); urban renewal (4); spatial quality (18); spatial syntax (5); spatial characteristics (4); spatial patterns (2); spatial structure (3); spatial quality optimization (3); spatial quality of streets (5); spatial quality of street (1); spatial quality in historic urban areas (2); spatial quality of historic urban areas (1); spatial quality of the historic urban area (1); spatial quality evaluation (2); spatial quality of streets in historic urban areas (1); spatial quality of urban streets (1); spatial quality of urban space (1); spatial quality of streets in historic urban areas (1); spatial quality of historic urban areas (1); spatial quality of streets (1); spatial quality in historic urban areas (1); spatial quality of historic areas (1); spatial quality of urban areas (1); spatial quality of urban streets (1); spatial quality of urban space (1); spatial quality of streets in historic urban areas (1); spatial quality of historic urban areas (1); spatial quality of streets (1); spatial quality in historic urban areas (1); spatial quality of historic areas (1); spatial quality of urban areas (1),,"{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the importance of historic urban areas in perpetuating local memories and symbolizing the image of a particular city, which aligns with the definition of collective emotional value.'}; {'value': 'Economic (Use)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions that the high-quality spatial quality of streets contributes to public perception and provides opportunities for social interaction, leisure, and relaxation, which contributes to the frequency of outdoor activities and physical and mental well-being of the public, thus activating the social vitality of the society, and also has a great impact on the promotion of urban image and economic value.'}; {'value': 'Political (Educational)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the role of historic urban areas in carrying traditional urban culture and their characteristic spatial patterns, morphological textures, and style features play a decisive role in the overall urban spatial structure.'}; {'value': 'Historic-Artistic', 'reasoning': 'The paper emphasizes the unique spatial patterns, cultural heritage, and style features of historic urban areas, which aligns with the definition of historic-artistic value.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical (Artistic)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the importance of visual elements and aesthetic qualities in shaping public perception and behavioral choices in historic urban environments.'}; {'value': 'Age (Existential)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions that historic urban areas symbolize the image of a particular city and perpetuate local memories, which aligns with the definition of existential value.'}",preservation (2); renewal (8); conservation (1),Suzhou Ancient Urban (12); Gusu Ancient Area (1),China (6); Jiangsu Province (2); Yangtze River Delta (1); Beijing (1),Song Dynasty (1),,"Although historical urban areas retain traditional physical spaces, urbanization demands changes in the physical environment to meet modern public needs. Therefore, understanding the public’s perception and evaluation of urban areas is essential for a human-centered approach to urban quality improvement",This research focuses on evaluating street quality perception in historic urban areas and combines the evaluation of urban physical attributes based on multisource data to explore methods of spatial quality optimization in historical urban areas.,Weibo (4); Sina Weibo (1),social media post data (2); text (3); images (11); street view images (10); geotagged social media posts (1); geographic coordinates (2); location data (1); road network data (3),geographic coordinates (2); location data (1); geotags (1),spatial hotspot analysis (1); spatial distribution (1),,likes (2); comments (2),location name (3); longitude (2); latitude (2); text content (2); date (2); number of comments (1); likes (2),"13,706 (1)",,,"11,744 street view images (1)",,,,,Suzhou Ancient Urban (1),scraping (1),Web Services API on the Baidu Map open platform (1),filtering out posts containing location information in the text and those with geotags (1),categorized and trained the data on positive and negative sentiment based on Snow NLP (1),Posts that include location data (1),human–computer confrontation model (2),25 members of the public from various regions of China and 10 local residents who have a better understanding of Suzhou Ancient Urban (1),"['beautiful–ugly (3)', 'neat–messy (3)', 'vibrant–depressing (3)', 'safe–dangerous (3)', 'characteristic–nondescript (3)', 'wide–crowded (3)']",,semantic segmentation (5); sentiment analysis (3); natural language processing (3); prediction (2); text classification (1),deep convolutional neural network (2); random forest model (3); Bayesian method (1),PSPNet (7); Snow NLP (6),trained on a 5 million microblog corpus from the Git Hub platform for sentiment analysis (1),,,street view elements (3); element proportions (1); text content (1),,"One-third of the samples were out-of-bag (OOB) data to assess the overall error and identify the importance of variables, while the remaining two-thirds were used for data ﬁtting and classiﬁcation (1)",,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,"The PSPNet model comprises two submodules: a feature map and a Pyramid Pooling Module. The former is used to extract image features, while the latter pools the feature layers from the former at different scales, thereby facilitating the mining of contextual semantic information (1)","For a given street view image, features are initially extracted in the feature map through CNN processing. Subsequently, within the Pyramid Pooling Module, the extracted feature map is resized through a POOL layer and then passed through a CONV convolution layer, reducing the number of its channels. An upsampling operation is then conducted to restore the feature map to its original size, which is then spliced with the original feature map. Finally, the number of channels in the CONV layer is reduced to the number of categories, yielding the segmented image (1)",yes,['social networks (1)'],POIs (1),connected these various POI spaces to their corresponding streets in Arc Py (1),,,,spatially joined to the streets in Arcpy (1),,,analyze the street functions in Suzhou Ancient Urban (1),accuracy rate (1),"['The accuracy of the PSPNet model on the ADE20K dataset was veriﬁed, achieving an accuracy rate of 80.04% (1)']",[],[],,"The results reveal that the streets with high-perception ratings, including Renmin Road, Dongbei Street, Pingjiang Road, Lindun Road, Zhongjie Road, Jingde Road, Shiqi Road, Shuyuan Lane Siqian Street, Ganjiang East Road, Ganjiang West Road, Faru Lane, Fenghuang Street, and Shiquan Street, are mainly situated in the thoroughfares of Suzhou Ancient Urban (1)",[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],perception map (3); spatial heat map (2),['regional culture is conﬁrmed as a crucial factor inﬂuencing urban vitality and economic development (1)'],[],['the imagery and legibility of Suzhou Ancient Urban are strong and that its historic collective memory of the public (1)'],"['the public’s perceptual evaluation of Suzhou Ancient Urban was overwhelmingly favorable, with very few negative emotional evaluations (1)']",[],[],"['assist tourists in creating personalized travel plans and efﬁcient travel routes in combination with map software (1)', 'recommend spaces suitable for walking and cycling to local residents (1)', 'drive targeted and comprehensive improvements in the spatial quality of speciﬁc streets based on public perception (1)']",['This research identiﬁes the characteristics of highly attractive and perceived streets in historic urban areas. These characteristics can be leveraged to enhance the attractiveness and quality of underperforming street spaces (1)'],"['develops a comprehensive method for evaluating public perception of visual quality in historic urban areas, considering factors such as aesthetics, orderliness, ambiance, and scale (1)', 'integrating street view imagery with inﬂuential evaluation factors based on the unique humanistic environment of historic urban areas (1)', 'merging subjective and objective evaluations to measure the spatial quality of historic urban areas, examining the spatial characteristics of these areas that are most appreciated by the public (1)']",[],[],"['the street view images for some major historical streets, such as Pingjiang Road and Dingxiang Lane, were incomplete (1)', 'the number of volunteers in this research was limited, and perceptions of historic urban areas can vary among different individuals (1)', 'this research focused speciﬁcally on Suzhou Ancient Urban as a sample (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request (1),"Some or all data, models, or codes that support the ﬁndings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request (1)",,,,Arc Py (1),,urban vitality (4); human-centered (3); human-oriented (1); humanistic environment (2); human–computer confrontation model (2); ADE20K data set (2); Baidu street view images (BSVIs) (1); OpenStreet Map (OSM) (3); Gaode Polygon Search API (2); Arcpy (3); Python (4); GIS (4); Depth Map (2); Pycharm (2); Scrapy (1); Web Services API (1); Snow NLP (6); Git Hub platform (1); microblog corpus (1); plain Bayesian method (1); natural breaks (Jenks) (1); thermal analysis (2); nuclear density analysis (1); linear regression analysis (1); trumpet creeper (1); multiﬂora rose (1); age-appropriate street space (1); ageappropriate evaluation index system (1),,,
_Xu et al.-2024-Tibetan microblogging user data.txt,Tibetan microblogging user data analysis and topic,,,2024,conference paper,"23rd International Conference on Trust, Security and Privacy in Computing and Communications (Trust Com)",IEEE,10.1109/Trust Com63139.2024.0015,https://www-cnnic-cn-s.vpn.muc.edu.cn:8118/n4/2024/0322/c8810964.html,,1077,cultural heritage (1),,,Culture (4); cultural (1); traditional culture (1),,,,,Tibetan areas (6); China (3); Sichuan (2); Qinghai (2); Tibet (4); U.S.A (1); Ali region (1); Ritu County (1),,5.2-magnitude earthquake struck Ritu County in Tibet's Ali region in April,"In the information age, the number of Tibetan microblog users grows, and the importance of network text data is highlighted. These data contain rich emotional, cultural and social significance, and in-depth mining and analysis can discover hot topics in a timely manner, support linguistic and social science research and information technology applications, help the government guide public opinion, and maintain the safety and harmony of Tibetan social networks.","Combined with the cultural background, topic identification technology is particularly important and challenging in Tibetan areas. Enhancing the collection and analysis of Tibetan microblog data and efficiently identifying hot topics is important for promoting the healthy development of the network environment, maintaining information security, and providing decision-making support for governments, enterprises, and individuals.",Weibo (2); Sina Weibo (2); microblog (14),text (4),,,,,user ID (2); user name (1); microblog details (1); authentication status (1); gender (3); microblog content (2); microblog comments (1),193687,7945,,,,,,January to April 2024,"Sichuan, Qinghai, and Tibet",crawler,Python (6),,"regular expressions are used to quickly extract Tibetan text based on the unicode encoding of Tibetan letters and vowels (\u0f40-\u0f68 and \u0f74, \u0f7a, \u0f7c, \u0f72), remove redundancy, and improve the data quality.",,,,[],,topic modeling (2); topic identification (4),Topic Models,LDA (11); BTM (10),,,,,,,,,,,50,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,Topic Coherence (3); C_V (2); U_Mass (1),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['Tibetan microblog data is often mixed with Chinese user data when crawling'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,Tibetan (23); Tibetan-language (2); Tibetan-speaking regions (1); Tibetan Buddhism (1); Lunar New Year (4); Spring Festival (4); Losar (1); network administrators (1); cybersecurity (3); network monitoring (1); ethnicity (1); administration (1),,,Security monitors use this to assess security incidents and provide data to support the development of emergency response strategies.
_Xu et al.-2025-Revealing youth-perceived cultur.txt,Revealing youth-perceived cultur,H. Xu; J. Duan; M. Ren; G. Zhao; Z. Liu,"School of Architecture and Urban Planning, Beijing University of Civil Engineering and Architecture, Beijing 100041, China; School of Business, University of Sydney, Sydney, NSW 2006, Australia; Research center for Building, Climate and Water technology, VIA University College, Horsens 8700, Denmark; Department of the Built Environment, Aalborg University, Aalorg 9220, Denmark; State Key Laboratory of Earth Surface Processes and Disaster Risk Reduction, Faculty of Geographical Science, Beijing Normal University, Beijing 100875, China; School of Natural Resources, Faculty of Geographical Science, Beijing Normal University, Beijing 100875, China",2025,journal article,,,doi. org/ 10. 1007/ s10980- 025- 02115-y,https:// doi. org/ 10. 1007/ s10980- 025- 02115-y,,,,,historical buildings (2); historical monuments (2); historic neighborhoods; imperial-era architecture; heritage sites,cultural heritage (2); cultural ecosystem services (CESs) (14); heritage value; cultural heritage; aesthetic values; historical value; religious and spiritual significance; heritage sites,,"{'SOCIAL VALUES': 'Emotional (collective) – The study highlights the importance of social recreation and community engagement in urban green spaces, indicating a focus on cultural identity and communal value for youth.'}; {'AESTHETICAL VALUES': 'Artistic – The study identifies artistic and cultural appreciation as a significant cultural ecosystem service, suggesting the importance of original creativity and imagination in urban green spaces.'}; {'HISTORIC VALUES': 'Educational – The study mentions historical monuments and heritage sites, indicating the potential for these assets to provide knowledge about the past.'}",,central Beijing (11); Beijing (10); Dongcheng; Xicheng; Chaoyang; Haidian; Fengtai; Shijingshan; Houhai lake; Xiangshan scenic area,China (3); Asia; European; Europe,,the pandemic,"To bridge this gap, there is an urgent need to assess the youth perceived CESs provided by the UGSs in high-density megacities to inform spatially optimized UGS planning strategies that address both physical and psychological wellbeing outcomes.",1. Which type of CES is perceived the most by the general youth? 2. What’s the spatial pattern of the CES that youth perceived in central Beijing? 3. What’s is the respective contribution of various UGSs in central Beijing for youth perceived each CES?,Little Red Book (LRB) (13); Weibo(2); Wechat (3); Instagram (2),geo-tagged images; photographs (10); captions; images; metadata-enabled notes; texts; themes; the point of interest (POI); latitude and longitude,geo-tagged; geotagged; GPS metadata; geotags; latitude and longitude,spatial distribution; spatial correlation; heatmaps; spatial clustering; spatial distribution; spatial aggregation,posting time; real-time; timed access,,username; account ID; profile information; posting device; timestamp fields; language; location tags; description fields,9022,85 million monthly active users (LRB),8580,"62,138",,,9022,May 2020 to December 2021,central Beijing,crawling,LRB’s API,preliminary search utilizing CES-related keywords and “Beijing” as keywords,excluded geotagged LRB notes outside central Beijing,Notes outside boundary were automatically flagged for removal (n = 423,human-labeled,two trained researchers,"['Artistic and cultural appreciation', 'Historical monuments', 'Landscape appreciation', 'Nature appreciation on animals, plants', 'Natural structures and monuments', 'Religious and spiritual values', 'Research and education', 'Social recreation', 'Sport recreation', 'Other photographs']",,classification,convolutional neural networks (2); residual network structure,Resnet model (3); ResNet50 (10); Sim CLRv2 (3),pretrained Res Net50 based on the Sim CLRv2 framework,,,image pixels,,fivefold stratified cross-validation,,,,,,,horizontal flipping/color jittering; data augmentations,{'temperature coefficient': 'τ\u2009=\u20090.5'},,,,,,,model depth (50 layers),adopted a two-stage training strategy combining self-supervised learning with supervised fine-tuning to achieve high classification accuracy while addressing data scarcity in our domain,no,[],,,,,,,,,,accuracy (2); F1 score (2),"['achieving 85.3% accuracy', '0.827 macro F1 score', '0.864 weighted F1 score']",[],[],,,[],[],"potential biases can affect classification accuracy. Dataset bias is a primary concern, as model performance heavily depends on the diversity of training images. Limited and imbalanced labeled data may lead to biased predictions, affecting generalization to underrepresented categories.",[],"['Dataset bias', 'Limited and imbalanced labeled data']",[],fivefold stratified cross-validation,,[],heatmaps,"['nature and landscape appreciation clustering in the northwest, eastern, and old-town areas', 'social recreation, artistic expression, and sports activities concentrated along the central axis of the old town']",[],[],[],[],"['Significant geographical differences were observed, as the overall number of CES points showed a decreasing trend from the center to the edge of the study area.', 'The capital core area has the highest concentration of CES distribution, and as one moves away from the city center, the number of CES distribution points decreases.']","['urban green space (UGS) planning and management', 'urban planners identify areas with high potential for cultural, aesthetic, and recreational investments, ultimately enhancing UGS quality']","['Prioritize micro-green spaces in buildings and built environments', 'Enhance social functionality in urban parks', 'Revitalize bluespaces through adaptive governance', 'Optimize UGS quality for guiding visitor management', 'Leverage digital platforms for participatory planning']",['examining the CES demands of young people using social media photos and deep learning methods'],"['revealed that social recreation is t