_Yu and Egger-2022-Looking behind the scenes at dar.txt,Looking behind the scenes at dark tourism: comparing academic publications and Instagram posts,Joanne Yu; Roman Egger,Department of Tourism and Service Management at MODUL University Vienna; Salzburg University of Applied Sciences at the Department of Innovation and Management in Tourism,2022,journal article,Journal of Heritage Tourism,Taylor & Francis,https://doi.org/10.1080/1743873X.2022.2097011,https://doi.org/10.1080/1743873X.2022.2097011,,548 562,,,dark sites (11); dark attractions (3); dissonant heritage (2); Holocaust sites (3); war-related sites (4); heritage sites (1),dark tourism (41); thanatourism (5); heritage tourism (1); dissonant heritage (2); dark heritage (1); spectrum of darkness (3); black spot tourism (1); warfare tourism (1); morbid tourism (1); slave castles (5); Holocaust sites (3); battleﬁelds (2); heritage sites (1); pilgrimages (3); heritage sites (2); last-chance tourism (3); atrocity tourism (1); horror tourism (1); hardship tourism (1),,"{'value': 'Social (Emotional - collective)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses how visiting dark sites can be shaped by one’s national and cultural identity, suggesting a connection to cultural identity and communal value.'}; {'value': 'Political (Educational)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions that dark sites are staged with narrative stories, which can be used for political purposes such as nation-building myths.'}; {'value': 'Historic (Educational)', 'reasoning': 'The paper states that the main motivation for visiting dark sites is to gain an understanding of past events, indicating the educational role of heritage assets.'}; {'value': 'Social (Spiritual)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions that a pilgrim’s journey to dark sites is reflective and spiritual, implying a quest toward understanding deep historical roots.'}",,"Vienna; Philadelphia; Estonia; Czech Republic; Alcatraz Island, the USA; Robben Island, South Africa; Auschwitz, Poland; Battleﬁelds of the Western Front, France and Belgium; Shoah memorial museum in Jerusalem, the USA; Jeju Peace Park, Korea; Churchill, Canada; Holocaust descendants in Israel; Slave Castles, Ghana; Bosnia and Herzegovina; Vilna Gaon Jewish State Museum and Museum of Genocide Victims, Lithuania; Hashemite Kingdom, Jordan; West Bank, Palestine; Gettysburg, Pennsylvania; Sichuan earthquake; Westerbork, the Netherlands; Battleﬁeld in the UK; Great War town of Ieper, Belgium",Vienna; Philadelphia; Estonia; Czech Republic; the USA; South Africa; Poland; France; Belgium; Jerusalem; Korea; Canada; Israel; Ghana; Bosnia and Herzegovina; Lithuania; Jordan; West Bank; Palestine; Pennsylvania; Sichuan; Netherlands; the UK; Germany; Eastern Europe; Asia; Cambodia; Japan; Australia; Ukraine; Russia; Spain; Paris; London,the late twentieth century,9/11 attacks,"Although scholars have discussed the topic of dark tourism from various angles, little is known from tourists’ perspectives. Thus, it remains largely unanswered whether the research agendas in the ﬁeld of dark tourism also correspond to the reality of tourism or whether relevant topics remain unaddressed.",This study uses natural language processing approaches to uncover the diﬀerences and similarities in knowledge between tourists and researchers based on academic publications and Instagram posts.,Instagram; Twitter; Facebook; You Tube,"text (posts, captions) (1); images (1); check-in location (1)",check-in location (1); geographic coordinates (1),geographic flow map (1),date of the post (1),followers (1),post URL (1); users’ account type (1),"33,881","6,650","26,581",,,,,March 2020,dark tourism spots,Data was crawled,Google’s geocoding API,"To ensure that individual travellers published the posts, 1,939 business accounts and their corresponding posts were removed. Non-English posts were excluded, resulting in 12,835 posts published by 4,711 personal accounts.","Data pre-processing was conducted using numerous natural language processing modules in Python. A list of stopwords was prepared, and non-informative texts (e.g. I, we, and, or) were removed. Unknown characters, numbers, references to users (e.g. @username), and whitespaces were excluded. These steps allow plotting scaled class-speciﬁc word and bigram counts into a two-dimensional space, which is explained in the paragraphs below. Slang words were reformed, and diacritics were converted to the basic format. Finally, text data was tokenised into small units.",Non-English posts were excluded,,,[],,entity recognition (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,precision (2); recall (2); non-redundancy (2); characteristicness (2),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],scatterplot (3),[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,Python (4),,ghost hunting (3); urbex (urban exploration) (4); necropolis (2); taphophiles (1),,,
