_Zhang et al.-2024-Rethinking Cultural Ecosystem Se.txt,Rethinking Cultural Ecosystem Services in Urban Forest Parks: An Analysis of Citizens’ Physical Activities Based on Social Media Data,"Zhang, H.; Yu, J.; Dong, X.; Zhai, X.; Shen, J.",,2024,journal article,Forests,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/f15091633,https://doi.org/10.3390/f15091633,,,,cultural,urban forest parks (7),cultural ecosystem services (CESs) (13); cultural experience (1); natural landscapes (5); ecological environment (3); cultural elements (1); ecological protection (1); historical relics (1); forest resources (1),,"{'Aesthetical Values': 'Artistic – The natural landscapes of the parks, including plants and water bodies, provide visual aesthetic experiences for visitors. Notable – The parks may contain unique or signature elements that enhance their artistic appeal. Conceptual – The design and layout of the parks may reflect conceptual intentions related to aesthetics.'}; {'Social Values': 'Emotional (collective) – The parks serve as centers for vibrant cultural and social interaction, fostering a sense of community and belonging. Entertainment – The parks offer opportunities for leisure, recreation, and entertainment, enhancing the quality of life for urban residents.'}; {'Economic Values': 'Use – The parks provide venues for physical activities and cultural experiences, contributing to the well-being of citizens. Entertainment – The parks play a role in the contemporary market, especially tourism, attracting visitors and generating economic activity.'}; {'Political Values': 'Educational – The parks can serve an educational role, promoting awareness of ecological diversity and the natural environment. Management – The incorporation of the parks into political strategies and policies related to urban planning and environmental conservation.'}; {'Scientific Values': 'Ecological Values – The parks contribute to the harmony between the built environment and the natural environment. Essential – The parks may incorporate ecological ideologies in their design and construction.'}",ecological protection (1); planning (3),Beijing Olympic Forest Park; Xishan Forest Park; Beigong Forest Park,Beijing; China; Belgium,,2008 Olympics (1),This study aims to reevaluate CESs by focusing on the physical activity experiences of park visitors to optimize park management and enhance citizen satisfaction.,"(1) Which CES elements are most important to citizens engaging in PAs in urban forest parks? (2) What is the emotional tendency of citizens engaging in PAs in urban forest parks, as expressed on social media? (3) Based on the IPA model, which factors influence citizens’ experiences in urban forest parks?",X; Flickr; Instagram; Dianping; Mafengwo; Weibo; Wikiloc,text; images,,,,star ratings,user ID; evaluation dates,"22,012",,,,,,,1 January 2019 to 1 February 2024,Beijing,web crawler,Python-based web crawler,,merging or removing duplicates and blank entries. Emojis lacking emotional expression were excluded,,,,[],,topic modeling; sentiment analysis,Embedding-based models,Word2Vec,,word2vec,,text,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,['semantic network'],frequently mentioned experience elements in resident feedback,co-occurrence frequency of these elements,,,,,,,"reveal key experience elements frequently mentioned in the comments and their co-occurrence relationships, which are essential for understanding the factors that significantly enhance or reduce citizens’ satisfaction.",,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],['82.58% of comments expressing positive sentiments'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['reliance on a specific theoretical framework may not cover all social, environmental, and economic impacts associated with urban forest parks', 'data from a single social media platform may limit representativeness', 'while text analysis and NLP technology are powerful, their accuracy and interpretative abilities are limited', 'the subject of this study is the urban forest parks in Beijing. While these findings are representative to some extent, the results may vary due to differences in national regions, cultures, climates, and economic backgrounds', 'the study focuses less on the connection between park environments and specific physiological health outcomes']",Data will be made available on request.,Data will be made available on request.,,,,,,sports activity dictionary,,,
_Zhang et al.-2024-Scrutinizing the cultural ecosys.txt,Scrutinizing the cultural ecosystem services of Chinese Classical Gardens: A,Jiangyue Zhang; Yun Luo; Haojie Cao; Shiliang Su,"Urban Computing and Visualization Lab, School of Resource and Environmental Sciences, Wuhan University, Wuhan, China",2024,journal article,Applied Geography,Elsevier Ltd,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apgeog.2024.103404,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apgeog.2024.103404,,,Cultural Heritage,cultural,Chinese Classical Gardens (10); world cultural heritage sites (1),cultural ecosystem services (CESs) (20); world cultural heritage sites (1); historical values (1); artistic values (1); cultural historical values (1); sustainable landscape conservation (1); indigenous knowledge (2),,"{'value': 'Aesthetical Values', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the aesthetic appreciation of Chinese Classical Gardens, highlighting the scenery and beauty valued by visitors through five-senses perceptions. This aligns with the artistic and notable aspects of aesthetical values.'}; {'value': 'Social Values', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions the contribution of cultural ecosystem services (CESs) to enhancing physical and mental well-being, creating a sense of place, and fostering a feeling of belonging. These aspects relate to emotional (collective) and spiritual social values.'}; {'value': 'Historic Values', 'reasoning': 'The paper refers to the historical, artistic, and cultural historical values embodied by Chinese Classical Gardens. This aligns with the historic-artistic and educational aspects of historic values.'}; {'value': 'Economic Values', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the potential for destination marketing and precision advertising strategies based on users’ multisensory inclinations, which relates to the entertainment aspect of economic values.'}",landscape management (3); landscape conservation (1),Chinese Classical Gardens (10); Humble Administrator’s Garden (4); Lion Forest Garden (5); Lingering Garden (3); Master-of-Nets Garden (4); Yu Garden (6),China (3); Yangtze River Delta (1); eastern coastal China (1); North America (1); Europe (1); Asia (1); Oceania (1); Hubei Province (1),,COVID-19 (1),This paper works to narrow the research gaps through scrutinizing the CESs of Chinese Classical Gardens from a multisensory perspective.,(1) establish a framework for categorizing the CESs typologies of Chinese Classical Gardens from a multisensory perspective; (2) develop a deep learning approach to measuring the CESs of Chinese Classical tions for landscape management.,Trip Advisor (3); Weibo (1); Flickr (2); Dianping (1); Twitter (1),texts (3); images (4); videos (2),,,posting time (2),,review’s title (1); posting time (1); full text (1),7117,7117,7117,,,,,September–November 2019,20 classical gardens in China’s National Cultural Heritage List,web crawler,Java,,reviews in or later than January 2020 are deleted,reviews in or later than January 2020 are deleted,manually annotating,Five experts specialized in landscape CESs,[],,Named Entity Recognition (NER) (5); sequence labeling (2),deep neural networks (DNNs) (2); Transformers models (1),BERT (5),"pretrained BERT model, specifically trained on the NER task using the Co NLL-2003 dataset. For fine-tuning, we adopted the BIOES tagging scheme, as proposed by Chiu and Nichols (2016), and attempted to maximize sentence-level log-likelihood to determine the optimal parameters.",,,,,,,Adam,0.01,,,,,{},,,Nvidia V100,single,,,"We employed the Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) model, introduced by Devlin et al. (2019), as our foundational language model. To further refine our model’s ability to label sequences accurately, we incorporated a Bi-directional Long Short-Term (CRF) layer, as proposed by Ma and Hovy (2016).","To train our NER model, we employed a transfer learning approach to enhance the model’s capabilities by integrating prior knowledge from corpora across different fields. Parameter optimization was conducted using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.01 on a single Nvidia V100 GPU.",yes,['co-occurrence network (4)'],nodes represent different CES categories (2),links represent the co-occurrence times of different CES categories in each review (2),,,network centrality (2),co-occurrence analysis (2),,,to unravel the interrelationships among the CESs (1),F1 score (3); precision (2); recall (2),['The F1 score reaches 0.874 for the deep learning approach'],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['landscape management (2)', 'destination marketing (1)']","['optimize managerial plans (1)', 'sustainable landscape conservation (1)', 'inheritance of indigenous knowledge (1)']",['a novel deep learning approach is proposed to extract CESs based on online reviews from a multisensory perspective (1)'],[],['our paper introduces the multi-senses to refresh existing CES theoretical frameworks (1)'],"['social media data have been frequently criticized for possessing an inherent bias (1)', 'the experts, stakeholders and interested parties are not involved into scrutinizing the CESs of Chinese Classical Gardens (1)', 'the Trip Advisor is the only data source for analysis and data collected from diverse social media platforms should contribute to more reliable estimations (1)', 'the sample size of embodied walking interviews is not large enough to reach general conclusions (1)', 'we do not compare the CESs among visitors with different origins due to data restrictions and ethical concerns (1)']",['all the participants were confirmed for privacy protection (1)'],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,,,online reviews (8); multisensory perspective (10); five senses (6); textual data (4); deep learning (4); social media (4); narrative texts (2); green amenities (1); sensory experience (4); visual perception (11); auditory perception (7); olfactory perceptions (5); haptic perception (6); gustatory perception (6); embodied walking interviews (5); NVivo-12 (2); tourist opinions (1); tourist experiences (2); landscape elements (1); human activities (1); sense organs (1); active body receptors (1); digital infrastructures (1); virtual reality (1); augmented reality (1); precision advertising strategies (1); text mining (3); web crawler (1); Java (1); DNN-based NER approach (1); BIOES tagging scheme (1); Viterbi algorithm (1); Gephi 0.9.2 (1); landscape marketers (1); landscape features (1); landscape preferences (2); landscape values (3); landscape conservation (1); landscape ecology (1); landscape character assessment (1); landscape and urban planning (1); landscape properties (1); landscape marketing (1); landscape benefits (1); landscape diversity (1); landscape elements (1); landscape management (1); landscape features (1); landscape preferences (1); landscape values (1); landscape conservation (1); landscape ecology (1); landscape character assessment (1); landscape and urban planning (1); landscape properties (1); landscape marketing (1); landscape benefits (1); landscape diversity (1); landscape elements (1); landscape management (1),,,
_Zhang et al.-2025-Assessing perception and equity.txt,Assessing perception and equity,Y. Z; S. H; L. Z,,2025,journal article,,Springer Nature,10 . 1038 / s 4 1 5 9 8 - 0 2 5 - 1 8 2 8 8 - 0,h t t p s : / / d o i . o r g / 1 0 . 1 0 3 8 / s 4 1 5 9 8 - 0 2 5 - 1 8 2 8 8 - 0,,,,,urban parks (17); historical monuments (2),,,,,Wuhan; Shahu Park; Zhongshan Park; Yellow Crane Tower Park,Hubei Province; China; WC district; HY; QK; JA district; JH district; HS district; QS district; Yangtze River,,,"Nevertheless, the spatial distribution of parks is uneven, leading to an imbalance between the CES supply and demand which seriously restricts the equitable access of CESs.",,Dianping.com,reviews; review text content,,,,scores (1–5 stars),usernames; review times,"33,920",,"26,657",,,,,"July 1, 2023 to June 30, 2024",central area of Wuhan,crawl,Application programming interfaces (APIs),,data cleaning,,,,[],,sentiment classification,deep learning framework; pre-trained language model,ERNIE,fine-tuned and trained on a large-scale manually labeled Chinese sentiment corpus,dynamic embedding updates,,Chinese texts with subjective descriptions,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.,,,,Python,,urban parks; social media data; sentiment analysis; natural language processing (NLP); deep learning framework; pre-trained language model; GIS data; Baidu Heat Maps; mobile signaling; Tencent Location Big Data,,,
_Zhang et al.-2025-Spatiotemporal Patterns of Touri.txt,Spatiotemporal Patterns of Tourist Flows and Their Influencing Factors: A Case Study of Beijing,Xue Zhang; Qian Yu; Xiaochen Chen; Jingjing Shi; Xiaohui Huang; Lin Liu; Dong Guo,,2025,journal article,,,,http://bzdt.ch.mnr.gov.cn/,,,Cultural Heritage (2); cultural heritage (1),cultural (1),historical heritage (1); scenic spots (4); historical and cultural blocks (2); royal gardens (1); traditional commercial blocks (1); tourist attractions (16); world cultural heritage sites (1),cultural connotation (1); cultural (3); historical (1); world cultural heritage sites (1),,"{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions the importance of cultural connotation and experiential aspects of destinations, suggesting a focus on cultural identity and a sense of place attachment.'}; {'value': 'Entertainment', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the role of tourism in the contemporary market and the shift towards cultural decoding, indicating the entertainment value of heritage assets.'}; {'value': 'Educational', 'reasoning': 'The paper highlights the growing tourist interest in local cultural characteristics and artistic experiences, suggesting an educational role of heritage assets.'}; {'value': 'Symbolic', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions the symbolic status of Tiananmen Square and the emergence of symbolic consumption, indicating the emblematic expressions of power and prosperity associated with the asset.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetic', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the shift towards cultural decoding and artistic experiences, suggesting an appreciation for the artistic and notable aspects of heritage sites.'}",sustainable development (1); tourism management (1); conservation (1),Beijing (18); Palace Museum (2); Tiananmen Square (2); Summer Palace (2); Temple of Heaven (1); Badaling Great Wall (2); South Luogu Lane (1); Shichahai (1); Yuanmingyuan (2); Tsinghua University (2); Peking University (2); Universal Studios (3),Beijing (13); China (4); Beijing–Tianjin–Hebei region (2); Yangtze River Delta (1); Hong Kong (1); South Korea (1); Yellow River Basin (1); Jiaodong Peninsula (1),,COVID-19 pandemic (2),"Despite its importance, the structural characteristics and underlying mechanisms of tourist flows between different scenic spots within Beijing remain underexplored. Most existing studies focus primarily on individual attractions or tourist satisfaction, lacking a network-based approach that quantifies interaction patterns and dynamic evolution processes","Therefore, this study aims to construct a dual-perspective analytical framework integrating “tourist perception” and “tourist flow networks.” Specifically, it combines gravity center models, SNA, and QAP regression analysis to examine the spatiotemporal evolution and influencing factors of tourist flows in Beijing from 2012 to 2024.",social media (3); online booking platforms (1); Qunar.com (3),digital footprint (5); location-based services (LBSs) (1); behavioral data (1); online travel diaries (6); text (2); images (2); comments (1); GPS traces (1); check-in data (1); spatial coordinates (1); mobile phone signaling data (1),,spatial patterns (4); spatial distribution (2); spatiotemporal evolution (5); spatial structure (5); spatial connectivity (2); spatial dynamics (1); spatial migration patterns (1); spatial scanning window (1),spatiotemporal evolution (5); temporal dynamics (1); temporal validity (1); temporal evolutionary characteristics (1); time periods (1); annual (1),ratings (2),,,,,,,,,2012 to 2024 (4),Beijing (1),publicly available APIs from third-party online travel platforms (1),,,"regional clipping, scenic spot consolidation, data deduplication, and the removal of isolated points (1)",,,,[],,sentiment analysis (2); text analysis (1),,ROSTCM6 (1),,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,"['social network (5)', 'tourism flow network (12)', 'spatial correlation network (2)', 'association network (1)']",tourist attractions (nodes) (6),tourist flows between attractions (2),,,degree centrality (4); closeness centrality (4); betweenness centrality (4),"Based on a binary matrix of tourist flows between attractions, an association network is constructed to examine the relationships among them (1)",,,examine the relationships among them (1),Frequency (4); intensity (3); percentage (7); correlation coefficient (2); significance level (2),[],[],[],P1 < 0.05 (3); P1 < 0.01 (3); P2 < 0.05 (1),"Overall tourist satisfaction remains high, with a marked increase in the proportion of positive emotional expressions. Tourist interest has gradually shifted from traditional iconic attractions to more diverse and culturally rich experiences, reflecting an ongoing upgrading of tourism demand (1)",[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,"['tourism experiences have shifted from “natural perception” to “cultural decoding” (1)', 'the co-occurrence intensity of the “culture–tourism–history” triad increased (1)']",[],[],"['Overall tourist satisfaction remains high, with a marked increase in the proportion of positive emotional expressions (1)']",[],"['the overall gravity center of Beijing’s tourism flow has remained relatively stable (1)', 'Spatial linkages among tourist attractions have generally strengthened over time, and the centrality and influence of core nodes have continued to rise (1)']","['evidence-based planning of tourism resources (1)', 'promotion of coordinated regional development (1)']",[],"['This dual-perspective approach provides a fresh analytical framework for examining urban tourism flows (1)', 'the study introduces the Baidu Index as a novel proxy for online attention to assess how online information-seeking behavior influences tourism decision-making in the digital era (1)']",[],[],"['Due to limitations in data access (i.e., reliance on publicly available APIs from third-party online travel platforms), we were unable to directly obtain key demographic characteristics (1)', 'the current analysis focuses exclusively on digital footprints within Beijing and does not account for spatial interactions between Beijing and its surrounding regions (1)', 'Although this study considers a wide range of influencing factors, due to time and resource constraints, some potentially important variables may still have been excluded (1)']",[],"['there may be concentration in user age, consumption habits, etc., which may introduce selection bias and limit the generalizability of findings to the broader tourist population (1)']",[],[],[],[],[],"['limitations of the data should be acknowledged (1)', 'limitations of indicator selection should be acknowledged (1)']",,Data are contained within the article (1),,,,,,digital footprint relational data (1); dual-perspective analytical framework (2); tourist perception–tourist flow network (1); center-of-gravity model (2); social network analysis (SNA) (7); regression models (1); QAP regression analysis (2); gravity center models (1); Quadratic Assignment Procedure (QAP) (2); Arc GIS (4); UCINET (3); Baidu Index (3); SWOT analysis (3); mobile phone signaling data (1),,,
_Zhang et al.-2025-Textual Analysis of Cities' Imag.txt,Textual Analysis of Cities’ Images in Publicity:,,,,other,,,,,,,,,cities,,,,,Shanghai; New York,Shanghai; New York; China; Asia; America; United States,,,comprehension of how these promotional videos resonate with potential visitors and what images they project to the audience remains a critical challenge.,"This study attempts to address this issue by utilising an approach that combines natural language processing (NLP) and corpus analysis techniques. We analysed the comments associated with promotional videos of both Shanghai and New York, focusing on their linguistic features and cultural nuances. Through a com- parative analysis of comments data, this research seeks to identify similarities and differences in the cities’ images shaped by these promotional efforts across distinctive cultural contexts (the eastern Shanghai and the western New York).",You Tube,text,,,,,,"7,265",,"7,265",,,,,,Shanghai and New York,,,,,,,,[],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,yes,['collocation'],,,,,Mutual Information,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['To ensure anonymity, poster ID information was deleted; the comments gathered are strictly for academic research.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,,spa Cy,,social computation,,,
_Zhao and Wang-2025-A multi-dimensional evaluation s.txt,Discover Artificial Intelligence (2025) 5:43,Yilei Wang,"1School of art and design, Dalian Polytechnic University, Dalian 116034, China.; 2College of Landscape Architecture, Huazhong Agricultural University, Wuhan 430070, China.",2025,other,,,,http:// creat iveco mmons. org/ licen ses/ by- nc- nd/4. 0/.,,,,,suburban parks (17); Wuhan Garden Expo Park (8); Traditional Garden (2),,,,,Wuhan Garden Expo Park,China (6); Wuhan (7); Qiaokou District (2); Shanghai,,,"While traditional visitor perception studies rely heavily on questionnaire surveys with limited samples, there is a lack of data-driven approaches that can comprehensively capture and analyze large-scale visitor feedback.","This study employs machine learning methods, particularly the Latent Dirichlet Allocation topic model, to analyze large-scale User Generated Content data collected from Xiecheng.com, one of China’s largest online review platforms. Based on the case study of Wuhan Garden Expo Park, this research constructing a multi-dimensional evaluation framework for visitor perceptions in suburban parks.",Xiecheng.com (5),User Generated Content (UGC) data (4); online reviews (3); review text; rating; review time; reviewer ID,,,review time,rating,reviewer ID; review time,5213 reviews collected,93 unique users,"3,099 valid reviews",,,,,"September 25, 2015 to August 31, 2024",Wuhan,A web crawler program was developed using Python programming language to collect all reviews of the Wuhan Garden Expo Park on Xiecheng.com.,Python programming language,,"Duplicate records were rigorously identified and removed based on a composite key of Reviewer ID and Review Time. Reviews were considered duplicates only if they shared identical user ID, timestamp, and content. For reviews from the same user at different time points, all unique entries were retained to capture potential temporal variations in visitor perceptions, even if they expressed contrasting opinions.","After removing duplicate reviews and those with empty content or containing only emoticons, punctuation marks were standardized, special characters were removed, and all text was converted to simplified Chinese.",,,[],,topic modeling; sentiment analysis,machine learning,Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) topic model (8); BERT; GPT,,,word segmentation; frequency statistics,review texts,,,,,,,100,,,{},,,,,,,,"First, we use the Natural Language Toolkit (NLTK) library in Python to perform word segmentation and frequency statistics on the preprocessed review texts [22]. By analyzing high-frequency words, we can preliminarily understand visitors’ overall impressions and focus points regarding the Wuhan Garden Expo Park. Subsequently, we use ROSTCM 6.0 software for semantic network analysis, constructing a word co-occurrence network to visualize the key elements of visitor perceptions and their interrelationships, thus revealing the structural features of visitor perceptions [23, 24]. Next, this study employs the Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) topic model to identify and extract potential dimensions of visitor perceptions [25].",yes,"['semantic network', 'word co-occurrence network']",feature words,associations between these feature words,,,,semantic network analysis,,Gephi software,"to visualize the key elements of visitor perceptions and their interrelationships, thus revealing the structural features of visitor perceptions",perplexity; coherence; chi-square goodness-of-fit test; one-way ANOVA; Tukey HSD tests,[],[],[],"𝜒2(3) = 294.7, p < 0.001; F(3, 3095) = 23.14 , p < 0.001; 휂2 = 0.12",,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],word clouds; semantic network graphs; py LDAvis library; scatter plots,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['park management', 'policymaking']","['balanced resource allocation', 'expectation management', 'development of cultural and educational experiences aspects']",['application of advanced deep learning models (such as BERT and GPT) could enhance sentiment analysis accuracy and capture more nuanced emotional expressions in visitor reviews'],"['Four primary dimensions were identified: service quality and overall evaluation, natural landscape, recreational activities and entertainment, and cultural and educational experiences.']",[],"['the utilization of UGC\ndata from Xiecheng.com faces inherent self-selection challenges, as online reviewers typically represent more extreme experiences, potentially overlooking moderate viewpoints', 'The platform’s user demographics may also not fully reflect the entire spectrum of park visitors.', 'the single-case study approach, while enabling in-depth analysis of Wuhan Garden Expo Park, presents challenges in broader applicability.', 'While the LDA topic model and sentiment analysis improve research efficiency and accuracy, they face challenges in model interpretability and translating results into actionable management suggestions', 'temporal aggregation of reviews (2015–2024) presents analytical challenges in capturing dynamic changes across seasons, special events, and evolving park management practices.']","['only publicly visible review information was collected, and no user privacy data was involved.']",[],[],[],[],[],"['This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-No Derivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it.']",[],,No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.,,,,Natural Language Toolkit (NLTK); Python’s gensim library; Snow NLP toolkit; py LDAvis library,,visitor perceptions; suburban parks; LDA topic model; User-generated content (UGC); Multi-dimensional evaluation,,,
_Zhao et al.-2022-Exploring the Spatial Characteri.txt,Exploring the Spatial Characteristics of Stay Areas in Walking Tours through the Lens of Volunteered GPS Trajectories: A Case Study of the Zhuhai–Macao Metropolitan Area,"Zhao, M.; Zhang, Q.; Shi, H.; Liu, M.; Liang, J.",,2022,journal article,Forests,MDPI,https://doi.org/10.3390/ f13111800,https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/ 4.0/,,,,,,,,,,Zhuhai–Macao metropolitan area; Zhuhai City; Macao SAR; Doumen District; Jinwan District; Xiangzhou District; Phoenix Cave; Baizu Ridge; Feisha Col; Qi’ao Island; Jinjing Haoyuan Community; Student Activity Center; Changlong International Ocean Resort; Venetian Casino; Jingshan Park; Mingting Park; Xianren Pavilion; Guanyin Temple; Mangzhou Wetland; Shipaiwan Country Park,Zhuhai; Macao Special Administrative Region (SAR); China; Guangdong Province,,pandemic,"The existing research focuses mainly on functional, top-down classiﬁcations of tourism, tourist behavior patterns, and route designs, but it has left tourists’ stay areas largely unaddressed.","To ﬁll this gap, we propose a new framework for the interpretation of stay areas in walking tours based on GPS trajectory data and accompanying photos uploaded by users.",,GPS trajectory data; geotagged photos,GPS data; geotagged,spatial distribution,,,,,,,,,,,,Zhuhai–Macao metropolitan area,shared trajectories and geotagged photos uploaded by individual users,,,,,image recognition program (ADE20K),MIT open-source code,"['vegetation (plants, ﬂowers, grass, trees, and palms)', 'water', 'buildings', 'sky', 'roads and sidewalks', 'earth (sand, rock, and ﬁelds)', 'sea', 'mountains and hills', 'structures (canopies, railings, awnings, poles, etc.)']",,semantic segmentation,,ADE20K,,,,photos,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],['The present paper provides a new analytical framework for the study of walking tours using data from volunteered GPS trajectories.'],[],[],"['With the positioning errors or upload interruptions of GPS trajectories [44] being further reduced, this method can be combined with ﬁeld research to provide relatively judicious delineations for planning and implementation.']",['It should also be mentioned that the issue of privacy in the use of trajectory data is avoided here owing to VGI’s openness and the individual nature of its contributions.'],"['The research samples have certain constraints. In most cases, the VGI platform is mainly aimed at speciﬁc groups (mountain climbers and other outdoor enthusiasts). It can reﬂect the walking tour information volunteered by some travel enthusiasts, but it cannot represent all tourists.']",[],[],[],[],[],"['In dense forest areas, some GPS trajectories may present issues such as positioning errors or upload interruptions, which is a major limiting factor for GPS data.']",ADE20K which can be found here: [https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content_cvpr_20 17/html/Zhou_Scene_Parsing_Through_CVPR_2017_paper.html (accessed on 19 September 2022)],The trajectory data comes from a website named “2bulu”. The data is presented openly: [https: //www.2bulu.com/ (accessed on 19 September 2022)],,,,,,walking; stay area; GPS trajectory; DBSCAN; Zhuhai–Macao metropolitan area; VGI; 2bulu,,,
_Zhao et al.-2023-Exploring the current situation.txt,Science Direct,Ningxin Zhao,,2023,other,Procedia Computer Science 221 (2023) 826–832,,,,,826–832,,cultural,famous cultural city (1); cultural tourism (4); cultural scenic spots (3); historical and cultural city (1); cultural tourist attractions (1),culture (10); cultural characteristics (3); cultural communication (1); cultural themes (1); historical (1),,"{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses how cultural scenic spots can create their own cultural themes to improve attraction, suggesting a focus on cultural identity and pride.'}; {'value': 'Economic (Entertainment)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions tourism as a pillar industry and suggests improving service levels and launching cultural products, indicating a focus on the role of heritage in the contemporary market, especially tourism.'}; {'value': 'Political (Management)', 'reasoning': 'The paper suggests strengthening top-level design and overall planning, formulating local laws and regulations, and promoting the construction of scenic spots from the level of policies and laws, indicating the incorporation of the asset into political strategies and policies.'}; {'value': 'Historic (Educational)', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions Nanjing as a famous historical and cultural city with accumulated material and spiritual culture, suggesting the potential of the asset to provide knowledge about the past.'}; {'value': 'Aesthetical (Artistic)', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses the importance of scenic spot style and creating an immersive feeling for tourists, indicating a focus on the original product of creativity and imagination.'}",,"Nanjing, Jiangsu Province, China (2); Nanjing (7)",China (1); Jiangsu Province (1),Republic of China (2),,"However, there are few researches specifically aimed at cultural tourism at present.","Based on the text mining technologies including word frequency statistics and LDA (Latent Dirichlet Allocation) model, this paper finds out some features of Nanjing tourism and draws some conclusions.",Ctrip website (1),text (1),,,,,title (1); publication time (1),724,,,,,,,2018 to 2023,Nanjing,crawler technology,,,python was used to clean the collected data,"If there are multiple identical data, delete the redundant data and keep only one data. (2) Delete the blank data in the travel notes. (3) Delete data suspected to be advertising. For example, some hotels and restaurants publish travelogue advertisements for promotion. (4) Punctuation marks are not involved in subsequent analysis of the travel notes, so punctuation marks in the travel notes are removed.",,,[],,clustering,Topic Models,LDA (Latent Dirichlet Allocation) (6),,,word frequency statistics (2),text (1),,,,,0.1,,50,,,{'β': '0.01'},,,,,,,,"The LDA model automatically encodes the travelogue text dataset into a set of substantively meaningful categories, which are topics [12]. The number of topics needs to be specified humanly, run the LDA model with the specified number of topics to get the probability distribution of words in each topic and the corresponding topic probability of each travelogue.",no,[],,,,,,,,,,perplexity (3); coherence (3),[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],['Cultural tourist attractions should make good use of this advantage to promote the close combination of tourism and cultural communication.'],"['Positioning for the scenic spot: Cultural scenic spots should create their own cultural themes, highlight the culture of the scenic spot and improve the attraction of the scenic spot.', 'Build informationized scenic spots: Develop a special information system to provide convenience for tourists, which can be used for booking tickets, planning tour routes, observing the number of scenic spots, etc.', 'Improve the hardware facilities of the scenic spot: (1) Hotels and catering in improving the level of service at the same time to launch cultural products; (2) For both inside and outside the scenic area, to create a convenient and distinctive green transportation mode; (3) Pay attention to the special needs of scenic spots, set up maternal and infant rooms, lounge, luggage storage areas, and add scenic spot guidance facilities.', ""Dynamic monitoring of tourist demand of scenic spots: pay more attention to tourist demand, ensure that the tourist destination has full appeal to tourists, and build tourists' image perception of the tourist destination."", 'Strengthen top-level design and overall planning, formulate local laws and regulations according to local conditions, and promote the construction of scenic spots from the level of policies and laws.']",[],[],[],"['In this paper, all the travel notes of a scenic spot are uniformly processed when the data model is built. If more detailed factors such as the age, gender and travel time of tourists can be reflected in the modeling of travel data, more detailed conclusions about cultural tourism can be drawn.', 'In addition, Nanjing selected in this paper is a popular cultural tourism scenic spot, and the extraction and analysis of its travel information can only serve as a reference for cultural scenic spots with a large number of tourists. Some cultural scenic spots are in a situation where the number of tourists is small and unknown, and the development of these cities with cultural characteristics that have not yet developed their tourism needs special research.']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],,,,,python,,,scenic spots (11); travel notes (7); tourism (14); Internet era (2); UGC (User-Generated Content) (5); text mining (3); word frequency (3); crawler technology (1); stop word list (1); high-frequency words (2); travelogue (5),,,
