_Zheng et al.-2025-On-the-Ground Application of Clo.txt,Article ABSTRACT: Post-occupancy evaluation is a critical mechanism for ensuring the sustained success and continuous improvement of industrial heritage revitalization initiatives. The quality of the visitor experience plays a key role in determining a project’s long-term vitality.,"Zheng, X.; Heath, T.; Guo, S.",,2025,other,Sustainability,MDPI,,https://www.miit.gov.cn/zwgk/zcwj/wjfb/tz/art/2024/art_fb72ba03c3f441a28ed521c4debc492a.html,,,industrial heritage (7),,industrial heritage site (2); 1933 Old Millfun (4); industrial heritage reuse contexts,historical preservation; historical integrity; historical and architectural elements; cultural significance; historical experience; restorative regeneration; architectural integrity; spatial aesthetics; heritage; sustainable development of industrial heritage sites; industrial heritage regeneration; unique value and character of the regenerated industrial heritage,,"{'value': 'Emotional (collective)', 'reasoning': ""The text mentions cultural legibility and the need for interpretive communication to convey the site's cultural significance, enhancing emotional engagement and a sense of place attachment.""}; {'value': 'Aesthetical Values', 'reasoning': 'The text discusses the aesthetic distinctiveness of the site, attributing it to the original 1933 design and meticulous preservation, highlighting artistic and notable aspects.'}; {'value': 'Historic Values', 'reasoning': 'The text emphasizes the historical preservation efforts, retaining high authenticity and historical integrity, and the immersive historical experience the building offers.'}; {'value': 'Economic Values', 'reasoning': 'The text discusses the commercial appeal of the site and the need for a more strategic integration of commerce with heritage to enhance the competitiveness of on-site retail outlets.'}",heritage revitalization initiatives; historical preservation; renovation,"1933 Old Millfun in Shanghai, China; Shanghai 1933 Old Millfun",Shanghai; China; Europe,1933,,Post-occupancy evaluation is a critical mechanism for ensuring the sustained success and continuous improvement of industrial heritage revitalization initiatives. The quality of the visitor experience plays a key role in determining a project’s long-term vitality.,This study focuses on assessing user satisfaction with a revitalized industrial heritage site by employing web crawling and data mining techniques to systematically collect and ana- lyze user-generated reviews from major online platforms.,online platforms,text; user-generated reviews; sentiment data,,,,,,3643,,,,,,,past four years,Shanghai,web crawling and data mining techniques,,,,,,,[],,sentiment analysis,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],,[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],"['Although this research adopts a user-centric perspective, public users have certain limitations. Individual users often focus on personal experiences and lack the ability to evaluate projects from a holistic or professional standpoint, as they are neither architects nor operators.']",,The authors confirm that the data supporting the findings of this study are available within the article.,,,,,,user-generated content; online platforms; web crawling; data mining; semantic analysis software; high-frequency keywords; fuzzy set theory; macro-level planning; urban infrastructure,,,
_Zheng et al.-2025-Quantifying tourist perception o.txt,Quantifying tourist perception of cultural landscapes in traditional towns using multimodal data and explainable AI,X.J. Zheng; Y. Huang; Z.M. Xu; A.M. Zhang,,2025,journal article,,,https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-025-01907-4,https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-025-01907-4,,,,cultural,traditional towns; traditional town landscapes,cultural landscape; cultural heritage conservation; cultural features; cultural background; cultural connotations; cultural atmosphere; cultural displays; cultural aesthetic experience; historical; spiritual cultural expressions; cultural heritage itself; cultural authenticity; cultural character,aesthetic value,aesthetic; economic; social; historic,cultural heritage conservation,traditional towns in China; Shanghai,China; Guangdong,,,,Thisquantiﬁestheimportanceofvariousfeaturesintouristevaluations.,Ctrip,text; images; multimodal data,,,,ratings,sentiment values; user ratings,,,"131,596","382,026",,,,,114traditionaltowns in China,,,,invalid and duplicate reviews were removed,reviews with fewer than 10 characters are removed,,eight university researchers in the ﬁeld of landscape design,"['environment', 'infrastructure', 'experience']",,classification; regression; sentiment analysis,Transformer models; Embedding-based models,SentenceTransformer; CLIP; XGBoost; SHAP,,embedding vectors,,text; images; fused features,,,,,,,,,Random horizontal ﬂipping; maximum 10-degree rotation,{},,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,Pearson correlation coefﬁcient,[],[],[],,,[],[],,[],[],[],,,[],Heatmap,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['cultural heritage conservation', 'landscape optimization', 'tourism management']",[],[],[],[],"['The data mainly rely on social media, which may lead to user group bias.', 'While the SHAP method offers good interpretability, it is affected by feature correlations and cannot accurately infer causal relationships.', 'The current label system focuses on environment, facilities, and experience, with limited representation of cultural features and tourist behavior patterns.', 'The study samples are concentrated in speciﬁc regions and have not yet systematically accounted for cultural background differences.']",[],[],[],[],['Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-No Derivatives 4.0 International License'],[],[],[],,No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.,,,,PyTorch; TensorFlow; scikit-learn,,traditional town; landscape design; sentiment scores; Jieba segmentation tool; Boson NLP tool; hash algorithm; traditional town tagging system; landscape characteristics; tourist experiences; SentenceTransformer model; CLIP (Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training) model; SHAP method; XGBoost; water towns; original ecological towns; religious and cultural towns; commercial and tradeoriented towns,,,
_Zhou et al.-2025-Evaluating Urban Visual Attracti.txt,Evaluating Urban Visual Attractiveness with Multimodal Large Language Models,Qian Zhou; Jiawei Zhang; Zhenhong Zhou,,2025,journal article,ISPRS Int. J. Geo-Inf.,MDPI,,,,,,,urban landscape; streetscapes; urban environments; historic neighbourhoods; historic streets; urban spaces,Cultural Significance; cultural heritage,,"{'value': 'Aesthetical', 'reasoning': 'The paper focuses on visual attractiveness and aesthetic appeal of urban environments, which directly relates to aesthetical values. The study evaluates the harmony of architectural elements, the presence and quality of green spaces, and the spatial layout.'}; {'value': 'Social', 'reasoning': 'The paper mentions that visual attractiveness has direct implications for well-being, economic vitality, and social cohesion. It also discusses how attractive urban landscapes improve subjective well-being and strengthen communal bonds and mutual support.'}; {'value': 'Economic', 'reasoning': 'The paper discusses how visual attractiveness plays a crucial role in fostering economic development by attracting tourists and investors, increasing property values, and retaining talent.'}",,Yuzhong District; Chongqing; China; Jiefangbei; Chaotianmen; Hongyadong; Shibati,Chongqing; China; Beijing; Kolkata; Stockholm; Sweden; Guangzhou; Poland; Shanghai; Jiangxi Province,,,"However, most empirical studies rely on single-source metrics or algorithm-centric pipelines that under-represent human perception. Addressing this gap, we introduce a fully reproducible, multimodal framework that measures and models this domain-specific facet of human intelligence by coupling Generative Pre-trained Transformer 4o (GPT-4o) with 1000 Street View images.","(1) It introduces an automated framework for assessing urban landscape attractiveness based on GPT-4o, a multimodal large language model. (2) Based on the pairwise comparison methodology of MIT Place Pulse 2.0, we constructed a dataset of 1020 real-world street-view images for visual attractiveness assessment. (3) The MLLM-driven framework proposed in this study offers a novel quantitative decision-support tool for urban design.",,images; street view images; street-view imagery; panoramic street-view imagery; remote sensing imagery,,spatial distribution; spatial syntax analysis; spatial configuration; spatial intelligibility; spatial prominence; spatial accessibility; spatial distribution mapping,,,,1000,,,8000,,,,between 2017 and 2022,Chongqing’s Yuzhong District,dataset was sourced from Baidu Street View,,"representative subset of 1020 images from the original dataset, numbered sequentially from 1 to 1020. To reduce the workload associated with human-model comparison, a randomised algorithm was employed to extract 100 images as the primary evaluation set.",,,human annotation,human raters,"['more attractive', 'indistinguishable', 'less attractive']",,image classification; aesthetic comparisons; pairwise comparison,Transformer models; Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN); multimodal large language models (MLLMs); deep learning models,GPT-4o; GPT-4; Deep U-Net; ECA-Mobile Net V3 (Large)+Seg Net; LLa VA,,,,street-view images; visual features; colour; proportion; contour,,,,,,,,,,{},Siamese CNN baseline,,,,,,,,no,[],,,,,,,,,,Spearman ρ; coefficient of determination (R2); Z-score normalisation,"['Spearman ρ = 0.76, p < 0.001', 'Siamese CNN baseline (ρ = 0.60)', 'GPT-4o vs. human scores: R2 = 0.695', 'Place Pulse 2.0 vs. human scores: R2 = 0.385']",[],[],,"humans tended to emphasise subjective emotion and immersive atmosphere—highlighting elements such as the striking red bridge, the sense of openness from the sky, and the emotional undertone of emptiness in the absence of people. In contrast, GPT-4o focused on structural balance and visual composition, emphasising the harmony between built and natural elements and identifying issues such as foreground ambiguity and lack of human activity that weakened overall expressiveness.",[],"GPT-4o exhibits significantly higher alignment with human perception compared to Place Pulse 2.0, underscoring its superior capacity to model subjective aesthetic judgement in urban environments.","a notable limitation of GPT-4o lies in its ability to perceive safety-related cues. For instance, images depicting construction zones or dense traffic are often scored significantly lower by humans, while GPT-4o and Place Pulse 2.0 may continue to assign moderate scores based on visual symmetry or colour harmony, overlooking the perceived sense of risk.",[],"MLLMs may have overfitted to clear, open street views and may thus under-recognise the aesthetic value of natural elements like shade and foliage. As a result, models misclassify these features as visual noise rather than aesthetic enhancers.",[],,Place Pulse 2.0,[],visual mapping; point density map,[],[],[],[],[],[],"['urban planning', 'landscape design', 'tourism', 'commerce', 'smart governance', 'academic research']",[],"['automated framework for assessing urban landscape attractiveness based on GPT-4o', 'dataset of 1020 real-world street-view images for visual attractiveness assessment', 'MLLM-driven framework offers a novel quantitative decision-support tool for urban design']",[],[],"['aesthetic judgement is inherently subjective and can vary across cultures and contexts, which makes it challenging for these models to account for all nuances in human perception', 'MLLMs may struggle with recognising finer scene details, potentially missing key aspects that are crucial for accurate urban evaluation', 'Training bias is another concern, as models may misinterpret regional symbols or historical architectural styles if their training data is not sufficiently diverse, resulting in a misalignment with authentic human perception', 'scale mismatch, as streetscapes involve multi-scale cues that range from small details, like façade elements, to large-scale features such as city skylines, which are difficult to capture accurately with fixedresolution models', 'the absence of dynamic and multisensory input limits the model’s ability to replicate the real-life ambiance of urban spaces, which is shaped by factors like pedestrian density, noise, olfactory cues, and changing light conditions throughout the day', 'these models lack a strong socio-functional context, which is crucial in urban aesthetics. Urban aesthetics are deeply intertwined with aspects like safety, accessibility, and cultural vibrancy, yet GPT’s semantic embeddings do not have structured representations of these dimensions, making them less useful for policy-making and urban planning']",[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],Data and materials are available from the authors upon request.,Data and materials are available from the authors upon request.,,,,,,visual attractiveness; aesthetic judgement; street view; urban planning; landscape physiognomy; visual complexity; spatial syntax; spatial intelligibility; spatial prominence; spatial accessibility; D/H ratio; building silhouette continuity; colour psychology; spatial openness; layout quality; natural elements; greenery; lighting; visual design; safety infrastructure; pedestrian pathways; traffic management systems; urban streetscapes; MLLMs; GPT-4o; CNNs; deep learning models; Baidu Street View; MIT Place Pulse 2.0; Yuzhong District; Jiefangbei; Chaotianmen; Hongyadong; Shibati,,,
